INSERT INTO `flashcards` (`id`, `deck_id`, `front`, `back`, `front_body`, `back_body`, `hint`, `tags`, `difficulty`, `source`, `position`, `created_at`) VALUES
('9768e8ad-0b80-416e-9c77-a0ed57224716','55abecb2-4d23-4b8c-aeb1-ae300eef1ea2','How are DNA fragments visualized after separation in gel electrophoresis?','The gel must be stained with a compound called Ethidium Bromide (EtBr) and then exposed to Ultraviolet (UV) radiation. The DNA bands appear as bright orange.',NULL,NULL,'Requires a toxic stain and blacklight.','["neet", "biology", "biotechnology", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',4,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('5c83539c-cafa-4bc1-b394-001cd81e52c0','55abecb2-4d23-4b8c-aeb1-ae300eef1ea2','What is the function of the \'ori\' (Origin of Replication) in a cloning vector?','It is the DNA sequence where replication starts. Any piece of DNA linked to this sequence can be made to replicate within the host cells. It is also responsible for controlling the copy number of the linked DNA.',NULL,NULL,'The starting line for DNA copying.','["neet", "biology", "biotechnology", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',5,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('5c70423d-b6f3-4754-aaa6-e811b9382449','55abecb2-4d23-4b8c-aeb1-ae300eef1ea2','Explain the concept of \'Insertional Inactivation\' using the beta-galactosidase gene.','When a foreign DNA is inserted into the coding sequence of the beta-galactosidase enzyme, it inactivates the gene. \nResult: Recombinant colonies cannot produce the enzyme and appear white, whereas non-recombinants produce the enzyme and appear blue in the presence of a chromogenic substrate.',NULL,NULL,'Blue = Non-recombinant. White = Recombinant.','["neet", "biology", "biotechnology", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',6,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('7346a28f-25dc-44cc-bbe4-8a6710bfb8ec','55abecb2-4d23-4b8c-aeb1-ae300eef1ea2','Name the direct gene transfer methods primarily used for animal cells and plant cells, respectively.','Animal cells: Microinjection (recombinant DNA is directly injected into the nucleus of an animal cell).\nPlant cells: Biolistics / Gene gun (cells are bombarded with high-velocity microparticles of gold or tungsten coated with DNA).',NULL,NULL,'Animals get shots (injections), plants get shot (guns).','["neet", "biology", "biotechnology", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',7,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('5ef1e420-5f75-4f6f-b5d9-b62a57743717','55abecb2-4d23-4b8c-aeb1-ae300eef1ea2','What are the three main steps of the Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR), and which specific enzyme is utilized?','1. Denaturation (Separating strands at high temp).\n2. Annealing (Primers bind to DNA).\n3. Extension (Synthesis of new strand).\nEnzyme used: Taq polymerase (a thermostable enzyme isolated from the bacterium *Thermus aquaticus*).',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "svg", "value": "<svg width=\\"300\\" height=\\"150\\" xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\"><rect x=\\"10\\" y=\\"10\\" width=\\"80\\" height=\\"130\\" fill=\\"#ffcccc\\" rx=\\"5\\"/><text x=\\"15\\" y=\\"30\\" font-family=\\"sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12\\" font-weight=\\"bold\\">1. Denature</text><text x=\\"15\\" y=\\"50\\" font-family=\\"sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"10\\">~94°C</text><rect x=\\"105\\" y=\\"10\\" width=\\"80\\" height=\\"130\\" fill=\\"#ccffcc\\" rx=\\"5\\"/><text x=\\"110\\" y=\\"30\\" font-family=\\"sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12\\" font-weight=\\"bold\\">2. Anneal</text><text x=\\"110\\" y=\\"50\\" font-family=\\"sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"10\\">~50-65°C</text><text x=\\"110\\" y=\\"80\\" font-family=\\"sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"10\\">Primers</text><text x=\\"110\\" y=\\"95\\" font-family=\\"sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"10\\">bind</text><rect x=\\"200\\" y=\\"10\\" width=\\"80\\" height=\\"130\\" fill=\\"#ccccff\\" rx=\\"5\\"/><text x=\\"205\\" y=\\"30\\" font-family=\\"sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12\\" font-weight=\\"bold\\">3. Extend</text><text x=\\"205\\" y=\\"50\\" font-family=\\"sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"10\\">~72°C</text><text x=\\"205\\" y=\\"80\\" font-family=\\"sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"10\\">Taq</text><text x=\\"205\\" y=\\"95\\" font-family=\\"sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"10\\">Polymerase</text></svg>"}]}','Unzip, Prime, Build.','["neet", "biology", "biotechnology", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',8,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('e31673f0-cbe5-4115-80c2-bb849fa72ebf','55abecb2-4d23-4b8c-aeb1-ae300eef1ea2','What does \'Downstream Processing\' involve in biotechnology?','It refers to the series of processes—primarily Separation and Purification—that a synthesized product must undergo before it is formulated with preservatives and marketed as a finished product.',NULL,NULL,'The final cleanup and packaging step.','["neet", "biology", "biotechnology", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',9,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('1f84de7c-f58c-4941-8a47-01fcbe16d130','55abecb2-4d23-4b8c-aeb1-ae300eef1ea2','How does the Bt toxin kill insects without harming the *Bacillus thuringiensis* bacterium?','The Bt toxin exists as an inactive protoxin within the bacterium. It converts into an active form only when exposed to the alkaline pH of the insect\'s gut. The active toxin binds to midgut epithelial cells, creating pores that cause cell swelling and lysis.',NULL,NULL,'Alkaline pH is the trigger.','["neet", "biology", "biotechnology", "applications", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',10,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('b2d5ee45-093f-410e-ae73-5dbc42ecfbc1','55abecb2-4d23-4b8c-aeb1-ae300eef1ea2','Which specific *cry* genes control the cotton bollworm and corn borer, respectively?','Cotton bollworm: Controlled by *cryIAc* and *cryIIAb*.\nCorn borer: Controlled by *cryIAb*.',NULL,NULL,'Ac and IIAb for Cotton. IAb for Corn.','["neet", "biology", "biotechnology", "applications", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',11,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('85056455-b4bd-4823-9787-8fc6b8c5b140','55abecb2-4d23-4b8c-aeb1-ae300eef1ea2','What is the biological mechanism used to make tobacco plants resistant to the nematode *Meloidogyne incognita*?','RNA interference (RNAi). It acts as a method of cellular defense. It involves silencing a specific mRNA of the nematode using a complementary double-stranded RNA (dsRNA), which binds to and prevents the translation of the mRNA.',NULL,NULL,'Silencing the messenger.','["neet", "biology", "biotechnology", "applications", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',12,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('4f70cd8c-1924-45d6-8b28-bab6f27c3d66','55abecb2-4d23-4b8c-aeb1-ae300eef1ea2','What was the major scientific challenge in producing human insulin in bacteria, and how did Eli Lilly (1983) solve it?','Challenge: Human pro-insulin contains an extra \'C-peptide\' that is naturally removed during maturation, which bacteria cannot do.\nSolution: Eli Lilly prepared two separate DNA sequences corresponding to Chain A and Chain B, introduced them into *E. coli* plasmids, extracted them separately, and linked them via disulfide bonds.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "svg", "value": "<svg width=\\"250\\" height=\\"120\\" xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\"><rect x=\\"50\\" y=\\"30\\" width=\\"150\\" height=\\"15\\" fill=\\"#66b3ff\\" rx=\\"5\\"/><text x=\\"100\\" y=\\"42\\" font-family=\\"sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"10\\" fill=\\"white\\">Chain A (21 aa)</text><rect x=\\"30\\" y=\\"80\\" width=\\"190\\" height=\\"15\\" fill=\\"#ff9999\\" rx=\\"5\\"/><text x=\\"100\\" y=\\"92\\" font-family=\\"sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"10\\" fill=\\"white\\">Chain B (30 aa)</text><line x1=\\"80\\" y1=\\"45\\" x2=\\"80\\" y2=\\"80\\" stroke=\\"black\\" stroke-width=\\"2\\" stroke-dasharray=\\"2\\"/><line x1=\\"160\\" y1=\\"45\\" x2=\\"160\\" y2=\\"80\\" stroke=\\"black\\" stroke-width=\\"2\\" stroke-dasharray=\\"2\\"/><text x=\\"65\\" y=\\"65\\" font-family=\\"sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"10\\" font-weight=\\"bold\\">S-S</text><text x=\\"165\\" y=\\"65\\" font-family=\\"sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"10\\" font-weight=\\"bold\\">S-S</text></svg>"}]}','They skipped the C-peptide and built A and B separately.','["neet", "biology", "biotechnology", "applications", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',13,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('936227dc-cd8d-4b21-9de4-6ee75a52a218','55abecb2-4d23-4b8c-aeb1-ae300eef1ea2','What was the first clinical gene therapy (1990) given for, and what is considered its ultimate cure?','Given for: Adenosine Deaminase (ADA) deficiency, which causes Severe Combined Immunodeficiency (SCID).\nUltimate cure: Isolating the ADA gene from marrow cells and introducing it into the patient\'s cells at the early embryonic stages.',NULL,NULL,'ADA is crucial for immune system function.','["neet", "biology", "biotechnology", "applications", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',14,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('b0a0436b-ed5d-46d1-9d96-eacdb9b56b33','55abecb2-4d23-4b8c-aeb1-ae300eef1ea2','What is the underlying principle of the ELISA test used for molecular diagnosis?','ELISA (Enzyme-Linked Immunosorbent Assay) is based on the principle of antigen-antibody interaction. Infection by a pathogen can be detected by the presence of antigens (proteins, glycoproteins) or by detecting the antibodies synthesized against the pathogen.',NULL,NULL,'Lock and key matching of immune system components.','["neet", "biology", "biotechnology", "applications", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',15,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('cd6d762b-adc9-4700-b4d7-a7ee6886bed9','55abecb2-4d23-4b8c-aeb1-ae300eef1ea2','What was the name of the first transgenic cow, and what was unique about her milk?','Name: Rosie (produced in 1997).\nUnique feature: Her milk was human alpha-lactalbumin enriched (2.4 grams/litre), making it a more nutritionally balanced product for human babies than natural cow milk.',NULL,NULL,'A rosy future for baby formula.','["neet", "biology", "biotechnology", "applications", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',16,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('95fa6a1d-6d69-4da8-bc22-3eb756c8675e','55abecb2-4d23-4b8c-aeb1-ae300eef1ea2','Which specific human respiratory disease is currently treated using a biological product (alpha-1-antitrypsin) obtained from transgenic animals?','Emphysema.',NULL,NULL,'A chronic lung condition often caused by smoking.','["neet", "biology", "biotechnology", "applications", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',17,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('4fd6bba4-5e5b-4adf-a603-33c42e6bc010','55abecb2-4d23-4b8c-aeb1-ae300eef1ea2','What is the GEAC in India, and what is its primary responsibility?','GEAC stands for the Genetic Engineering Appraisal Committee (set up by the Indian Government).\nResponsibility: It makes decisions regarding the validity of Genetically Modified (GM) research and assesses the safety of introducing GM organisms for public services.',NULL,NULL,'The biotech watchdog of India.','["neet", "biology", "biotechnology", "ethics", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',18,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('2495e587-3c80-48a1-9061-21e77e3f9cac','55abecb2-4d23-4b8c-aeb1-ae300eef1ea2','Define \'Biopiracy\' and provide a classic example involving India.','Biopiracy is the use of bio-resources by multinational companies and other organizations without proper authorization from the countries and people concerned, without compensatory payment.\nExample: An American company gaining a US patent for Basmati rice in 1997.',NULL,NULL,'Stealing indigenous biological knowledge.','["neet", "biology", "biotechnology", "ethics", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',19,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('d2082985-744a-4861-897a-4fc423af105d','55abecb2-4d23-4b8c-aeb1-ae300eef1ea2','In a continuous culture system (bioreactors), why is fresh medium added continuously while used medium is drained?','To maintain the cells in their physiologically most active log/exponential phase. This produces a larger biomass and higher yields of the desired recombinant protein compared to a closed batch culture.',NULL,NULL,'Keeps the cells \'young and hungry\'.','["neet", "biology", "biotechnology", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',20,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('9d8bc063-b0ae-43f3-bd3b-fb70f47cea99','59515d1a-17a4-48cc-a95f-cf8eb28f3d94','Which concentration terms are independent of temperature and which are dependent?','Temperature Independent: Molality ($m$), Mole fraction ($x$), Mass percentage (w/w). \nTemperature Dependent: Molarity ($M$), Volume percentage (v/v), Mass by volume percentage (w/v).',NULL,NULL,'Volume changes with temperature, mass does not.','["chemistry", "solutions", "concentration", "neet-favorite"]','medium','Neet Buddy',0,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('10ee9aef-3aaf-4250-97ed-c198aa3fa15f','59515d1a-17a4-48cc-a95f-cf8eb28f3d94','State Henry\'s Law regarding the solubility of a gas in a liquid.','The partial pressure of the gas in the vapour phase ($p$) is proportional to the mole fraction of the gas ($x$) in the solution.\n$p = K_H \\cdot x$',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "Higher the value of $K_H$ at a given pressure, lower is the solubility of the gas in the liquid."}]}','$K_H$ is Henry\'s law constant.','["chemistry", "solutions", "henrys-law"]','medium','Neet Buddy',1,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('8367674c-653e-4a88-9ca7-8d848f63f7fd','59515d1a-17a4-48cc-a95f-cf8eb28f3d94','State Raoult\'s Law for a solution of volatile liquids.','For a solution of volatile liquids, the partial vapour pressure of each component of the solution is directly proportional to its mole fraction present in solution.\n$p_1 = p_1^0 x_1$',NULL,NULL,'$p_1^0$ is the vapour pressure of the pure component.','["chemistry", "solutions", "raoults-law"]','medium','Neet Buddy',2,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('ae957105-0d74-49e9-a7a8-60bb85e40f5e','59515d1a-17a4-48cc-a95f-cf8eb28f3d94','What are the thermodynamic conditions for an Ideal Solution?','1. It obeys Raoult\'s law over the entire range of concentration.\n2. Enthalpy of mixing: $\\Delta_{mix}H = 0$\n3. Volume of mixing: $\\Delta_{mix}V = 0$',NULL,NULL,'Intermolecular attractive forces between A-A and B-B are nearly equal to A-B.','["chemistry", "solutions", "ideal-solution"]','medium','Neet Buddy',3,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('321cef42-00f5-4b1d-a602-8527f2db1eaf','59515d1a-17a4-48cc-a95f-cf8eb28f3d94','Describe the intermolecular forces and volume change for a solution showing Positive Deviation from Raoult\'s Law. Give an example.','A-B interactions are WEAKER than A-A and B-B interactions. \n$\\Delta_{mix}V > 0$ and $\\Delta_{mix}H > 0$ (endothermic).\nExample: Ethanol + Acetone or Carbon disulphide + Acetone.',NULL,NULL,'Weaker forces mean molecules escape easily, increasing vapour pressure.','["chemistry", "solutions", "non-ideal-solution", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',4,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('7f6cc2b5-4ea2-48fa-9162-1b5f64d2608c','59515d1a-17a4-48cc-a95f-cf8eb28f3d94','What is the van\'t Hoff factor ($i$) conceptually?','$i = \\frac{\\text{Normal molar mass}}{\\text{Abnormal molar mass}} = \\frac{\\text{Observed colligative property}}{\\text{Calculated colligative property}}$\nIt accounts for the extent of dissociation or association of a solute in solution.',NULL,NULL,'$i$ corrects the colligative properties for electrolytes.','["chemistry", "solutions", "vant-hoff"]','medium','Neet Buddy',5,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('13ca8730-19ce-443e-8ab1-8d9c39ba6a97','59515d1a-17a4-48cc-a95f-cf8eb28f3d94','What are Azeotropes (Azeotropic Mixtures)?','Binary mixtures having the same composition in liquid and vapour phase and boil at a constant temperature. Their components cannot be separated by fractional distillation.',NULL,NULL,'Constant boiling mixtures.','["chemistry", "solutions", "azeotropes"]','medium','Neet Buddy',6,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('7260ea5e-4309-4b0b-84c1-b619c94c84b1','59515d1a-17a4-48cc-a95f-cf8eb28f3d94','Which type of non-ideal solution forms a Minimum Boiling Azeotrope?','Solutions showing a large POSITIVE deviation from Raoult\'s law form minimum boiling azeotropes at a specific composition (e.g., 95% ethanol-water mixture).',NULL,NULL,'High vapour pressure (positive deviation) means it boils at a lower temperature.','["chemistry", "solutions", "azeotropes"]','medium','Neet Buddy',7,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('4d01cd92-f805-49fb-b87a-04bc695e4ebf','59515d1a-17a4-48cc-a95f-cf8eb28f3d94','Define Colligative Properties.','Properties of dilute solutions that depend only on the NUMBER of solute particles (molecules or ions) present in the solution, and not on the chemical nature of the solute.',NULL,NULL,'Colligative means \'tied together\'. Depends on quantity, not quality.','["chemistry", "solutions", "colligative-properties"]','medium','Neet Buddy',8,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('2462e1e5-ade2-4cdc-811c-bf0611235ec2','59515d1a-17a4-48cc-a95f-cf8eb28f3d94','Write the formula for Relative Lowering of Vapour Pressure (RLVP).','$\\frac{p_1^0 - p_1}{p_1^0} = x_2$\nWhere $p_1^0$ is VP of pure solvent, $p_1$ is VP of solution, and $x_2$ is mole fraction of the non-volatile solute.',NULL,NULL,'RLVP is equal to the mole fraction of the solute.','["chemistry", "solutions", "rlvp", "formulas"]','medium','Neet Buddy',9,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('b679d712-2457-4065-95ed-8790deae560d','59515d1a-17a4-48cc-a95f-cf8eb28f3d94','What is the formula for Elevation of Boiling Point ($\\Delta T_b$)?','$\\Delta T_b = K_b \\cdot m$\nWhere $K_b$ is the molal elevation constant (Ebullioscopic constant) and $m$ is the molality of the solution.',NULL,NULL,'Boiling point goes UP when a non-volatile solute is added.','["chemistry", "solutions", "boiling-point"]','medium','Neet Buddy',10,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('c9fdbbd7-6063-495d-99ad-9293b30ba4c9','59515d1a-17a4-48cc-a95f-cf8eb28f3d94','What is the formula for Depression of Freezing Point ($\\Delta T_f$)?','$\\Delta T_f = K_f \\cdot m$\nWhere $K_f$ is the molal depression constant (Cryoscopic constant) and $m$ is the molality of the solution.',NULL,NULL,'Freezing point goes DOWN when a solute is added (e.g., salting icy roads).','["chemistry", "solutions", "freezing-point"]','medium','Neet Buddy',11,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('a4f97332-3d3e-45f0-be41-1e1d6b8de6ba','59515d1a-17a4-48cc-a95f-cf8eb28f3d94','Define Osmotic Pressure ($\\pi$) and give its formula.','The excess pressure that must be applied to a solution to prevent osmosis (the passage of solvent into it through a semipermeable membrane).\n$\\pi = C R T$',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\' viewBox=\'0 0 250 150\'><rect x=\'50\' y=\'50\' width=\'150\' height=\'80\' fill=\'none\' stroke=\'black\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><line x1=\'125\' y1=\'50\' x2=\'125\' y2=\'130\' stroke=\'red\' stroke-width=\'4\' stroke-dasharray=\'4,4\'/><text x=\'125\' y=\'145\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'10\' text-anchor=\'middle\'>Semi-Permeable Membrane</text><text x=\'85\' y=\'90\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'12\' text-anchor=\'middle\'>Solvent</text><text x=\'165\' y=\'90\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'12\' text-anchor=\'middle\'>Solution</text><line x1=\'100\' y1=\'100\' x2=\'120\' y2=\'100\' stroke=\'blue\' stroke-width=\'2\' marker-end=\'url(#arrow)\'/><defs><marker id=\'arrow\' viewBox=\'0 0 10 10\' refX=\'5\' refY=\'5\' markerWidth=\'6\' markerHeight=\'6\' orient=\'auto-start-reverse\'><path d=\'M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\' fill=\'blue\'/></marker></defs><rect x=\'130\' y=\'20\' width=\'60\' height=\'10\' fill=\'gray\'/><text x=\'160\' y=\'15\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'12\' text-anchor=\'middle\'>Apply Pressure (pi)</text><line x1=\'160\' y1=\'20\' x2=\'160\' y2=\'40\' stroke=\'black\' stroke-width=\'2\' marker-end=\'url(#arrowBlack)\'/><defs><marker id=\'arrowBlack\' viewBox=\'0 0 10 10\' refX=\'5\' refY=\'5\' markerWidth=\'6\' markerHeight=\'6\' orient=\'auto-start-reverse\'><path d=\'M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\' fill=\'black\'/></marker></defs></svg>"}]}','$C$ is molarity, $R$ is gas constant, $T$ is temperature in Kelvin.','["chemistry", "solutions", "osmotic-pressure"]','medium','Neet Buddy',12,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('835be43a-53c8-45f5-b3cf-e720bf00be5f','59515d1a-17a4-48cc-a95f-cf8eb28f3d94','Why is Osmotic Pressure the preferred method for determining the molar mass of biomolecules (proteins/polymers)?','1. It is measured at room temperature (proteins are unstable at high temps).\n2. Molarity is used instead of molality.\n3. The magnitude of osmotic pressure is large even for very dilute solutions.',NULL,NULL,'Biomolecules degrade at boiling points and have very high molar masses.','["chemistry", "solutions", "osmotic-pressure", "neet-theory"]','medium','Neet Buddy',13,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('b08d8b7f-c187-43f5-a936-c9b89971903b','59515d1a-17a4-48cc-a95f-cf8eb28f3d94','What are Isotonic solutions?','Two solutions having the same osmotic pressure at a given temperature. When separated by a semipermeable membrane, no osmosis occurs between them.',NULL,NULL,'They have the same molar concentration (if $i = 1$).','["chemistry", "solutions", "osmotic-pressure"]','medium','Neet Buddy',14,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('a334e3d0-e802-41e8-a6b6-c0c2e8d19165','59515d1a-17a4-48cc-a95f-cf8eb28f3d94','What is Reverse Osmosis (RO) and what is its main application?','If a pressure larger than the osmotic pressure is applied to the solution side, the direction of osmosis reverses, and pure solvent flows out of the solution.\nApplication: Desalination of seawater.',NULL,NULL,'Forcing water against its natural flow gradient.','["chemistry", "solutions", "osmosis"]','medium','Neet Buddy',15,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('ce320847-dc4d-4837-ba5f-93fbd58076ed','59515d1a-17a4-48cc-a95f-cf8eb28f3d94','What is the value of the van\'t Hoff factor ($i$) for Association and Dissociation?','For Association (e.g., ethanoic acid in benzene): $i < 1$\nFor Dissociation (e.g., $NaCl$ in water): $i > 1$\nFor Non-electrolytes (e.g., glucose, urea): $i = 1$',NULL,NULL,'Dissociation creates more particles ($i > 1$). Association combines particles ($i < 1$).','["chemistry", "solutions", "vant-hoff", "neet-favorite"]','medium','Neet Buddy',16,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('1be16a08-be9f-465d-a1b9-fb5b3405fc00','59515d1a-17a4-48cc-a95f-cf8eb28f3d94','What is the formula relating van\'t Hoff factor ($i$) and Degree of Dissociation ($\\alpha$)?','$\\alpha = \\frac{i - 1}{n - 1}$\nWhere $n$ is the number of ions produced per formula unit of the electrolyte.',NULL,NULL,'For $AlCl_3$, $n = 4$ ($1 Al^{3+} + 3 Cl^-$).','["chemistry", "solutions", "vant-hoff", "formulas"]','medium','Neet Buddy',17,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('6022e1c1-3fbd-4251-9f25-3aa2cac74b03','59515d1a-17a4-48cc-a95f-cf8eb28f3d94','Write the modified equations for colligative properties when a solute undergoes dissociation or association.','RLVP: $\\frac{\\Delta p}{p_1^0} = i \\cdot x_2$\nBoiling Pt Elevation: $\\Delta T_b = i \\cdot K_b \\cdot m$\nFreezing Pt Depression: $\\Delta T_f = i \\cdot K_f \\cdot m$\nOsmotic Pressure: $\\pi = i \\cdot C R T$',NULL,NULL,'Just multiply the ideal formulas by the van\'t Hoff factor ($i$).','["chemistry", "solutions", "colligative-properties", "formulas"]','medium','Neet Buddy',18,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('0d7a6aaf-9b3b-4e93-abed-caeaa4b881d8','59515d1a-17a4-48cc-a95f-cf8eb28f3d94','Describe a solution showing Negative Deviation from Raoult\'s Law and give an example.','A-B interactions are STRONGER than A-A and B-B interactions. \n$\\Delta_{mix}V < 0$ and $\\Delta_{mix}H < 0$ (exothermic).\nExample: Chloroform + Acetone (due to hydrogen bonding) or Phenol + Aniline.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\" xmlns:xlink=\\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\\" width=\\"912\\" height=\\"694\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 912 694\\">\\n  <image xlink:href=\\"data:image/png;base64,\\" x=\\"0\\" y=\\"0\\" width=\\"912\\" height=\\"694\\"/>\\n</svg>"}]}','Stronger forces hold molecules back, decreasing vapour pressure.','["chemistry", "solutions", "non-ideal-solution"]','medium','Neet Buddy',19,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('15f6310b-cab9-4569-9205-bd0e948e9c7d','595322c4-5fcf-4885-96c1-652f28a76bd4','State Kepler\'s First Law (Law of Orbits).','All planets move in elliptical orbits with the Sun situated at one of the foci of the ellipse.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "All planets move in elliptical orbits with the Sun situated at one of the foci of the ellipse."}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg viewBox=\'0 0 200 100\' width=\'100%\'><ellipse cx=\'100\' cy=\'50\' rx=\'80\' ry=\'40\' fill=\'none\' stroke=\'gray\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><circle cx=\'60\' cy=\'50\' r=\'6\' fill=\'orange\'/><text x=\'50\' y=\'75\' font-size=\'12\' fill=\'gray\'>Sun</text><circle cx=\'180\' cy=\'50\' r=\'4\' fill=\'blue\'/><text x=\'155\' y=\'75\' font-size=\'12\' fill=\'gray\'>Planet</text></svg>"}]}','Not a perfect circle!','["physics", "gravitation", "keplers-laws"]','medium','Neet Buddy',0,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('1e0519da-ae6a-40b5-9e19-ba181c9dd1c5','595322c4-5fcf-4885-96c1-652f28a76bd4','State Newton\'s Universal Law of Gravitation.','Every particle attracts every other particle with a force directly proportional to the product of their masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between them: F = G(m₁ × m₂) / r².',NULL,NULL,'G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² / kg²','["physics", "gravitation", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',1,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('a3b4fc1d-278a-4892-a855-7d18fa1f53f0','5b3dfe44-3eb9-4872-b35f-96d2edf7c44f','Describe the three layers of the pericarp in a drupe fruit (like a mango or coconut).','1. Epicarp: Outer thin layer (skin).\n2. Mesocarp: Middle fleshy, edible layer (in mango) or fibrous layer (in coconut).\n3. Endocarp: Inner stony hard layer enclosing the seed.',NULL,NULL,'Drupes develop from monocarpellary superior ovaries.','["biology", "plant-morphology", "fruit", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',5,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('ef5309d6-2bc8-47cc-9b3a-a83da8e66dd5','595322c4-5fcf-4885-96c1-652f28a76bd4','State Kepler\'s Second Law (Law of Areas) and its underlying conservation principle.','The line joining a planet to the sun sweeps out equal areas in equal intervals of time. It is based on the Conservation of Angular Momentum.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "The line joining the planet to the sun sweeps out equal areas in equal time intervals. (Constant areal velocity). Principle: Conservation of Angular Momentum."}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg viewBox=\'0 0 200 100\' width=\'100%\'><ellipse cx=\'100\' cy=\'50\' rx=\'80\' ry=\'40\' fill=\'none\' stroke=\'gray\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><circle cx=\'60\' cy=\'50\' r=\'5\' fill=\'orange\'/><path d=\'M 60 50 L 180 50 A 80 40 0 0 0 160 23 Z\' fill=\'rgba(0, 150, 255, 0.4)\' stroke=\'blue\' stroke-width=\'1\'/><path d=\'M 60 50 L 25 35 A 80 40 0 0 0 22 60 Z\' fill=\'rgba(0, 150, 255, 0.4)\' stroke=\'blue\' stroke-width=\'1\'/><text x=\'70\' y=\'90\' font-size=\'10\' fill=\'gray\'>Shaded areas are equal if Δt is equal</text></svg>"}]}','Planets move faster when closest to the sun (perihelion).','["physics", "gravitation", "keplers-laws"]','medium','Neet Buddy',2,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('b8d12cff-be08-4c29-8d6f-fbf4140b9cda','595322c4-5fcf-4885-96c1-652f28a76bd4','State Kepler\'s Third Law (Law of Periods).','The square of the time period (T) of a planet\'s revolution is directly proportional to the cube of the semi-major axis (a) of its elliptical orbit: T² ∝ a³.',NULL,NULL,'Formula: T² = (4π² / GM) * a³','["physics", "gravitation", "keplers-laws"]','medium','Neet Buddy',3,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('66005de2-4f44-4719-93a8-b23873771217','595322c4-5fcf-4885-96c1-652f28a76bd4','What is the formula for acceleration due to gravity (g) on the surface of the Earth?','g = GM / R², where M is Earth\'s mass and R is Earth\'s radius.',NULL,NULL,'Independent of the falling object\'s mass (m).','["physics", "gravitation"]','medium','Neet Buddy',4,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('a1f045ee-ff8f-4660-8cd5-ce34b412a62d','595322c4-5fcf-4885-96c1-652f28a76bd4','How does acceleration due to gravity (g) vary with altitude (h)?','Exact formula: g\' = g / (1 + h/R)². \nApproximation (for h << R): g\' ≈ g(1 - 2h/R).',NULL,NULL,'g decreases as you go higher.','["physics", "gravitation", "variation-of-g"]','medium','Neet Buddy',5,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('8af7aa83-a7b8-48fa-96f9-cec1ad0192dd','595322c4-5fcf-4885-96c1-652f28a76bd4','How does acceleration due to gravity (g) vary with depth (d)?','g\' = g(1 - d/R).',NULL,NULL,'g decreases linearly with depth. At the center of the Earth (d = R), g\' = 0.','["physics", "gravitation", "variation-of-g"]','medium','Neet Buddy',6,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('350ef727-ae9a-4853-9323-18b7d599543f','595322c4-5fcf-4885-96c1-652f28a76bd4','Draw the graph showing the variation of \'g\' with distance \'r\' from the center of the Earth.','Inside the Earth (r < R), g is directly proportional to r. Outside the Earth (r > R), g is inversely proportional to r².',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "Inside (r < R): g ∝ r\\nOutside (r > R): g ∝ 1/r²"}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg viewBox=\'0 0 200 150\' width=\'100%\'><line x1=\'20\' y1=\'130\' x2=\'180\' y2=\'130\' stroke=\'gray\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><line x1=\'20\' y1=\'130\' x2=\'20\' y2=\'20\' stroke=\'gray\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><text x=\'180\' y=\'145\' font-size=\'12\' fill=\'gray\'>r</text><text x=\'5\' y=\'25\' font-size=\'12\' fill=\'gray\'>g</text><line x1=\'90\' y1=\'130\' x2=\'90\' y2=\'135\' stroke=\'gray\'/><text x=\'85\' y=\'145\' font-size=\'12\' fill=\'gray\'>R</text><path d=\'M 20 130 L 90 40\' fill=\'none\' stroke=\'blue\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><path d=\'M 90 40 Q 120 120 180 125\' fill=\'none\' stroke=\'red\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><text x=\'30\' y=\'70\' font-size=\'10\' fill=\'blue\'>g ∝ r</text><text x=\'110\' y=\'70\' font-size=\'10\' fill=\'red\'>g ∝ 1/r²</text></svg>"}]}','Linear increase from center, then an exponential curve downward from the surface.','["physics", "gravitation", "variation-of-g", "neet-high-yield"]','medium','Neet Buddy',7,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('6844c058-171a-4a94-b0a2-4bd93f9feb67','595322c4-5fcf-4885-96c1-652f28a76bd4','What is the effect of Earth\'s rotation on \'g\' at a latitude λ?','g\' = g - Rω²cos²(λ)',NULL,NULL,'At poles (λ=90°), g is maximum. At equator (λ=0°), g is minimum.','["physics", "gravitation", "variation-of-g"]','medium','Neet Buddy',8,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('70339f21-6811-48a4-8e64-8b2a61c3eb16','595322c4-5fcf-4885-96c1-652f28a76bd4','Define Gravitational Potential (V) and give its relation to intensity (E).','Potential energy per unit mass. V = -GM / r. \nRelation to intensity: E = -dV/dr.',NULL,NULL,'A scalar quantity. Units: J/kg.','["physics", "gravitation", "potential"]','medium','Neet Buddy',9,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('8ebee131-a5f7-427b-8f36-e7043d7b2b66','595322c4-5fcf-4885-96c1-652f28a76bd4','What is Escape Velocity (Ve)? Provide the formulas.','The minimum velocity required to project an object so it never returns to the planet. \nFormulas: Ve = √(2GM / R) or √(2gR).',NULL,NULL,'For Earth, Ve ≈ 11.2 km/s. Independent of the mass of the projected body.','["physics", "gravitation", "satellites"]','medium','Neet Buddy',10,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('ed7aa6f0-107a-4aef-b042-598bfbff736d','595322c4-5fcf-4885-96c1-652f28a76bd4','What is Orbital Velocity (Vo)? Provide the formula for a satellite close to Earth.','The velocity required to keep a satellite in its orbit. \nFormula (close to surface): Vo = √(GM / R) or √(gR).',NULL,NULL,'Relation to escape velocity: Ve = √2 × Vo','["physics", "gravitation", "satellites"]','medium','Neet Buddy',11,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('4ddbb342-1591-44b9-acee-830726444697','595322c4-5fcf-4885-96c1-652f28a76bd4','What is the Total Energy (E) of an orbiting satellite? Relate it to Kinetic (K) and Potential (U) Energy.','Total Energy E = -GMm / (2r). \nRelations: E = -K, and U = -2K. Therefore, E = U/2.',NULL,NULL,'The negative sign implies it is a bound system.','["physics", "gravitation", "satellites"]','medium','Neet Buddy',12,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('36cf9324-78f1-4f42-922b-246c1a18a774','595322c4-5fcf-4885-96c1-652f28a76bd4','Distinguish between Geostationary and Polar satellites.','1. Geostationary: Equatorial orbit, T = 24 hours, height ≈ 35,800 km, used for communication.\n2. Polar: Polar orbit (pole to pole), T ≈ 100 mins, height ≈ 500-800 km, used for remote sensing and weather.',NULL,NULL,'Geostationary appears stationary relative to Earth.','["physics", "gravitation", "satellites"]','medium','Neet Buddy',13,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('58baf8e5-5d43-4c3e-b0c7-d39a7d1094d2','595322c4-5fcf-4885-96c1-652f28a76bd4','Define Gravitational Potential Energy (U) and write its formula.','The work done in bringing a mass from infinity to a point in the gravitational field.\nFormula: U = -G(m₁ × m₂) / r.',NULL,NULL,'It is always negative. Maximum value is zero at infinity.','["physics", "gravitation", "potential"]','medium','Neet Buddy',14,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('55f224d9-57cf-4f6a-897a-87a568d982c9','5b3dfe44-3eb9-4872-b35f-96d2edf7c44f','What are the three distinct regions of a root tip, from the apex upwards?','1. Region of Meristematic Activity (cells divide rapidly).\n2. Region of Elongation (cells undergo rapid enlargement).\n3. Region of Maturation (cells differentiate and mature).',NULL,NULL,'Root hairs, which absorb water, arise from the zone of maturation.','["biology", "plant-morphology", "root", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',0,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('f01187eb-1fac-4705-879b-50774e7476b7','5b3dfe44-3eb9-4872-b35f-96d2edf7c44f','Differentiate between a rhizome, a tuber, and a corm with one example each.','Rhizome: Underground stem that grows horizontally (e.g., Ginger).\nTuber: Swollen ends of underground branches (e.g., Potato).\nCorm: Underground stem that grows vertically (e.g., Colocasia).',NULL,NULL,'These are all modifications of the stem for food storage.','["biology", "plant-morphology", "stem", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',1,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('9cf2ad4a-3bf6-4200-9cd3-b2c2d0569ed5','5b3dfe44-3eb9-4872-b35f-96d2edf7c44f','What type of inflorescence is a diagnostic feature of the family Compositae (Asteraceae)?','Capitulum (or Head) inflorescence. It consists of a flattened receptacle bearing small sessile flowers called florets (Ray florets at the periphery, Disc florets in the center).',NULL,NULL,'A sunflower is not a single flower, but a whole inflorescence.','["biology", "plant-morphology", "families", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',7,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('411f2c0a-8e43-4c2b-94dc-39a5a574a4c7','5b3dfe44-3eb9-4872-b35f-96d2edf7c44f','What are the main functional and structural differences between Parenchyma, Collenchyma, and Sclerenchyma?','Parenchyma: Living, thin-walled (cellulose); functions in photosynthesis and storage.\nCollenchyma: Living, walls thickened at corners (cellulose, hemicellulose, pectin); provides mechanical support to growing parts.\nSclerenchyma: Dead, highly thickened lignified walls; provides mechanical support to mature parts.',NULL,NULL,'PCS: Thin, Corner-thick, Dead-thick.','["biology", "plant-anatomy", "tissues", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',8,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('b3fe7c3f-d619-4ff0-bae4-e132ca8d01e4','5b3dfe44-3eb9-4872-b35f-96d2edf7c44f','Which crucial water-conducting elements are present in Angiosperms but generally absent in Gymnosperms?','Xylem Vessels. Gymnosperms rely almost entirely on tracheids for water conduction.',NULL,NULL,'Vessels are long, cylindrical tube-like structures made of many cells with dissolved end walls.','["biology", "plant-anatomy", "xylem", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',9,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('d651fe8a-9026-45ae-b74f-7deea1b8279c','5b3dfe44-3eb9-4872-b35f-96d2edf7c44f','How do the vascular bundles of a Dicot stem differ from a Monocot stem?','Dicot Stem: Vascular bundles are arranged in a ring, and they are \'open\' (cambium is present between xylem and phloem, allowing secondary growth).\nMonocot Stem: Vascular bundles are scattered throughout the ground tissue, and they are \'closed\' (no cambium).',NULL,NULL,'Ring = Dicot. Scattered = Monocot.','["biology", "plant-anatomy", "stem", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',10,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('7835196b-2847-4cdf-91a8-e579e71ac4c5','5b3dfe44-3eb9-4872-b35f-96d2edf7c44f','Where in the human body would you find Simple Squamous Epithelium, and what is its main function?','Found in the walls of blood vessels (endothelium) and air sacs of lungs (alveoli).\nFunction: Forms a diffusion boundary for the exchange of gases and nutrients.',NULL,NULL,'Squamous cells are flat and tile-like, ideal for rapid diffusion.','["biology", "animal-tissues", "epithelium", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',11,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('d5277de5-26d5-4879-9ebf-07b4a0d755bb','5b3dfe44-3eb9-4872-b35f-96d2edf7c44f','Differentiate between Tendons and Ligaments in terms of function and tissue type.','Tendons: Attach skeletal muscle to bone. They are made of dense regular connective tissue (tough, inelastic).\nLigaments: Attach bone to bone. They are also dense regular connective tissue but contain more elastin (flexible).',NULL,NULL,'Mnemonic: MT-B (Muscle-Tendon-Bone) and B-L-B (Bone-Ligament-Bone).','["biology", "animal-tissues", "connective", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',12,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('44e8e581-b5a3-428e-9dd8-56e1523df5ae','5b3dfe44-3eb9-4872-b35f-96d2edf7c44f','What is the main developmental difference between Racemose and Cymose inflorescence?','Racemose: The main axis continues to grow, and flowers are borne laterally in an acropetal succession (youngest at the top).\nCymose: The main axis terminates in a flower, limiting growth. Flowers are borne in a basipetal succession (oldest at the top).',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "Racemose: The main axis continues to grow, and flowers are borne laterally in an acropetal succession (youngest at the top).\\nCymose: The main axis terminates in a flower, limiting growth. Flowers are borne in a basipetal succession (oldest at the top)."}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\" xmlns:xlink=\\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\\" width=\\"378\\" height=\\"293\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 478 293\\">\\n  <image xlink:href=\\"data:image/png;base64,\\" x=\\"0\\" y=\\"0\\" width=\\"478\\" height=\\"293\\"/>\\n</svg>"}]}','Acropetal = Acro (apex) + petal (seeking). Basipetal = Base seeking.','["biology", "plant-morphology", "inflorescence", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',13,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('12891e99-750d-4b7f-81de-5441c85badbd','5b3dfe44-3eb9-4872-b35f-96d2edf7c44f','What are the two main types of cells found in Neural Tissue?','1. Neurons: The excitable cells that transmit electrical impulses.\n2. Neuroglia (Glial cells): Support, protect, and nourish the neurons. They make up more than half the volume of neural tissue.',NULL,NULL,'Neurons do the firing, Glia do the wiring/maintenance.','["biology", "animal-tissues", "neural", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',14,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('cb278e73-b90b-4c10-b28c-cae02e4cf3e8','5b3dfe44-3eb9-4872-b35f-96d2edf7c44f','How can you distinguish a male frog from a female frog externally (Sexual Dimorphism)?','Male frogs possess two distinguishing features:\n1. Sound-producing vocal sacs (for mating calls).\n2. A copulatory pad (nuptial pad) on the first digit of the forelimbs.',NULL,NULL,'These features become prominent during the breeding season.','["biology", "frog", "morphology", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',15,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('c2c6b216-ab4c-4b96-8de3-714cb04286fb','5b3dfe44-3eb9-4872-b35f-96d2edf7c44f','Why is the alimentary canal (specifically the intestine) of a frog relatively short?','Adult frogs are carnivores (eating insects, worms). A carnivorous diet is easier and faster to digest than plant matter, thereby requiring a shorter intestinal length.',NULL,NULL,'Herbivores (like tadpoles) need longer intestines to digest tough cellulose.','["biology", "frog", "digestive-system", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',16,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('5f5e0ad3-1844-4002-b30d-56377911987c','5b3dfe44-3eb9-4872-b35f-96d2edf7c44f','Describe the chambers and accessory structures of a Frog\'s heart.','A frog has a 3-chambered heart (2 atria and 1 ventricle).\nAccessory structures:\n1. Sinus venosus: A triangular structure on the dorsal side that receives deoxygenated blood from the vena cava.\n2. Conus arteriosus: A sac on the ventral side where the ventricle pumps blood out.',NULL,NULL,'Amphibians have mixed blood in their single ventricle.','["biology", "frog", "circulatory-system", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',17,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('a41d0d43-58ef-40eb-8281-1dcdab51f615','5b3dfe44-3eb9-4872-b35f-96d2edf7c44f','What are the three modes of respiration in an adult frog?','1. Cutaneous Respiration: Breathing through moist skin (in water and during hibernation).\n2. Buccal Respiration: Gas exchange through the lining of the buccal cavity.\n3. Pulmonary Respiration: Breathing through lungs (on land).',NULL,NULL,'Skin is the most important; lungs are mainly used when oxygen demand is high.','["biology", "frog", "respiratory-system", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',18,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('8e639617-b96f-4fdf-ae83-dd5c2648491b','5b3dfe44-3eb9-4872-b35f-96d2edf7c44f','Trace the pathway of sperm from the testes to the exterior in a male frog.','Testes → Vasa efferentia (10-12 in number) → enter the Kidney → Bidder\'s canal → Urinogenital duct → Cloaca.',NULL,NULL,'The frog\'s cloaca is a common chamber for excretory, digestive, and reproductive tracts.','["biology", "frog", "reproductive-system", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',19,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('38cc9600-b340-49d8-b9c0-a4a5c9826c17','d65afa3c-7664-4e36-b666-09606bf85bef','What is the Einstein\'s Photoelectric Equation?','$h\\nu = h\\nu_0 + K.E._{max}$ (or $E = \\Phi + K.E.$)',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "Where $h\\\\nu$ is incident energy, $h\\\\nu_0$ (or $\\\\Phi$) is the threshold energy (work function), and $K.E._{max}$ is the maximum kinetic energy of the ejected electron."}]}','Energy of photon = Work function + Kinetic Energy','["chemistry", "atomic-structure", "photoelectric-effect"]','medium','Neet Buddy',0,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('674caf74-4708-43c7-866e-fa036034c8b7','d65afa3c-7664-4e36-b666-09606bf85bef','What does the Azimuthal (Angular Momentum) Quantum Number ($l$) determine, and what are its possible values?','It determines the 3D shape of the orbital. Possible values are $0$ to $(n-1)$.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "It also determines the orbital angular momentum: $L = \\\\sqrt{l(l+1)} \\\\frac{h}{2\\\\pi}$"}]}','s=0, p=1, d=2, f=3','["chemistry", "atomic-structure", "quantum-numbers"]','medium','Neet Buddy',9,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('b9a7f10d-1fb0-4ee9-9bb3-2229b5465341','5b3dfe44-3eb9-4872-b35f-96d2edf7c44f','Describe Vexillary aestivation and name the family it is characteristic of.','In vexillary aestivation, the largest posterior petal (standard/vexillum) overlaps two lateral petals (wings/alae), which in turn overlap two smallest anterior, fused petals (keel/carina).\nCharacteristic of family Leguminosae (Fabaceae).',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "In vexillary aestivation, the largest posterior petal (standard/vexillum) overlaps two lateral petals (wings/alae), which in turn overlap two smallest anterior, fused petals (keel/carina).\\nCharacteristic of family Leguminosae (Fabaceae)."}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg width=\\"300\\" height=\\"220\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 700 820\\" xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\">\\n\\n  <style>\\n    .label {\\n      font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;\\n      font-size: 34px;\\n      font-weight: bold;\\n      fill: #000000;\\n    }\\n\\n    .title {\\n      font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;\\n      font-size: 28px;\\n      fill: #222222;\\n    }\\n  </style>\\n\\n  <!-- Background -->\\n  <rect width=\\"100%\\" height=\\"100%\\" fill=\\"white\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Standard -->\\n  <path\\n    d=\\"M100,120\\n       Q350,20 600,120\\n       Q670,220 640,360\\n       Q600,500 350,500\\n       Q100,500 60,360\\n       Q30,220 100,120 Z\\"\\n    fill=\\"#F1C40F\\"/>\\n\\n  <text x=\\"350\\" y=\\"190\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\" class=\\"label\\">\\n    Standard\\n  </text>\\n\\n  <!-- Left Wing -->\\n  <path\\n    d=\\"M170,480\\n       Q80,590 120,710\\n       Q180,790 300,700\\n       L300,570 Z\\"\\n    fill=\\"#F39C12\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Right Wing -->\\n  <path\\n    d=\\"M530,480\\n       Q620,590 580,710\\n       Q520,790 400,700\\n       L400,570 Z\\"\\n    fill=\\"#F39C12\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Keel -->\\n  <path\\n    d=\\"M300,570\\n       Q350,610 400,570\\n       Q390,700 350,790\\n       Q310,700 300,570 Z\\"\\n    fill=\\"#E67E22\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Labels -->\\n  <text x=\\"120\\" y=\\"660\\" class=\\"label\\">Wing</text>\\n\\n  <text x=\\"505\\" y=\\"660\\" class=\\"label\\">Wing</text>\\n\\n  <text x=\\"350\\" y=\\"760\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\" class=\\"label\\">\\n    Keel\\n  </text>\\n\\n  <!-- Title -->\\n  <text x=\\"350\\" y=\\"810\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\" class=\\"title\\">\\n    Papilionaceous Corolla Structure\\n  </text>\\n\\n</svg>"}]}','Papilionaceous corolla (butterfly-like).','["biology", "plant-morphology", "flower", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',20,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('38f8f90e-a866-43da-a9c9-b7c0daa97a71','6870bcd9-d31a-4aa4-98f2-e32cbe381f9a','Why are Zinc (Zn), Cadmium (Cd), and Mercury (Hg) not considered true transition elements?','Because they have completely filled d-orbitals ($d^{10}$) in their ground state as well as in their common oxidation states.',NULL,NULL,'Transition elements require partially filled d-orbitals.','["chemistry", "d-block", "class-12", "fundamentals"]','medium','Neet Buddy',0,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('6cf915f6-3627-4832-9a60-16cb4f2da801','6870bcd9-d31a-4aa4-98f2-e32cbe381f9a','Write the anomalous electronic configurations of Chromium (Cr, Z=24) and Copper (Cu, Z=29).','Chromium: $[Ar] 3d^5 4s^1$ \nCopper: $[Ar] 3d^{10} 4s^1$',NULL,NULL,'Half-filled and fully-filled stability shifts an s-electron to the d-subshell.','["chemistry", "d-block", "electronic-configuration"]','medium','Neet Buddy',1,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('90c7ab40-55c1-4e02-9780-ce9e6ef5edec','6870bcd9-d31a-4aa4-98f2-e32cbe381f9a','Describe the trend of Atomic Radii across the 3d transition series (from Sc to Zn).','1. Decreases initially (Sc to Mn) due to increasing effective nuclear charge. \n2. Remains almost constant (Fe, Co, Ni) as shielding by d-electrons balances nuclear charge. \n3. Increases slightly at the end (Cu, Zn) due to increased interelectronic repulsions in the filled d-orbitals.',NULL,NULL,'Decrease -> Constant -> Increase.','["chemistry", "d-block", "atomic-radii"]','medium','Neet Buddy',2,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('a7d9afd2-1cf0-4f84-b2f8-4439df527d9c','6870bcd9-d31a-4aa4-98f2-e32cbe381f9a','Which element in the 3d series exhibits the maximum number of oxidation states, and what is its highest state?','**Manganese (Mn)** exhibits the maximum number of oxidation states, ranging from +2 to **+7**.',NULL,NULL,'It has the configuration 3d5 4s2, allowing it to lose up to 7 electrons.','["chemistry", "d-block", "oxidation-states"]','medium','Neet Buddy',3,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('040c3092-82d7-4f95-9171-5aff69a90c27','6870bcd9-d31a-4aa4-98f2-e32cbe381f9a','Which two elements in the 3d series show only a single non-zero oxidation state?','**Scandium (Sc)** shows only +3.\n**Zinc (Zn)** shows only +2.',NULL,NULL,'Sc reaches noble gas config at +3; Zn reaches stable d10 at +2.','["chemistry", "d-block", "oxidation-states", "neet-favorite"]','medium','Neet Buddy',4,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('56b27ae7-8da7-453c-b0b2-c2223535d50c','6870bcd9-d31a-4aa4-98f2-e32cbe381f9a','What is the most important consequence of the Lanthanoid Contraction on the transition metals of the 4d and 5d series?','The atomic radii of the second (4d) and third (5d) transition series elements become almost identical (e.g., **Zr and Hf** have almost the same radius). \nThis makes them exist together in nature and very difficult to separate.',NULL,NULL,'Zr (Z=40) and Hf (Z=72) are chemical twins.','["chemistry", "f-block", "lanthanoid-contraction", "consequences", "neet-favorite"]','medium','Neet Buddy',5,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('a2880d6d-1eb7-4ebc-809f-6960f4f4f701','6870bcd9-d31a-4aa4-98f2-e32cbe381f9a','Why are most transition metal ions colored in aqueous solutions?','Due to the presence of unpaired d-electrons which undergo **d-d transitions**. They absorb light of a certain wavelength from the visible region to promote an electron, transmitting the complementary color.',NULL,NULL,'Electrons jumping between split d-orbitals.','["chemistry", "d-block", "color"]','medium','Neet Buddy',6,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('06328dc2-ecef-4bac-b9f5-64612719bbfb','6870bcd9-d31a-4aa4-98f2-e32cbe381f9a','Potassium permanganate ($KMnO_4$) is intensely purple, yet $Mn^{7+}$ has a $d^0$ configuration. Why is it colored?','The color is due to **Ligand-to-Metal Charge Transfer (LMCT)**, not d-d transitions. An electron is temporarily transferred from the oxygen ligand to the empty d-orbital of manganese.',NULL,NULL,'This is a classic NEET exception! No d-electrons = no d-d transition.','["chemistry", "d-block", "color", "exceptions", "neet-favorite"]','medium','Neet Buddy',7,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('833c1218-6281-43ec-9959-35b863cd66e3','6870bcd9-d31a-4aa4-98f2-e32cbe381f9a','Why do transition metals and their compounds act as excellent catalysts?','1. They can exhibit **variable oxidation states**, allowing them to form intermediate complexes.\n2. They provide a large surface area for the adsorption of reactant molecules.',NULL,NULL,'Think of Fe in the Haber process or V2O5 in the Contact process.','["chemistry", "d-block", "catalyst"]','medium','Neet Buddy',8,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('2a309c5e-e9f4-41ad-8249-2ce72f1883b7','6870bcd9-d31a-4aa4-98f2-e32cbe381f9a','What are Interstitial Compounds? State two of their key characteristics.','Compounds formed when small atoms (H, C, N) get trapped inside the crystal lattices of metals. \nCharacteristics: \n1. Very hard (some approach diamond in hardness). \n2. High melting points (higher than pure metals).\n3. Chemically inert.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\' viewBox=\'0 0 200 150\'><circle cx=\'50\' cy=\'50\' r=\'20\' fill=\'lightgray\' stroke=\'black\'/><circle cx=\'95\' cy=\'50\' r=\'20\' fill=\'lightgray\' stroke=\'black\'/><circle cx=\'140\' cy=\'50\' r=\'20\' fill=\'lightgray\' stroke=\'black\'/><circle cx=\'50\' cy=\'95\' r=\'20\' fill=\'lightgray\' stroke=\'black\'/><circle cx=\'95\' cy=\'95\' r=\'20\' fill=\'lightgray\' stroke=\'black\'/><circle cx=\'140\' cy=\'95\' r=\'20\' fill=\'lightgray\' stroke=\'black\'/><circle cx=\'72\' cy=\'72\' r=\'6\' fill=\'red\'/><text x=\'100\' y=\'140\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'12\' text-anchor=\'middle\'>Small atom (H, C, N) trapped in void</text><line x1=\'75\' y1=\'80\' x2=\'100\' y2=\'125\' stroke=\'red\' stroke-width=\'2\'/></svg>"}]}','Like filling the gaps in a box of oranges with sand.','["chemistry", "d-block", "interstitial-compounds"]','medium','Neet Buddy',9,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('c99c892c-e799-4885-9921-7fb7593a3a61','6870bcd9-d31a-4aa4-98f2-e32cbe381f9a','Why do transition metals readily form alloys with each other?','Because their atomic radii are very similar (differing by less than 15%). This allows atoms of one metal to easily substitute for atoms of another in the crystal lattice.',NULL,NULL,'Similar size means they can swap places without distorting the structure much.','["chemistry", "d-block", "alloys"]','medium','Neet Buddy',10,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('a096e869-e377-4d78-807f-5b929edc95f3','6870bcd9-d31a-4aa4-98f2-e32cbe381f9a','In the 3d series, which element has a POSITIVE standard reduction potential ($E^\\circ_{M^{2+}/M}$), and why?','**Copper (Cu)** has a positive $E^\\circ$ (+0.34 V). \nReason: The high energy required to atomize and ionize Cu (high enthalpy of atomization + ionization) is not balanced by its hydration enthalpy.',NULL,NULL,'It\'s the only 3d metal that won\'t release H2 gas when reacted with dilute acids.','["chemistry", "d-block", "electrode-potential", "neet-favorite"]','medium','Neet Buddy',11,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('c351df84-0362-4103-a726-6305737a1ba4','6870bcd9-d31a-4aa4-98f2-e32cbe381f9a','Describe the pH-dependent equilibrium between Chromate ($CrO_4^{2-}$) and Dichromate ($Cr_2O_7^{2-}$) ions.','In acidic medium ($H^+$), yellow Chromate changes to orange Dichromate. \nIn basic medium ($OH^-$), orange Dichromate changes back to yellow Chromate. \n$2CrO_4^{2-} + 2H^+ \\rightleftharpoons Cr_2O_7^{2-} + H_2O$',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\' viewBox=\'0 0 350 100\'><rect x=\'10\' y=\'20\' width=\'120\' height=\'60\' fill=\'yellow\' stroke=\'black\' rx=\'5\'/><text x=\'70\' y=\'50\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'14\' text-anchor=\'middle\' font-weight=\'bold\'>Chromate</text><text x=\'70\' y=\'70\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'12\' text-anchor=\'middle\'>(Yellow)</text><rect x=\'220\' y=\'20\' width=\'120\' height=\'60\' fill=\'orange\' stroke=\'black\' rx=\'5\'/><text x=\'280\' y=\'50\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'14\' text-anchor=\'middle\' font-weight=\'bold\'>Dichromate</text><text x=\'280\' y=\'70\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'12\' text-anchor=\'middle\'>(Orange)</text><path d=\'M 140 40 L 210 40\' stroke=\'blue\' stroke-width=\'3\' marker-end=\'url(#arrowB)\'/><text x=\'175\' y=\'35\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'12\' text-anchor=\'middle\' fill=\'blue\'>+ H⁺ (Acid)</text><path d=\'M 210 60 L 140 60\' stroke=\'red\' stroke-width=\'3\' marker-end=\'url(#arrowR)\'/><text x=\'175\' y=\'75\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'12\' text-anchor=\'middle\' fill=\'red\'>+ OH⁻ (Base)</text><defs><marker id=\'arrowB\' viewBox=\'0 0 10 10\' refX=\'5\' refY=\'5\' markerWidth=\'6\' markerHeight=\'6\' orient=\'auto-start-reverse\'><path d=\'M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\' fill=\'blue\'/></marker><marker id=\'arrowR\' viewBox=\'0 0 10 10\' refX=\'5\' refY=\'5\' markerWidth=\'6\' markerHeight=\'6\' orient=\'auto-start-reverse\'><path d=\'M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\' fill=\'red\'/></marker></defs></svg>"}]}','Acid = Orange (Dichromate). Base = Yellow (Chromate).','["chemistry", "d-block", "dichromate"]','medium','Neet Buddy',12,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('00af8417-38e3-4dab-a529-edf07f963f24','6870bcd9-d31a-4aa4-98f2-e32cbe381f9a','What is the equivalent weight of $KMnO_4$ in Acidic, Neutral (faintly basic), and strongly Alkaline mediums?','Acidic: $M/5$ (n-factor = 5, $Mn^{+7} \\rightarrow Mn^{+2}$)\nNeutral/Faintly Basic: $M/3$ (n-factor = 3, $Mn^{+7} \\rightarrow Mn^{+4}$)\nStrongly Basic: $M/1$ (n-factor = 1, $Mn^{+7} \\rightarrow Mn^{+6}$)',NULL,NULL,'Mnemonic: BAN 153 (Basic=1, Acidic=5, Neutral=3).','["chemistry", "d-block", "kmno4", "equivalent-weight", "neet-numerical"]','medium','Neet Buddy',13,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('b9523457-3e8c-43ad-82e6-0e979cd8b0f0','6870bcd9-d31a-4aa4-98f2-e32cbe381f9a','What does $KMnO_4$ oxidize iodides ($I^-$) to in Acidic medium vs. Neutral/Faintly Basic medium?','Acidic medium: Oxidizes $I^-$ to **$I_2$** (Iodine).\nNeutral/Faintly Basic medium: Oxidizes $I^-$ to **$IO_3^-$** (Iodate).',NULL,NULL,'Neutral medium pushes it all the way to Iodate.','["chemistry", "d-block", "kmno4", "reactions"]','medium','Neet Buddy',14,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('c7a72252-9523-40af-9ee1-efa9c666f18e','6870bcd9-d31a-4aa4-98f2-e32cbe381f9a','Define Lanthanoid Contraction.','The steady and gradual decrease in the atomic and ionic radii of lanthanoids with increasing atomic number (from La to Lu).',NULL,NULL,'It happens because 4f electrons provide very poor shielding for the increasing nuclear charge.','["chemistry", "f-block", "lanthanoids"]','medium','Neet Buddy',15,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('1143ddfe-3fec-4e92-b957-2d858d53d7d6','6870bcd9-d31a-4aa4-98f2-e32cbe381f9a','What is the most common oxidation state for Lanthanoids? Name two elements that exhibit different notable oxidation states.','Most common oxidation state is **+3**.\nNotable exceptions: \n**Cerium (Ce)** exhibits **+4** (to attain a stable $f^0$ noble gas config). \n**Europium (Eu)** exhibits **+2** (to attain a stable half-filled $f^7$ config).',NULL,NULL,'Ce(IV) is a good oxidizing agent. Eu(II) is a good reducing agent.','["chemistry", "f-block", "lanthanoids", "oxidation-states"]','medium','Neet Buddy',16,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('b2376e42-23b8-4726-b6c8-63f313f9d328','6870bcd9-d31a-4aa4-98f2-e32cbe381f9a','Why is the Actinoid Contraction greater from element to element than the Lanthanoid Contraction?','Because the **5f** electrons in actinoids have even poorer shielding effect than the **4f** electrons in lanthanoids, causing the nucleus to pull the outer electrons more strongly.',NULL,NULL,'5f orbitals are more diffuse and spread out than 4f.','["chemistry", "f-block", "actinoids", "contraction"]','medium','Neet Buddy',17,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('96645f89-02ca-4585-b282-306eaab1f35a','6870bcd9-d31a-4aa4-98f2-e32cbe381f9a','Why do Actinoids show a much larger number of oxidation states compared to Lanthanoids?','Because the energy levels of the **5f, 6d, and 7s subshells are very close (comparable) to each other**, allowing electrons from all these subshells to participate in bonding.',NULL,NULL,'Small energy gaps mean more electrons can be removed easily.','["chemistry", "f-block", "actinoids", "oxidation-states"]','medium','Neet Buddy',18,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('e6dcc4db-4efd-4cbf-8dc4-ab6422e03a60','6870bcd9-d31a-4aa4-98f2-e32cbe381f9a','What is Mischmetall, and what is its primary use?','Mischmetall is a well-known alloy of Lanthanoid metals (~95% lanthanoid metals, ~5% Iron, and traces of S, C, Ca, Al).\nPrimary Use: It is used in magnesium-based alloys to produce **bullets, shells, and lighter flints**.',NULL,NULL,'It creates the spark in a cigarette lighter.','["chemistry", "f-block", "alloys", "uses"]','medium','Neet Buddy',19,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('be85f8eb-b89d-4e3a-b2eb-f5d74097d064','6870bcd9-d31a-4aa4-98f2-e32cbe381f9a','State the ‘Spin-Only’ formula for calculating the magnetic moment (μ).','$\\mu = \\sqrt{n(n+2)} \\text{ B.M.}$ \nWhere $n$ is the number of unpaired electrons and B.M. is Bohr Magneton.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "$\\\\mu = \\\\sqrt{n(n+2)} \\\\text{ B.M.}$ \\nWhere $n$ is the number of unpaired electrons and B.M. is Bohr Magneton."}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\' viewBox=\'0 0 300 120\'><rect x=\'10\' y=\'10\' width=\'280\' height=\'100\' fill=\'#f9f9f9\' stroke=\'#333\' stroke-width=\'2\' rx=\'10\'/><text x=\'150\' y=\'35\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'14\' text-anchor=\'middle\' font-weight=\'bold\'>Shortcut for NEET:</text><text x=\'150\' y=\'60\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'12\' text-anchor=\'middle\'>n = 1 → μ = 1.73 BM</text><text x=\'150\' y=\'75\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'12\' text-anchor=\'middle\'>n = 2 → μ = 2.84 BM</text><text x=\'150\' y=\'90\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'12\' text-anchor=\'middle\'>n = 3 → μ = 3.87 BM</text><text x=\'150\' y=\'105\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'12\' text-anchor=\'middle\' fill=\'red\'>Notice: The integer part of μ equals \'n\'!</text></svg>"}]}','If n=1, $\\mu \\approx 1.73$. If n=2, $\\mu \\approx 2.84$.','["chemistry", "d-block", "magnetic-properties", "formulas"]','medium','Neet Buddy',20,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('7b97f631-ba7e-479e-b482-0fdb58ff0584','6ef152a1-51ad-4b9d-8dbe-7fc2bc5f1ee7','What is the truth table output of a NOR gate if BOTH inputs A and B are LOW (0)?','Output is HIGH (1).',NULL,NULL,'NOR is NOT-OR. Since A OR B would be 0, the inversion makes it 1.','["physics", "logic-gates", "truth-table"]','medium','Neet Buddy',0,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('42b2f9cc-bc22-484e-9e0a-1cbfc0b160c7','6ef152a1-51ad-4b9d-8dbe-7fc2bc5f1ee7','State the Mass Action Law for semiconductors.','$n_e \\times n_h = n_i^2$',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "Where $n_e$ is electron concentration, $n_h$ is hole concentration, and $n_i$ is intrinsic carrier concentration."}]}','Relates the concentration of electrons, holes, and intrinsic carriers in thermal equilibrium.','["physics", "semiconductors", "class-12", "formulas"]','medium','Neet Buddy',1,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('499e372f-aa62-4566-9365-1fa9eceb3cd1','6ef152a1-51ad-4b9d-8dbe-7fc2bc5f1ee7','What type of extrinsic semiconductor is formed when Silicon is doped with a Group 15 (pentavalent) impurity like Phosphorus?','n-type semiconductor.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "In an n-type semiconductor, electrons are the majority charge carriers and holes are the minority charge carriers."}]}','Pentavalent means 5 valence electrons. 4 bond with Si, 1 is \'extra\'.','["physics", "semiconductors", "doping"]','medium','Neet Buddy',2,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('54d0cecd-dd7d-48fa-9dac-abf962db94fb','6ef152a1-51ad-4b9d-8dbe-7fc2bc5f1ee7','What type of extrinsic semiconductor is formed when Silicon is doped with a Group 13 (trivalent) impurity like Boron or Aluminum?','p-type semiconductor.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "In a p-type semiconductor, holes are the majority charge carriers and electrons are the minority charge carriers."}]}','Trivalent means 3 valence electrons, creating a vacancy or \'hole\' in the crystal lattice.','["physics", "semiconductors", "doping"]','medium','Neet Buddy',3,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('c26a7961-ff31-4ba6-af20-1b8f6edcb2cf','6ef152a1-51ad-4b9d-8dbe-7fc2bc5f1ee7','Why is the depletion region in a p-n junction practically devoid of mobile charge carriers?','Because electrons from the n-side and holes from the p-side diffuse across the junction and recombine, leaving behind immobile ionized atoms.',NULL,NULL,'It creates a \'barrier\'.','["physics", "semiconductors", "pn-junction"]','medium','Neet Buddy',4,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('7dbb4bb0-7bc3-43aa-8c64-8cf0749bb92e','6ef152a1-51ad-4b9d-8dbe-7fc2bc5f1ee7','What happens to the width of the depletion layer and the barrier potential in a p-n junction under Forward Bias?','The width of the depletion layer decreases and the barrier potential is reduced.',NULL,NULL,'External voltage opposes the built-in potential.','["physics", "semiconductors", "biasing"]','medium','Neet Buddy',5,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('60ee79b8-5a63-400f-b0b2-a568c9e2dad2','6ef152a1-51ad-4b9d-8dbe-7fc2bc5f1ee7','What happens to the width of the depletion layer and the barrier potential in a p-n junction under Reverse Bias?','The width of the depletion layer increases and the barrier potential is increased.',NULL,NULL,'External voltage aids the built-in potential.','["physics", "semiconductors", "biasing"]','medium','Neet Buddy',6,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('dd26e059-73bd-40a5-a321-405515b1bba5','6ef152a1-51ad-4b9d-8dbe-7fc2bc5f1ee7','Which two logic gates are known as \'Universal Gates\'?','NAND Gate and NOR Gate.',NULL,NULL,'They are called \'universal\' because any basic logic gate (AND, OR, NOT) can be constructed using only these gates.','["physics", "logic-gates", "conceptual"]','medium','Neet Buddy',7,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('fb113ca8-8a03-42d6-895e-cc1a063330fc','6ef152a1-51ad-4b9d-8dbe-7fc2bc5f1ee7','What is the truth table output of a NAND gate if BOTH inputs A and B are HIGH (1)?','Output is LOW (0).',NULL,NULL,'NAND is NOT-AND. Since A AND B would be 1, the inversion makes it 0.','["physics", "logic-gates", "truth-table"]','medium','Neet Buddy',8,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('bcde4bc2-ae91-4283-b9d3-18b9149a5ed2','6ef152a1-51ad-4b9d-8dbe-7fc2bc5f1ee7','Write the formula for the dynamic resistance ($r_d$) of a p-n junction diode.','$r_d = \\frac{\\Delta V}{\\Delta I}$',NULL,NULL,'It is the reciprocal of the slope of the I-V characteristic curve at a given operating point.','["physics", "semiconductors", "formulas"]','medium','Neet Buddy',9,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('8ddafa52-2822-47cd-be96-0dddcb6b8ff0','6ef152a1-51ad-4b9d-8dbe-7fc2bc5f1ee7','If the input frequency of an AC signal is 50 Hz, what is the output ripple frequency of a Half-Wave Rectifier and a Full-Wave Rectifier?','Half-wave: 50 Hz (Same as input). \nFull-wave: 100 Hz (Twice the input).',NULL,NULL,'Full-wave rectifies both the positive and negative half-cycles.','["physics", "semiconductors", "rectifiers"]','medium','Neet Buddy',10,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('fcb89b7a-f840-4398-ae28-3637405d5f8a','6ef152a1-51ad-4b9d-8dbe-7fc2bc5f1ee7','What is the primary function of a Photodiode and in what bias is it operated?','It detects optical signals (light intensity) and is operated in reverse bias.',NULL,NULL,'Reverse bias makes it easier to observe fractional changes in the minority carrier current caused by incident light.','["physics", "semiconductors", "optoelectronics"]','medium','Neet Buddy',11,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('e15c3972-3811-4f10-969c-dd657120b0c2','6ef152a1-51ad-4b9d-8dbe-7fc2bc5f1ee7','What is the required order of bandgap energy ($E_g$) for a Light Emitting Diode (LED) to emit visible light?','Between 1.8 eV and 3.0 eV.',NULL,NULL,'Corresponds to the energy range of visible light photons. Semiconductors like GaAsP are used.','["physics", "semiconductors", "optoelectronics"]','medium','Neet Buddy',12,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('7121ee44-47e5-4b92-9955-c38aa3aaf9aa','6ef152a1-51ad-4b9d-8dbe-7fc2bc5f1ee7','In which biasing region does a Zener diode operate when used as a voltage regulator?','Reverse breakdown region.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "It is always connected in parallel to the load."}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg width=\\"900\\" height=\\"420\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 900 420\\"\\n     xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\">\\n\\n  <!-- Background -->\\n  <rect width=\\"100%\\" height=\\"100%\\" fill=\\"white\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Styles -->\\n  <style>\\n    .wire {\\n      stroke: black;\\n      stroke-width: 5;\\n      fill: none;\\n      stroke-linecap: round;\\n      stroke-linejoin: round;\\n    }\\n\\n    .component {\\n      stroke: black;\\n      stroke-width: 4;\\n      fill: white;\\n    }\\n\\n    .label {\\n      font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;\\n      font-size: 28px;\\n      fill: black;\\n    }\\n\\n    .small {\\n      font-size: 24px;\\n    }\\n  </style>\\n\\n  <!-- Top Input Wire -->\\n  <line class=\\"wire\\" x1=\\"60\\" y1=\\"100\\" x2=\\"240\\" y2=\\"100\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Series Resistor Rs -->\\n  <rect class=\\"component\\" x=\\"240\\" y=\\"78\\" width=\\"120\\" height=\\"44\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Resistor Label -->\\n  <text class=\\"label small\\" x=\\"285\\" y=\\"58\\">R<tspan baseline-shift=\\"sub\\" font-size=\\"18\\">S</tspan></text>\\n\\n  <!-- Wire after Rs -->\\n  <line class=\\"wire\\" x1=\\"360\\" y1=\\"100\\" x2=\\"560\\" y2=\\"100\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Vertical Branch -->\\n  <line class=\\"wire\\" x1=\\"560\\" y1=\\"100\\" x2=\\"560\\" y2=\\"340\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Load Resistor RL -->\\n  <rect class=\\"component\\" x=\\"620\\" y=\\"170\\" width=\\"42\\" height=\\"120\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- RL Connections -->\\n  <line class=\\"wire\\" x1=\\"560\\" y1=\\"100\\" x2=\\"641\\" y2=\\"100\\"/>\\n  <line class=\\"wire\\" x1=\\"641\\" y1=\\"100\\" x2=\\"641\\" y2=\\"170\\"/>\\n\\n  <line class=\\"wire\\" x1=\\"641\\" y1=\\"290\\" x2=\\"641\\" y2=\\"340\\"/>\\n  <line class=\\"wire\\" x1=\\"641\\" y1=\\"340\\" x2=\\"560\\" y2=\\"340\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Zener Diode -->\\n  <!-- Triangle -->\\n  <polygon points=\\"500,250 560,160 620,250\\"\\n           fill=\\"white\\"\\n           stroke=\\"black\\"\\n           stroke-width=\\"5\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Cathode Bar -->\\n  <line class=\\"wire\\" x1=\\"500\\" y1=\\"160\\" x2=\\"620\\" y2=\\"160\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Zener Bends -->\\n  <line class=\\"wire\\" x1=\\"500\\" y1=\\"160\\" x2=\\"480\\" y2=\\"140\\"/>\\n  <line class=\\"wire\\" x1=\\"620\\" y1=\\"160\\" x2=\\"640\\" y2=\\"180\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Bottom Connection -->\\n  <line class=\\"wire\\" x1=\\"560\\" y1=\\"250\\" x2=\\"560\\" y2=\\"340\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Bottom Return Line -->\\n  <line class=\\"wire\\" x1=\\"60\\" y1=\\"340\\" x2=\\"560\\" y2=\\"340\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Labels -->\\n  <text class=\\"label\\" x=\\"60\\" y=\\"72\\">\\n    Unregulated DC\\n  </text>\\n\\n  <text class=\\"label\\" x=\\"690\\" y=\\"230\\">\\n    R<tspan baseline-shift=\\"sub\\" font-size=\\"18\\">L</tspan>\\n  </text>\\n\\n  <text class=\\"label small\\" x=\\"690\\" y=\\"265\\">\\n    (Load)\\n  </text>\\n\\n</svg>"}]}','Voltage remains nearly constant despite large changes in current.','["physics", "semiconductors", "zener"]','medium','Neet Buddy',13,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('af3d8c2e-7d2f-495a-908b-3e697aafb204','d65afa3c-7664-4e36-b666-09606bf85bef','How do you calculate the number of Radial Nodes and Angular Nodes for an orbital?','Radial Nodes = $n - l - 1$ \nAngular Nodes = $l$ \nTotal Nodes = $n - 1$',NULL,NULL,'Angular nodes always equal the azimuthal quantum number.','["chemistry", "atomic-structure", "orbitals", "neet-favorite"]','medium','Neet Buddy',10,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('c7f318ff-64e5-495f-a7c7-3635fea42472','6ef152a1-51ad-4b9d-8dbe-7fc2bc5f1ee7','Identify the Logic Gate: Its output is HIGH (1) if ANY of its inputs are HIGH (1). Write its Boolean expression.','OR Gate. \n$Y = A + B$',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\" width=\\"420\\" height=\\"180\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 420 180\\">\\n  <defs>\\n    <style>\\n      .gate {\\n        fill: none;\\n        stroke: #000;\\n        stroke-width: 4;\\n        stroke-linecap: round;\\n        stroke-linejoin: round;\\n      }\\n      .wire {\\n        fill: none;\\n        stroke: #000;\\n        stroke-width: 4;\\n        stroke-linecap: round;\\n      }\\n      .label {\\n        font-family: \\"Arial\\", sans-serif;\\n        font-size: 24px;\\n        dominant-baseline: middle;\\n      }\\n    </style>\\n  </defs>\\n\\n  <!-- Input wires -->\\n  <line class=\\"wire\\" x1=\\"30\\" y1=\\"55\\" x2=\\"120\\" y2=\\"55\\"/>\\n  <line class=\\"wire\\" x1=\\"30\\" y1=\\"125\\" x2=\\"120\\" y2=\\"125\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- OR gate body -->\\n  <path class=\\"gate\\"\\n        d=\\"M120,30\\n           Q200,30 255,90\\n           Q200,150 120,150\\n           Q150,90 120,30 Z\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Output wire -->\\n  <line class=\\"wire\\" x1=\\"255\\" y1=\\"90\\" x2=\\"360\\" y2=\\"90\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Labels -->\\n  <text class=\\"label\\" x=\\"10\\" y=\\"55\\">A</text>\\n  <text class=\\"label\\" x=\\"10\\" y=\\"125\\">B</text>\\n  <text class=\\"label\\" x=\\"372\\" y=\\"90\\">Y</text>\\n</svg>"}]}','Output is LOW (0) only when all inputs are LOW (0).','["physics", "logic-gates", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',14,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('e8453cf6-8f7d-45fb-9788-0d6074abdb8e','6ef152a1-51ad-4b9d-8dbe-7fc2bc5f1ee7','Identify the Logic Gate: Its output is HIGH (1) ONLY when ALL its inputs are HIGH (1). Write its Boolean expression.','AND Gate. \n$Y = A \\cdot B$',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\" width=\\"600\\" height=\\"260\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 600 260\\">\\n  <defs>\\n    <style>\\n      .gate {\\n        fill: none;\\n        stroke: #000;\\n        stroke-width: 10;\\n        stroke-linecap: square;\\n        stroke-linejoin: miter;\\n      }\\n\\n      .wire {\\n        fill: none;\\n        stroke: #000;\\n        stroke-width: 10;\\n        stroke-linecap: square;\\n      }\\n\\n      .label {\\n        font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;\\n        font-size: 48px;\\n        fill: #000;\\n        dominant-baseline: middle;\\n      }\\n    </style>\\n  </defs>\\n\\n  <!-- Input wires -->\\n  <line class=\\"wire\\" x1=\\"40\\" y1=\\"80\\" x2=\\"170\\" y2=\\"80\\"/>\\n  <line class=\\"wire\\" x1=\\"40\\" y1=\\"180\\" x2=\\"170\\" y2=\\"180\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- AND gate body -->\\n  <path class=\\"gate\\"\\n        d=\\"\\n          M170 30\\n          L290 30\\n          A90 90 0 0 1 290 230\\n          L170 230\\n          Z\\n        \\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Output wire -->\\n  <line class=\\"wire\\" x1=\\"380\\" y1=\\"130\\" x2=\\"540\\" y2=\\"130\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Labels -->\\n  <text class=\\"label\\" x=\\"10\\" y=\\"80\\">A</text>\\n  <text class=\\"label\\" x=\\"10\\" y=\\"180\\">B</text>\\n  <text class=\\"label\\" x=\\"560\\" y=\\"130\\">Y</text>\\n</svg>"}]}','If any input is LOW, the output is LOW.','["physics", "logic-gates"]','medium','Neet Buddy',15,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('b87d6b14-a3b8-4c36-af1f-df01d8f23965','6ef152a1-51ad-4b9d-8dbe-7fc2bc5f1ee7','What happens to the resistance of an intrinsic semiconductor when its temperature increases?','Its resistance decreases.',NULL,NULL,'Higher temperature breaks more covalent bonds, increasing the number of free charge carriers (electrons and holes).','["physics", "semiconductors", "conceptual"]','medium','Neet Buddy',16,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('e4069e86-9472-402e-a74f-d2d6f309560e','6ef152a1-51ad-4b9d-8dbe-7fc2bc5f1ee7','In which quadrant of the V-I characteristics does a Solar Cell operate?','The Fourth Quadrant.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "svg", "value": "<svg width=\\"800\\" height=\\"500\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 800 500\\"\\n     xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\">\\n\\n  <!-- Background -->\\n  <rect width=\\"100%\\" height=\\"100%\\" fill=\\"white\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Axes -->\\n  <line x1=\\"120\\" y1=\\"40\\" x2=\\"120\\" y2=\\"430\\"\\n        stroke=\\"black\\" stroke-width=\\"3\\"/>\\n\\n  <line x1=\\"40\\" y1=\\"150\\" x2=\\"700\\" y2=\\"150\\"\\n        stroke=\\"black\\" stroke-width=\\"3\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- I-V Curve -->\\n  <path d=\\"\\n      M120 360\\n      C220 360, 340 340, 430 290\\n      C500 250, 560 180, 600 150\\"\\n      fill=\\"none\\"\\n      stroke=\\"#0033ff\\"\\n      stroke-width=\\"6\\"\\n      stroke-linecap=\\"round\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Labels -->\\n  <text x=\\"30\\" y=\\"385\\"\\n        font-family=\\"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\\"\\n        font-size=\\"28\\"\\n        fill=\\"black\\">\\n    I<tspan baseline-shift=\\"sub\\" font-size=\\"18\\">SC</tspan>\\n  </text>\\n\\n  <text x=\\"615\\" y=\\"135\\"\\n        font-family=\\"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\\"\\n        font-size=\\"28\\"\\n        fill=\\"black\\">\\n    V<tspan baseline-shift=\\"sub\\" font-size=\\"18\\">OC</tspan>\\n  </text>\\n\\n  <text x=\\"615\\" y=\\"170\\"\\n        font-family=\\"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\\"\\n        font-size=\\"18\\"\\n        fill=\\"black\\">\\n    (Open-Circuit Voltage)\\n  </text>\\n\\n  <text x=\\"360\\" y=\\"315\\"\\n        font-family=\\"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\\"\\n        font-size=\\"30\\"\\n        fill=\\"red\\">\\n    Fourth Quadrant\\n  </text>\\n\\n</svg>"}]}','It draws no current from an external source but instead generates an EMF to supply power to a load.','["physics", "semiconductors", "optoelectronics"]','medium','Neet Buddy',17,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('5ae0db3f-e8ad-49f5-b4f7-f16a5b9aeb1f','6ef152a1-51ad-4b9d-8dbe-7fc2bc5f1ee7','Draw the general I-V characteristic curve of a p-n junction diode.','Forward bias features a knee voltage before exponential growth. Reverse bias features a very small constant saturation current until breakdown voltage.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "svg", "value": "<svg width=\\"700\\" height=\\"600\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 700 600\\"\\n     xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\">\\n\\n  <!-- Background -->\\n  <rect width=\\"100%\\" height=\\"100%\\" fill=\\"white\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Axes -->\\n  <line x1=\\"350\\" y1=\\"30\\" x2=\\"350\\" y2=\\"560\\"\\n        stroke=\\"black\\" stroke-width=\\"3\\"/>\\n\\n  <line x1=\\"40\\" y1=\\"300\\" x2=\\"660\\" y2=\\"300\\"\\n        stroke=\\"black\\" stroke-width=\\"3\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Forward Bias Curve -->\\n  <path d=\\"\\n      M 350 300\\n      L 470 300\\n      C 510 300, 540 280, 560 220\\n      C 575 170, 585 120, 595 60\\n    \\"\\n    fill=\\"none\\"\\n    stroke=\\"#0055ff\\"\\n    stroke-width=\\"6\\"\\n    stroke-linecap=\\"round\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Reverse Bias Curve -->\\n  <path d=\\"\\n      M 350 300\\n      L 150 315\\n      C 135 317, 125 340, 120 420\\n      C 116 500, 112 540, 108 565\\n    \\"\\n    fill=\\"none\\"\\n    stroke=\\"#d40000\\"\\n    stroke-width=\\"6\\"\\n    stroke-linecap=\\"round\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Labels -->\\n  <text x=\\"385\\" y=\\"95\\"\\n        font-family=\\"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\\"\\n        font-size=\\"28\\"\\n        fill=\\"black\\">\\n    I<tspan baseline-shift=\\"sub\\" font-size=\\"20\\">F</tspan> (mA)\\n  </text>\\n\\n  <text x=\\"385\\" y=\\"530\\"\\n        font-family=\\"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\\"\\n        font-size=\\"28\\"\\n        fill=\\"black\\">\\n    I<tspan baseline-shift=\\"sub\\" font-size=\\"20\\">R</tspan> (µA)\\n  </text>\\n\\n  <text x=\\"555\\" y=\\"265\\"\\n        font-family=\\"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\\"\\n        font-size=\\"28\\"\\n        fill=\\"black\\">\\n    V<tspan baseline-shift=\\"sub\\" font-size=\\"20\\">F</tspan>\\n  </text>\\n\\n  <text x=\\"40\\" y=\\"265\\"\\n        font-family=\\"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\\"\\n        font-size=\\"28\\"\\n        fill=\\"black\\">\\n    V<tspan baseline-shift=\\"sub\\" font-size=\\"20\\">R</tspan>\\n    <tspan font-size=\\"24\\"> (Breakdown)</tspan>\\n  </text>\\n\\n</svg>"}]}','Think about the 1st and 3rd quadrants.','["physics", "semiconductors", "graphs"]','medium','Neet Buddy',18,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('26f24134-8b7e-452d-b95e-88542984cf45','6ef152a1-51ad-4b9d-8dbe-7fc2bc5f1ee7','Identify the Logic Gate: It has only one input and inverts it. Write its Boolean expression.','NOT Gate (Inverter). \n$Y = \\overline{A}$',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\" width=\\"600\\" height=\\"260\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 600 260\\">\\n  <defs>\\n    <style>\\n      .gate {\\n        fill: none;\\n        stroke: #000;\\n        stroke-width: 10;\\n        stroke-linecap: square;\\n        stroke-linejoin: miter;\\n      }\\n\\n      .wire {\\n        fill: none;\\n        stroke: #000;\\n        stroke-width: 10;\\n        stroke-linecap: square;\\n      }\\n\\n      .label {\\n        font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;\\n        font-size: 48px;\\n        fill: #000;\\n        dominant-baseline: middle;\\n      }\\n    </style>\\n  </defs>\\n\\n  <!-- Input wire -->\\n  <line class=\\"wire\\" x1=\\"40\\" y1=\\"130\\" x2=\\"170\\" y2=\\"130\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- NOT gate triangle -->\\n  <polygon class=\\"gate\\"\\n           points=\\"170,50 170,210 350,130\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Inversion bubble -->\\n  <circle class=\\"gate\\" cx=\\"385\\" cy=\\"130\\" r=\\"28\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Output wire -->\\n  <line class=\\"wire\\" x1=\\"413\\" y1=\\"130\\" x2=\\"540\\" y2=\\"130\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Labels -->\\n  <text class=\\"label\\" x=\\"10\\" y=\\"130\\">A</text>\\n  <text class=\\"label\\" x=\\"560\\" y=\\"130\\">Y</text>\\n</svg>"}]}','High in -> Low out. Low in -> High out.','["physics", "logic-gates"]','medium','Neet Buddy',19,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('f165ccd6-ea66-4f28-a198-90a8c435dfc6','761543ca-30d2-4ba5-9a95-840ff6911303','Who proposed the concept of \'Omnis cellula-e cellula\', and what does it mean?','Rudolf Virchow (1855) proposed it, meaning that new cells arise from pre-existing cells, thereby completing the Cell Theory.',NULL,NULL,'Modified the theory initially proposed by Schleiden and Schwann.','["biology", "cell-the-unit-of-life", "class-11", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',0,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('08d9d968-de2c-45d3-93c5-eb33869bfa6d','761543ca-30d2-4ba5-9a95-840ff6911303','What is the primary function of mesosomes in prokaryotic cells?','Mesosomes are infoldings of the cell membrane that help in respiration, secretion, increasing the surface area of the plasma membrane, and DNA replication/distribution to daughter cells.',NULL,NULL,'They act as the prokaryotic equivalent of mitochondria.','["biology", "cell-the-unit-of-life", "prokaryotes", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',1,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('fbe655be-728b-489f-852b-dcab0d77a7c7','761543ca-30d2-4ba5-9a95-840ff6911303','According to the Fluid Mosaic Model, what restricts the \'flip-flop\' movement of proteins in the cell membrane?','The amphipathic nature of membrane proteins and the hydrophobic core of the lipid bilayer restrict the flip-flop (transverse) movement of proteins. Lipids can flip-flop rarely, but proteins generally cannot.',NULL,NULL,'Proteins only show lateral movement within the overall bilayer.','["biology", "cell-the-unit-of-life", "cell-membrane", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',2,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('ab9135bc-b743-41a5-a1ed-c32ddceb198d','761543ca-30d2-4ba5-9a95-840ff6911303','Which organelles constitute the \'Endomembrane System\', and why are mitochondria excluded?','The endomembrane system includes the Endoplasmic Reticulum (ER), Golgi complex, Lysosomes, and Vacuoles. Mitochondria (along with chloroplasts and peroxisomes) are excluded because their functions are not coordinated with these components.',NULL,NULL,'Remember: ER → Golgi → Lysosomes/Vacuoles form a coordinated secretory/digestive pathway.','["biology", "cell-the-unit-of-life", "organelles", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',3,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('767f3178-ad04-4575-83e9-bfd095cd72eb','761543ca-30d2-4ba5-9a95-840ff6911303','Differentiate between the \'cis\' and \'trans\' faces of the Golgi apparatus.','The \'cis\' face (forming face) is convex and receives vesicles from the ER. The \'trans\' face (maturing face) is concave and releases the packaged and modified vesicles.',NULL,NULL,'Cis = facing the nucleus/ER. Trans = facing the plasma membrane.','["biology", "cell-the-unit-of-life", "organelles", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',4,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('4d78bd29-f01d-4aa5-a90c-124bdd80029c','761543ca-30d2-4ba5-9a95-840ff6911303','Why are lysosomes called \'suicidal bags\', and at what pH do their enzymes function optimally?','They contain powerful hydrolytic enzymes (hydrolases - lipases, proteases, carbohydrases) capable of digesting cellular components if the membrane bursts. They function optimally at an acidic pH (around pH 5).',NULL,NULL,'The acidic pH is maintained by pumping protons (H+) into the lumen.','["biology", "cell-the-unit-of-life", "organelles", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',5,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('490a7e55-9d84-401e-be03-8d924d90d241','761543ca-30d2-4ba5-9a95-840ff6911303','Name the four types of chromosomes based on the position of the centromere.','1. Metacentric (middle centromere, V-shape).\n2. Sub-metacentric (slightly away from middle, L-shape).\n3. Acrocentric (close to one end, J-shape).\n4. Telocentric (terminal centromere, i-shape).',NULL,NULL,'The shape is observed during anaphase as they are pulled to the poles.','["biology", "cell-the-unit-of-life", "nucleus", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',6,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('c3583199-50c7-4cc8-bcb9-c4900177ae58','761543ca-30d2-4ba5-9a95-840ff6911303','Identify the primary bonds linking the monomers in Proteins, Polysaccharides, and Nucleic Acids.','Proteins: Peptide bonds (between amino acids).\nPolysaccharides: Glycosidic bonds (between monosaccharides).\nNucleic Acids: Phosphodiester bonds (between nucleotides).',NULL,NULL,'All three bonds are formed by dehydration synthesis (removal of water).','["biology", "biomolecules", "class-11", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',7,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('1f6ade9b-fa48-4ee6-abf3-639774a3b0bb','761543ca-30d2-4ba5-9a95-840ff6911303','Which level of protein structure is absolutely necessary for the biological activities of proteins (like enzymes)?','The Tertiary Structure. It creates the 3-dimensional folding of the protein, forming specific active sites necessary for the protein\'s function.',NULL,NULL,'Primary is sequence, secondary is local folding (alpha-helix/beta-sheet), tertiary is overall 3D shape.','["biology", "biomolecules", "proteins", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',8,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('809be8a3-54be-411c-9924-16cb3937b654','761543ca-30d2-4ba5-9a95-840ff6911303','Describe the B-DNA double helix according to the Watson-Crick model (pitch and base pairs per turn).','The pitch (length of one complete turn) is 34 Å (3.4 nm).\nThere are 10 base pairs (bp) per turn.\nThe distance between two consecutive base pairs is 3.4 Å (0.34 nm).',NULL,NULL,'DNA strands are antiparallel and complementary.','["biology", "biomolecules", "nucleic-acids", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',9,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('0cd0e49a-a573-4740-81db-ed1aba5b2b2c','761543ca-30d2-4ba5-9a95-840ff6911303','Explain Competitive Inhibition with the classic example of succinic dehydrogenase.','In competitive inhibition, the inhibitor closely resembles the substrate and competes for the active site of the enzyme.\nExample: Inhibition of succinic dehydrogenase by malonate, which closely resembles the true substrate, succinate.',NULL,NULL,'Competitive inhibition can be overcome by increasing the substrate concentration. $V_{max}$ remains the same, but $K_m$ increases.','["biology", "biomolecules", "enzymes", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',10,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('6f39411f-2ba5-441b-b755-25ba9853da37','761543ca-30d2-4ba5-9a95-840ff6911303','What does the Michaelis constant ($K_m$) indicate about an enzyme\'s affinity for its substrate?','$K_m$ is inversely proportional to the enzyme\'s affinity for the substrate. A low $K_m$ indicates a high affinity (less substrate is needed to reach half-maximal velocity).',NULL,NULL,'$K_m$ is the substrate concentration at which the reaction velocity is half of $V_{max}$.','["biology", "biomolecules", "enzymes", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',11,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('90308b26-a7d9-4f12-9651-77ae8fc283c1','761543ca-30d2-4ba5-9a95-840ff6911303','What happens to the amount of DNA (C) and the chromosome number (N) during the S phase of the cell cycle?','The amount of DNA doubles (e.g., from 2C to 4C).\nHowever, the chromosome number remains the same (e.g., a diploid cell 2N remains 2N, but each chromosome now has two sister chromatids).',NULL,NULL,'S phase = Synthesis phase (DNA replication).','["biology", "cell-cycle", "class-11", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',12,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('59d42aea-72b8-4e40-9553-9f570a416d3f','761543ca-30d2-4ba5-9a95-840ff6911303','What is the defining feature of Metaphase in mitosis?','All chromosomes align at the equator of the cell, forming the metaphase plate. Spindle fibres attach to the kinetochores of the chromosomes.',NULL,NULL,'This is the best stage to study chromosome morphology (shape and number).','["biology", "cell-cycle", "mitosis", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',13,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('6b84d5d0-9ce4-4131-b7c0-3ecd67153661','761543ca-30d2-4ba5-9a95-840ff6911303','List the five substages of Prophase I in Meiosis in their correct sequence.','1. Leptotene\n2. Zygotene\n3. Pachytene\n4. Diplotene\n5. Diakinesis',NULL,NULL,'Mnemonic: Little Zara Please Dance Daily, OR Lazy Zebras Ponder Difficult Dreams.','["biology", "cell-cycle", "meiosis", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',14,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('977e9fb2-c49f-42ae-b571-bf68b6da8bf2','761543ca-30d2-4ba5-9a95-840ff6911303','During which specific stage of Meiosis I does crossing over occur, and what is the enzyme complex involved?','Crossing over (exchange of genetic material between non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes) occurs during the Pachytene stage.\nIt is an enzyme-mediated process involving the Recombinase enzyme complex.',NULL,NULL,'This leads to genetic recombination and variation.','["biology", "cell-cycle", "meiosis", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',15,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('0eb5e98e-6b1b-4ecd-80e7-9dc086c2e45f','761543ca-30d2-4ba5-9a95-840ff6911303','What is \'synapsis\' and in which stage does it occur?','Synapsis is the pairing of homologous chromosomes to form a bivalent (or tetrad). It occurs during the Zygotene stage of Prophase I.',NULL,NULL,'Accompanied by the formation of the synaptonemal complex.','["biology", "cell-cycle", "meiosis", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',16,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('929dea15-911c-41b0-991e-bfbeaa489b17','761543ca-30d2-4ba5-9a95-840ff6911303','Differentiate between the events of Anaphase I of meiosis and Anaphase of mitosis.','Anaphase I (Meiosis): Homologous chromosomes separate and move to opposite poles, but sister chromatids remain attached at their centromeres (no centromere splitting).\nAnaphase (Mitosis): Centromeres split, and sister chromatids separate to move to opposite poles.',NULL,NULL,'Reduction of chromosome number happens because of Anaphase I.','["biology", "cell-cycle", "comparison", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',17,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('55d74e64-f83d-45d7-9752-8a561c797b56','761543ca-30d2-4ba5-9a95-840ff6911303','Distinguish between the microtubular arrangement in cilia/flagella versus centrioles.','Cilia and flagella have a \'9+2\' axonemal arrangement (9 peripheral doublets + 2 central singlets).\nCentrioles have a \'9+0\' arrangement (9 peripheral triplets + 0 central microtubules, possessing a central proteinaceous \'hub\' instead).',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "Cilia and flagella have a \'9+2\' axonemal arrangement (9 peripheral doublets + 2 central singlets).\\nCentrioles have a \'9+0\' arrangement (9 peripheral triplets + 0 central microtubules, possessing a central proteinaceous \'hub\' instead)."}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg width=\\"300\\" height=\\"250\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 1200 600\\" xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\">\\n  <style>\\n    .title {\\n      font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\n      font-size: 28px;\\n      font-weight: bold;\\n      fill: #111;\\n    }\\n    .subtitle {\\n      font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\n      font-size: 18px;\\n      fill: #444;\\n    }\\n    .label {\\n      font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\n      font-size: 20px;\\n      font-weight: bold;\\n      fill: #222;\\n    }\\n  </style>\\n\\n  <!-- Background -->\\n  <rect width=\\"100%\\" height=\\"100%\\" fill=\\"white\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- LEFT DIAGRAM -->\\n  <g transform=\\"translate(300,250)\\">\\n    \\n    <!-- Outer Circle -->\\n    <circle cx=\\"0\\" cy=\\"0\\" r=\\"180\\"\\n            fill=\\"none\\"\\n            stroke=\\"#1e88e5\\"\\n            stroke-width=\\"5\\"/>\\n\\n    <!-- Nucleus -->\\n    <circle cx=\\"0\\" cy=\\"0\\" r=\\"32\\"\\n            fill=\\"none\\"\\n            stroke=\\"#2ecc71\\"\\n            stroke-width=\\"4\\"/>\\n\\n    <!-- Central particles -->\\n    <circle cx=\\"-10\\" cy=\\"0\\" r=\\"10\\" fill=\\"#e53935\\"/>\\n    <circle cx=\\"10\\" cy=\\"0\\" r=\\"10\\" fill=\\"#e53935\\"/>\\n\\n    <!-- Peripheral paired dots -->\\n    <g fill=\\"#1f2d3d\\">\\n      <!-- Top -->\\n      <circle cx=\\"-10\\" cy=\\"-180\\" r=\\"8\\"/>\\n      <circle cx=\\"10\\" cy=\\"-180\\" r=\\"8\\"/>\\n\\n      <!-- Top Right -->\\n      <circle cx=\\"95\\" cy=\\"-150\\" r=\\"8\\"/>\\n      <circle cx=\\"108\\" cy=\\"-138\\" r=\\"8\\"/>\\n\\n      <!-- Right -->\\n      <circle cx=\\"180\\" cy=\\"-10\\" r=\\"8\\"/>\\n      <circle cx=\\"180\\" cy=\\"10\\" r=\\"8\\"/>\\n\\n      <!-- Bottom Right -->\\n      <circle cx=\\"108\\" cy=\\"138\\" r=\\"8\\"/>\\n      <circle cx=\\"95\\" cy=\\"150\\" r=\\"8\\"/>\\n\\n      <!-- Bottom -->\\n      <circle cx=\\"-10\\" cy=\\"180\\" r=\\"8\\"/>\\n      <circle cx=\\"10\\" cy=\\"180\\" r=\\"8\\"/>\\n\\n      <!-- Bottom Left -->\\n      <circle cx=\\"-108\\" cy=\\"138\\" r=\\"8\\"/>\\n      <circle cx=\\"-95\\" cy=\\"150\\" r=\\"8\\"/>\\n\\n      <!-- Left -->\\n      <circle cx=\\"-180\\" cy=\\"-10\\" r=\\"8\\"/>\\n      <circle cx=\\"-180\\" cy=\\"10\\" r=\\"8\\"/>\\n\\n      <!-- Top Left -->\\n      <circle cx=\\"-108\\" cy=\\"-138\\" r=\\"8\\"/>\\n      <circle cx=\\"-95\\" cy=\\"-150\\" r=\\"8\\"/>\\n    </g>\\n\\n    <!-- Labels -->\\n    <text x=\\"0\\" y=\\"240\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\" class=\\"title\\">\\n      9+2 Arrangement\\n    </text>\\n\\n    <text x=\\"0\\" y=\\"275\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\" class=\\"subtitle\\">\\n      Typical Cilia and Flagella Axoneme\\n    </text>\\n  </g>\\n\\n  <!-- RIGHT DIAGRAM -->\\n  <g transform=\\"translate(900,250)\\">\\n\\n    <!-- Outer Circle -->\\n    <circle cx=\\"0\\" cy=\\"0\\" r=\\"180\\"\\n            fill=\\"none\\"\\n            stroke=\\"#9b59b6\\"\\n            stroke-width=\\"5\\"/>\\n\\n    <!-- Central body -->\\n    <circle cx=\\"0\\" cy=\\"0\\" r=\\"22\\"\\n            fill=\\"#8e44ad\\"/>\\n\\n    <!-- Connecting line -->\\n    <line x1=\\"0\\" y1=\\"-22\\" x2=\\"0\\" y2=\\"-165\\"\\n          stroke=\\"#6c757d\\"\\n          stroke-width=\\"4\\"/>\\n\\n    <!-- Top pair -->\\n    <circle cx=\\"-8\\" cy=\\"-180\\" r=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#1f2d3d\\"/>\\n    <circle cx=\\"8\\" cy=\\"-180\\" r=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#1f2d3d\\"/>\\n\\n    <!-- Labels -->\\n    <text x=\\"0\\" y=\\"240\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\" class=\\"title\\">\\n      9+0 Arrangement\\n    </text>\\n\\n    <text x=\\"0\\" y=\\"275\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\" class=\\"subtitle\\">\\n      Centriole / Basal Body (Hub &amp; Spoke Pattern)\\n    </text>\\n  </g>\\n</svg>"}]}','Centrioles are part of the centrosome and form the basal body of cilia/flagella.','["biology", "cell-the-unit-of-life", "cytoskeleton", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',18,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('1c437eac-1878-4cf1-96ae-333f06b5fa8e','761543ca-30d2-4ba5-9a95-840ff6911303','What are \'cristae\', and what is their role in mitochondria?','Cristae are the numerous infoldings of the inner mitochondrial membrane towards the matrix. They significantly increase the surface area for the attachment of enzymes needed for ATP production (Oxidative phosphorylation).',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "Cristae are the numerous infoldings of the inner mitochondrial membrane towards the matrix. They significantly increase the surface area for the attachment of enzymes needed for ATP production (Oxidative phosphorylation)."}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg width=\\"300\\" height=\\"250\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 900 500\\" xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\">\\n  <defs>\\n    <style>\\n      .label {\\n        font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;\\n        font-size: 28px;\\n        fill: #222;\\n      }\\n      .main-outline {\\n        fill: #f8f5ec;\\n        stroke: #c96a00;\\n        stroke-width: 6;\\n      }\\n      .inner-membrane {\\n        fill: none;\\n        stroke: #d35400;\\n        stroke-width: 5;\\n        stroke-linecap: round;\\n        stroke-linejoin: round;\\n      }\\n      .pointer {\\n        stroke: #555;\\n        stroke-width: 2.5;\\n      }\\n    </style>\\n  </defs>\\n\\n  <!-- Outer membrane -->\\n  <ellipse cx=\\"450\\" cy=\\"240\\" rx=\\"360\\" ry=\\"150\\" class=\\"main-outline\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Cristae / Inner membrane -->\\n  <path\\n    d=\\"\\n      M170 240\\n      C200 170, 250 170, 280 240\\n      S360 310, 390 240\\n      S470 170, 500 240\\n      S580 310, 610 240\\n      S690 170, 720 240\\n    \\"\\n    class=\\"inner-membrane\\"\\n  />\\n\\n  <!-- Label: Outer Membrane -->\\n  <text x=\\"450\\" y=\\"55\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\" class=\\"label\\">\\n    Outer Membrane\\n  </text>\\n\\n  <!-- Pointer line -->\\n  <line x1=\\"450\\" y1=\\"75\\" x2=\\"450\\" y2=\\"120\\" class=\\"pointer\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Label: Cristae -->\\n  <text x=\\"450\\" y=\\"445\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\" class=\\"label\\">\\n    Cristae (Inner Membrane)\\n  </text>\\n\\n  <!-- Pointer line -->\\n  <line x1=\\"450\\" y1=\\"390\\" x2=\\"450\\" y2=\\"340\\" class=\\"pointer\\"/>\\n</svg>"}]}','The site of the Electron Transport System (ETS).','["biology", "cell-the-unit-of-life", "organelles", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',19,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('9dc8cbf8-7daa-42fd-a995-0de56766a049','77b930dc-78bc-46d3-997d-b334f00ca931','How do you calculate the Enthalpy of a Reaction ($\\Delta_r H$) using Enthalpies of Formation vs using Bond Enthalpies?','Using Formation: $\\Delta_r H = \\sum \\Delta_f H^\\circ(\\text{Products}) - \\sum \\Delta_f H^\\circ(\\text{Reactants})$ \nUsing Bond Energy: $\\Delta_r H = \\sum BE(\\text{Reactants}) - \\sum BE(\\text{Products})$',NULL,NULL,'Watch the order! Bond energy calculation is Reactants - Products (Energy absorbed to break bonds - Energy released forming bonds).','["chemistry", "thermodynamics", "calculations", "neet-favorite"]','medium','Neet Buddy',0,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('f043ebb3-7ca2-4ae1-9d7e-7ac9857618cc','77b930dc-78bc-46d3-997d-b334f00ca931','What kind of property results from the ratio of two extensive properties?','An intensive property. \nExample: Mass (extensive) / Volume (extensive) = Density (intensive).',NULL,NULL,'Extensive ÷ Extensive = Intensive','["chemistry", "thermodynamics", "conceptual"]','medium','Neet Buddy',1,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('ebfc4526-dc59-4bd6-b4db-815285081548','77b930dc-78bc-46d3-997d-b334f00ca931','Identify which of the following are path functions and which are state functions: Internal Energy ($U$), Heat ($q$), Work ($w$), Enthalpy ($H$), Entropy ($S$).','**Path functions:** Heat ($q$) and Work ($w$). \n**State functions:** Internal Energy ($U$), Enthalpy ($H$), Entropy ($S$). (Also $P, V, T, G$)',NULL,NULL,'State functions only care about initial and final states, not how you got there.','["chemistry", "thermodynamics", "state-functions"]','medium','Neet Buddy',2,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('1220981a-f7e4-4739-8180-4b5791ddd755','77b930dc-78bc-46d3-997d-b334f00ca931','Write the mathematical expression for the First Law of Thermodynamics and define its terms.','$\\Delta U = q + w$ \nWhere $\\Delta U$ is the change in internal energy, $q$ is heat, and $w$ is work.',NULL,NULL,'Law of conservation of energy.','["chemistry", "thermodynamics", "first-law"]','medium','Neet Buddy',3,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('387ad548-0acc-4bcf-9501-a445684e2e3c','d65afa3c-7664-4e36-b666-09606bf85bef','What are the spectral regions for the Lyman and Balmer series in the hydrogen emission spectrum?','Lyman Series: Ultraviolet (UV) region. Balmer Series: Visible region.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\' viewBox=\'0 0 300 150\'><line x1=\'50\' y1=\'130\' x2=\'250\' y2=\'130\' stroke=\'black\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><text x=\'10\' y=\'135\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'12\'>n=1</text><line x1=\'50\' y1=\'90\' x2=\'250\' y2=\'90\' stroke=\'black\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><text x=\'10\' y=\'95\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'12\'>n=2</text><line x1=\'50\' y1=\'60\' x2=\'250\' y2=\'60\' stroke=\'black\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><text x=\'10\' y=\'65\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'12\'>n=3</text><line x1=\'50\' y1=\'40\' x2=\'250\' y2=\'40\' stroke=\'black\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><text x=\'10\' y=\'45\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'12\'>n=4</text><path d=\'M 100 40 L 100 125\' stroke=\'blue\' stroke-width=\'2\' fill=\'none\'/><polygon points=\'97,125 103,125 100,130\' fill=\'blue\'/><text x=\'70\' y=\'145\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'10\' fill=\'blue\'>Lyman (UV)</text><path d=\'M 200 40 L 200 85\' stroke=\'red\' stroke-width=\'2\' fill=\'none\'/><polygon points=\'197,85 203,85 200,90\' fill=\'red\'/><text x=\'170\' y=\'105\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'10\' fill=\'red\'>Balmer (Visible)</text></svg>"}]}','Lyman is highest energy, Balmer is what we can see.','["chemistry", "atomic-structure", "hydrogen-spectrum"]','medium','Neet Buddy',1,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('6656776c-7724-4515-955b-959dbe41b2a4','77b930dc-78bc-46d3-997d-b334f00ca931','According to the IUPAC sign convention, what are the signs for heat absorbed by the system and work done BY the system (expansion)?','Heat absorbed by the system: **Positive** ($q > 0$). \nWork done BY the system (expansion): **Negative** ($w < 0$).',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\' viewBox=\'0 0 200 150\'><rect x=\'50\' y=\'50\' width=\'100\' height=\'80\' fill=\'#f0f0f0\' stroke=\'black\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><rect x=\'50\' y=\'40\' width=\'100\' height=\'10\' fill=\'gray\' stroke=\'black\'/><text x=\'100\' y=\'100\' text-anchor=\'middle\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'16\'>SYSTEM</text><line x1=\'10\' y1=\'100\' x2=\'40\' y2=\'100\' stroke=\'red\' stroke-width=\'3\' marker-end=\'url(#arrowRed)\'/><text x=\'15\' y=\'90\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'14\' fill=\'red\'>+q</text><text x=\'5\' y=\'120\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'10\' fill=\'red\'>(Absorbed)</text><line x1=\'100\' y1=\'40\' x2=\'100\' y2=\'10\' stroke=\'blue\' stroke-width=\'3\' marker-end=\'url(#arrowBlue)\'/><text x=\'110\' y=\'25\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'14\' fill=\'blue\'>-w</text><text x=\'110\' y=\'40\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'10\' fill=\'blue\'>(Expansion)</text><defs><marker id=\'arrowRed\' viewBox=\'0 0 10 10\' refX=\'5\' refY=\'5\' markerWidth=\'6\' markerHeight=\'6\' orient=\'auto-start-reverse\'><path d=\'M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\' fill=\'red\'/></marker><marker id=\'arrowBlue\' viewBox=\'0 0 10 10\' refX=\'5\' refY=\'5\' markerWidth=\'6\' markerHeight=\'6\' orient=\'auto-start-reverse\'><path d=\'M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\' fill=\'blue\'/></marker></defs></svg>"}]}','Energy entering the system is positive (+). Energy leaving is negative (-).','["chemistry", "thermodynamics", "sign-convention"]','medium','Neet Buddy',4,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('e01efa8e-196d-4496-8527-5dbbf7788271','77b930dc-78bc-46d3-997d-b334f00ca931','What is the work done ($w$) during the free expansion of an ideal gas in a vacuum?','$w = 0$. \nSince the external pressure ($P_{ext}$) is zero in a vacuum, work $w = -P_{ext}\\Delta V = 0$.',NULL,NULL,'No opposing force = no work done.','["chemistry", "thermodynamics", "work"]','medium','Neet Buddy',5,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('5afc1701-dc6e-42bb-bf93-9d9227253e51','77b930dc-78bc-46d3-997d-b334f00ca931','State the formula for the maximum work done during the reversible isothermal expansion of an ideal gas.','$w_{rev} = -2.303 nRT \\log \\left(\\frac{V_2}{V_1}\\right)$ \nOR \n$w_{rev} = -2.303 nRT \\log \\left(\\frac{P_1}{P_2}\\right)$',NULL,NULL,'Reversible work is always the maximum magnitude of work.','["chemistry", "thermodynamics", "work", "formulas"]','medium','Neet Buddy',6,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('e3760b58-7d7d-437c-b0a4-c95ec63720f0','77b930dc-78bc-46d3-997d-b334f00ca931','How is the change in Enthalpy ($\\Delta H$) related to the change in Internal Energy ($\\Delta U$) for a reaction involving gases?','$\\Delta H = \\Delta U + \\Delta n_g RT$ \nWhere $\\Delta n_g = (\\text{moles of gaseous products}) - (\\text{moles of gaseous reactants})$.',NULL,NULL,'Only count the species in the (g) state!','["chemistry", "thermodynamics", "enthalpy", "neet-numerical"]','medium','Neet Buddy',7,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('dfee4572-dc0d-431c-a364-1f609fb24104','77b930dc-78bc-46d3-997d-b334f00ca931','What is the relationship between molar heat capacity at constant pressure ($C_p$) and at constant volume ($C_v$) for an ideal gas?','$C_p - C_v = R$ \n(Where $R$ is the universal gas constant).',NULL,NULL,'Known as Mayer\'s relation. Cp is always greater than Cv.','["chemistry", "thermodynamics", "heat-capacity"]','medium','Neet Buddy',8,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('b2cd3f1f-63d1-42be-a94d-3f821ddd35a7','77b930dc-78bc-46d3-997d-b334f00ca931','State Hess\'s Law of Constant Heat Summation.','If a reaction takes place in several steps, then its standard reaction enthalpy is the sum of the standard enthalpies of the intermediate reactions into which the overall reaction may be divided at the same temperature.',NULL,NULL,'Enthalpy is a state function; the path doesn\'t matter.','["chemistry", "thermodynamics", "hess-law"]','medium','Neet Buddy',9,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('f2a8c4cb-583b-48f4-9570-af94e9370bb1','77b930dc-78bc-46d3-997d-b334f00ca931','What is the Standard Enthalpy of Formation ($\\Delta_f H^\\circ$) of an element in its most stable reference state? Give examples.','It is taken as **zero** by convention. \nExamples: $\\Delta_f H^\\circ$ of $O_2(g)$, $C(graphite)$, $S_8(rhombic)$, and $Br_2(l)$ are all exactly zero.',NULL,NULL,'You cannot \'form\' an element in its natural state; it already exists.','["chemistry", "thermodynamics", "enthalpy"]','medium','Neet Buddy',10,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('9d1e67f2-4f96-4a18-998d-b31106fa57b9','77b930dc-78bc-46d3-997d-b334f00ca931','Why is the Enthalpy of Combustion ($\\Delta_c H$) always negative?','Combustion reactions are always exothermic (they release heat energy to the surroundings).',NULL,NULL,'Burning things produces heat.','["chemistry", "thermodynamics", "enthalpy"]','medium','Neet Buddy',11,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('cdb848bd-828e-4191-82d9-c1736f379b53','77b930dc-78bc-46d3-997d-b334f00ca931','What is the Enthalpy of Neutralization for a strong acid and a strong base?','It is a constant value of approximately $-57.1 \\text{ kJ/mol}$ (or $-13.7 \\text{ kcal/mol}$). \nThis is because it is simply the heat of formation of water: $H^+_{(aq)} + OH^-_{(aq)} \\rightarrow H_2O_{(l)}$.',NULL,NULL,'If a weak acid/base is used, the value is less negative because some energy is used to dissociate the weak electrolyte.','["chemistry", "thermodynamics", "enthalpy"]','medium','Neet Buddy',12,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('77b4d1f7-ad03-459c-a7be-5e8cc61ce54a','77b930dc-78bc-46d3-997d-b334f00ca931','What is the criteria for a spontaneous process according to the Second Law of Thermodynamics?','The total entropy of the universe must increase for a spontaneous process. \n$\\Delta S_{univ} = \\Delta S_{sys} + \\Delta S_{surr} > 0$',NULL,NULL,'The universe is constantly getting more disordered.','["chemistry", "thermodynamics", "entropy", "second-law"]','medium','Neet Buddy',13,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('767b3176-ebf4-4c02-b8a9-ed61372af54b','77b930dc-78bc-46d3-997d-b334f00ca931','Write the formula to calculate the change in entropy ($\\Delta S$) for a reversible phase transition (like melting or boiling) at constant temperature.','$\\Delta S = \\frac{\\Delta H_{transition}}{T}$ \n(For boiling, $\\Delta S = \\Delta H_{vap} / T_b$)',NULL,NULL,'Units are typically J / (K mol).','["chemistry", "thermodynamics", "entropy"]','medium','Neet Buddy',14,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('4a9dbcbb-1a15-4395-b06d-b9e296696db6','77b930dc-78bc-46d3-997d-b334f00ca931','Write the Gibbs equation relating $\\Delta G$, $\\Delta H$, and $\\Delta S$, and state the condition for spontaneity at constant T and P.','$\\Delta G = \\Delta H - T\\Delta S$ \nCondition: A process is spontaneous if **$\\Delta G < 0$** (negative).',NULL,NULL,'Free energy must decrease for a reaction to proceed on its own.','["chemistry", "thermodynamics", "gibbs-energy"]','medium','Neet Buddy',15,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('046b5513-b862-4c93-a0f8-9785c5820231','77b930dc-78bc-46d3-997d-b334f00ca931','At dynamic equilibrium, what are the values of $\\Delta G$ (Gibbs free energy change) and $\\Delta S_{univ}$?','At equilibrium, $\\Delta G = 0$ and $\\Delta S_{univ} = 0$.',NULL,NULL,'Don\'t confuse $\\Delta G$ with $\\Delta G^\\circ$ (Standard Gibbs Energy, which is not necessarily zero at equilibrium).','["chemistry", "thermodynamics", "equilibrium"]','medium','Neet Buddy',16,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('856d3739-c005-4921-ab75-5ad3f0198758','77b930dc-78bc-46d3-997d-b334f00ca931','What is the relationship between Standard Gibbs Free Energy ($\\Delta G^\\circ$) and the Equilibrium Constant ($K$)?','$\\Delta G^\\circ = -RT \\ln K$ \nOR \n$\\Delta G^\\circ = -2.303 RT \\log K$',NULL,NULL,'If K > 1, $\\Delta G^\\circ$ is negative (products are favored).','["chemistry", "thermodynamics", "equilibrium", "formulas"]','medium','Neet Buddy',17,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('68b62bc4-12d9-4cb2-a494-437cdcb551fe','77b930dc-78bc-46d3-997d-b334f00ca931','What is the difference between Intensive and Extensive properties? Give two examples of each.','Intensive properties do NOT depend on the quantity or size of matter (e.g., Temperature, Density, Pressure). Extensive properties depend on the quantity of matter present (e.g., Mass, Volume, Internal Energy, Enthalpy).',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\" xmlns:xlink=\\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\\" width=\\"1405\\" height=\\"587\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 1405 587\\">\\n  <image xlink:href=\\"data:image/png;base64,\\" x=\\"0\\" y=\\"0\\" width=\\"1405\\" height=\\"587\\"/>\\n</svg>"}]}','IN-tensive = IN-dependent of quantity.','["chemistry", "thermodynamics", "class-11"]','medium','Neet Buddy',18,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('5e28d6f3-43b9-44f8-a1e5-15deab3e979c','77b930dc-78bc-46d3-997d-b334f00ca931','If a reaction is Endothermic ($+\\Delta H$) and increases in entropy ($+\\Delta S$), under what temperature conditions will it be spontaneous?','It will be spontaneous only at **HIGH temperatures**. \n(At high T, the $T\\Delta S$ term becomes larger than $\\Delta H$, making $\\Delta G$ negative).',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\" xmlns:xlink=\\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\\" width=\\"1420\\" height=\\"689\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 1420 689\\">\\n  <image xlink:href=\\"data:image/png;base64,\\" x=\\"0\\" y=\\"0\\" width=\\"1420\\" height=\\"689\\"/>\\n</svg>"}]}','High temperatures favor entropy-driven processes.','["chemistry", "thermodynamics", "spontaneity"]','medium','Neet Buddy',19,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('64ec336f-8898-4aa3-aece-bc1860a6dd25','8005c54f-c1f4-4558-b44f-2a1a42860836','Define Incomplete Dominance and give an example.','A genetic situation where one allele does not completely dominate another, resulting in a new, blended phenotype in the heterozygote.\n\nExample: Flower colour in Snapdragon (Antirrhinum majus) where Red (RR) x White (rr) gives Pink (Rr).',NULL,NULL,'Think: Blending of traits (like red + white = pink).','["neet", "biology", "genetics", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',0,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('c2eb38c2-b826-4491-9971-4b8e19c39603','8005c54f-c1f4-4558-b44f-2a1a42860836','What is the genetic basis of the ABO blood group system in humans?','It is an example of Multiple Alleles and Co-dominance. The gene \'I\' has three alleles: IA, IB, and i. IA and IB are co-dominant (expressing both A and B antigens), while \'i\' is recessive (producing no antigen).',NULL,NULL,'Multiple alleles exist in a population, but an individual only has two.','["neet", "biology", "genetics", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',1,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('94f8033d-dff3-4af5-ad29-833aa520d5fe','8005c54f-c1f4-4558-b44f-2a1a42860836','Differentiate between Pleiotropy and Polygenic Inheritance with examples.','Pleiotropy: A single gene controls multiple phenotypic traits (e.g., Phenylketonuria, which causes mental retardation and reduction in hair/skin pigmentation).\n\nPolygenic Inheritance: Multiple genes control a single phenotypic trait, often showing a gradient (e.g., Human skin colour, human height).',NULL,NULL,'Pleio = many (effects), Poly = many (genes).','["neet", "biology", "genetics", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',2,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('04ea68d2-25a7-42b5-926e-bb1d76894b23','8005c54f-c1f4-4558-b44f-2a1a42860836','Explain the sex determination mechanism in Honey Bees.','It is a haplo-diploid sex determination system.\nFemales (queens and workers) are diploid (32 chromosomes) and develop from fertilized eggs.\nMales (drones) are haploid (16 chromosomes) and develop parthenogenetically from unfertilized eggs.',NULL,NULL,'Males have a grandfather but no father.','["neet", "biology", "genetics", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',3,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('81081d42-71a9-4a3c-80e1-09311c0dba50','8005c54f-c1f4-4558-b44f-2a1a42860836','Who proposed the Chromosomal Theory of Inheritance, and what does it state?','Proposed by Walter Sutton and Theodor Boveri.\nIt states that chromosomes are the vehicles of genetic heredity. The pairing and separation of a pair of chromosomes during meiosis lead to the segregation of a pair of factors (genes) they carry.',NULL,NULL,'Sutton and Boveri noticed chromosomes parallel gene behavior.','["neet", "biology", "genetics", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',4,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('4ace17d7-9274-4455-82ce-1de2ea4bc361','8005c54f-c1f4-4558-b44f-2a1a42860836','Briefly describe the chromosomal configurations for Down\'s, Klinefelter\'s, and Turner\'s syndromes.','Down\'s syndrome: Trisomy of chromosome 21 (47, XX or 47, XY).\nKlinefelter\'s syndrome: Extra X chromosome in males (47, XXY).\nTurner\'s syndrome: Absence of one X chromosome in females (45, XO).',NULL,NULL,'Trisomy vs Monosomy.','["neet", "biology", "genetics", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',5,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('8982412a-6fda-4016-a009-065ef8002614','8005c54f-c1f4-4558-b44f-2a1a42860836','What was the Hershey-Chase experiment, and what did it prove?','Alfred Hershey and Martha Chase used bacteriophages labeled with radioactive Phosphorus (32P, labels DNA) and radioactive Sulfur (35S, labels protein). It proved unequivocally that DNA is the genetic material that enters the bacteria, not protein.',NULL,NULL,'DNA has P, Protein has S.','["neet", "biology", "molecular-biology", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',6,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('8a8080cf-9892-4eb3-b573-b7284bc178d2','8005c54f-c1f4-4558-b44f-2a1a42860836','Describe the structure of a Nucleosome.','The basic repeating structural unit of chromatin. It consists of about 200 base pairs of negatively charged DNA wrapped around a positively charged histone octamer (composed of two molecules each of H2A, H2B, H3, and H4).',NULL,NULL,'Beads on a string.','["neet", "biology", "molecular-biology", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',7,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('855da8f1-e08d-4f34-a75c-915dcc685675','8005c54f-c1f4-4558-b44f-2a1a42860836','State the Central Dogma of Molecular Biology.','Proposed by Francis Crick, it describes the unidirectional flow of genetic information: DNA is transcribed into mRNA, which is then translated into Protein.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "svg", "value": "<svg width=\\"350\\" height=\\"100\\" xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\"><g font-family=\\"sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"14\\" fill=\\"black\\"><rect x=\\"10\\" y=\\"30\\" width=\\"60\\" height=\\"30\\" fill=\\"#add8e6\\" rx=\\"5\\"/><text x=\\"22\\" y=\\"50\\">DNA</text><path d=\\"M 75 45 L 115 45\\" stroke=\\"black\\" stroke-width=\\"2\\"/><polygon points=\\"115,40 125,45 115,50\\" fill=\\"black\\"/><text x=\\"75\\" y=\\"35\\" font-size=\\"10\\">Transcription</text><rect x=\\"130\\" y=\\"30\\" width=\\"60\\" height=\\"30\\" fill=\\"#90ee90\\" rx=\\"5\\"/><text x=\\"140\\" y=\\"50\\">mRNA</text><path d=\\"M 195 45 L 235 45\\" stroke=\\"black\\" stroke-width=\\"2\\"/><polygon points=\\"235,40 245,45 235,50\\" fill=\\"black\\"/><text x=\\"195\\" y=\\"35\\" font-size=\\"10\\">Translation</text><rect x=\\"250\\" y=\\"30\\" width=\\"60\\" height=\\"30\\" fill=\\"#ffb6c1\\" rx=\\"5\\"/><text x=\\"255\\" y=\\"50\\">Protein</text></g><path d=\\"M 25 30 C 25 10, 55 10, 55 30\\" stroke=\\"black\\" stroke-width=\\"2\\" fill=\\"none\\"/><polygon points=\\"50,25 55,30 60,25\\" fill=\\"black\\"/><text x=\\"15\\" y=\\"15\\" font-size=\\"10\\">Replication</text></svg>"}]}','DNA -> RNA -> Protein','["neet", "biology", "molecular-biology", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',8,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('a4e86d37-97df-4708-9265-b3a5467d8432','d65afa3c-7664-4e36-b666-09606bf85bef','State the Rydberg formula for calculating the wavenumber of hydrogen spectral lines.','$\\bar{\\nu} = \\frac{1}{\\lambda} = R_H Z^2 \\left( \\frac{1}{n_1^2} - \\frac{1}{n_2^2} \\right)$',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "Where $R_H = 109677 \\\\text{ cm}^{-1}$ (Rydberg constant), $Z$ is atomic number, and $n_2 > n_1$."}]}','Don\'t forget Z^2 for hydrogen-like species!','["chemistry", "atomic-structure", "formulas"]','medium','Neet Buddy',2,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('dd6d99a2-d6cf-4e3a-b8af-4ca477165feb','d65afa3c-7664-4e36-b666-09606bf85bef','What is Bohr\'s postulate regarding the angular momentum of an electron?','The angular momentum ($mvr$) of an electron is quantized and is an integral multiple of $h / 2\\pi$.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "$mvr = \\\\frac{nh}{2\\\\pi}$ (where $n = 1, 2, 3, \\\\dots$)"}]}','mvr = ...','["chemistry", "atomic-structure", "bohr-model"]','medium','Neet Buddy',3,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('2b471995-45de-4074-912a-9d6f3fb0febb','8005c54f-c1f4-4558-b44f-2a1a42860836','What are the components and function of the Lac Operon?','It is an inducible operon governing lactose metabolism in E. coli. Components:\n- Regulator gene (i): codes for repressor.\n- Promoter (p): RNA polymerase binding site.\n- Operator (o): repressor binding site.\n- Structural genes (z, y, a): code for beta-galactosidase, permease, and transacetylase.\nLactose (inducer) binds the repressor, freeing the operator and allowing transcription.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "svg", "value": "<svg width=\\"350\\" height=\\"80\\" xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\"><g font-family=\\"sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\"><rect x=\\"10\\" y=\\"20\\" width=\\"40\\" height=\\"30\\" fill=\\"#ffcccb\\" stroke=\\"#000\\"/><text x=\\"30\\" y=\\"40\\">i</text><rect x=\\"50\\" y=\\"20\\" width=\\"40\\" height=\\"30\\" fill=\\"#add8e6\\" stroke=\\"#000\\"/><text x=\\"70\\" y=\\"40\\">p</text><rect x=\\"90\\" y=\\"20\\" width=\\"40\\" height=\\"30\\" fill=\\"#add8e6\\" stroke=\\"#000\\"/><text x=\\"110\\" y=\\"40\\">o</text><rect x=\\"130\\" y=\\"20\\" width=\\"50\\" height=\\"30\\" fill=\\"#fdfd96\\" stroke=\\"#000\\"/><text x=\\"155\\" y=\\"40\\">z</text><rect x=\\"180\\" y=\\"20\\" width=\\"50\\" height=\\"30\\" fill=\\"#fdfd96\\" stroke=\\"#000\\"/><text x=\\"205\\" y=\\"40\\">y</text><rect x=\\"230\\" y=\\"20\\" width=\\"50\\" height=\\"30\\" fill=\\"#fdfd96\\" stroke=\\"#000\\"/><text x=\\"255\\" y=\\"40\\">a</text><text x=\\"30\\" y=\\"65\\" font-size=\\"10\\">Repressor</text><text x=\\"205\\" y=\\"65\\" font-size=\\"10\\">Structural Genes</text></g></svg>"}]}','i-p-o-z-y-a','["neet", "biology", "molecular-biology", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',9,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('7a040ee5-0d10-4533-b577-6d4953432db0','8005c54f-c1f4-4558-b44f-2a1a42860836','What is the role of VNTRs in DNA Fingerprinting?','VNTRs (Variable Number of Tandem Repeats) are mini-satellites of non-coding DNA. They show a high degree of genetic polymorphism. Because the number of repeats varies significantly between individuals, they form unique banding patterns on a gel, serving as the basis for DNA fingerprinting.',NULL,NULL,'VNTR = Genetic Barcode','["neet", "biology", "molecular-biology", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',10,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('47402c36-755a-4462-9f1e-9a76ddb30d89','8005c54f-c1f4-4558-b44f-2a1a42860836','State the Hardy-Weinberg Principle and its algebraic equation.','The principle states that allele and genotype frequencies in a population remain constant from generation to generation in the absence of evolutionary influences (like mutation, selection, gene flow). \nEquation: p² + 2pq + q² = 1 (where p and q are the frequencies of dominant and recessive alleles).',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "svg", "value": "<svg width=\\"200\\" height=\\"70\\" xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\"><rect x=\\"10\\" y=\\"10\\" width=\\"180\\" height=\\"50\\" fill=\\"#f0f8ff\\" rx=\\"10\\" stroke=\\"#000\\" stroke-width=\\"2\\"/><text x=\\"25\\" y=\\"40\\" font-family=\\"sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"18\\" font-weight=\\"bold\\">p² + 2pq + q² = 1</text></svg>"}]}','Binomial expansion of (p+q)^2 = 1.','["neet", "biology", "evolution", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',11,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('5d3fcfdf-04eb-4c7a-8770-eceea2361f1e','8005c54f-c1f4-4558-b44f-2a1a42860836','Contrast Homologous and Analogous organs and mention the type of evolution they represent.','Homologous organs: Same basic anatomical structure/origin, different functions (e.g., Forelimbs of whales, bats, cheetah, humans). Represents Divergent Evolution.\n\nAnalogous organs: Different anatomical structure/origin, same functions (e.g., Wings of a butterfly and a bird). Represents Convergent Evolution.',NULL,NULL,'HD (Homologous=Divergent) / AC (Analogous=Convergent).','["neet", "biology", "evolution", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',12,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('0e266ed2-b47f-48bd-849f-d9fd9126475d','8005c54f-c1f4-4558-b44f-2a1a42860836','What is Adaptive Radiation? Give one classic example.','It is an evolutionary process where species originating from a single common ancestor radiate to different geographical areas and adapt to different ecological niches.\nExample: Darwin\'s Finches in the Galapagos Islands (diverged for different beak types based on diet).',NULL,NULL,'Radiating outwards from a central ancestral point.','["neet", "biology", "evolution", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',13,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('9cc86764-82f1-439e-9b9e-142b100a847a','8005c54f-c1f4-4558-b44f-2a1a42860836','List the chronological sequence of major ancestors in Human Evolution.','Dryopithecus -> Ramapithecus -> Australopithecus -> Homo habilis -> Homo erectus -> Neanderthal man -> Homo sapiens.',NULL,NULL,'Dr. Ram Austin Has Erected New Statues.','["neet", "biology", "evolution", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',14,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('fb111c41-3b7e-4660-b54e-7596393e6fbd','8005c54f-c1f4-4558-b44f-2a1a42860836','Differentiate between Thalassemia and Sickle-cell anemia in terms of genetic defect.','Thalassemia is a quantitative problem: synthesizing too few globin molecules (reduced rate of synthesis of alpha or beta chains).\nSickle-cell anemia is a qualitative problem: synthesizing an incorrectly functioning globin (point mutation causing valine to replace glutamic acid).',NULL,NULL,'Quantity (Thalassemia) vs. Quality (Sickle-cell).','["neet", "biology", "genetics", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',15,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('b2ce9aee-a07f-4ff5-8194-5585412b2e7c','8005c54f-c1f4-4558-b44f-2a1a42860836','What was the setup and conclusion of the Miller-Urey experiment?','Setup: Recreated primitive Earth atmosphere using a sealed flask containing CH4, H2, NH3, and water vapor at 800°C, subjected to electric discharges.\nConclusion: Observed the formation of organic compounds (amino acids), experimentally proving Oparin-Haldane\'s theory of Chemical Evolution.',NULL,NULL,'Spark-discharge experiment creating life\'s building blocks.','["neet", "biology", "evolution", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',16,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('7b3799e1-6af0-4404-bc1f-fd390f547577','8005c54f-c1f4-4558-b44f-2a1a42860836','Define Linkage and Recombination.','Linkage: The physical association of genes located close together on the same chromosome, resulting in a tendency to be inherited together.\nRecombination: The generation of non-parental gene combinations due to the physical crossing over between homologous chromosomes during meiosis.',NULL,NULL,'Linkage keeps traits together; Recombination shuffles them.','["neet", "biology", "genetics", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',17,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('389d24bd-6917-43d5-852a-d8990ddbc8f7','8005c54f-c1f4-4558-b44f-2a1a42860836','What are the salient features of the Genetic Code?','1. Triplet code (3 bases = 1 amino acid).\n2. Unambiguous and specific (one codon = one amino acid).\n3. Degenerate (some amino acids are coded by more than one codon).\n4. Universal (mostly the same across all organisms).\n5. Contiguous (no commas or overlaps).',NULL,NULL,'64 codons for 20 amino acids -> Degeneracy.','["neet", "biology", "molecular-biology", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',18,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('e29e4cd0-817a-49bb-a799-6d938094032c','8005c54f-c1f4-4558-b44f-2a1a42860836','Describe semi-conservative DNA replication and name the primary enzyme.','Semi-conservative replication means the two strands of the parent DNA double helix unwind, and each strand acts as a template for synthesizing a new complementary strand. The resulting DNA molecules have one old (parental) strand and one newly synthesized strand.\nPrimary enzyme: DNA-dependent DNA polymerase.',NULL,NULL,'Proved by Meselson and Stahl using heavy nitrogen (15N).','["neet", "biology", "molecular-biology", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',19,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('60c43ea7-86e1-4c45-a413-f310f90820c6','8505e43f-24b1-4b04-98ff-06fecbe7c516','State the spherical mirror formula and the expression for linear magnification ($m$).','Formula: $\\frac{1}{v} + \\frac{1}{u} = \\frac{1}{f}$. Magnification: $m = -\\frac{v}{u} = \\frac{h_i}{h_o}$.',NULL,NULL,'Remember the negative sign in the magnification formula for mirrors!','["physics", "ray-optics", "mirrors"]','medium','Neet Buddy',0,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('7f42230f-eb16-40f4-980a-6da5326d81e8','8505e43f-24b1-4b04-98ff-06fecbe7c516','What is the formula for refraction at a single spherical surface separating two media?','$\\frac{n_2}{v} - \\frac{n_1}{u} = \\frac{n_2 - n_1}{R}$','{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "Write the formula relating object distance ($u$), image distance ($v$), and radius of curvature ($R$)."}]}',NULL,'$n_1$ is the medium where the object is placed, $n_2$ is the other medium.','["physics", "ray-optics", "refraction"]','medium','Neet Buddy',1,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('13c119e5-45a7-4cb8-b8eb-c4b02d15283f','8505e43f-24b1-4b04-98ff-06fecbe7c516','Write the Lens Maker\'s Formula.','$\\frac{1}{f} = (n_{21} - 1) \\left(\\frac{1}{R_1} - \\frac{1}{R_2}\\right)$',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "Where $n_{21} = \\\\frac{n_2}{n_1}$ is the refractive index of the lens relative to the surrounding medium."}]}','Used by opticians to design lenses of a desired focal length.','["physics", "ray-optics", "lenses"]','medium','Neet Buddy',2,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('544917d9-657e-4132-b7a9-e407caf78d31','8505e43f-24b1-4b04-98ff-06fecbe7c516','State the Thin Lens Formula and its magnification ($m$).','Formula: $\\frac{1}{v} - \\frac{1}{u} = \\frac{1}{f}$. Magnification: $m = \\frac{v}{u}$.',NULL,NULL,'Notice the sign difference compared to the mirror formula.','["physics", "ray-optics", "lenses"]','medium','Neet Buddy',3,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('8afe916f-7656-425c-9808-4e04435439ca','8505e43f-24b1-4b04-98ff-06fecbe7c516','How do you calculate Equivalent Power ($P$), Focal Length ($F$), and Magnification ($m$) for thin lenses in contact?','$P_{eq} = P_1 + P_2 + ...$ \\n$\\frac{1}{F_{eq}} = \\frac{1}{f_1} + \\frac{1}{f_2} + ...$ \\n$m_{eq} = m_1 \\times m_2 \\times ...$',NULL,NULL,'Powers add up, magnifications multiply.','["physics", "ray-optics", "lens-combination"]','medium','Neet Buddy',4,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('c28935e7-2128-4c83-9cb1-a109f953a3c5','8505e43f-24b1-4b04-98ff-06fecbe7c516','What is the formula for the refractive index ($\\mu$) of a prism at the angle of minimum deviation ($\\delta_m$)?','$\\mu = \\frac{\\sin\\left(\\frac{A + \\delta_m}{2}\\right)}{\\sin\\left(\\frac{A}{2}\\right)}$',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "Where $A$ is the angle of the prism."}]}','At minimum deviation, angle of incidence ($i$) = angle of emergence ($e$), and the refracted ray is parallel to the base.','["physics", "ray-optics", "prism"]','medium','Neet Buddy',5,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('811c32e4-4873-455f-a17a-e036fe4d92ea','8505e43f-24b1-4b04-98ff-06fecbe7c516','What is the formula for the magnifying power of a Compound Microscope in normal adjustment (image at infinity)?','$m = m_o \\times m_e \\approx \\left(\\frac{L}{f_o}\\right) \\times \\left(\\frac{D}{f_e}\\right)$',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "$L$ = tube length, $D$ = least distance of distinct vision (25 cm), $f_o$ = focal length of objective, $f_e$ = focal length of eyepiece."}]}','Product of objective and eyepiece magnifications.','["physics", "ray-optics", "optical-instruments"]','medium','Neet Buddy',6,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('007c3b10-b3b8-4440-b18b-7b742a931ddf','8505e43f-24b1-4b04-98ff-06fecbe7c516','Write the magnifying power and tube length formula for an Astronomical Telescope in normal adjustment.','Magnification: $m = \\frac{f_o}{f_e}$ \\nTube Length: $L = f_o + f_e$',NULL,NULL,'Objective lens is much larger than the eyepiece here.','["physics", "ray-optics", "optical-instruments"]','medium','Neet Buddy',7,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('09ac50e6-fa9f-4c13-8f6c-ef1c4d538280','8505e43f-24b1-4b04-98ff-06fecbe7c516','Define a Wavefront and state Huygens\' Principle.','A wavefront is the continuous locus of all particles of a medium vibrating in the same phase. \\nHuygens\' Principle: Every point on a given wavefront acts as a fresh source of secondary spherical wavelets.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "The new wavefront is the forward envelope of these secondary wavelets."}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg viewBox=\\"0 0 200 100\\" xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\"><line x1=\\"50\\" y1=\\"20\\" x2=\\"50\\" y2=\\"80\\" stroke=\\"black\\" stroke-width=\\"2\\"/><circle cx=\\"50\\" cy=\\"30\\" r=\\"20\\" fill=\\"none\\" stroke=\\"blue\\" stroke-width=\\"1\\"/><circle cx=\\"50\\" cy=\\"50\\" r=\\"20\\" fill=\\"none\\" stroke=\\"blue\\" stroke-width=\\"1\\"/><circle cx=\\"50\\" cy=\\"70\\" r=\\"20\\" fill=\\"none\\" stroke=\\"blue\\" stroke-width=\\"1\\"/><line x1=\\"70\\" y1=\\"20\\" x2=\\"70\\" y2=\\"80\\" stroke=\\"red\\" stroke-width=\\"2\\" stroke-dasharray=\\"4\\"/><text x=\\"10\\" y=\\"95\\" font-size=\\"8\\">Primary Wavefront</text><text x=\\"80\\" y=\\"95\\" font-size=\\"8\\">New Wavefront (Envelope)</text></svg>"}]}','Explains wave propagation geometrically.','["physics", "wave-optics", "huygens"]','medium','Neet Buddy',8,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('d1b84857-3a0b-46e8-a5c8-7f44733364f3','8505e43f-24b1-4b04-98ff-06fecbe7c516','What are Coherent Sources?','Sources that emit light waves of the same frequency and have a zero or constant phase difference.',NULL,NULL,'Essential condition for sustained interference.','["physics", "wave-optics", "interference"]','medium','Neet Buddy',9,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('943f9b4c-04b8-4f92-9432-a4b794b53f94','8505e43f-24b1-4b04-98ff-06fecbe7c516','In Young\'s Double Slit Experiment (YDSE), what are the path difference ($\\Delta x$) conditions for Constructive and Destructive interference?','Constructive (Bright Fringes): $\\Delta x = n\\lambda$ \\nDestructive (Dark Fringes): $\\Delta x = (n - \\frac{1}{2})\\lambda$',NULL,NULL,'$n = 1, 2, 3...$','["physics", "wave-optics", "interference"]','medium','Neet Buddy',10,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('7233b8cb-07c9-45f8-b5ec-3ebd1cb63149','8505e43f-24b1-4b04-98ff-06fecbe7c516','What is the expression for fringe width ($\\beta$) in Young\'s Double Slit Experiment?','$\\beta = \\frac{\\lambda D}{d}$',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "$\\\\lambda$ = wavelength, $D$ = distance to screen, $d$ = distance between slits."}]}','Distance between two consecutive bright or dark fringes.','["physics", "wave-optics", "interference"]','medium','Neet Buddy',11,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('d81d4044-ed75-4bc9-8c83-4e278d71c0b3','8505e43f-24b1-4b04-98ff-06fecbe7c516','How does the fringe width in YDSE change if the entire apparatus is immersed in water?','The fringe width decreases.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "In water, the wavelength becomes $\\\\lambda\' = \\\\frac{\\\\lambda}{\\\\mu}$. Since $\\\\beta \\\\propto \\\\lambda$, the new fringe width becomes $\\\\beta\' = \\\\frac{\\\\beta}{\\\\mu}$."}]}','Think about what happens to wavelength in a denser medium.','["physics", "wave-optics", "conceptual"]','medium','Neet Buddy',12,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('a02bcf78-b626-43bd-9265-a31bfa71d233','8505e43f-24b1-4b04-98ff-06fecbe7c516','What is the condition for the $n^{th}$ minimum in single-slit diffraction?','$a \\sin\\theta = n\\lambda$',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "Where $a$ is the width of the slit and $n = 1, 2, 3...$"}]}','Notice it\'s the exact opposite of the condition for maxima in interference!','["physics", "wave-optics", "diffraction"]','medium','Neet Buddy',13,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('f44e1b90-bfca-4a37-8ed4-c558ee7c295c','8505e43f-24b1-4b04-98ff-06fecbe7c516','Write the expressions for the Angular Width and Linear Width of the central maximum in single-slit diffraction.','Angular Width: $\\frac{2\\lambda}{a}$ \\nLinear Width: $\\frac{2\\lambda D}{a}$',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "svg", "value": "<svg viewBox=\\"0 0 200 100\\" xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\"><line x1=\\"0\\" y1=\\"80\\" x2=\\"200\\" y2=\\"80\\" stroke=\\"black\\" stroke-width=\\"1\\"/><path d=\\"M 20 80 Q 30 70 40 80 Q 50 70 60 80 Q 80 10 100 10 Q 120 10 140 80 Q 150 70 160 80 Q 170 70 180 80\\" fill=\\"none\\" stroke=\\"blue\\" stroke-width=\\"2\\"/><text x=\\"75\\" y=\\"95\\" font-size=\\"10\\">Central Max</text><line x1=\\"60\\" y1=\\"80\\" x2=\\"140\\" y2=\\"80\\" stroke=\\"red\\" stroke-width=\\"3\\"/></svg>"}]}','It is twice as wide as the secondary maxima.','["physics", "wave-optics", "diffraction"]','medium','Neet Buddy',14,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('87735def-2eb5-4b9a-bf6b-3dcb92e70a82','8505e43f-24b1-4b04-98ff-06fecbe7c516','What is Brewster\'s Law for plane-polarized light?','$\\mu = \\tan i_p$',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "When light is incident at the polarizing angle ($i_p$), the reflected and refracted rays are perpendicular to each other ($90^\\\\circ$)."}]}','Relates refractive index to polarizing angle.','["physics", "wave-optics", "polarization"]','medium','Neet Buddy',15,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('3741ff45-5ec9-47f0-9589-e8a3682f4db7','8505e43f-24b1-4b04-98ff-06fecbe7c516','State Malus\' Law.','$I = I_0 \\cos^2\\theta$',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "Where $I_0$ is the maximum intensity and $\\\\theta$ is the angle between the transmission axes of the polarizer and the analyzer."}]}','Intensity of light after passing through an analyzer.','["physics", "wave-optics", "polarization"]','medium','Neet Buddy',16,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('b8525d52-5a02-48cb-882a-67ca70d44301','8505e43f-24b1-4b04-98ff-06fecbe7c516','Why is a reflecting telescope (Cassegrain) preferred over a refracting telescope?','1) No chromatic aberration. 2) Reduced spherical aberration (using parabolic mirrors). 3) Mirrors are easier to support mechanically. 4) Higher resolving power due to larger easily manufactured apertures.',NULL,NULL,'Mirrors vs. Lenses.','["physics", "ray-optics", "optical-instruments"]','medium','Neet Buddy',17,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('da096174-1be8-49bb-b326-ae28e06b78c2','8505e43f-24b1-4b04-98ff-06fecbe7c516','What happens to the intensity of unpolarized light of intensity $I_0$ when it passes through a single ideal polaroid?','The intensity is halved: $I = \\frac{I_0}{2}$.',NULL,NULL,'The polaroid blocks half of the electric field vibrations.','["physics", "wave-optics", "polarization"]','medium','Neet Buddy',18,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('03bd82c8-53c1-4b1a-a6bb-7cd4f517b5a6','8505e43f-24b1-4b04-98ff-06fecbe7c516','What is Total Internal Reflection (TIR) and its essential conditions?','TIR is the complete reflection of light back into the same medium. Conditions: 1) Light must travel from a denser to a rarer medium. 2) Angle of incidence ($i$) > Critical angle ($i_c$).',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "TIR is the complete reflection of light back into the same medium. Conditions: 1) Light must travel from a denser to a rarer medium. 2) Angle of incidence ($i$) > Critical angle ($i_c$)."}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\" xmlns:xlink=\\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\\" width=\\"461\\" height=\\"285\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 461 285\\">\\n  <image xlink:href=\\"data:image/png;base64,\\" x=\\"0\\" y=\\"0\\" width=\\"461\\" height=\\"285\\"/>\\n</svg>"}]}','Mirages and optical fibers work on this principle.','["physics", "ray-optics", "tir"]','medium','Neet Buddy',19,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('6a4a8095-3c75-4566-8000-268c0e899f77','8a4254b1-1ea2-4950-982d-aa0ef43e9cc3','Does a square planar complex of type $[Mabcd]$ show optical isomerism?','**No.**\nSquare planar complexes do not show optical isomerism because they possess a plane of symmetry (the plane of the molecule itself) and a center of symmetry.',NULL,NULL,'Flat molecules are superimposable on their mirror images.','["chemistry", "coordination-compounds", "isomerism", "optical"]','medium','Neet Buddy',0,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('7ba7424d-eaf9-49e9-89df-9c4f6516628a','8a4254b1-1ea2-4950-982d-aa0ef43e9cc3','According to Werner\'s Theory, what are the two types of valencies shown by a metal in a coordination compound?','1. **Primary Valency:** Corresponds to the oxidation state of the metal (ionizable, satisfied by negative ions).\n2. **Secondary Valency:** Corresponds to the coordination number of the metal (non-ionizable, satisfied by neutral molecules or negative ions).',NULL,NULL,'Primary = Oxidation State. Secondary = Coordination Number.','["chemistry", "coordination-compounds", "werners-theory"]','medium','Neet Buddy',1,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('b04b0436-d4ea-4e09-8cdf-53d5950811d4','8a4254b1-1ea2-4950-982d-aa0ef43e9cc3','What is an Ambidentate Ligand? Give two examples.','A unidentate ligand that can ligate (bind) through two different donor atoms.\nExamples: \n1. $NO_2^-$ (can bind through N or O)\n2. $SCN^-$ (can bind through S or N)',NULL,NULL,'Ambi = both (like ambidextrous).','["chemistry", "coordination-compounds", "ligands"]','medium','Neet Buddy',2,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('77fdc7fc-6539-4c8c-8598-e749508efe6d','8a4254b1-1ea2-4950-982d-aa0ef43e9cc3','What is the Denticity and the charge of an EDTA ion?','Denticity: **Hexadentate** (6 donor atoms: two Nitrogen and four Oxygen).\nCharge: **-4** (Ethylene diamine tetraacetate: $EDTA^{4-}$).',NULL,NULL,'Used in estimating water hardness and treating lead poisoning.','["chemistry", "coordination-compounds", "ligands", "neet-favorite"]','medium','Neet Buddy',3,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929');
INSERT INTO `flashcards` (`id`, `deck_id`, `front`, `back`, `front_body`, `back_body`, `hint`, `tags`, `difficulty`, `source`, `position`, `created_at`) VALUES
('7c6cd8ef-0af4-4a69-b7ca-6be51160145d','8a4254b1-1ea2-4950-982d-aa0ef43e9cc3','What is the Chelate Effect?','The enhanced thermodynamic stability of coordination compounds containing cyclic ring structures formed by di- or polydentate ligands, compared to similar complexes with monodentate ligands.',NULL,NULL,'Rings bring stability (entropy driven).','["chemistry", "coordination-compounds", "chelation"]','medium','Neet Buddy',4,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('7c72cc0a-37f2-40e4-bcb1-0c2f82fcd6cc','8a4254b1-1ea2-4950-982d-aa0ef43e9cc3','Write the IUPAC name for $K_3[Fe(CN)_6]$.','**Potassium hexacyanoferrate(III)**',NULL,NULL,'Name the cation first. The complex is anionic, so use the suffix \'-ate\' for the metal.','["chemistry", "coordination-compounds", "iupac"]','medium','Neet Buddy',5,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('7b122018-9c84-4121-a592-a0ebce0fc187','8a4254b1-1ea2-4950-982d-aa0ef43e9cc3','Write the IUPAC name for $[Co(NH_3)_5Cl]Cl_2$.','**Pentaamminechloridocobalt(III) chloride**',NULL,NULL,'Alphabetical order for ligands: ammine before chlorido. Metal has normal name because complex is cationic.','["chemistry", "coordination-compounds", "iupac"]','medium','Neet Buddy',6,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('eafc4c93-94b2-40cc-bbeb-75d4041f8a87','8a4254b1-1ea2-4950-982d-aa0ef43e9cc3','Identify the type of structural isomerism: \n$[Co(NH_3)_5(SO_4)]Br$ and $[Co(NH_3)_5Br]SO_4$','**Ionization Isomerism**.\nThey give different ions in solution ($Br^-$ vs $SO_4^{2-}$).',NULL,NULL,'They exchange ions between the coordination sphere and the ionization sphere.','["chemistry", "coordination-compounds", "isomerism"]','medium','Neet Buddy',7,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('23fd64b3-71ca-4d93-9fd2-674316adb1ff','8a4254b1-1ea2-4950-982d-aa0ef43e9cc3','Identify the type of structural isomerism: \n$[Co(NH_3)_5(NO_2)]Cl_2$ and $[Co(NH_3)_5(ONO)]Cl_2$','**Linkage Isomerism**.\nIt occurs in complexes containing ambidentate ligands (like $NO_2^-$ binding through N vs O).',NULL,NULL,'Same ligand, different point of attachment.','["chemistry", "coordination-compounds", "isomerism"]','medium','Neet Buddy',8,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('927599cd-0c76-45af-91dd-ebf08d25131e','8a4254b1-1ea2-4950-982d-aa0ef43e9cc3','What is Coordination Isomerism?','It arises when both the cation and anion are complex ions, and there is an interchange of ligands between the cationic and anionic entities.\nExample: $[Co(NH_3)_6][Cr(CN)_6]$ and $[Cr(NH_3)_6][Co(CN)_6]$.',NULL,NULL,'Swapping ligands between two different coordination spheres.','["chemistry", "coordination-compounds", "isomerism"]','medium','Neet Buddy',9,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('d3411b61-389f-4583-afd1-dd1bcc88b78e','8a4254b1-1ea2-4950-982d-aa0ef43e9cc3','Explain Facial (fac) and Meridional (mer) isomerism. In which type of octahedral complex does it occur?','It occurs in octahedral complexes of the type **$[Ma_3b_3]$** (e.g., $[Co(NH_3)_3Cl_3]$).\n**Facial (fac):** Three identical ligands occupy the corners of one triangular face of the octahedron.\n**Meridional (mer):** Three identical ligands occupy positions around the meridian (equator) of the octahedron.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\' viewBox=\'0 0 350 150\'><text x=\'50\' y=\'20\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'14\' font-weight=\'bold\'>fac-Isomer</text><polygon points=\'80,50 40,90 120,90\' fill=\'lightblue\' opacity=\'0.5\' stroke=\'blue\'/><circle cx=\'80\' cy=\'80\' r=\'5\' fill=\'black\'/><text x=\'75\' y=\'75\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'10\' fill=\'white\'>M</text><circle cx=\'80\' cy=\'40\' r=\'8\' fill=\'red\'/><text x=\'76\' y=\'44\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'10\' fill=\'white\'>a</text><circle cx=\'30\' cy=\'100\' r=\'8\' fill=\'red\'/><text x=\'26\' y=\'104\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'10\' fill=\'white\'>a</text><circle cx=\'130\' cy=\'100\' r=\'8\' fill=\'red\'/><text x=\'126\' y=\'104\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'10\' fill=\'white\'>a</text><text x=\'200\' y=\'20\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'14\' font-weight=\'bold\'>mer-Isomer</text><polygon points=\'230,40 190,80 230,120 270,80\' fill=\'lightgreen\' opacity=\'0.5\' stroke=\'green\'/><circle cx=\'230\' cy=\'80\' r=\'5\' fill=\'black\'/><text x=\'225\' y=\'75\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'10\' fill=\'white\'>M</text><circle cx=\'230\' cy=\'30\' r=\'8\' fill=\'red\'/><text x=\'226\' y=\'34\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'10\' fill=\'white\'>a</text><circle cx=\'180\' cy=\'80\' r=\'8\' fill=\'red\'/><text x=\'176\' y=\'84\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'10\' fill=\'white\'>a</text><circle cx=\'230\' cy=\'130\' r=\'8\' fill=\'red\'/><text x=\'226\' y=\'134\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'10\' fill=\'white\'>a</text><text x=\'25\' y=\'140\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'10\' fill=\'gray\'>Ligands \'a\' form a triangle</text><text x=\'175\' y=\'140\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'10\' fill=\'gray\'>Ligands \'a\' form a T-shape/plane</text></svg>"}]}','Fac = face, Mer = meridian.','["chemistry", "coordination-compounds", "isomerism", "stereoisomerism"]','medium','Neet Buddy',10,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('86571b3e-ee52-4d3f-a004-f60e7a5ef9a1','8a4254b1-1ea2-4950-982d-aa0ef43e9cc3','Which specific geometric isomer of $[M(AA)_2a_2]$ (where AA is a bidentate ligand like \'en\') is optically active: cis or trans?','Only the **cis-isomer** is optically active (chiral).\nThe trans-isomer is optically inactive because it has a plane of symmetry.',NULL,NULL,'Trans is too symmetric (ligands are opposite).','["chemistry", "coordination-compounds", "isomerism", "neet-favorite"]','medium','Neet Buddy',11,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('5a091437-73cb-4c8b-b286-23fe1e5ad093','8a4254b1-1ea2-4950-982d-aa0ef43e9cc3','According to Valence Bond Theory (VBT), what is the hybridization and geometry of $[Ni(CO)_4]$?','Hybridization: **$sp^3$**\nGeometry: **Tetrahedral**\n(Note: $CO$ is a strong field ligand. Nickel is in 0 oxidation state ($3d^8 4s^2$). The $4s$ electrons pair up in $3d$, making it $3d^{10}$, leaving $4s$ and $4p$ empty for $sp^3$ hybridization).',NULL,NULL,'Nickel Carbonyl is an exception to the \'strong field = square planar\' generalization for Ni.','["chemistry", "coordination-compounds", "vbt", "geometry"]','medium','Neet Buddy',12,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('35e6f525-d5e5-49bf-9417-84872d51a3f9','8a4254b1-1ea2-4950-982d-aa0ef43e9cc3','According to VBT, what is the hybridization and magnetic behavior of $[Ni(CN)_4]^{2-}$?','Hybridization: **$dsp^2$** (Square planar geometry)\nMagnetic behavior: **Diamagnetic** (all electrons are paired).\n(Note: $Ni^{2+}$ is $3d^8$. $CN^-$ is a strong field ligand, forcing pairing of electrons in 3d, freeing up one d-orbital).',NULL,NULL,'Strong ligand + 4 coordination = usually square planar for d8.','["chemistry", "coordination-compounds", "vbt", "magnetism"]','medium','Neet Buddy',13,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('4cfbf13a-47b8-49c4-b83f-3f4b2dccaf00','8a4254b1-1ea2-4950-982d-aa0ef43e9cc3','In Crystal Field Theory (CFT) for an Octahedral complex, how do the d-orbitals split?','They split into two sets of different energies:\n1. **$t_{2g}$ set:** $d_{xy}, d_{yz}, d_{zx}$ (Lower energy, degenerate set of 3).\n2. **$e_g$ set:** $d_{x^2-y^2}, d_{z^2}$ (Higher energy, degenerate set of 2).',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\' viewBox=\'0 0 300 150\'><line x1=\'50\' y1=\'80\' x2=\'90\' y2=\'80\' stroke=\'black\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><text x=\'15\' y=\'85\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'10\'>5 degenerate</text><text x=\'15\' y=\'95\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'10\'>d-orbitals</text><path d=\'M 100 80 L 150 40\' stroke=\'red\' stroke-dasharray=\'4\'/><path d=\'M 100 80 L 150 110\' stroke=\'blue\' stroke-dasharray=\'4\'/><line x1=\'160\' y1=\'40\' x2=\'200\' y2=\'40\' stroke=\'red\' stroke-width=\'3\'/><line x1=\'210\' y1=\'40\' x2=\'250\' y2=\'40\' stroke=\'red\' stroke-width=\'3\'/><text x=\'260\' y=\'45\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'12\' font-weight=\'bold\' fill=\'red\'>eg</text><line x1=\'160\' y1=\'110\' x2=\'185\' y2=\'110\' stroke=\'blue\' stroke-width=\'3\'/><line x1=\'195\' y1=\'110\' x2=\'220\' y2=\'110\' stroke=\'blue\' stroke-width=\'3\'/><line x1=\'230\' y1=\'110\' x2=\'255\' y2=\'110\' stroke=\'blue\' stroke-width=\'3\'/><text x=\'265\' y=\'115\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'12\' font-weight=\'bold\' fill=\'blue\'>t2g</text><path d=\'M 130 40 L 130 110\' stroke=\'green\' marker-start=\'url(#arrow)\' marker-end=\'url(#arrow)\'/><text x=\'135\' y=\'80\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'12\' fill=\'green\'>Δo</text><defs><marker id=\'arrow\' viewBox=\'0 0 10 10\' refX=\'5\' refY=\'5\' markerWidth=\'6\' markerHeight=\'6\' orient=\'auto-start-reverse\'><path d=\'M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\' fill=\'green\'/></marker></defs></svg>"}]}','Axial orbitals face more repulsion from ligands approaching along the axes.','["chemistry", "coordination-compounds", "cft"]','medium','Neet Buddy',14,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('8da47b39-9fa6-421b-a26c-cd2fe93aecbc','8a4254b1-1ea2-4950-982d-aa0ef43e9cc3','What is the relationship between Crystal Field Splitting Energy ($\\Delta_o$) and Pairing Energy ($P$) for a High Spin complex vs a Low Spin complex?','**High Spin Complex (Weak field ligand):** $\\Delta_o < P$ (Electrons prefer to jump to $e_g$ rather than pair up in $t_{2g}$).\n**Low Spin Complex (Strong field ligand):** $\\Delta_o > P$ (Electrons prefer to pair up in $t_{2g}$ before filling $e_g$).',NULL,NULL,'If the gap ($\\Delta_o$) is huge, electrons are too lazy to jump, so they pair up (Low Spin).','["chemistry", "coordination-compounds", "cft", "spin"]','medium','Neet Buddy',15,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('941a43a5-7a6d-425c-b948-2cd76c3e663a','8a4254b1-1ea2-4950-982d-aa0ef43e9cc3','How do you calculate the Crystal Field Stabilization Energy (CFSE) for a $d^4$ high-spin octahedral complex?','Configuration for high-spin $d^4$ is $t_{2g}^3 e_g^1$.\nCFSE $= [-0.4(n_{t2g}) + 0.6(n_{eg})] \\Delta_o$\nCFSE $= [-0.4(3) + 0.6(1)] \\Delta_o = -1.2 + 0.6 = **-0.6 \\Delta_o**$',NULL,NULL,'Each t2g electron lowers energy by 0.4. Each eg electron raises energy by 0.6.','["chemistry", "coordination-compounds", "cft", "calculations"]','medium','Neet Buddy',16,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('75263c9f-5288-4b44-b881-2c947be7bf36','8a4254b1-1ea2-4950-982d-aa0ef43e9cc3','Why is anhydrous $CuSO_4$ white, while hydrated $CuSO_4 \\cdot 5H_2O$ is blue?','In anhydrous $CuSO_4$, there are no water ligands, so there is no crystal field splitting, hence no **d-d transitions** possible. \nIn the hydrated form, water acts as a ligand causing d-orbital splitting, allowing for d-d transitions that absorb red light and transmit blue.',NULL,NULL,'No ligand = No splitting = No color.','["chemistry", "coordination-compounds", "color", "cft"]','medium','Neet Buddy',17,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('c12d7893-a312-4750-8e9e-a69d26616542','8a4254b1-1ea2-4950-982d-aa0ef43e9cc3','What is Synergic Bonding (Pi-backbonding) in metal carbonyls?','It involves a dual bond between metal and CO:\\n\\n1. \\(\\sigma\\)-bond: Donation of a lone pair from carbon to an empty d-orbital of the metal.\\n\\n2. \\(\\pi\\)-bond: Donation of electron density from a filled metal d-orbital into the empty antibonding \\(\\pi^*\\) orbital of carbon monoxide.\\n\\nThis strengthens the M-C bond and weakens the C-O bond.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "This synergy makes metal carbonyls highly stable."}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\' viewBox=\'0 0 350 120\'><circle cx=\'80\' cy=\'60\' r=\'30\' fill=\'lightgray\' stroke=\'black\'/><text x=\'70\' y=\'65\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'16\'>M</text><circle cx=\'220\' cy=\'60\' r=\'20\' fill=\'pink\' stroke=\'black\'/><text x=\'213\' y=\'65\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'14\'>C</text><circle cx=\'280\' cy=\'60\' r=\'20\' fill=\'lightblue\' stroke=\'black\'/><text x=\'273\' y=\'65\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'14\'>O</text><path d=\'M 195 55 L 115 55\' stroke=\'black\' stroke-width=\'2\' marker-end=\'url(#arrow)\'/><text x=\'130\' y=\'45\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'10\'>σ donation (C to M)</text><path d=\'M 115 75 Q 155 100 195 75\' stroke=\'blue\' stroke-dasharray=\'4\' stroke-width=\'2\' fill=\'none\' marker-end=\'url(#arrowB)\'/><text x=\'110\' y=\'105\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'10\' fill=\'blue\'>π back-donation (M to π* of CO)</text><defs><marker id=\'arrow\' viewBox=\'0 0 10 10\' refX=\'5\' refY=\'5\' markerWidth=\'6\' markerHeight=\'6\' orient=\'auto-start-reverse\'><path d=\'M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\' fill=\'black\'/></marker><marker id=\'arrowB\' viewBox=\'0 0 10 10\' refX=\'5\' refY=\'5\' markerWidth=\'6\' markerHeight=\'6\' orient=\'auto-start-reverse\'><path d=\'M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\' fill=\'blue\'/></marker></defs></svg>"}]}','Give and take relationship creates synergy.','["chemistry", "coordination-compounds", "metal-carbonyls", "bonding"]','medium','Neet Buddy',18,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('3b08dafc-fde5-4681-975f-51405b456388','8a4254b1-1ea2-4950-982d-aa0ef43e9cc3','Identify the central metal ion in the following naturally occurring coordination compounds: \n1. Chlorophyll\n2. Hemoglobin\n3. Vitamin $B_{12}$','1. Chlorophyll: **Magnesium ($Mg^{2+}$)**\n2. Hemoglobin: **Iron ($Fe^{2+}$)**\n3. Vitamin $B_{12}$ (Cyanocobalamin): **Cobalt ($Co^{3+}$)**',NULL,NULL,'Mg for green, Fe for red, Co for B12.','["chemistry", "coordination-compounds", "applications", "biology"]','medium','Neet Buddy',19,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('6d7b1c6f-d661-4488-9985-fa2e7a9522cc','8a4254b1-1ea2-4950-982d-aa0ef43e9cc3','How is coordination chemistry used in the extraction of Silver (Ag) and Gold (Au) via the Macarthur-Forrest Cyanide Process?','The finely divided ore is treated with dilute $KCN$ / $NaCN$ in the presence of air ($O_2$) to form a water-soluble coordination complex. \n$4Au + 8CN^- + O_2 + 2H_2O \\rightarrow 4[Au(CN)_2]^- + 4OH^-$\nThe metal is then recovered by displacement with Zinc.',NULL,NULL,'Gold dissolves as a dicyanoaurate complex.','["chemistry", "coordination-compounds", "applications", "metallurgy"]','medium','Neet Buddy',20,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('dce297a7-28b0-4128-a0fb-22d9aa1cfcbd','8a4254b1-1ea2-4950-982d-aa0ef43e9cc3','Name the specific coordination compound used to treat lead poisoning.','**Calcium EDTA complex** ($CaEDTA$ or $CaNa_2EDTA$).\nLead ions in the body replace the calcium in the complex (forming a more stable Pb-EDTA complex), which is then excreted in urine.',NULL,NULL,'EDTA forms highly stable chelates with heavy metals.','["chemistry", "coordination-compounds", "applications", "medicine"]','medium','Neet Buddy',21,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('5f095628-bf9a-4eac-8afc-1f3a86881e8c','8a4254b1-1ea2-4950-982d-aa0ef43e9cc3','What is Wilkinson’s Catalyst, and what is its application?','Formula: **$[RhCl(PPh_3)_3]$** (Chloridotris(triphenylphosphine)rhodium(I)).\nApplication: It is used as a homogeneous catalyst for the **hydrogenation of alkenes**.',NULL,NULL,'Contains Rhodium and triphenylphosphine.','["chemistry", "coordination-compounds", "applications", "catalysis"]','medium','Neet Buddy',22,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('b94fa850-59ac-4223-a764-3cfa0f73d14a','8a4254b1-1ea2-4950-982d-aa0ef43e9cc3','State the formula for Effective Atomic Number (EAN) and calculate it for $[Fe(CN)_6]^{4-}$.','EAN = $Z - (	ext{Oxidation State}) + 2 	imes (	ext{Coordination Number})$\nFor $[Fe(CN)_6]^{4-}$:\nFe Atomic number (Z) = 26.\nOxidation state = +2.\nCoordination Number = 6.\nEAN = $26 - 2 + (2 	imes 6) = 24 + 12 = **36**$ (Noble gas configuration of Krypton).',NULL,NULL,'Atomic number minus lost electrons plus gained electrons from ligands.','["chemistry", "coordination-compounds", "ean", "calculations"]','medium','Neet Buddy',23,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('45887d32-6dc9-40d5-828f-c75785036f27','8e87e7dc-9dd1-4661-974c-132e2ab3b91c','State Faraday\'s Law of Electromagnetic Induction.','The magnitude of the induced EMF in a circuit is directly proportional to the rate of change of magnetic flux linked with the circuit. \ne = - dΦ/dt',NULL,NULL,'The negative sign is explained by Lenz\'s Law.','["physics", "electromagnetic-induction", "faradays-law", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',0,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('ebcad7a6-0853-48c0-b2d3-5f8cd0cc67c8','8e87e7dc-9dd1-4661-974c-132e2ab3b91c','State Lenz\'s Law and its underlying conservation principle.','The direction of the induced EMF (or current) is always such that it opposes the change in magnetic flux that produced it. It is based on the Conservation of Energy.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "Opposes the cause: Approaching N-pole induces anti-clockwise (N) current to repel."}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg viewBox=\'0 0 200 120\' width=\'100%\'><rect x=\'20\' y=\'40\' width=\'50\' height=\'30\' fill=\'gray\'/><text x=\'30\' y=\'60\' fill=\'white\'>S</text><text x=\'55\' y=\'60\' fill=\'white\'>N</text><line x1=\'75\' y1=\'55\' x2=\'105\' y2=\'55\' stroke=\'red\' stroke-width=\'3\' marker-end=\'url(#arrow)\'/><text x=\'80\' y=\'45\' fill=\'red\' font-size=\'12\'>v</text><ellipse cx=\'150\' cy=\'55\' rx=\'15\' ry=\'40\' fill=\'none\' stroke=\'blue\' stroke-width=\'3\'/><path d=\'M 140 30 Q 150 15 160 30\' fill=\'none\' stroke=\'blue\' stroke-width=\'2\' marker-end=\'url(#arrow)\'/><text x=\'135\' y=\'55\' font-size=\'14\' fill=\'blue\'>N</text><text x=\'120\' y=\'100\' font-size=\'10\'>Anti-clockwise I</text></svg>"}]}','If a North pole approaches a coil, the coil face becomes a North pole to repel it.','["physics", "electromagnetic-induction", "lenzs-law"]','medium','Neet Buddy',1,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('334cc75a-8cfe-46f4-84dd-4ab0b371fa16','8e87e7dc-9dd1-4661-974c-132e2ab3b91c','What is the formula for Motional EMF induced in a straight conductor moving in a uniform magnetic field?','e = B v l \n(Provided B, v, and l are all mutually perpendicular). General form: e = Bvl sin(θ).',NULL,NULL,'The moving conductor acts like a battery. Force required to keep it moving: F = B²l²v / R.','["physics", "electromagnetic-induction", "motional-emf", "neet-high-yield"]','medium','Neet Buddy',2,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('dc9554a6-28ab-460a-bd47-b6216def99ed','8e87e7dc-9dd1-4661-974c-132e2ab3b91c','What are Eddy Currents and how can their energy loss be minimized?','Eddy currents are circulating currents induced in bulk pieces of metal when subjected to a changing magnetic flux. \nMinimized by using laminated metallic cores (thin sheets separated by insulation).',NULL,NULL,'Applications include magnetic braking in trains, induction furnaces, and speedometers.','["physics", "electromagnetic-induction", "eddy-currents"]','medium','Neet Buddy',3,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('27841d5d-ec9e-43cb-8d38-916078735544','8e87e7dc-9dd1-4661-974c-132e2ab3b91c','Write the formula for the Self-Inductance (L) of a long solenoid.','L = μ₀ n² A l = μ₀ N² A / l\nWhere \'n\' is turns per unit length (N/l), \'A\' is cross-sectional area, and \'l\' is length.',NULL,NULL,'Depends purely on the geometry of the coil and the core medium (L = μ₀μ_r n² A l).','["physics", "electromagnetic-induction", "inductance"]','medium','Neet Buddy',4,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('f9dd58cd-94e2-4d09-a0dd-7ef228a08356','8e87e7dc-9dd1-4661-974c-132e2ab3b91c','What is the formula for the Potential Energy (U) stored in an inductor?','U = ½ L I² \nWhere L is inductance and I is the current.',NULL,NULL,'Energy is stored in the form of a Magnetic Field (analogous to ½ C V² in capacitors).','["physics", "electromagnetic-induction", "energy"]','medium','Neet Buddy',5,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('b31aa8ab-8ed5-4ed7-94ad-d96a52aba1ed','8e87e7dc-9dd1-4661-974c-132e2ab3b91c','What is the relation between the Mutual Inductance (M) of two coils and their individual Self-Inductances (L₁, L₂)?','M = k √(L₁ L₂)\nWhere \'k\' is the coefficient of coupling (0 ≤ k ≤ 1).',NULL,NULL,'k=1 for perfectly coupled coils (wound over each other). k=0 for perpendicular coils.','["physics", "electromagnetic-induction", "inductance"]','medium','Neet Buddy',6,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('7a0437cb-c8d1-41eb-a57f-bfe0a1783b91','8e87e7dc-9dd1-4661-974c-132e2ab3b91c','Describe the phase relationship between Voltage and Current in purely Inductive and purely Capacitive circuits.','Pure Inductor (L): Voltage LEADS Current by π/2 (90°).\nPure Capacitor (C): Current LEADS Voltage by π/2 (90°).',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "ELI the ICE man. Phasor diagrams:"}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg viewBox=\'0 0 250 100\' width=\'100%\'><g transform=\'translate(40, 50)\'><line x1=\'0\' y1=\'0\' x2=\'40\' y2=\'0\' stroke=\'blue\' stroke-width=\'2\' marker-end=\'url(#arrow)\'/><text x=\'45\' y=\'5\' fill=\'blue\'>I</text><line x1=\'0\' y1=\'0\' x2=\'0\' y2=\'-40\' stroke=\'red\' stroke-width=\'2\' marker-end=\'url(#arrow)\'/><text x=\'-15\' y=\'-35\' fill=\'red\'>VL</text><text x=\'-20\' y=\'30\' font-size=\'12\'>Pure Inductor</text></g><g transform=\'translate(170, 50)\'><line x1=\'0\' y1=\'0\' x2=\'40\' y2=\'0\' stroke=\'blue\' stroke-width=\'2\' marker-end=\'url(#arrow)\'/><text x=\'45\' y=\'5\' fill=\'blue\'>I</text><line x1=\'0\' y1=\'0\' x2=\'0\' y2=\'40\' stroke=\'red\' stroke-width=\'2\' marker-end=\'url(#arrow)\'/><text x=\'-20\' y=\'45\' fill=\'red\'>VC</text><text x=\'-20\' y=\'-20\' font-size=\'12\'>Pure Capacitor</text></g></svg>"}]}','Mnemonic: \'ELI the ICE man\'. (E leads I in L; I leads E in C).','["physics", "alternating-current", "phase"]','medium','Neet Buddy',7,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('1a2e12f9-8fdc-45f7-a15f-355aa325da20','8e87e7dc-9dd1-4661-974c-132e2ab3b91c','What is the relationship between Peak (I₀) and RMS (Iᵣₘₛ) values of Alternating Current?','Iᵣₘₛ = I₀ / √2 ≈ 0.707 I₀\nVᵣₘₛ = V₀ / √2 ≈ 0.707 V₀',NULL,NULL,'RMS value is the \'effective\' value used for calculating average power and heat dissipation. Standard household 220V is an RMS value.','["physics", "alternating-current", "rms-value", "neet-high-yield"]','medium','Neet Buddy',8,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('63524adc-88d7-4f04-a11a-6fada6c51537','8e87e7dc-9dd1-4661-974c-132e2ab3b91c','What is the condition for Resonance in a series LCR circuit?','Resonance occurs when Inductive Reactance equals Capacitive Reactance: $X_L$ = $X_C$.\nUnder this condition, Impedance is minimum (Z = R) and Current is maximum.',NULL,NULL,'The circuit behaves as a purely resistive circuit (voltage and current are in phase).','["physics", "alternating-current", "resonance"]','medium','Neet Buddy',9,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('504a3e3e-54b1-4067-888a-faaa6d07f9a1','8e87e7dc-9dd1-4661-974c-132e2ab3b91c','What is the Mean (Average) value of an Alternating Current over a full cycle and over a half cycle?','Full cycle: $I_avg$ = 0 \nHalf cycle: $I_avg$ = 2I₀ / π ≈ 0.637 I₀',NULL,NULL,'Because the positive half completely cancels the negative half over a full cycle.','["physics", "alternating-current", "average-value"]','medium','Neet Buddy',10,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('bbb6ffc6-eeda-4a0e-b9cd-78177df35ccb','8e87e7dc-9dd1-4661-974c-132e2ab3b91c','Write the formulas for Inductive Reactance ($X_L$) and Capacitive Reactance ($X_C$).','Inductive Reactance: $X_L$ = ωL = 2πfL (Increases with frequency).\nCapacitive Reactance: $X_C$ = 1 / (ωC) = 1 / (2πfC) (Decreases with frequency).',NULL,NULL,'For DC (f = 0), an inductor acts as a short circuit (X_L=0) and a capacitor acts as an open circuit (X_C=∞).','["physics", "alternating-current", "reactance"]','medium','Neet Buddy',11,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('da964f85-6d0f-497d-84cf-b856ebda5367','8e87e7dc-9dd1-4661-974c-132e2ab3b91c','Define Power Factor (cos φ) and state its value for purely resistive and purely reactive (L or C) circuits.','Power factor is the ratio of true power to apparent power. cos(φ) = R / Z.\nPurely Resistive: cos(φ) = 1 (φ = 0°, Maximum power).\nPure L or Pure C: cos(φ) = 0 (φ = 90°, Zero power).',NULL,NULL,'In an LCR circuit at resonance, Z=R, so the power factor is exactly 1.','["physics", "alternating-current", "power-factor", "neet-high-yield"]','medium','Neet Buddy',12,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('99fd4490-2d63-4c87-bbac-14a632503383','8e87e7dc-9dd1-4661-974c-132e2ab3b91c','Name the four main causes of energy loss in a Transformer.','1. Copper loss (Joule heating in wires: I²R).\n2. Eddy current loss (heating in iron core, reduced by laminations).\n3. Hysteresis loss (energy spent reversing magnetization, reduced by using soft iron).\n4. Flux leakage (not all primary flux links to secondary).',NULL,NULL,'A transformer only works on AC, never DC.','["physics", "alternating-current", "transformer", "losses"]','medium','Neet Buddy',13,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('6d69bcbb-1f29-4225-83e0-4142f99bba7c','8e87e7dc-9dd1-4661-974c-132e2ab3b91c','What is the formula for the instantaneous EMF (e) induced in an AC Generator?','e = N B A ω sin(ωt) = e₀ sin(ωt)\nWhere Peak EMF e₀ = N B A ω.',NULL,NULL,'Operates on the principle of Electromagnetic Induction. A coil rotating in a uniform magnetic field produces a sinusoidal voltage.','["physics", "alternating-current", "generator"]','medium','Neet Buddy',14,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('4afe7b92-5de4-4780-907b-911d6164abf3','8e87e7dc-9dd1-4661-974c-132e2ab3b91c','If an isolated charged parallel plate capacitor (battery disconnected) has a dielectric slab (constant K) inserted, what happens to its Capacitance, Voltage, and Energy?','Capacitance (C) INCREASES to K*C₀.\nVoltage (V) DECREASES to V₀/K (since Q is constant and V = Q/C).\nEnergy (U) DECREASES to U₀/K (since U = Q²/2C).',NULL,NULL,'If the battery remained CONNECTED, Voltage would stay constant, and Energy would INCREASE (U = ½ K C₀ V²).','["physics", "capacitors", "dielectrics", "shortcuts"]','medium','Neet Buddy',15,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('6cff0ea8-1f7d-4419-b2b6-711b59094b2e','8e87e7dc-9dd1-4661-974c-132e2ab3b91c','Write the Transformer Ratio formula relating Voltages (V), Currents (I), and Number of turns (N).','($V_s / V_p$) = ($N_s / N_p$) = ($I_p / I_s$)\nWhere \'s\' stands for secondary coil and \'p\' for primary coil.',NULL,NULL,'A Step-Up transformer increases voltage (N_s > N_p) but proportionately DECREASES current, keeping power constant (ideal case).','["physics", "alternating-current", "transformer", "neet-high-yield"]','medium','Neet Buddy',16,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('6088b82b-f44f-4ff1-893a-aacb50bd1e3c','8e87e7dc-9dd1-4661-974c-132e2ab3b91c','What is the formula for Average Power (P) dissipated in an AC circuit?','P = Vᵣₘₛ * Iᵣₘₛ * cos(φ)\nWhere cos(φ) is the Power Factor of the circuit.',NULL,NULL,'True power is only consumed by the Resistor (P = I_rms² R). Inductors and capacitors consume zero average power.','["physics", "alternating-current", "power"]','medium','Neet Buddy',17,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('237b83de-21c8-4cde-9332-36cb251eabba','8e87e7dc-9dd1-4661-974c-132e2ab3b91c','What is Wattless Current?','It is the component of alternating current (Iᵣₘₛ * sin φ) that leads or lags the voltage by exactly 90°, resulting in ZERO average power dissipation.',NULL,NULL,'Flows in pure inductors or pure capacitors. The power-consuming component is the \'Wattful\' current (I_rms * cos φ).','["physics", "alternating-current", "power"]','medium','Neet Buddy',18,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('4b4c4663-af29-42a9-a23a-4a681db17591','8e87e7dc-9dd1-4661-974c-132e2ab3b91c','What is the formula for the Impedance (Z) of a series LCR circuit?','Z = √[ R² + ($X_L$ - $X_C$)² ]\nPhase angle φ is given by tan(φ) = ($X_L$ - $X_C$) / R',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "Z = √[ R² + ($X_L$ - $X_C$)² ]\\nPhase angle φ is given by tan(φ) = ($X_L$ - $X_C$) / R"}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\" xmlns:xlink=\\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\\" width=\\"496\\" height=\\"257\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 496 257\\">\\n  <image xlink:href=\\"data:image/png;base64,\\" x=\\"0\\" y=\\"0\\" width=\\"496\\" height=\\"257\\"/>\\n</svg>"}]}','Impedance is the net opposition to AC current flow.','["physics", "alternating-current", "impedance", "neet-high-yield"]','medium','Neet Buddy',19,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('513ba67b-4142-4801-8634-fe1a4dea295a','8e87e7dc-9dd1-4661-974c-132e2ab3b91c','Define the Q-factor (Quality Factor) of a resonant LCR circuit and provide its formula.','Q-factor measures the sharpness of resonance. \nQ = (1 / R) * √(L / C) = $ω_r$ L / R',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "Q-factor measures the sharpness of resonance. \\nQ = (1 / R) * √(L / C) = $ω_r$ L / R"}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\" xmlns:xlink=\\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\\" width=\\"1165\\" height=\\"692\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 1165 692\\">\\n  <image xlink:href=\\"data:image/png;base64,\\" x=\\"0\\" y=\\"0\\" width=\\"1165\\" height=\\"692\\"/>\\n</svg>"}]}','A higher Q-factor means a sharper, narrower resonance peak (more selective tuning circuit).','["physics", "alternating-current", "resonance"]','medium','Neet Buddy',20,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('ae967dbe-ff6b-4723-a887-1ebcf19c97b0','8e87e7dc-9dd1-4661-974c-132e2ab3b91c','Write the formula for the Resonant Frequency (fᵣ) of a series LCR circuit.','$f_r$ = 1 / [ 2π √(LC) ] \nAngular resonant frequency: $ω_r$ = 1 / √(LC)',NULL,NULL,'Derived by setting X_L = X_C  =>  ωL = 1/(ωC).','["physics", "alternating-current", "resonance", "formulas"]','medium','Neet Buddy',21,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('6a55200e-08b8-427f-ab0d-a194b81a3179','97e0d2c4-c9a3-4e41-bd5d-d63301f2d891','What is the result of \'Double Fertilization\' in angiosperms?','It results in the formation of a diploid zygote (syngamy) and a triploid primary endosperm nucleus (triple fusion).',NULL,NULL,'Syngamy + Triple Fusion = Double Fertilization.','["biology", "reproduction-in-plants", "class-12", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',0,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('1d568677-6bb2-458c-9eed-db9b4f4ba1c7','97e0d2c4-c9a3-4e41-bd5d-d63301f2d891','What are \'Outbreeding devices\' and why do plants evolve them?','Mechanisms like dioecy, self-incompatibility, and heterostyly that discourage self-pollination and promote cross-pollination to increase genetic diversity.',NULL,NULL,'Plants want to avoid inbreeding depression.','["biology", "reproduction-in-plants", "class-12", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',1,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('5f390d7c-9e28-44e8-ad9c-ba34d54ea3c2','97e0d2c4-c9a3-4e41-bd5d-d63301f2d891','Differentiate between Apomixis and Parthenocarpy.','Apomixis: Seed production without fertilization (asexual reproduction mimicking sexual). Parthenocarpy: Fruit formation without fertilization (fruit is seedless).',NULL,NULL,'Apomixis = seed but no sex; Parthenocarpy = fruit but no seed.','["biology", "reproduction-in-plants", "class-12", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',2,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('e4bf6d5f-4730-4592-85a8-77eb120286aa','97e0d2c4-c9a3-4e41-bd5d-d63301f2d891','What is the function of the Sertoli cells found in the seminiferous tubules?','They provide nutrition to the developing germ cells (spermatids) and secrete androgen-binding protein (ABP) and Inhibin.',NULL,NULL,'Think of them as \'nurse cells\' for sperm.','["biology", "human-reproduction", "class-12", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',3,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('8817a187-11af-41d0-a80e-7442ad316e99','97e0d2c4-c9a3-4e41-bd5d-d63301f2d891','What triggers the completion of Meiosis II in the secondary oocyte?','The entry of the sperm into the cytoplasm of the secondary oocyte during fertilization.',NULL,NULL,'Without fertilization, it remains arrested in Metaphase II.','["biology", "human-reproduction", "oogenesis", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',4,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('d0340f1d-99aa-4f52-8cbb-713f86ac8f17','97e0d2c4-c9a3-4e41-bd5d-d63301f2d891','What is the structural composition and primary function of the placenta?','The placenta is a structural and functional unit between the fetus and maternal body. It facilitates the supply of $O_2$ and nutrients to the fetus and the removal of $CO_2$ and excretory wastes.',NULL,NULL,'It also acts as an endocrine tissue secreting hCG, hPL, estrogen, and progesterone.','["biology", "human-reproduction", "pregnancy", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',5,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('eb0ec766-f3cf-431e-a499-d53f64899e9a','97e0d2c4-c9a3-4e41-bd5d-d63301f2d891','What are the common side effects of hormonal contraceptive pills and their mode of action?','They contain synthetic progesterone or estrogen-progesterone combinations. They inhibit ovulation and implantation, and alter the quality of cervical mucus to prevent sperm entry.',NULL,NULL,'Side effects include nausea, breast tenderness, and mood changes.','["biology", "reproductive-health", "contraception", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',6,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('8ca6265c-1faf-4518-95ce-66c8cb313008','97e0d2c4-c9a3-4e41-bd5d-d63301f2d891','What is the difference between ZIFT and GIFT assisted reproductive technologies?','ZIFT (Zygote Intra-Fallopian Transfer): Zygote (formed via IVF) is transferred into the Fallopian tube.\nGIFT (Gamete Intra-Fallopian Transfer): Ovum collected from a donor is transferred into the Fallopian tube of another female who cannot produce eggs.',NULL,NULL,'ZIFT = Zygote; GIFT = Gamete.','["biology", "reproductive-health", "art", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',7,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('74c5eeb7-2e79-417e-a85e-379498a3fa71','97e0d2c4-c9a3-4e41-bd5d-d63301f2d891','What is the biological significance of the \'acrosome reaction\' during fertilization?','The acrosome releases digestive enzymes (like hyaluronidase) that help the sperm penetrate the layers of the ovum (corona radiata and zona pellucida).',NULL,NULL,'The acrosome is a cap-like structure on the sperm head.','["biology", "human-reproduction", "fertilization", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',8,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('4ba21ca8-db5a-41e6-ad12-d16cfd6498b7','97e0d2c4-c9a3-4e41-bd5d-d63301f2d891','Explain the role of the corpus luteum in the maintenance of pregnancy.','It secretes large amounts of progesterone, which is essential for the maintenance of the endometrium for embryo implantation.',NULL,NULL,'If fertilization does not occur, the corpus luteum degenerates.','["biology", "human-reproduction", "class-12", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',9,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('1f544081-b15e-498f-b118-7a852545666a','97e0d2c4-c9a3-4e41-bd5d-d63301f2d891','What is the definition of MTP (Medical Termination of Pregnancy) and when is it legally considered safe?','Intentional or voluntary termination of pregnancy before full term. It is relatively safe during the first trimester (up to 12 weeks).',NULL,NULL,'It is mainly used to reduce population and handle unwanted pregnancies.','["biology", "reproductive-health", "mtp", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',10,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('c0eebefd-2560-4572-b02a-b1f989f6e68b','97e0d2c4-c9a3-4e41-bd5d-d63301f2d891','How does Amniocentesis work, and why is it legally restricted?','It is a prenatal diagnostic technique analyzing amniotic fluid for chromosomal abnormalities. It is restricted because it is often misused for female foeticide based on sex determination.',NULL,NULL,'Amniocentesis is a sex-determination test, which is legally banned in India.','["biology", "reproductive-health", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',11,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('a9d616d5-1d7c-4c9c-9df6-dade406e7c02','97e0d2c4-c9a3-4e41-bd5d-d63301f2d891','Differentiate between Embryo transfer at the 8-blastomere stage and the >8-blastomere stage.','Transfer at the 8-blastomere stage is called ZIFT (Zygote Intra-Fallopian Transfer).\nTransfer at the >8-blastomere stage is called IUT (Intra-Uterine Transfer), placing it directly into the uterus.',NULL,NULL,'ZIFT = tube; IUT = uterus.','["biology", "reproductive-health", "art", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',12,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('b767b9cd-34a4-4e7b-8bba-52d20f6a2d9e','97e0d2c4-c9a3-4e41-bd5d-d63301f2d891','What are the three layers of the uterus from outermost to innermost?','Perimetrium (external), Myometrium (middle, thick smooth muscle), and Endometrium (internal, glandular).',NULL,NULL,'The endometrium undergoes cyclic changes during the menstrual cycle.','["biology", "human-reproduction", "anatomy", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',13,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('e82edbbe-bf99-451c-b5eb-f061609a6457','97e0d2c4-c9a3-4e41-bd5d-d63301f2d891','What is the significance of the \'colostrum\' produced during initial lactation?','It is the yellowish fluid produced during initial days of lactation, rich in antibodies (specifically IgA) that provide essential immunity to the newborn.',NULL,NULL,'Breastfeeding is recommended for healthy infant development.','["biology", "human-reproduction", "lactation", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',14,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('40d134b2-d8b7-4d1d-be65-b413692251a7','97e0d2c4-c9a3-4e41-bd5d-d63301f2d891','Define Polyspermy and explain how the ovum prevents it.','Polyspermy is the fertilization of an egg by more than one sperm. The ovum prevents it by undergoing a change in membrane permeability (zona reaction) to block other sperm entry upon the first sperm\'s entry.',NULL,NULL,'Only one sperm can fertilize the ovum.','["biology", "human-reproduction", "fertilization", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',15,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('2ded8ec0-8071-49b0-aa6e-b90ca830dd17','97e0d2c4-c9a3-4e41-bd5d-d63301f2d891','Which hormones are responsible for the contraction of the uterus during Parturition?','Oxytocin (the \'birth hormone\') acts on the uterine muscle to cause stronger contractions, while Relaxin (produced by the ovary/placenta) relaxes the pelvic ligaments.',NULL,NULL,'Parturition is a complex neuro-endocrine mechanism.','["biology", "human-reproduction", "parturition", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',16,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('0b50832c-267d-4e91-a939-0cda37ef60d1','97e0d2c4-c9a3-4e41-bd5d-d63301f2d891','List three common STDs and their respective causative pathogens.','1. Syphilis (Treponema pallidum)\n2. Gonorrhea (Neisseria gonorrhoeae)\n3. Genital Herpes (Herpes simplex virus).',NULL,NULL,'Prevention: Use of condoms, avoiding contact with infected partners.','["biology", "reproductive-health", "stds", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',17,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('0dbac09a-24b1-46f5-92e5-3c1fc260b9b7','97e0d2c4-c9a3-4e41-bd5d-d63301f2d891','What is the main advantage of IUDs (Intra Uterine Devices) as a contraceptive method?','They are highly effective, long-acting (years), and require no daily intervention. Copper-releasing IUDs (like CuT) increase phagocytosis of sperm and suppress sperm motility.',NULL,NULL,'IUDs are one of the most widely accepted methods of contraception in India.','["biology", "reproductive-health", "contraception", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',18,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('eeaa5b7d-3f2c-45b0-887e-9c6e2e89d43f','97e0d2c4-c9a3-4e41-bd5d-d63301f2d891','Describe the hormonal shifts that trigger ovulation at day 14 of the menstrual cycle.','A massive surge in Luteinizing Hormone (LH surge) triggers the rupture of the Graafian follicle and release of the ovum.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "A massive surge in Luteinizing Hormone (LH surge) triggers the rupture of the Graafian follicle and release of the ovum."}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\" xmlns:xlink=\\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\\" width=\\"300\\" height=\\"255\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 527 255\\">\\n  <image xlink:href=\\"data:image/png;base64,\\" x=\\"0\\" y=\\"0\\" width=\\"527\\" height=\\"255\\"/>\\n</svg>"}]}','FSH also peaks, but the LH surge is the specific trigger for ovulation.','["biology", "human-reproduction", "menstrual-cycle", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',19,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('4bcc29e5-c28f-4ada-858f-66d8e7cc0e71','9a2b8e85-6073-4662-8871-fa06c27fc6af','State Faraday\'s First Law of Electrolysis.','The mass of a substance deposited or liberated at an electrode is directly proportional to the quantity of electricity (charge) passed through the electrolyte.\n$m = Z \\cdot I \\cdot t$',NULL,NULL,'$Z$ is electrochemical equivalent. Charge $Q = I \\times t$.','["chemistry", "electrochemistry", "electrolysis"]','medium','Neet Buddy',0,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('438f00a4-8d63-42f2-8a90-7f644bcf01f9','9a2b8e85-6073-4662-8871-fa06c27fc6af','What are the rules for the oxidation state of Oxygen in regular oxides, peroxides, and superoxides?','Regular Oxides: -2\nPeroxides (e.g., $H_2O_2$): -1\nSuperoxides (e.g., $KO_2$): -1/2',NULL,NULL,'Oxygen\'s oxidation state increases (becomes less negative) as the oxygen-to-metal ratio increases.','["chemistry", "redox", "oxidation-state"]','medium','Neet Buddy',1,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('311a7210-612b-4e0b-a885-c6436c9c9384','9a2b8e85-6073-4662-8871-fa06c27fc6af','What is the relationship between the Electrochemical Equivalent ($Z$) and Equivalent Weight ($E$)?','$Z = \\frac{E}{96500}$',NULL,NULL,'1 Faraday ($96500 \\text{ C}$) deposits 1 Gram Equivalent of a substance.','["chemistry", "electrochemistry", "electrolysis"]','medium','Neet Buddy',2,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('fecdde6e-b4ac-400c-a41f-2cc43df8e973','9a2b8e85-6073-4662-8871-fa06c27fc6af','Calculate the oxidation number of Chromium (Cr) in $CrO_5$.','The oxidation number of Cr is +6.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "Calculated by formula it gives +10 (incorrect). The structure has 4 oxygen atoms in peroxide bonds (-1) and 1 in a double bond (-2).\\n$x + 4(-1) + 1(-2) = 0 \\\\implies x = +6$."}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\' viewBox=\'0 0 200 150\'><text x=\'90\' y=\'80\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'16\' font-weight=\'bold\'>Cr</text><text x=\'90\' y=\'30\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'16\'>O</text><text x=\'40\' y=\'60\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'16\'>O</text><text x=\'140\' y=\'60\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'16\'>O</text><text x=\'40\' y=\'110\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'16\'>O</text><text x=\'140\' y=\'110\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'16\'>O</text><line x1=\'95\' y1=\'65\' x2=\'95\' y2=\'35\' stroke=\'black\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><line x1=\'105\' y1=\'65\' x2=\'105\' y2=\'35\' stroke=\'black\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><line x1=\'85\' y1=\'75\' x2=\'55\' y2=\'65\' stroke=\'black\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><line x1=\'85\' y1=\'85\' x2=\'55\' y2=\'105\' stroke=\'black\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><line x1=\'115\' y1=\'75\' x2=\'135\' y2=\'65\' stroke=\'black\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><line x1=\'115\' y1=\'85\' x2=\'135\' y2=\'105\' stroke=\'black\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><line x1=\'45\' y1=\'70\' x2=\'45\' y2=\'95\' stroke=\'black\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><line x1=\'145\' y1=\'70\' x2=\'145\' y2=\'95\' stroke=\'black\' stroke-width=\'2\'/></svg>"}]}','Remember its \'butterfly\' structure. It has peroxide linkages.','["chemistry", "redox", "exceptions", "neet-favorite"]','medium','Neet Buddy',3,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('dcd44a3c-26a9-465a-a394-19b1653bef30','9a2b8e85-6073-4662-8871-fa06c27fc6af','Define a Disproportionation reaction.','A redox reaction in which the same element is simultaneously oxidized and reduced.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "Example: $2H_2O_2 \\\\rightarrow 2H_2O + O_2$\\nOxygen goes from -1 in $H_2O_2$ to -2 in $H_2O$ (reduction) and 0 in $O_2$ (oxidation)."}]}','One reactant splits into two products with different oxidation states.','["chemistry", "redox", "reaction-types"]','medium','Neet Buddy',4,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('3b1ab586-02fb-46e6-9a84-95bb23ce0887','9a2b8e85-6073-4662-8871-fa06c27fc6af','State the LOAN mnemonic for a Galvanic (Voltaic) cell.','**L**eft side, **O**xidation occurs, it is the **A**node, and it has a **N**egative charge.',NULL,NULL,'In electrolytic cells, the anode is positive. LOAN is strictly for Galvanic cells!','["chemistry", "electrochemistry", "galvanic-cell", "mnemonics"]','medium','Neet Buddy',5,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('3d389b32-5f21-4b89-8c54-f815b9a9ce3b','9a2b8e85-6073-4662-8871-fa06c27fc6af','What is the formula for Standard Cell Potential ($E^\\circ_{cell}$)?','$E^\\circ_{cell} = E^\\circ_{cathode} - E^\\circ_{anode}$',NULL,NULL,'Both values must be Standard REDUCTION Potentials.','["chemistry", "electrochemistry", "cell-potential"]','medium','Neet Buddy',6,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('ab7f9a07-5493-4cc0-aeda-0704b60382f3','9a2b8e85-6073-4662-8871-fa06c27fc6af','How do you identify the strongest oxidizing and reducing agents from the Electrochemical Series?','Highest standard reduction potential ($+ve$): Strongest **Oxidizing Agent** (e.g., $F_2$).\nLowest standard reduction potential ($-ve$): Strongest **Reducing Agent** (e.g., $Li$).',NULL,NULL,'High reduction potential means it wants to be reduced, forcing oxidation on others.','["chemistry", "electrochemistry", "electrochemical-series"]','medium','Neet Buddy',7,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('e3210d33-42a7-4d18-93b6-1f53c12f0d31','9a2b8e85-6073-4662-8871-fa06c27fc6af','Write the Nernst Equation for a general electrochemical cell at 298 K ($25^\\circ\\text{C}$).','$E_{cell} = E^\\circ_{cell} - \\frac{0.0591}{n} \\log Q$',NULL,NULL,'$n$ = moles of electrons transferred, $Q$ = reaction quotient = $[Products]/[Reactants]$.','["chemistry", "electrochemistry", "nernst-equation", "formulas"]','medium','Neet Buddy',8,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('c2c6b6f2-5c44-463e-99c7-959da9d2b429','9a2b8e85-6073-4662-8871-fa06c27fc6af','What is the relationship between Standard Gibbs Free Energy ($\\Delta G^\\circ$) and Standard Cell Potential ($E^\\circ_{cell}$)?','$\\Delta G^\\circ = -n F E^\\circ_{cell}$',NULL,NULL,'For a spontaneous reaction, $\\Delta G^\\circ$ must be negative, meaning $E^\\circ_{cell}$ must be positive.','["chemistry", "electrochemistry", "thermodynamics"]','medium','Neet Buddy',9,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('20fc8087-4b63-4c11-ae07-f8b55d786bc9','9a2b8e85-6073-4662-8871-fa06c27fc6af','What is the relationship between Conductivity ($\\kappa$), Conductance ($G$), and Cell Constant ($G^*$)?','$\\kappa = G \\times G^*$ \n(Conductivity = Conductance $\\times$ Cell Constant)',NULL,NULL,'Cell constant $G^* = l/A$ (length / area).','["chemistry", "electrochemistry", "conductance"]','medium','Neet Buddy',10,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('16e0e459-e59a-4483-ada5-15f438e9660a','9a2b8e85-6073-4662-8871-fa06c27fc6af','Provide the formula for Molar Conductivity ($\\Lambda_m$) and state its most common units.','$\\Lambda_m = \\frac{\\kappa \\times 1000}{M}$ \nUnits: $S \\text{ cm}^2 \\text{ mol}^{-1}$',NULL,NULL,'$\\kappa$ must be in $S \\text{ cm}^{-1}$ and $M$ is Molarity (mol/L).','["chemistry", "electrochemistry", "molar-conductivity", "formulas"]','medium','Neet Buddy',11,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('846f9197-fc7c-4e97-89cb-ce9a1f2892c3','9a2b8e85-6073-4662-8871-fa06c27fc6af','State Kohlrausch\'s Law of Independent Migration of Ions.','Limiting molar conductivity ($\\Lambda_m^\\circ$) of an electrolyte can be represented as the sum of the individual contributions of the anion and cation of the electrolyte.',NULL,NULL,'$\\Lambda_m^\\circ(A_xB_y) = x \\lambda^\\circ_+(A^{y+}) + y \\lambda^\\circ_-(B^{x-})$','["chemistry", "electrochemistry", "kohlrausch-law"]','medium','Neet Buddy',12,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('3bd42293-fa5f-4736-aad7-3fb83deafa13','9a2b8e85-6073-4662-8871-fa06c27fc6af','What is the primary application of Kohlrausch\'s Law?','It is used to calculate the limiting molar conductivity ($\\Lambda_m^\\circ$) of **weak electrolytes**, which cannot be obtained experimentally by extrapolating the plot of $\\Lambda_m$ vs $\\sqrt{c}$.',NULL,NULL,'You use strong electrolytes to find the value for a weak one.','["chemistry", "electrochemistry", "kohlrausch-law", "applications"]','medium','Neet Buddy',13,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('d4bb3aa0-0fea-493b-9822-c6ac1bb25e2e','9a2b8e85-6073-4662-8871-fa06c27fc6af','During the electrolysis of aqueous NaCl, why is $H_2$ gas evolved at the cathode instead of Na metal?','Because the standard reduction potential of water (to form $H_2$) is higher (less negative) than the standard reduction potential of $Na^+$.',NULL,NULL,'Nature takes the easiest path. $H_2O$ is easier to reduce than $Na^+$.','["chemistry", "electrochemistry", "electrolysis", "products"]','medium','Neet Buddy',14,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('f47e4b9c-6706-4095-adc0-e730af15e361','9a2b8e85-6073-4662-8871-fa06c27fc6af','What is \'Overpotential\' (or Overvoltage) and how does it affect the electrolysis of aqueous NaCl at the anode?','Overpotential is the extra voltage required to drive a kinetically slow reaction. \nAt the anode, oxidation of $H_2O$ to $O_2$ is thermodynamically favored, but due to the high overpotential of oxygen, $Cl^-$ is oxidized to $Cl_2$ gas instead.',NULL,NULL,'Kinetics beats thermodynamics in this specific case.','["chemistry", "electrochemistry", "overpotential", "neet-favorite"]','medium','Neet Buddy',15,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('8460c027-6538-45dd-8404-412973d6331c','9a2b8e85-6073-4662-8871-fa06c27fc6af','Identify the Anode, Cathode, and Electrolyte in a Lead Storage Battery.','Anode: Lead (Pb)\nCathode: Lead dioxide ($PbO_2$)\nElectrolyte: $38\\%$ (by mass) Sulfuric acid ($H_2SO_4$)',NULL,NULL,'In your car battery, both electrodes turn into $PbSO_4$ when discharged.','["chemistry", "electrochemistry", "batteries"]','medium','Neet Buddy',16,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('adceab7d-4c6b-4775-a177-7a07c47c73c7','9a2b8e85-6073-4662-8871-fa06c27fc6af','What is a Fuel Cell and what is its thermodynamic efficiency formula?','A galvanic cell that converts the energy of combustion of fuels (like $H_2$, $CH_4$, $CH_3OH$) directly into electrical energy.\nEfficiency ($\\eta$) = $\\frac{\\Delta G}{\\Delta H}$',NULL,NULL,'The Apollo space missions used $H_2-O_2$ fuel cells.','["chemistry", "electrochemistry", "fuel-cells"]','medium','Neet Buddy',17,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('ed56b72c-a2ee-4c40-9fda-2023c44e0c5e','9a2b8e85-6073-4662-8871-fa06c27fc6af','What is the Standard Hydrogen Electrode (SHE) and its assigned potential?','SHE is the reference electrode used to measure standard electrode potentials. \nIt consists of a Pt electrode in 1M $H^+$ solution, with $H_2$ gas bubbled at 1 atm. \nIts standard potential is assigned exactly **$0.00 \\text{ V}$** at all temperatures.',NULL,NULL,'The zero mark on the electrochemical ruler.','["chemistry", "electrochemistry", "reference-electrode"]','medium','Neet Buddy',18,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('7afd43f8-7ba3-4ded-8ea5-b5cc79e163e7','9a2b8e85-6073-4662-8871-fa06c27fc6af','How does Molar Conductivity ($\\Lambda_m$) vary with dilution for Strong and Weak electrolytes?','Strong Electrolytes: Increases slightly with dilution due to decreased inter-ionic attractions.\nWeak Electrolytes: Increases steeply on dilution due to a significant increase in the degree of dissociation ($\\alpha$).',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\" xmlns:xlink=\\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\\" width=\\"1069\\" height=\\"688\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 1069 688\\">\\n  <image xlink:href=\\"data:image/png;base64,\\" x=\\"0\\" y=\\"0\\" width=\\"1069\\" height=\\"688\\"/>\\n</svg>"}]}','Dilution = decreasing concentration ($c \\rightarrow 0$).','["chemistry", "electrochemistry", "molar-conductivity"]','medium','Neet Buddy',19,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('3261cd18-0cb8-4cf9-bd42-09694c09723c','a7f13abe-2638-4800-9b9c-25d78130a1d8','What is the correct order of dipole moment for halomethanes ($CH_3X$) where X = F, Cl, Br, I?','$CH_3Cl > CH_3F > CH_3Br > CH_3I$. Note the anomaly: $CH_3Cl$ has a higher dipole moment than $CH_3F$ because the larger C-Cl bond length outweighs the higher electronegativity of Fluorine.',NULL,NULL,'Dipole moment = charge $\\times$ distance. Fluorine\'s tiny size is the catch.','["chemistry", "haloalkanes", "dipole-moment", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',0,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('40468d97-4618-4141-9937-6046fe93dea2','a7f13abe-2638-4800-9b9c-25d78130a1d8','Why is Thionyl Chloride ($SOCl_2$) preferred over $PX_3$ or $PX_5$ for preparing alkyl chlorides from alcohols?','The reaction produces alkyl chloride along with gases ($SO_2$ and $HCl$). Since the byproducts are escapable gases, the reaction yields pure alkyl chloride without the need for complex separation.',NULL,NULL,'Think about the physical state of the byproducts.','["chemistry", "haloalkanes", "preparation"]','medium','Neet Buddy',1,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('3110a79a-c3d1-4590-b8bb-41d67d91eee7','a7f13abe-2638-4800-9b9c-25d78130a1d8','Identify the reaction and its purpose: $R-X + NaI \\xrightarrow{\\text{dry acetone}} R-I + NaX$ (where X = Cl, Br).','This is the **Finkelstein reaction**. It is a halogen exchange method specifically used for the preparation of Alkyl Iodides.',NULL,NULL,'NaI is soluble in dry acetone, but NaCl and NaBr precipitate out.','["chemistry", "haloalkanes", "name-reaction", "finkelstein"]','medium','Neet Buddy',2,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('31a33e6f-0cfb-4148-a602-5f267e00d79c','a7f13abe-2638-4800-9b9c-25d78130a1d8','Which reaction is best suited for synthesizing Alkyl Fluorides, and what reagents are used?','**Swarts Reaction**. It involves heating an alkyl chloride/bromide in the presence of heavy metal fluorides such as $AgF$, $Hg_2F_2$, $CoF_3$, or $SbF_3$.',NULL,NULL,'Exchange with a transition/heavy metal fluoride.','["chemistry", "haloalkanes", "name-reaction", "swarts"]','medium','Neet Buddy',3,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('551d6f02-40bc-4042-8c32-3eedf2239be4','a7f13abe-2638-4800-9b9c-25d78130a1d8','How do the reagents differ between the Sandmeyer and Gattermann reactions for synthesizing haloarenes?','Both start with a diazonium salt.\n**Sandmeyer:** Uses Cuprous halides dissolved in corresponding acid ($Cu_2Cl_2/HCl$ or $Cu_2Br_2/HBr$).\n**Gattermann:** Uses Copper powder in the presence of corresponding acid ($Cu/HCl$ or $Cu/HBr$).',NULL,NULL,'Sandmeyer = Cuprous salt; Gattermann = Copper powder.','["chemistry", "haloarenes", "name-reaction", "preparation"]','medium','Neet Buddy',4,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('d54c754d-3538-47b3-857f-3b926663f3dc','a7f13abe-2638-4800-9b9c-25d78130a1d8','How does the boiling point vary among isomeric haloalkanes?','The boiling point decreases with an increase in branching. Branching makes the molecule more spherical, decreasing the surface area of contact, which weakens the intermolecular van der Waals forces.',NULL,NULL,'Spheres have the smallest surface area for a given volume.','["chemistry", "haloalkanes", "physical-properties"]','medium','Neet Buddy',5,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('f2af2110-30fd-4fbe-a6d4-99e431c50e4a','a7f13abe-2638-4800-9b9c-25d78130a1d8','What is the reactivity order of alkyl halides towards $S_N2$ mechanism, and what is the key reason?','$1^\\circ > 2^\\circ > 3^\\circ > \\text{Methyl halide}$. \nThe reactivity is governed by **steric hindrance**. Bulky alkyl groups block the approach of the incoming nucleophile from the backside.','{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "What is the reactivity order of alkyl halides towards $S_N2$ mechanism? Explain the stereochemical outcome with a diagram."}]}','{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "Stereochemical outcome: Complete Walden Inversion (like an umbrella turning inside out)."}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg width=\'300\' height=\'120\' viewBox=\'0 0 300 120\' xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\'><text x=\'10\' y=\'65\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'14\' fill=\'#d93025\'>Nu-</text><path d=\'M40 60 Q 60 40 75 55\' fill=\'none\' stroke=\'#d93025\' stroke-width=\'2\' stroke-dasharray=\'4,4\'/><polygon points=\'75,55 70,45 65,55\' fill=\'#d93025\'/><circle cx=\'90\' cy=\'60\' r=\'15\' fill=\'#3c4043\'/><line x1=\'105\' y1=\'60\' x2=\'135\' y2=\'60\' stroke=\'#333\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><circle cx=\'145\' cy=\'60\' r=\'10\' fill=\'#188038\'/><text x=\'140\' y=\'65\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'12\' fill=\'#fff\'>X</text><line x1=\'75\' y1=\'60\' x2=\'60\' y2=\'30\' stroke=\'#333\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><line x1=\'75\' y1=\'60\' x2=\'60\' y2=\'90\' stroke=\'#333\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><text x=\'170\' y=\'65\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'20\'>&#8594;</text><circle cx=\'240\' cy=\'60\' r=\'15\' fill=\'#3c4043\'/><line x1=\'225\' y1=\'60\' x2=\'195\' y2=\'60\' stroke=\'#d93025\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><text x=\'170\' y=\'65\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'14\' fill=\'#d93025\'>Nu</text><line x1=\'255\' y1=\'60\' x2=\'270\' y2=\'30\' stroke=\'#333\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><line x1=\'255\' y1=\'60\' x2=\'270\' y2=\'90\' stroke=\'#333\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><text x=\'270\' y=\'65\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'14\'>+ X-</text><text x=\'80\' y=\'110\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'12\'>Reactant</text><text x=\'210\' y=\'110\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'12\'>Inverted Product</text></svg>"}]}','Less crowding = faster reaction.','["chemistry", "haloalkanes", "reaction-mechanism", "sn2"]','medium','Neet Buddy',6,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('9fc0136e-bd39-4d81-9534-f57dc5ef0df7','a7f13abe-2638-4800-9b9c-25d78130a1d8','What is the reactivity order of alkyl halides towards $S_N1$ mechanism, and what is the stereochemical outcome?','$3^\\circ > 2^\\circ > 1^\\circ$.\nThe reactivity is governed by the **stability of the carbocation** intermediate. The stereochemical outcome is **racemization** (a mixture of retention and inversion of configuration) because the nucleophile can attack the planar carbocation from either side.',NULL,NULL,'Two-step reaction; intermediate is a planar carbocation.','["chemistry", "haloalkanes", "reaction-mechanism", "sn1"]','medium','Neet Buddy',7,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('33a28d78-16f4-410f-bca0-4de970e07d98','a7f13abe-2638-4800-9b9c-25d78130a1d8','Why do allylic and benzylic halides show high reactivity towards the $S_N1$ mechanism?','Although they might be primary halides, they ionize to form allylic and benzylic carbocations, which are highly stabilized by **resonance**.',NULL,NULL,'Check the stability of the intermediate formed after the halogen leaves.','["chemistry", "haloalkanes", "reaction-mechanism", "sn1", "resonance"]','medium','Neet Buddy',8,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('15f8b927-1a19-437d-a5fe-1a436ff8730c','a7f13abe-2638-4800-9b9c-25d78130a1d8','What products are formed when an alkyl halide ($R-X$) reacts with $KCN$ vs $AgCN$?','$KCN$ yields predominantly **alkyl cyanides (nitriles)**, $R-CN$. \n$AgCN$ yields predominantly **alkyl isocyanides**, $R-NC$.',NULL,NULL,'$KCN$ is ionic (free $C^-$ attacks), $AgCN$ is covalent (Nitrogen lone pair attacks).','["chemistry", "haloalkanes", "nucleophilic-substitution", "ambident"]','medium','Neet Buddy',9,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('aa3e554f-c759-432d-9992-894d682eff48','a7f13abe-2638-4800-9b9c-25d78130a1d8','What products are formed when an alkyl halide ($R-X$) reacts with $KNO_2$ vs $AgNO_2$?','$KNO_2$ yields **alkyl nitrites** ($R-O-N=O$).\n$AgNO_2$ yields **nitroalkanes** ($R-NO_2$).',NULL,NULL,'Nitrite ($NO_2^-$) is an ambident nucleophile. O attacks in ionic salt, N attacks in covalent salt.','["chemistry", "haloalkanes", "nucleophilic-substitution", "ambident"]','medium','Neet Buddy',10,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('f2cc2eae-37d9-451a-b342-cb0efd11c428','a7f13abe-2638-4800-9b9c-25d78130a1d8','How does the choice of solvent affect the reaction of alkyl halides with KOH?','**Aqueous KOH** favors nucleophilic substitution ($S_N$) yielding **alcohols** ($R-OH$).\n**Alcoholic KOH** (alc. KOH) acts as a strong base and favors elimination ($E2$) yielding **alkenes**.',NULL,NULL,'Aqueous = $OH^-$ is a good nucleophile. Alcoholic = $RO^-$ is a stronger base.','["chemistry", "haloalkanes", "elimination-vs-substitution"]','medium','Neet Buddy',11,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('fb5d13d4-a50f-4308-ae58-a0f0aa3ab1a1','a7f13abe-2638-4800-9b9c-25d78130a1d8','Why must Grignard Reagents ($R-Mg-X$) be prepared under strictly anhydrous conditions?','Grignard reagents are highly reactive. Traces of moisture (water) provide protons, which react with the highly basic $R^-$ group of the Grignard reagent to form **hydrocarbons** ($RH$), destroying the reagent.\nReaction: $R-Mg-X + H_2O \\rightarrow R-H + Mg(OH)X$',NULL,NULL,'Water acts as a strong acid towards these reagents.','["chemistry", "haloalkanes", "organometallic"]','medium','Neet Buddy',12,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('501ff3af-4d58-49b7-883e-5a2a106aed0a','a7f13abe-2638-4800-9b9c-25d78130a1d8','Give two main reasons why haloarenes are extremely less reactive towards nucleophilic substitution compared to haloalkanes.','1. **Resonance Effect:** The lone pair on the halogen delocalizes with the benzene ring, giving the C-X bond a partial double bond character (harder to break).\n2. **Hybridization:** The carbon attached to X in haloarenes is $sp^2$ hybridized (more electronegative, holds electron pair tighter) compared to $sp^3$ in haloalkanes.',NULL,NULL,'Think about bond strength and carbon\'s electron hold.','["chemistry", "haloarenes", "reactivity"]','medium','Neet Buddy',13,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('4953bac9-4555-4bf7-8051-ed27fb7044cd','a7f13abe-2638-4800-9b9c-25d78130a1d8','What are the specific temperature and pressure conditions for Dow\'s Process (conversion of chlorobenzene to phenol)?','Chlorobenzene is fused with $NaOH$ at **$623\\text{ K}$** and **$300\\text{ atm}$** pressure, followed by acidification.',NULL,NULL,'Drastic conditions are needed because haloarenes resist nucleophilic attack.','["chemistry", "haloarenes", "dows-process"]','medium','Neet Buddy',14,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('1eb4b10f-1fba-41de-b7c3-bb8ef7291d7d','a7f13abe-2638-4800-9b9c-25d78130a1d8','Differentiate between Wurtz-Fittig and Fittig reactions with respect to reactants and products.','**Wurtz-Fittig:** Alkyl halide + Aryl halide + $Na$ $\\xrightarrow{\\text{dry ether}}$ **Alkylbenzene**.\n**Fittig:** Aryl halide + Aryl halide + $Na$ $\\xrightarrow{\\text{dry ether}}$ **Diaryls (Biphenyl)**.',NULL,NULL,'Wurtz = Alkyl, Fittig = Aryl. Wurtz-Fittig is a mix of both.','["chemistry", "haloarenes", "name-reaction", "coupling"]','medium','Neet Buddy',15,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('a5fde68a-cbb5-43d9-9f65-b141ddebdcfc','a7f13abe-2638-4800-9b9c-25d78130a1d8','In electrophilic aromatic substitution of haloarenes, halogens are deactivating but ortho-para directing. Why?','They deactivate the ring due to their strong electron-withdrawing **-I effect**. However, they are ortho-para directing because their **+R (resonance) effect** stabilizes the intermediate carbocation specifically at the ortho and para positions.',NULL,NULL,'-I controls overall reactivity (speed); +R controls orientation (direction).','["chemistry", "haloarenes", "electrophilic-substitution", "directing-groups"]','medium','Neet Buddy',16,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('b93e4b3c-a44a-4e94-9d5d-55fb1cb9a5ac','a7f13abe-2638-4800-9b9c-25d78130a1d8','Why is Chloroform ($CHCl_3$) stored in closed, dark-colored bottles completely filled to the brim?','Chloroform is slowly oxidized by air in the presence of light to form **Phosgene ($COCl_2$)**, which is a highly poisonous gas.\n$2CHCl_3 + O_2 \\xrightarrow{\\text{light}} 2COCl_2 + 2HCl$',NULL,NULL,'Light and oxygen create a toxic carbonyl compound.','["chemistry", "polyhalogen-compounds", "chloroform"]','medium','Neet Buddy',17,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('2f2e08bf-8f78-4810-9c48-75588d723833','a7f13abe-2638-4800-9b9c-25d78130a1d8','What are Freons and how do they damage the environment?','Freons are the chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs) of methane and ethane (e.g., Freon-12, $CCl_2F_2$). They drift into the stratosphere, where UV light breaks C-Cl bonds to release **chlorine free radicals**, which catalyze the **depletion of the ozone layer**.',NULL,NULL,'CFCs + UV = Chlorine radicals.','["chemistry", "polyhalogen-compounds", "freons", "environment"]','medium','Neet Buddy',18,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('95ce9767-0fe0-414d-8506-28bfdab10d6d','a7f13abe-2638-4800-9b9c-25d78130a1d8','What is the structural formula of DDT and what is its major environmental drawback?','DDT is p,p\'-Dichlorodiphenyltrichloroethane. Drawback: It is highly stable and **non-biodegradable**, accumulating in fatty tissues and causing **biomagnification** in the food chain.','{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "What is DDT and its major environmental drawback? Visualize its structure."}]}','{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "p,p\'-Dichlorodiphenyltrichloroethane. It causes biomagnification."}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\" xmlns:xlink=\\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\\" width=\\"1152\\" height=\\"556\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 1152 556\\">\\n  <image xlink:href=\\"data:image/png;base64,\\" x=\\"0\\" y=\\"0\\" width=\\"1152\\" height=\\"556\\"/>\\n</svg>"}]}','Two chlorobenzenes attached to a trichloroethane skeleton.','["chemistry", "polyhalogen-compounds", "ddt", "environment"]','medium','Neet Buddy',19,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('6aa5246a-b5d6-4fd7-a1a7-d091ca1c9484','aa655c57-0932-4da1-b3bf-46af437e1a11','For the reaction $aA + bB \\rightarrow cC + dD$, write the expression for the average rate of reaction in terms of the reactants and products.','Rate $= -\\frac{1}{a}\\frac{\\Delta[A]}{\\Delta t} = -\\frac{1}{b}\\frac{\\Delta[B]}{\\Delta t} = +\\frac{1}{c}\\frac{\\Delta[C]}{\\Delta t} = +\\frac{1}{d}\\frac{\\Delta[D]}{\\Delta t}$',NULL,NULL,'Divide the rate of disappearance/appearance by the stoichiometric coefficient.','["chemistry", "chemical-kinetics", "rate-of-reaction", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',0,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('eb543725-6ede-4ecf-9e0e-7309f5333af4','aa655c57-0932-4da1-b3bf-46af437e1a11','State three key differences between the Order and Molecularity of a reaction.','1. **Order** is determined experimentally; **Molecularity** is a theoretical concept.\n2. **Order** can be zero, fractional, or an integer; **Molecularity** is always a positive integer (1, 2, 3).\n3. **Order** applies to the overall reaction; **Molecularity** applies only to a single elementary step.',NULL,NULL,'Molecularity is just the number of colliding molecules in one step.','["chemistry", "chemical-kinetics", "order-molecularity"]','medium','Neet Buddy',1,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('f68700c3-cb58-42b1-8b4f-3308e025a243','aa655c57-0932-4da1-b3bf-46af437e1a11','What is the general formula for the unit of the rate constant ($k$) for an $n^{th}$ order reaction?','Unit of $k = (\\text{mol L}^{-1})^{1-n} \\text{s}^{-1}$ \nOR\n$\\text{mol}^{1-n} \\text{L}^{n-1} \\text{s}^{-1}$',NULL,NULL,'Plug in n=0 for zero order, n=1 for first order.','["chemistry", "chemical-kinetics", "rate-constant", "neet-shortcuts"]','medium','Neet Buddy',2,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('508ab906-7ad1-45e8-a6a7-9514b66f90d2','aa655c57-0932-4da1-b3bf-46af437e1a11','What are the units of the rate constant ($k$) for Zero-order and First-order reactions?','Zero-order: $\\text{mol L}^{-1} \\text{s}^{-1}$ (same as the rate of reaction)\nFirst-order: $\\text{s}^{-1}$',NULL,NULL,'For 1st order, concentration terms cancel out.','["chemistry", "chemical-kinetics", "units"]','medium','Neet Buddy',3,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('7dfcaacd-eed6-40b3-8a7f-e7b5b69ab014','aa655c57-0932-4da1-b3bf-46af437e1a11','Write the integrated rate equation for a Zero-order reaction.','$[A] = [A]_0 - kt$ \n(where $[A]_0$ is initial concentration, $[A]$ is concentration at time $t$)',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\' viewBox=\'0 0 250 150\'><line x1=\'40\' y1=\'120\' x2=\'220\' y2=\'120\' stroke=\'black\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><line x1=\'40\' y1=\'120\' x2=\'40\' y2=\'20\' stroke=\'black\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><text x=\'110\' y=\'140\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'14\'>Time (t)</text><text x=\'10\' y=\'80\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'14\' transform=\'rotate(-90 10,80)\'>[A]</text><line x1=\'40\' y1=\'40\' x2=\'200\' y2=\'100\' stroke=\'red\' stroke-width=\'3\'/><text x=\'50\' y=\'35\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'12\' fill=\'red\'>[A]₀</text><text x=\'130\' y=\'60\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'12\' fill=\'red\'>Slope = -k</text></svg>"}]}','It resembles the equation of a straight line: y = mx + c','["chemistry", "chemical-kinetics", "zero-order", "formulas"]','medium','Neet Buddy',4,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('f60a2896-2867-4e5a-badd-03ed8f3e8b81','aa655c57-0932-4da1-b3bf-46af437e1a11','Write the integrated rate equation for a First-order reaction (in terms of log base 10).','$k = \\frac{2.303}{t} \\log \\frac{[A]_0}{[A]}$',NULL,NULL,'Use 2.303 to convert natural log (ln) to base 10 log.','["chemistry", "chemical-kinetics", "first-order", "formulas"]','medium','Neet Buddy',5,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('8c5a12ec-0ff5-4ad3-8732-1c45563c8e79','aa655c57-0932-4da1-b3bf-46af437e1a11','For a first-order reaction, what is the slope of the graph when $\\log[A]$ is plotted against time ($t$)?','Slope = $-\\frac{k}{2.303}$',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\' viewBox=\'0 0 250 150\'><line x1=\'40\' y1=\'120\' x2=\'220\' y2=\'120\' stroke=\'black\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><line x1=\'40\' y1=\'120\' x2=\'40\' y2=\'20\' stroke=\'black\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><text x=\'110\' y=\'140\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'14\'>Time (t)</text><text x=\'10\' y=\'90\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'12\' transform=\'rotate(-90 10,90)\'>log [A]</text><line x1=\'40\' y1=\'30\' x2=\'200\' y2=\'110\' stroke=\'blue\' stroke-width=\'3\'/><text x=\'110\' y=\'50\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'12\' fill=\'blue\'>Slope = -k / 2.303</text></svg>"}]}','Because $\\log[A] = \\log[A]_0 - \\frac{kt}{2.303}$','["chemistry", "chemical-kinetics", "graphs", "neet-favorite"]','medium','Neet Buddy',6,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('423a0624-2c49-41de-b266-f59219f2033b','aa655c57-0932-4da1-b3bf-46af437e1a11','Write the formula for the Half-life ($t_{1/2}$) of a Zero-order reaction. How does it depend on initial concentration?','$t_{1/2} = \\frac{[A]_0}{2k}$\nIt is **directly proportional** to the initial concentration ($[A]_0$).',NULL,NULL,'More stuff takes longer to halve if the rate is constant.','["chemistry", "chemical-kinetics", "half-life", "zero-order"]','medium','Neet Buddy',7,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('b5e40f36-b99c-404a-b963-53acd2e9c6c1','aa655c57-0932-4da1-b3bf-46af437e1a11','Write the formula for the Half-life ($t_{1/2}$) of a First-order reaction. What is its unique characteristic?','$t_{1/2} = \\frac{0.693}{k}$\nUnique characteristic: It is **independent** of the initial concentration ($[A]_0$).',NULL,NULL,'Radioactive decay always takes the same time to halve, regardless of sample size.','["chemistry", "chemical-kinetics", "half-life", "first-order", "neet-favorite"]','medium','Neet Buddy',8,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('5da8b8ac-caed-4454-8c2b-f9a5fbcd9b20','aa655c57-0932-4da1-b3bf-46af437e1a11','What is the general relationship between the half-life ($t_{1/2}$) and initial concentration ($[A]_0$) for an $n^{th}$ order reaction?','$t_{1/2} \\propto \\frac{1}{([A]_0)^{n-1}}$',NULL,NULL,'Test it: If n=1, proportional to 1 (constant). If n=0, proportional to [A]0.','["chemistry", "chemical-kinetics", "half-life", "neet-shortcuts"]','medium','Neet Buddy',9,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('5f03d60e-cab7-4559-abae-077d2948da75','aa655c57-0932-4da1-b3bf-46af437e1a11','What is a Pseudo First-Order Reaction? Give one classic example.','A reaction that is not truly first-order but behaves as first-order because one of the reactants is present in large excess.\nExample: Acid-catalyzed hydrolysis of ester ($CH_3COOC_2H_5 + H_2O \\xrightarrow{H^+} CH_3COOH + C_2H_5OH$).',NULL,NULL,'Water is in such huge excess that its concentration barely changes.','["chemistry", "chemical-kinetics", "pseudo-order"]','medium','Neet Buddy',10,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('344c8a3d-f585-4120-912f-c407efecf45d','aa655c57-0932-4da1-b3bf-46af437e1a11','What is the Rate-Determining Step (RDS) in a complex reaction?','The **slowest step** in a multi-step (complex) reaction is the rate-determining step. It controls the overall rate of the reaction.',NULL,NULL,'A chain is only as strong as its weakest (slowest) link.','["chemistry", "chemical-kinetics", "mechanisms"]','medium','Neet Buddy',11,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('721928aa-1846-42bc-8f0c-6436724d1967','aa655c57-0932-4da1-b3bf-46af437e1a11','State the Arrhenius Equation and identify its terms.','$k = A e^{-E_a / RT}$\nWhere:\n$k$ = Rate constant\n$A$ = Arrhenius factor (Frequency factor)\n$E_a$ = Activation Energy\n$R$ = Gas constant\n$T$ = Temperature in Kelvin',NULL,NULL,'The exponential term represents the fraction of molecules with energy $\\ge E_a$.','["chemistry", "chemical-kinetics", "arrhenius", "formulas"]','medium','Neet Buddy',12,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('869be169-3a47-40d8-a4bf-7a1478a0fbbe','aa655c57-0932-4da1-b3bf-46af437e1a11','Write the logarithmic form of the Arrhenius equation used to calculate Activation Energy ($E_a$) from rate constants at two different temperatures ($T_1$ and $T_2$).','$\\log \\frac{k_2}{k_1} = \\frac{E_a}{2.303 R} \\left( \\frac{T_2 - T_1}{T_1 T_2} \\right)$',NULL,NULL,'Ensure Temperature is in Kelvin and R is $8.314 \\text{ J K}^{-1}\\text{mol}^{-1}$.','["chemistry", "chemical-kinetics", "arrhenius", "calculations", "neet-numerical"]','medium','Neet Buddy',13,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('0e03e23c-a58b-4e82-b21c-530c8a10852f','d65afa3c-7664-4e36-b666-09606bf85bef','Write the formulas for the Radius ($r_n$) and Energy ($E_n$) of the $n^{th}$ Bohr orbit for hydrogen-like species.','$r_n = 52.9 \\frac{n^2}{Z} \\text{ pm}$ \n$E_n = -2.18 \\times 10^{-18} \\frac{Z^2}{n^2} \\text{ J}$ (or $-13.6 \\frac{Z^2}{n^2} \\text{ eV}$)',NULL,NULL,'Radius is proportional to n^2. Energy is proportional to 1/n^2.','["chemistry", "atomic-structure", "bohr-model", "neet-numerical"]','medium','Neet Buddy',4,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('98e94a49-a9bf-4e8e-b63e-f190e796c180','d65afa3c-7664-4e36-b666-09606bf85bef','What are the Stark effect and the Zeeman effect? (Limitations of Bohr\'s Model)','Stark effect: Splitting of spectral lines in the presence of an electric field. \nZeeman effect: Splitting of spectral lines in the presence of a magnetic field.',NULL,NULL,'Stark = E-field, Zeeman = B-field','["chemistry", "atomic-structure", "bohr-model"]','medium','Neet Buddy',5,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('064d704b-7ebb-45c3-b8af-fc8194ecef7b','aa655c57-0932-4da1-b3bf-46af437e1a11','How does adding a positive catalyst increase the rate of a chemical reaction according to Collision Theory?','A catalyst provides an alternative reaction pathway with a **lower Activation Energy ($E_a$)**. This increases the fraction of molecules capable of crossing the energy barrier.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\' viewBox=\'0 0 250 150\'><line x1=\'30\' y1=\'130\' x2=\'230\' y2=\'130\' stroke=\'black\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><line x1=\'30\' y1=\'130\' x2=\'30\' y2=\'10\' stroke=\'black\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><text x=\'100\' y=\'145\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'12\'>Reaction Progress</text><text x=\'10\' y=\'90\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'12\' transform=\'rotate(-90 10,90)\'>Energy</text><path d=\'M 30 100 C 80 100, 100 20, 150 20 C 180 20, 200 80, 230 80\' stroke=\'red\' stroke-width=\'2\' fill=\'none\'/><path d=\'M 30 100 C 80 100, 100 60, 150 60 C 180 60, 200 80, 230 80\' stroke=\'green\' stroke-width=\'2\' stroke-dasharray=\'4\' fill=\'none\'/><text x=\'110\' y=\'18\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'10\' fill=\'red\'>Uncatalyzed Ea</text><text x=\'155\' y=\'60\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'10\' fill=\'green\'>Catalyzed Ea</text></svg>"}]}','It lowers the hill you have to climb.','["chemistry", "chemical-kinetics", "catalyst"]','medium','Neet Buddy',14,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('511041b2-376e-4540-99d4-54958d7f5c28','aa655c57-0932-4da1-b3bf-46af437e1a11','Does a catalyst change the Enthalpy of reaction ($\\Delta H$) or the Equilibrium constant ($K$)?','**No.** A catalyst does NOT change $\\Delta H$, $\\Delta G$, or the Equilibrium constant ($K$). It only helps achieve equilibrium faster by lowering $E_a$ for both forward and backward reactions equally.',NULL,NULL,'It changes the path, not the start and end points.','["chemistry", "chemical-kinetics", "catalyst", "conceptual"]','medium','Neet Buddy',15,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('93b9c546-46f1-4248-8ab6-4f0d9c072f69','aa655c57-0932-4da1-b3bf-46af437e1a11','What two key factors must be satisfied for a collision to be \'effective\' according to Collision Theory?','1. **Energy Factor:** Colliding molecules must possess energy equal to or greater than the Threshold Energy.\n2. **Orientation Factor:** Molecules must collide with the proper spatial orientation to break old bonds and form new ones.',NULL,NULL,'They must hit hard enough AND in the right spot.','["chemistry", "chemical-kinetics", "collision-theory"]','medium','Neet Buddy',16,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('0ef4d6a8-474e-43ae-88a2-6a92f130eb53','aa655c57-0932-4da1-b3bf-46af437e1a11','What is Threshold Energy?','Threshold Energy is the minimum total kinetic energy that the reacting molecules must possess for a collision to result in a chemical reaction.\n$\\text{Threshold Energy} = \\text{Activation Energy } (E_a) + \\text{Average Kinetic Energy of Reactants}$',NULL,NULL,'The absolute height of the energy hill from the bottom (zero energy), not just from the reactant energy level.','["chemistry", "chemical-kinetics", "energy-concepts"]','medium','Neet Buddy',17,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('b68c9574-320e-45a8-aaa8-afac1a95397e','aa655c57-0932-4da1-b3bf-46af437e1a11','A first-order reaction is 75% complete in 60 minutes. Find its half-life.','$t_{1/2} = 30$ minutes.\n\n**Reasoning:** 75% completion means 25% (1/4th) is left. \nGoing from 100% $\\rightarrow$ 50% (1st half-life) $\\rightarrow$ 25% (2nd half-life) takes 2 half-lives.\n$2 \\times t_{1/2} = 60 \\implies t_{1/2} = 30$ min.',NULL,NULL,'75% complete = 2 half-lives. 87.5% complete = 3 half-lives. 93.75% = 4 half-lives.','["chemistry", "chemical-kinetics", "half-life", "neet-numerical", "shortcuts"]','medium','Neet Buddy',18,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('c511a3c7-80a1-4d67-8302-7c4fc04d71d8','aa655c57-0932-4da1-b3bf-46af437e1a11','What is the Temperature Coefficient (μ) of a reaction, and what is its typical value','It is the ratio of rate constants of a reaction at two temperatures differing by $10^\\circ\\text{C}$ (usually 298 K and 308 K).\n$\\mu = \\frac{k_{t+10}}{k_t}$\nTypical value: For most reactions, the rate **doubles** (value is $\\approx 2$) for every $10^\\circ\\text{C}$ rise.',NULL,NULL,'A $10^\\circ$ increase doubles the speed.','["chemistry", "chemical-kinetics", "temperature-effect"]','medium','Neet Buddy',19,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('133f6aaa-14ff-4aa7-baf9-6e86cf28aeeb','b378e83a-7b4d-4807-b411-23047a63e134','Define Work in terms of Force and Displacement.','Work is the dot product of Force and Displacement vectors: W = F · d = F d cos(θ), where θ is the angle between them.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "W = F d cos(θ). Work is only done by the component of force along the displacement."}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg viewBox=\'0 0 200 120\' width=\'100%\'><line x1=\'20\' y1=\'100\' x2=\'180\' y2=\'100\' stroke=\'black\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><rect x=\'40\' y=\'60\' width=\'40\' height=\'40\' fill=\'lightblue\' stroke=\'black\'/><line x1=\'80\' y1=\'80\' x2=\'140\' y2=\'40\' stroke=\'red\' stroke-width=\'2\' marker-end=\'url(#arrow)\'/><text x=\'145\' y=\'35\' fill=\'red\'>F</text><line x1=\'80\' y1=\'80\' x2=\'150\' y2=\'80\' stroke=\'blue\' stroke-dasharray=\'4\'/><text x=\'155\' y=\'85\' fill=\'blue\'>d</text><path d=\'M 110 80 A 30 30 0 0 0 100 65\' fill=\'none\' stroke=\'black\'/><text x=\'115\' y=\'72\'>θ</text></svg>"}]}','Work is a scalar quantity. SI Unit: Joule (J).','["physics", "work-energy-power", "work"]','medium','Neet Buddy',0,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('daf5c917-6672-4ce9-b279-08f45759a2c1','b378e83a-7b4d-4807-b411-23047a63e134','Under what three conditions is Work Done equal to zero?','1. Displacement is zero (e.g., pushing a wall).\n2. Force is zero.\n3. Angle between Force and Displacement is 90° (cos 90° = 0) (e.g., tension in a pendulum).',NULL,NULL,'Centripetal force never does work!','["physics", "work-energy-power", "work"]','medium','Neet Buddy',1,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('c1d39f6a-362e-4274-9d43-42f89dccc52b','b378e83a-7b4d-4807-b411-23047a63e134','How do you calculate Work done by a Variable Force?','Work done is the integration of Force with respect to displacement: W = ∫ F dx. Graphically, it is the Area under the Force-Position (F-x) graph.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "W = Area under F-x curve."}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg viewBox=\'0 0 200 120\' width=\'100%\'><line x1=\'20\' y1=\'100\' x2=\'180\' y2=\'100\' stroke=\'black\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><line x1=\'20\' y1=\'100\' x2=\'20\' y2=\'10\' stroke=\'black\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><text x=\'185\' y=\'105\'>x</text><text x=\'5\' y=\'15\'>F</text><path d=\'M 40 100 L 60 40 L 120 40 L 140 100 Z\' fill=\'rgba(0,150,255,0.4)\' stroke=\'blue\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><text x=\'75\' y=\'75\' font-weight=\'bold\' fill=\'blue\'>Work</text></svg>"}]}','Area above the x-axis is positive work; area below is negative work.','["physics", "work-energy-power", "work", "graphs"]','medium','Neet Buddy',2,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('8c4ad846-52f3-42a5-8cc1-b7c313074a93','b378e83a-7b4d-4807-b411-23047a63e134','What is the relation between Kinetic Energy (K) and Linear Momentum (p)?','K = p² / (2m)  OR  p = √(2mK).',NULL,NULL,'If momentum is increased by x%, use this formula to find the % change in kinetic energy.','["physics", "work-energy-power", "energy", "momentum"]','medium','Neet Buddy',3,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('a9cc5d0d-85fc-445d-a85c-1840ae87ee2c','b378e83a-7b4d-4807-b411-23047a63e134','Differentiate between Conservative and Non-conservative forces.','Conservative: Work done is independent of the path taken and zero in a closed loop (e.g., Gravity, Electrostatic, Spring force).\nNon-conservative: Work done depends on the path (e.g., Friction, Air drag, Viscous force).',NULL,NULL,'Potential energy is ONLY defined for conservative forces.','["physics", "work-energy-power", "forces"]','medium','Neet Buddy',4,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('eba33577-5660-482e-afa1-cf8e6296b57e','b378e83a-7b4d-4807-b411-23047a63e134','What is the formula for the Potential Energy (U) stored in an ideal spring?','U = ½ k x², where \'k\' is the spring constant and \'x\' is the extension or compression from the natural length.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "U = ½ k x². The graph of U vs x is a parabola."}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg viewBox=\'0 0 200 120\' width=\'100%\'><line x1=\'10\' y1=\'100\' x2=\'190\' y2=\'100\' stroke=\'black\'/><line x1=\'100\' y1=\'110\' x2=\'100\' y2=\'10\' stroke=\'black\'/><text x=\'185\' y=\'115\'>x</text><text x=\'105\' y=\'15\'>U</text><path d=\'M 40 20 Q 100 180 160 20\' fill=\'none\' stroke=\'red\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><text x=\'65\' y=\'95\'>-x</text><text x=\'125\' y=\'95\'>+x</text></svg>"}]}','The Work done by the spring force is W = -½ k x².','["physics", "work-energy-power", "energy", "springs"]','medium','Neet Buddy',5,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('d02a4027-ee5b-4351-8b71-b735637801a8','b378e83a-7b4d-4807-b411-23047a63e134','How do you determine Stable Equilibrium from a Potential Energy (U) vs. Position (x) graph?','At equilibrium, F = -dU/dx = 0 (slope is zero).\nFor STABLE equilibrium, U is at a minimum, so d²U/dx² is positive.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "Stable: U is minimum. d²U/dx² > 0.\\nUnstable: U is maximum. d²U/dx² < 0.\\nNeutral: U is constant."}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg viewBox=\'0 0 300 100\' width=\'100%\'><path d=\'M 10 30 Q 50 90 90 30\' fill=\'none\' stroke=\'black\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><circle cx=\'50\' cy=\'60\' r=\'10\' fill=\'blue\'/><text x=\'35\' y=\'85\' font-size=\'10\'>Stable</text><path d=\'M 110 80 Q 150 20 190 80\' fill=\'none\' stroke=\'black\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><circle cx=\'150\' cy=\'40\' r=\'10\' fill=\'red\'/><text x=\'130\' y=\'95\' font-size=\'10\'>Unstable</text><line x1=\'210\' y1=\'80\' x2=\'290\' y2=\'80\' stroke=\'black\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><circle cx=\'250\' cy=\'70\' r=\'10\' fill=\'green\'/><text x=\'235\' y=\'95\' font-size=\'10\'>Neutral</text></svg>"}]}','Think of a ball resting at the bottom of a bowl.','["physics", "work-energy-power", "equilibrium", "graphs"]','medium','Neet Buddy',6,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('6082f43b-1285-462c-a282-6364818e479c','b378e83a-7b4d-4807-b411-23047a63e134','What is the formula for the Power of a water pump lifting mass \'m\' to height \'h\' in time \'t\'?','P = (m g h) / t. \nIf water is also ejected with velocity \'v\', P = [mgh + ½mv²] / t.',NULL,NULL,'Mass (m) can be written as Density (ρ) × Volume (V). So m/t = ρ(V/t).','["physics", "work-energy-power", "power", "neet-high-yield"]','medium','Neet Buddy',7,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('3f60111e-12d6-4af7-aa89-b3ac88f74690','b378e83a-7b4d-4807-b411-23047a63e134','What are the core differences between Elastic and Inelastic Collisions?','Elastic Collision: Both Momentum and Kinetic Energy are conserved. (e = 1)\nInelastic Collision: Momentum is conserved, but Kinetic Energy is NOT conserved (some is lost to heat/sound). (0 < e < 1)',NULL,NULL,'Momentum is conserved in ALL isolated collisions, regardless of type.','["physics", "work-energy-power", "collisions"]','medium','Neet Buddy',8,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('3700335b-d06a-4d36-84fb-84cdccc12425','b378e83a-7b4d-4807-b411-23047a63e134','State the Law of Conservation of Mechanical Energy.','If only conservative forces are doing work on a system, the total mechanical energy (Kinetic + Potential) remains constant. K$_i$ + U$_i$ = K$_f$ + U$_f$.',NULL,NULL,'If friction is present, use: W_friction = ΔMechanical_Energy.','["physics", "work-energy-power", "conservation"]','medium','Neet Buddy',9,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('38f7e0f7-95bf-49e2-bc3e-e0821b4bfd51','b378e83a-7b4d-4807-b411-23047a63e134','Define Power and Instantaneous Power.','Power is the rate of doing work. Pₐᵥ₉ = W / Δt.\n\nInstantaneous Power is the dot product of Force and Velocity:\n\nP = F · v = Fv cos(θ)',NULL,NULL,'1 Horsepower (HP) = 746 Watts.','["physics", "work-energy-power", "power"]','medium','Neet Buddy',10,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('16470dff-db3a-4d40-9c5a-689f974e244f','b378e83a-7b4d-4807-b411-23047a63e134','In a vertical circle (string of length r), what is the MINIMUM velocity required at the lowest point to complete the full circle?','v_bottom = √(5gr)',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "Minimum velocities for full loop:\\nBottom: √(5gr)\\nHorizontal: √(3gr)\\nTop: √(gr)"}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg viewBox=\'0 0 200 200\' width=\'100%\'><circle cx=\'100\' cy=\'100\' r=\'80\' fill=\'none\' stroke=\'black\' stroke-dasharray=\'5\'/><circle cx=\'100\' cy=\'100\' r=\'4\' fill=\'black\'/><line x1=\'100\' y1=\'100\' x2=\'100\' y2=\'180\' stroke=\'gray\'/><circle cx=\'100\' cy=\'180\' r=\'10\' fill=\'blue\'/><text x=\'120\' y=\'185\'>√(5gr)</text><circle cx=\'100\' cy=\'20\' r=\'10\' fill=\'red\'/><text x=\'120\' y=\'25\'>√(gr)</text><circle cx=\'180\' cy=\'100\' r=\'10\' fill=\'orange\'/><text x=\'125\' y=\'105\'>√(3gr)</text></svg>"}]}','At this velocity, the tension at the highest point becomes exactly zero. Velocity at the top will be √(gr).','["physics", "work-energy-power", "vertical-circle", "neet-high-yield"]','medium','Neet Buddy',11,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('58411e51-c91d-4c9d-83e6-a8fb215ca92b','b378e83a-7b4d-4807-b411-23047a63e134','What is the relation between the momentum (p) of a photon and its energy (E)?','p = E / c, where \'c\' is the speed of light.',NULL,NULL,'Though photons are massless (rest mass = 0), they carry energy and momentum. (A bridge concept to Modern Physics).','["physics", "work-energy-power", "modern-physics-link"]','medium','Neet Buddy',12,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('22ae8635-dee7-4010-a636-3d86dcd6c05e','b378e83a-7b4d-4807-b411-23047a63e134','If a block of mass \'m\' is dragged on a rough horizontal surface (coefficient of friction μ) by distance \'d\', what is the work done by friction?','Answer Given Below:',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "Answer Given Below:"}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\" xmlns:xlink=\\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\\" width=\\"172\\" height=\\"45\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 172 45\\">\\n  <image xlink:href=\\"data:image/png;base64,\\" x=\\"0\\" y=\\"0\\" width=\\"172\\" height=\\"45\\"/>\\n</svg>"}]}','Friction opposes motion, hence the angle is 180° and cos(180°) = -1. Work done is negative, dissipating heat.','["physics", "work-energy-power", "work", "friction"]','medium','Neet Buddy',13,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('d7b85bf8-2002-417d-8d25-72526e40cd80','b378e83a-7b4d-4807-b411-23047a63e134','What is the formula for the fraction of Kinetic Energy retained by a bouncing ball after \'n\' collisions with the floor?','Kₙ = K₀ (e²ⁿ).\n\nThe velocity after \'n\' bounces is vₙ = v₀ (eⁿ).\n\nHeight after \'n\' bounces is hₙ = h₀ (e²ⁿ).',NULL,NULL,'e is the coefficient of restitution. Note that Energy and Height scale by e², while Velocity scales by e.','["physics", "work-energy-power", "collisions", "shortcuts"]','medium','Neet Buddy',14,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('2312c2a4-89a7-4590-98e1-148fbf5a8965','b378e83a-7b4d-4807-b411-23047a63e134','In a vertical circle, what happens if the velocity at the lowest point (v$_L$) is between √(2gr) and √(5gr)?','The body will leave the circular path (string slacks) before reaching the top, acting as a projectile.',NULL,NULL,'If v_L ≤ √(2gr), the body merely oscillates in the lower half of the circle like a pendulum.','["physics", "work-energy-power", "vertical-circle"]','medium','Neet Buddy',15,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('d0abaec2-f5d1-4f50-9b2a-29c3c3410bab','b378e83a-7b4d-4807-b411-23047a63e134','Define the Coefficient of Restitution (e).','e = (Relative Velocity of Separation after collision) / (Relative Velocity of Approach before collision).\ne = (v$_2$ - v$_1​$) / (u$_1$ - u$_2$).',NULL,NULL,'e = 1 (Elastic), e = 0 (Perfectly Inelastic).','["physics", "work-energy-power", "collisions"]','medium','Neet Buddy',16,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('7ae38c13-4c8d-4a7d-b7eb-0dc7a731a7b3','b378e83a-7b4d-4807-b411-23047a63e134','What happens in a 1D Elastic Collision if the two masses are EQUAL (m$_1$ = m$_2$)?','The bodies simply exchange their velocities! v$_1$ = u$_2$ and v$_2$ = u$_1$.',NULL,NULL,'Highly tested shortcut in NEET. If a moving billiard ball hits an identical stationary one head-on, it stops, and the second one moves with the original velocity.','["physics", "work-energy-power", "collisions", "shortcuts"]','medium','Neet Buddy',17,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('a7117d0f-86e3-4964-8a29-0edd9559b085','b378e83a-7b4d-4807-b411-23047a63e134','State the Work-Energy Theorem.','The net work done by ALL forces (conservative, non-conservative, external) on a particle equals its change in kinetic energy:\n\nWₙₑₜ = ΔK = Kբᵢₙₐₗ − Kᵢₙᵢₜᵢₐₗ',NULL,NULL,'This is the most powerful tool in mechanics! Use it when time is not given.','["physics", "work-energy-power", "energy", "neet-high-yield"]','medium','Neet Buddy',18,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('bff9424c-e721-4293-ab26-bbcd0bd51220','b378e83a-7b4d-4807-b411-23047a63e134','What is a Perfectly Inelastic Collision?','A collision where the two bodies stick together after impact and move with a common final velocity (v). Maximum kinetic energy is lost. e = 0.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "A collision where the two bodies stick together after impact and move with a common final velocity (v). Maximum kinetic energy is lost. e = 0."}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg width=\\"900\\" height=\\"220\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 900 220\\" xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\">\\n  <defs>\\n    <marker id=\\"arrow\\" markerWidth=\\"10\\" markerHeight=\\"10\\" \\n            refX=\\"8\\" refY=\\"3\\" orient=\\"auto\\" markerUnits=\\"strokeWidth\\">\\n      <path d=\\"M0,0 L0,6 L9,3 z\\" fill=\\"black\\"/>\\n    </marker>\\n\\n    <style>\\n      .label {\\n        font-family: \\"Times New Roman\\", serif;\\n        font-size: 26px;\\n        font-style: italic;\\n      }\\n      .mass {\\n        fill: white;\\n        font-family: \\"Times New Roman\\", serif;\\n        font-size: 28px;\\n        font-style: italic;\\n        text-anchor: middle;\\n        dominant-baseline: middle;\\n      }\\n      .velocity {\\n        font-family: \\"Times New Roman\\", serif;\\n        font-size: 24px;\\n        font-style: italic;\\n      }\\n    </style>\\n  </defs>\\n\\n  <!-- m1 -->\\n  <circle cx=\\"100\\" cy=\\"120\\" r=\\"45\\" fill=\\"#0B3DFF\\"/>\\n  <text x=\\"100\\" y=\\"120\\" class=\\"mass\\">\\n    m<tspan baseline-shift=\\"sub\\" font-size=\\"18\\">1</tspan>\\n  </text>\\n\\n  <!-- velocity u1 -->\\n  <line x1=\\"150\\" y1=\\"120\\" x2=\\"260\\" y2=\\"120\\"\\n        stroke=\\"black\\" stroke-width=\\"3\\"\\n        marker-end=\\"url(#arrow)\\"/>\\n  <text x=\\"185\\" y=\\"95\\" class=\\"velocity\\">\\n    u<tspan baseline-shift=\\"sub\\" font-size=\\"16\\">1</tspan>\\n  </text>\\n\\n  <!-- m2 -->\\n  <circle cx=\\"360\\" cy=\\"120\\" r=\\"45\\" fill=\\"#FF1A1A\\"/>\\n  <text x=\\"360\\" y=\\"120\\" class=\\"mass\\">\\n    m<tspan baseline-shift=\\"sub\\" font-size=\\"18\\">2</tspan>\\n  </text>\\n\\n  <!-- collision arrow -->\\n  <line x1=\\"430\\" y1=\\"120\\" x2=\\"560\\" y2=\\"120\\"\\n        stroke=\\"black\\" stroke-width=\\"3\\"\\n        marker-end=\\"url(#arrow)\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- combined mass -->\\n  <circle cx=\\"670\\" cy=\\"120\\" r=\\"45\\" fill=\\"#800080\\"/>\\n  <circle cx=\\"740\\" cy=\\"120\\" r=\\"45\\" fill=\\"#800080\\"/>\\n\\n  <text x=\\"705\\" y=\\"120\\" class=\\"mass\\" font-size=\\"24\\">\\n    m<tspan baseline-shift=\\"sub\\" font-size=\\"16\\">1</tspan>\\n    +\\n    m<tspan baseline-shift=\\"sub\\" font-size=\\"16\\">2</tspan>\\n  </text>\\n\\n  <!-- final velocity -->\\n  <line x1=\\"785\\" y1=\\"120\\" x2=\\"870\\" y2=\\"120\\"\\n        stroke=\\"black\\" stroke-width=\\"3\\"\\n        marker-end=\\"url(#arrow)\\"/>\\n  <text x=\\"820\\" y=\\"95\\" class=\\"velocity\\">v</text>\\n</svg>"}]}','Common velocity v = (m$_1$u$_1$ + m$_2$u$_2$) / (m$_1$ + m$_2$).','["physics", "work-energy-power", "collisions"]','medium','Neet Buddy',19,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('df70f071-45bd-49dd-88d0-17b386c7f6c7','c27771fd-0a32-47f0-966c-d6ff68c24ba8','What is the formula for the Center of Mass ($R_{cm}$) of a discrete multi-particle system?','$R_{cm} = \\frac{m_1 r_1 + m_2 r_2 + ... + m_n r_n}{m_1 + m_2 + ... + m_n} = \\frac{\\sum m_i r_i}{\\sum m_i}$',NULL,NULL,'The position of the CoM is independent of the coordinate system chosen, but its coordinates depend on the origin.','["physics", "rotational-motion", "center-of-mass", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',0,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('2ccfb807-b92d-435b-9380-98469891b446','c27771fd-0a32-47f0-966c-d6ff68c24ba8','How do you calculate the Center of Mass of a body with a portion removed (Cavity Problems)?','$x_{cm} = \\frac{A_1 x_1 - A_2 x_2}{A_1 - A_2}$ \nWhere $A_1$ is the area/volume of the complete body and $A_2$ is the area/volume of the removed part.',NULL,NULL,'Treat the removed portion as a \'negative mass\'.','["physics", "rotational-motion", "center-of-mass", "shortcuts"]','medium','Neet Buddy',1,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('6265e529-b59b-4bab-8030-e5ccd112dc72','c27771fd-0a32-47f0-966c-d6ff68c24ba8','What happens to the trajectory of the Center of Mass of a projectile (like a firecracker) if it explodes in mid-air?','The Center of Mass continues to move along the original parabolic trajectory. Internal forces of explosion do not affect the motion of the CoM.',NULL,NULL,'Only external forces ($F_{ext}$) can change the acceleration of the CoM.','["physics", "rotational-motion", "center-of-mass"]','medium','Neet Buddy',2,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('67f634bc-c577-4a09-8273-d2966c46e7c9','c27771fd-0a32-47f0-966c-d6ff68c24ba8','What is the vector relation between linear velocity ($v$) and angular velocity ($\\omega$)?','$\\vec{v} = \\vec{\\omega} \\times \\vec{r}$',NULL,NULL,'Order matters! It is NOT $\\vec{r} \\times \\vec{\\omega}$.','["physics", "rotational-motion", "kinematics", "vectors"]','medium','Neet Buddy',3,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('44f90be1-d443-49da-9323-ad112e0fd365','c27771fd-0a32-47f0-966c-d6ff68c24ba8','Define Torque ($\\tau$) and state its vector formula.','Torque is the rotational analogue of force (moment of force). \n$\\vec{\\tau} = \\vec{r} \\times \\vec{F}$ \nMagnitude: $\\tau = r F \\sin(\\theta)$',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "$\\\\vec{\\\\tau} = \\\\vec{r} \\\\times \\\\vec{F}$"}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg viewBox=\'0 0 200 120\' width=\'100%\'><circle cx=\'30\' cy=\'60\' r=\'5\' fill=\'black\'/><line x1=\'30\' y1=\'60\' x2=\'120\' y2=\'60\' stroke=\'black\' stroke-width=\'2\' marker-end=\'url(#arrow)\'/><text x=\'70\' y=\'50\' font-size=\'12\'>r</text><line x1=\'120\' y1=\'60\' x2=\'160\' y2=\'20\' stroke=\'red\' stroke-width=\'2\' marker-end=\'url(#arrow)\'/><text x=\'165\' y=\'25\' font-size=\'12\' fill=\'red\'>F</text><path d=\'M 140 60 A 20 20 0 0 0 130 50\' fill=\'none\' stroke=\'blue\'/><text x=\'145\' y=\'55\' font-size=\'10\' fill=\'blue\'>θ</text><line x1=\'120\' y1=\'60\' x2=\'170\' y2=\'60\' stroke=\'gray\' stroke-dasharray=\'4\'/></svg>"}]}','Maximum torque occurs when the force is applied perpendicular to the position vector ($\\theta = 90^\\circ$).','["physics", "rotational-motion", "torque"]','medium','Neet Buddy',4,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('8a76a01c-fbf9-4977-998d-eec36e214130','c27771fd-0a32-47f0-966c-d6ff68c24ba8','Define Angular Momentum ($L$) for a particle and its relation to linear momentum ($p$).','Angular momentum is the moment of linear momentum. \n$\\vec{L} = \\vec{r} \\times \\vec{p}$ \nMagnitude: $L = mvr \\sin(\\theta)$',NULL,NULL,'For a rigid body rotating about a fixed axis, $L = I\\omega$.','["physics", "rotational-motion", "angular-momentum"]','medium','Neet Buddy',5,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('dde1577b-6b8e-429e-b34d-c66cd1b694d6','c27771fd-0a32-47f0-966c-d6ff68c24ba8','State the relationship between Torque and Angular Momentum (Newton\'s Second Law for Rotation).','The net external torque acting on a system is equal to the rate of change of its angular momentum. \n$\\vec{\\tau}_{ext} = \\frac{d\\vec{L}}{dt}$',NULL,NULL,'Analogous to $\\vec{F} = \\frac{d\\vec{p}}{dt}$.','["physics", "rotational-motion", "dynamics"]','medium','Neet Buddy',6,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('3900b380-deaa-4d4e-a271-9d86e80936ab','c27771fd-0a32-47f0-966c-d6ff68c24ba8','State the Law of Conservation of Angular Momentum.','If the net external torque on a system is zero ($\\tau_{ext} = 0$), the total angular momentum remains constant. \n$\\vec{L}_{initial} = \\vec{L}_{final}$ or $I_1\\omega_1 = I_2\\omega_2$.',NULL,NULL,'Example: An ice skater spinning faster when pulling their arms in (decreasing $I$ increases $\\omega$).','["physics", "rotational-motion", "conservation-laws", "neet-high-yield"]','medium','Neet Buddy',7,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('ed203136-688b-4c9c-8bea-06ef8b5ced3c','c27771fd-0a32-47f0-966c-d6ff68c24ba8','What are the two conditions for the Mechanical Equilibrium of a rigid body?','1. Translational Equilibrium: Net external force is zero ($\\sum \\vec{F} = 0$).\n2. Rotational Equilibrium: Net external torque is zero ($\\sum \\vec{\\tau} = 0$).',NULL,NULL,'A body can be in translational equilibrium but not rotational (e.g., a couple acting on a steering wheel).','["physics", "rotational-motion", "equilibrium"]','medium','Neet Buddy',8,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('3de65773-d36e-4095-a44e-0f3bb4a29d9e','c27771fd-0a32-47f0-966c-d6ff68c24ba8','Define Radius of Gyration ($k$).','The perpendicular distance from the axis of rotation to a point where the total mass of the body could be concentrated to have the same moment of inertia. \n$I = M k^2 \\implies k = \\sqrt{\\frac{I}{M}}$',NULL,NULL,'Depends on the axis of rotation and mass distribution, but NOT on the total mass itself.','["physics", "rotational-motion", "moment-of-inertia"]','medium','Neet Buddy',9,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('49558d0d-e61d-4af4-8768-f75b971d7abc','c27771fd-0a32-47f0-966c-d6ff68c24ba8','What is the Moment of Inertia of a uniform Ring and a Disc about an axis passing through the center and perpendicular to the plane?','Ring: $I = MR^2$ \nDisc: $I = \\frac{1}{2}MR^2$',NULL,NULL,'A ring has all its mass at distance R. A disc has its mass distributed from 0 to R, so its MoI is half.','["physics", "rotational-motion", "moment-of-inertia", "neet-high-yield"]','medium','Neet Buddy',10,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('a5dffe8a-8f49-4559-af68-3d0c06528eff','c27771fd-0a32-47f0-966c-d6ff68c24ba8','What is the Moment of Inertia of a Solid Sphere and a Hollow Sphere (Spherical Shell) about their diameters?','Solid Sphere: $I = \\frac{2}{5}MR^2$ \nGiven Shell (Hollow): $I = \\frac{2}{3}MR^2$',NULL,NULL,'Hollow sphere has its mass concentrated further away from the center, hence $2/3 > 2/5$.','["physics", "rotational-motion", "moment-of-inertia", "neet-high-yield"]','medium','Neet Buddy',11,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('fb1865d2-02a0-466a-b3fd-d8e6bfb693c3','c27771fd-0a32-47f0-966c-d6ff68c24ba8','What is the Moment of Inertia of a uniform rod of length $L$ and mass $M$ about its Center and about its End?','About Center: $I = \\frac{ML^2}{12}$ \nAbout End: $I = \\frac{ML^2}{3}$',NULL,NULL,'Use Parallel Axis Theorem to verify: $I_{end} = \\frac{ML^2}{12} + M(\\frac{L}{2})^2 = \\frac{ML^2}{3}$.','["physics", "rotational-motion", "moment-of-inertia"]','medium','Neet Buddy',12,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('580614ca-3358-44d3-b7d1-557afac5f01c','c27771fd-0a32-47f0-966c-d6ff68c24ba8','Write the formula for Rotational Kinetic Energy and the Work-Energy Theorem for rotational motion.','Rotational K.E. = $\\frac{1}{2}I\\omega^2$. \nWork-Energy Theorem: The work done by net torque equals the change in rotational kinetic energy. \n$W = \\frac{1}{2}I\\omega_f^2 - \\frac{1}{2}I\\omega_i^2$',NULL,NULL,'Analogous to $W = \\Delta K = \\frac{1}{2}mv_f^2 - \\frac{1}{2}mv_i^2$.','["physics", "rotational-motion", "work-energy"]','medium','Neet Buddy',13,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('6ca6eeea-dfca-4d62-846a-a03bfc2bf414','c27771fd-0a32-47f0-966c-d6ff68c24ba8','What is the kinematic condition for \'Pure Rolling\' (rolling without slipping) on a stationary surface?','$v_{cm} = R\\omega$ \nThe velocity of the point of contact with respect to the surface must be ZERO.',NULL,NULL,'If $v_{cm} > R\\omega$, it is forward slipping (skidding). If $R\\omega > v_{cm}$, it is backward slipping (spinning).','["physics", "rotational-motion", "rolling"]','medium','Neet Buddy',14,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('cc9901ca-3a25-4e1e-8b32-db50acc559cf','c27771fd-0a32-47f0-966c-d6ff68c24ba8','What is the Total Kinetic Energy of a body undergoing pure rolling motion?','$K_{total} = K_{translational} + K_{rotational}$ \n$K_{total} = \\frac{1}{2}Mv_{cm}^2 + \\frac{1}{2}I\\omega^2$ \nUsing $I = Mk^2$ and $\\omega = v/R$, it simplifies to: $K_{total} = \\frac{1}{2}Mv_{cm}^2(1 + \\frac{k^2}{R^2})$',NULL,NULL,'The term $(1 + \\frac{k^2}{R^2})$ is the rolling factor. Always find $k^2/R^2$ first for rolling problems.','["physics", "rotational-motion", "rolling", "neet-high-yield"]','medium','Neet Buddy',15,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('6a1827c7-852d-449a-b73e-bad9cf236914','c27771fd-0a32-47f0-966c-d6ff68c24ba8','What is the acceleration ($a$) of a symmetric body rolling down an inclined plane of angle $\\theta$?','$a = \\frac{g \\sin\\theta}{1 + \\frac{k^2}{R^2}}$',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "$a = \\\\frac{g \\\\sin\\\\theta}{1 + \\\\frac{k^2}{R^2}}$"}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg viewBox=\'0 0 200 120\' width=\'100%\'><polygon points=\'20,100 160,100 20,40\' fill=\'lightgray\' stroke=\'black\'/><circle cx=\'80\' cy=\'60\' r=\'15\' fill=\'none\' stroke=\'blue\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><circle cx=\'80\' cy=\'60\' r=\'2\' fill=\'black\'/><line x1=\'80\' y1=\'60\' x2=\'120\' y2=\'77\' stroke=\'red\' stroke-width=\'2\' marker-end=\'url(#arrow)\'/><text x=\'110\' y=\'95\' font-size=\'12\' fill=\'red\'>a</text><path d=\'M 130 100 A 30 30 0 0 0 115 80\' fill=\'none\' stroke=\'green\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><text x=\'135\' y=\'90\' font-size=\'12\' fill=\'green\'>θ</text><path d=\'M 70 45 A 20 20 0 0 1 95 55\' fill=\'none\' stroke=\'orange\' marker-end=\'url(#arrow)\'/><text x=\'95\' y=\'45\' font-size=\'10\' fill=\'orange\'>ω</text></svg>"}]}','Since $(1 + k^2/R^2) > 1$, the rolling acceleration is always less than the sliding acceleration ($g \\sin\\theta$).','["physics", "rotational-motion", "rolling", "neet-high-yield"]','medium','Neet Buddy',16,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('7d565d6a-31c1-47d0-9d79-d295dba25ccd','c27771fd-0a32-47f0-966c-d6ff68c24ba8','What is the velocity ($v$) of a body at the bottom of an inclined plane of height $h$ after pure rolling from rest?','$v = \\sqrt{\\frac{2gh}{1 + \\frac{k^2}{R^2}}}$',NULL,NULL,'Derived directly from conservation of mechanical energy: $mgh = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2(1 + \\frac{k^2}{R^2})$.','["physics", "rotational-motion", "rolling", "energy"]','medium','Neet Buddy',17,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('fac9863b-bb4e-4319-962f-da1aa2629ab8','c27771fd-0a32-47f0-966c-d6ff68c24ba8','State the Theorem of Perpendicular Axes.','The moment of inertia of a planar body about an axis perpendicular to its plane is equal to the sum of its MoI about two mutually perpendicular axes lying in its plane. \n$I_z = I_x + I_y$',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "The moment of inertia of a planar body about an axis perpendicular to its plane is equal to the sum of its MoI about two mutually perpendicular axes lying in its plane. \\n$I_z = I_x + I_y$"}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\" xmlns:xlink=\\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\\" width=\\"770\\" height=\\"653\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 770 653\\">\\n  <image xlink:href=\\"data:image/png;base64,\\" x=\\"0\\" y=\\"0\\" width=\\"770\\" height=\\"653\\"/>\\n</svg>"}]}','Applicable ONLY to 2D Planar bodies (lamina, ring, disc).','["physics", "rotational-motion", "theorems"]','medium','Neet Buddy',18,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('7667187f-2a60-4dca-a756-16773c56f4db','c27771fd-0a32-47f0-966c-d6ff68c24ba8','If a Solid Sphere, a Disc, a Solid Cylinder, and a Ring are released simultaneously from the top of an incline, in what order do they reach the bottom?','1st: Solid Sphere ($k^2/R^2 = 0.4$) \n2nd: Disc / Solid Cylinder ($k^2/R^2 = 0.5$) \nLast: Ring / Hollow Cylinder ($k^2/R^2 = 1.0$)',NULL,NULL,'The body with the LOWEST Moment of Inertia (lowest $k^2/R^2$) has the highest acceleration and reaches the bottom FIRST.','["physics", "rotational-motion", "rolling", "shortcuts"]','medium','Neet Buddy',19,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('2b2f7190-d50d-4af7-a1fe-92b308ed1eba','c27771fd-0a32-47f0-966c-d6ff68c24ba8','What is the minimum coefficient of static friction ($\\mu_s$) required for pure rolling down an inclined plane of angle $\\theta$?','$\\mu_s \\ge \\frac{\\tan\\theta}{1 + \\frac{R^2}{k^2}}$',NULL,NULL,'Note the denominator is $R^2/k^2$, NOT $k^2/R^2$. If $\\mu$ is less than this, the body will roll with slipping.','["physics", "rotational-motion", "rolling", "friction"]','medium','Neet Buddy',20,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('37b5e5d4-e846-4443-8d66-79e599866b8e','c27771fd-0a32-47f0-966c-d6ff68c24ba8','State the Theorem of Parallel Axes.','The moment of inertia ($I$) about any axis is equal to the MoI about a parallel axis passing through the Center of Mass ($I_{cm}$) plus the product of mass ($M$) and the square of the distance ($d$) between them. \n$I = I_{cm} + M d^2$',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "The moment of inertia ($I$) about any axis is equal to the MoI about a parallel axis passing through the Center of Mass ($I_{cm}$) plus the product of mass ($M$) and the square of the distance ($d$) between them. \\n$I = I_{cm} + M d^2$"}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\" xmlns:xlink=\\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\\" width=\\"693\\" height=\\"693\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 693 693\\">\\n  <image xlink:href=\\"data:image/png;base64,\\" x=\\"0\\" y=\\"0\\" width=\\"693\\" height=\\"693\\"/>\\n</svg>"}]}','Applicable to ALL rigid bodies (1D, 2D, 3D).','["physics", "rotational-motion", "theorems", "neet-high-yield"]','medium','Neet Buddy',21,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('91e9501f-395b-4a47-8c51-2d344491d28e','c7b695fa-6019-49eb-a34c-c15bd4224eec','What are the \'defining properties\' of living organisms versus \'characteristic features\'?','Defining properties (with no exceptions) are Metabolism, Cellular organization, and Consciousness. Characteristic features (have exceptions) are Growth and Reproduction.',NULL,NULL,'Think about mules (cannot reproduce) or mountains (can \'grow\' by accumulation).','["biology", "the-living-world", "class-11", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',0,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('ca7df90d-877f-476b-9b6f-f0ae5ee782e6','d65afa3c-7664-4e36-b666-09606bf85bef','State the de Broglie wavelength equation relating to the dual nature of matter.','$\\lambda = \\frac{h}{mv} = \\frac{h}{p}$',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "It can also be written in terms of Kinetic Energy ($K$): $\\\\lambda = \\\\frac{h}{\\\\sqrt{2mK}}$"}]}','Wavelength depends inversely on momentum.','["chemistry", "atomic-structure", "quantum-mechanics"]','medium','Neet Buddy',6,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('4e3accf1-b018-4216-9ff9-34ff0bbaf23e','d65afa3c-7664-4e36-b666-09606bf85bef','State the Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle.','It is impossible to determine simultaneously, the exact position and exact momentum (or velocity) of a microscopic particle.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "$\\\\Delta x \\\\cdot \\\\Delta p \\\\ge \\\\frac{h}{4\\\\pi}$ \\nOR \\n$\\\\Delta x \\\\cdot \\\\Delta v \\\\ge \\\\frac{h}{4\\\\pi m}$"}]}','Delta x times Delta p...','["chemistry", "atomic-structure", "quantum-mechanics"]','medium','Neet Buddy',7,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('3cf1e48e-cf4d-4484-8ccd-7e7db65d7c14','c7b695fa-6019-49eb-a34c-c15bd4224eec','What is the correct sequence of the Taxonomical Hierarchy from broadest to most specific?','Kingdom → Phylum (or Division for plants) → Class → Order → Family → Genus → Species.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "svg", "value": "<svg viewBox=\\"0 0 200 220\\" xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\"><polygon points=\\"100,10 190,190 10,190\\" fill=\\"#e8f4f8\\" stroke=\\"#2c3e50\\" stroke-width=\\"2\\"/><line x1=\\"88\\" y1=\\"35\\" x2=\\"112\\" y2=\\"35\\" stroke=\\"#2c3e50\\"/><text x=\\"100\\" y=\\"30\\" font-size=\\"12\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\" font-family=\\"sans-serif\\">Kingdom</text><line x1=\\"75\\" y1=\\"60\\" x2=\\"125\\" y2=\\"60\\" stroke=\\"#2c3e50\\"/><text x=\\"100\\" y=\\"55\\" font-size=\\"12\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\" font-family=\\"sans-serif\\">Phylum</text><line x1=\\"62\\" y1=\\"85\\" x2=\\"138\\" y2=\\"85\\" stroke=\\"#2c3e50\\"/><text x=\\"100\\" y=\\"80\\" font-size=\\"12\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\" font-family=\\"sans-serif\\">Class</text><line x1=\\"50\\" y1=\\"110\\" x2=\\"150\\" y2=\\"110\\" stroke=\\"#2c3e50\\"/><text x=\\"100\\" y=\\"105\\" font-size=\\"12\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\" font-family=\\"sans-serif\\">Order</text><line x1=\\"38\\" y1=\\"135\\" x2=\\"162\\" y2=\\"135\\" stroke=\\"#2c3e50\\"/><text x=\\"100\\" y=\\"130\\" font-size=\\"12\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\" font-family=\\"sans-serif\\">Family</text><line x1=\\"25\\" y1=\\"160\\" x2=\\"175\\" y2=\\"160\\" stroke=\\"#2c3e50\\"/><text x=\\"100\\" y=\\"155\\" font-size=\\"12\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\" font-family=\\"sans-serif\\">Genus</text><text x=\\"100\\" y=\\"180\\" font-size=\\"12\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\" font-family=\\"sans-serif\\">Species</text></svg>"}]}','Mnemonic: Keep Pots Clean Or Family Gets Sick','["biology", "taxonomy", "class-11", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',1,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('adcdf420-0fa0-4814-9bbb-e21c89f5687d','c7b695fa-6019-49eb-a34c-c15bd4224eec','State the universal rules of Binomial Nomenclature.','1. Biological names are usually in Latin and written in italics. 2. First word is Genus (capitalized), second is specific epithet (lowercase). 3. When handwritten, both words are separately underlined.',NULL,NULL,'Introduced by Carolus Linnaeus.','["biology", "taxonomy", "class-11", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',2,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('f50cc699-da00-4bfb-924b-83c23cd086ad','c7b695fa-6019-49eb-a34c-c15bd4224eec','What were the main criteria used by R.H. Whittaker for his Five Kingdom Classification?','Cell structure, body organization (thallus), mode of nutrition, reproduction, and phylogenetic relationships.',NULL,NULL,'There are 5 main criteria for the 5 kingdoms.','["biology", "biological-classification", "class-11", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',3,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('d86f1c42-a3a7-4554-9a2a-8aee9c7b1f89','c7b695fa-6019-49eb-a34c-c15bd4224eec','How do Archaebacteria differ from Eubacteria to survive in extreme conditions?','Archaebacteria have a different cell wall structure (lack peptidoglycan, have branched-chain lipids in the membrane) which is responsible for their survival in extreme environments.',NULL,NULL,'Think methanogens, halophiles, and thermoacidophiles.','["biology", "monera", "class-11", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',4,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('2139b9ad-5eaa-42c8-a1a3-5c3f3fb6b42b','c7b695fa-6019-49eb-a34c-c15bd4224eec','What are the characteristic features of Diatoms (Chrysophytes)?','They have indestructible cell walls embedded with silica forming two overlapping shells (like a soapbox). They are the chief producers in the oceans and form \'Diatomaceous earth\'.',NULL,NULL,'Belong to Kingdom Protista.','["biology", "protista", "class-11", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',5,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('b7b7edcc-c037-45c7-96fc-137c8a862a85','c7b695fa-6019-49eb-a34c-c15bd4224eec','Name the four major classes of Fungi and their defining sexual spores.','1. Phycomycetes (Zygospores)\n2. Ascomycetes (Ascospores - endogenous)\n3. Basidiomycetes (Basidiospores - exogenous)\n4. Deuteromycetes (Sexual reproduction absent/unknown).',NULL,NULL,'Sac fungi, Club fungi, Fungi imperfecti.','["biology", "fungi", "class-11", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',6,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('8b5f8e80-46e9-4950-9f83-87284f5a0e90','c7b695fa-6019-49eb-a34c-c15bd4224eec','Differentiate between Viruses, Viroids, and Prions based on their infectious agents.','Viruses: Nucleoprotein (DNA or RNA + protein coat).\nViroids: Free, infectious, low molecular weight RNA (lacks protein coat).\nPrions: Abnormally folded infectious proteins (lacks nucleic acid).',NULL,NULL,'Viroids cause Potato Spindle Tuber disease; Prions cause Mad Cow disease.','["biology", "viruses", "class-11", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',7,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('05c8e56d-e8d0-493c-8d1f-cfdff30ca233','c7b695fa-6019-49eb-a34c-c15bd4224eec','What are Lichens, and what are their two components called?','Lichens are a symbiotic (mutually useful) association between algae and fungi. The algal component is \'phycobiont\' (autotrophic) and the fungal component is \'mycobiont\' (heterotrophic).',NULL,NULL,'Excellent pollution indicators (do not grow in SO2 polluted areas).','["biology", "lichens", "class-11", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',8,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('e255f4c3-1029-479d-8a1f-a3e1cc617101','c7b695fa-6019-49eb-a34c-c15bd4224eec','Compare the major photosynthetic pigments and stored food in the three classes of Algae.','Green Algae (Chlorophyceae): Chl a, b; Starch.\nBrown Algae (Phaeophyceae): Chl a, c, Fucoxanthin; Mannitol, Laminarin.\nRed Algae (Rhodophyceae): Chl a, d, Phycoerythrin; Floridean starch.',NULL,NULL,'Floridean starch is structurally similar to amylopectin and glycogen.','["biology", "plant-kingdom", "algae", "class-11", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',9,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('265e6bf2-beaf-4dfe-80ad-113427ec8eee','c7b695fa-6019-49eb-a34c-c15bd4224eec','Why are Bryophytes called the \'amphibians of the plant kingdom\'?','Because these plants can live in soil but are strictly dependent on water for sexual reproduction (swimming of flagellated antherozoids to the archegonium).',NULL,NULL,'Includes mosses and liverworts. Main plant body is haploid (gametophyte).','["biology", "plant-kingdom", "bryophytes", "class-11", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',10,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('be977fb9-2bd7-45cd-902b-bf5506e71dc1','c7b695fa-6019-49eb-a34c-c15bd4224eec','What are the salient features of Pteridophytes?','They are the first terrestrial plants to possess vascular tissues (xylem and phloem). The main plant body is a sporophyte (diploid) which is differentiated into true root, stem, and leaves.',NULL,NULL,'Examples: Ferns, Selaginella, Equisetum.','["biology", "plant-kingdom", "pteridophytes", "class-11", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',11,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('5d1e6476-687c-43af-b8fa-5d4438474dee','c7b695fa-6019-49eb-a34c-c15bd4224eec','What does the term \'Gymnosperm\' mean, and what is the ploidy of their endosperm?','Gymnosperm means \'naked seeds\' (ovules are not enclosed by any ovary wall). Their endosperm is haploid (n) because it forms before fertilization.',NULL,NULL,'Examples: Cycas (coralloid roots), Pinus (mycorrhiza).','["biology", "plant-kingdom", "gymnosperms", "class-11", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',12,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('610482bf-a4da-4b1b-8d9e-2b21aa251cc6','c7b695fa-6019-49eb-a34c-c15bd4224eec','Explain the terms Acoelomate, Pseudocoelomate, and Coelomate with one phylum example for each.','Acoelomate: No body cavity (e.g., Platyhelminthes).\nPseudocoelomate: Body cavity is not lined by mesoderm; mesoderm is in scattered pouches (e.g., Aschelminthes).\nCoelomate: True body cavity lined by mesoderm (e.g., Annelida to Chordata).',NULL,NULL,'Coelom is the space between the body wall and gut wall.','["biology", "animal-kingdom", "class-11", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',13,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('d8d58d0e-9520-49d7-bbe7-e2b4f9b8d235','c7b695fa-6019-49eb-a34c-c15bd4224eec','What is the specialized cell found only in Porifera (sponges) that lines the spongocoel and canals?','Choanocytes (collar cells). They are flagellated and help in maintaining the water flow through the canal system.',NULL,NULL,'Essential for food gathering, respiratory exchange, and waste removal.','["biology", "animal-kingdom", "porifera", "class-11", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',14,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('acd2fa33-2d4a-49f3-885b-d6c675b4201c','c7b695fa-6019-49eb-a34c-c15bd4224eec','What is \'Metagenesis\' and in which phylum is it observed?','Metagenesis is the alternation of generation between asexual polyps and sexual medusae. It is observed in Phylum Cnidaria (e.g., Obelia).',NULL,NULL,'Polyp produces medusa asexually; Medusa produces polyp sexually.','["biology", "animal-kingdom", "cnidaria", "class-11", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',15,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('949bdc50-d909-42ba-90da-58410102ac52','c7b695fa-6019-49eb-a34c-c15bd4224eec','Name the specialized excretory and osmoregulatory structures in Platyhelminthes and Aschelminthes.','Platyhelminthes (Flatworms): Flame cells.\nAschelminthes (Roundworms): Renette cells / Excretory tube with an excretory pore.',NULL,NULL,'Flatworms are acoelomate; roundworms are pseudocoelomate.','["biology", "animal-kingdom", "class-11", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',16,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('62635aa6-32b8-4731-95ce-9391d3451bec','c7b695fa-6019-49eb-a34c-c15bd4224eec','What are the defining characteristics of Phylum Arthropoda (the largest phylum)?','They have jointed appendages, a chitinous exoskeleton, a body divided into head, thorax, and abdomen, and an open circulatory system.',NULL,NULL,'Arthro = joint, poda = appendages.','["biology", "animal-kingdom", "arthropoda", "class-11", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',17,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('c51a15fb-e7e0-4149-9967-2e3ce71628dc','c7b695fa-6019-49eb-a34c-c15bd4224eec','What is unique about the symmetry in Phylum Echinodermata?','Adult echinoderms exhibit radial symmetry (usually pentamerous), while their larvae exhibit bilateral symmetry.',NULL,NULL,'They also feature a unique water vascular system. Example: Starfish.','["biology", "animal-kingdom", "echinodermata", "class-11", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',18,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('ff973006-1001-4047-9abd-a07a5ae3e4e8','c7b695fa-6019-49eb-a34c-c15bd4224eec','Differentiate between Chondrichthyes and Osteichthyes based on skeleton, gills, and swim bladder.','Chondrichthyes (Cartilaginous fish): Cartilaginous endoskeleton, gills lack an operculum (cover), no swim bladder (must swim constantly).\nOsteichthyes (Bony fish): Bony endoskeleton, gills covered by operculum, swim bladder present (provides buoyancy).',NULL,NULL,'Shark vs. Rohu.','["biology", "animal-kingdom", "vertebrata", "class-11", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',19,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('76e0d7c6-e380-4a5a-b7e2-544633f65d2d','c7b695fa-6019-49eb-a34c-c15bd4224eec','How do the heart chambers vary across the classes of Tetrapods?','Amphibians: 3-chambered (2 atria, 1 ventricle).\nReptiles: 3-chambered (except Crocodiles, which have 4).\nAves (Birds) & Mammals: 4-chambered (2 atria, 2 ventricles).',NULL,NULL,'Evolution drives separation of oxygenated and deoxygenated blood.','["biology", "animal-kingdom", "vertebrata", "class-11", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',20,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('817f94df-79b4-4d86-8aad-dd2431e67292','c7b695fa-6019-49eb-a34c-c15bd4224eec','What are the four fundamental diagnostic characters of Chordates?','1. Presence of a Notochord\n2. A dorsal hollow nerve cord\n3. Paired pharyngeal gill slits\n4. A post-anal tail.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\" xmlns:xlink=\\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\\" width=\\"1344\\" height=\\"647\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 1344 647\\">\\n  <image xlink:href=\\"data:image/png;base64,\\" x=\\"0\\" y=\\"0\\" width=\\"1344\\" height=\\"647\\"/>\\n</svg>"}]}','Found in at least some stage of their embryonic development.','["biology", "animal-kingdom", "chordata", "class-11", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',21,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('d3af9ed3-eeb5-4ce9-835b-e880e681a1e1','c7c037a9-ec12-44c4-865a-c760dfe5fa0e','State the three most critical assumptions of the Kinetic Theory of Gases regarding volume, forces, and collisions.','1. Volume of individual molecules is negligible compared to the total volume of the gas.\n2. There are NO intermolecular forces of attraction (except during collisions).\n3. All collisions (between molecules, and with the walls) are perfectly elastic.',NULL,NULL,'These assumptions explain why internal energy is entirely kinetic.','["physics", "kinetic-theory", "assumptions"]','medium','Neet Buddy',0,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('58db61e8-6784-4961-b27d-0f0c81635634','c7c037a9-ec12-44c4-865a-c760dfe5fa0e','What is Avogadro\'s number ($N_A$) and how does it relate the Universal Gas Constant (R) to the Boltzmann Constant ($k_B$)?','$N_A$ = 6.022 × 10²³ mol⁻¹ (Number of molecules in one mole). \nRelation: R = $N_A$ × $k_B$',NULL,NULL,'k_B is essentially the gas constant per molecule.','["physics", "kinetic-theory", "constants"]','medium','Neet Buddy',1,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('22118ee7-93b7-47dc-99ae-0bc00fd5ce21','c7c037a9-ec12-44c4-865a-c760dfe5fa0e','Write the Ideal Gas Equation in terms of both the number of moles (n) and the total number of molecules (N).','1. In terms of moles: PV = nRT \n2. In terms of molecules: PV = N $k_B$ T',NULL,NULL,'R is the Universal Gas Constant. k_B is the Boltzmann Constant.','["physics", "kinetic-theory", "ideal-gas", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',2,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('2d91b2a9-83a0-44dc-9975-27f988e12742','c7c037a9-ec12-44c4-865a-c760dfe5fa0e','Relate the average translational kinetic energy of a single gas molecule (Eₐᵥ$_g$) to its absolute temperature (T).','Eₐᵥ$_g$ = (3/2) $k_B$ T',NULL,NULL,'This is the Kinetic Interpretation of Temperature. Temperature is a direct measure of average molecular kinetic energy.','["physics", "kinetic-theory", "temperature", "energy"]','medium','Neet Buddy',3,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('7cef6fbf-63a3-4ab0-9d7c-8f6d62522887','c7c037a9-ec12-44c4-865a-c760dfe5fa0e','What are the Degrees of Freedom (f) for a Monoatomic gas (like He, Ne)?','f = 3 \n(They only possess 3 translational degrees of freedom along x, y, and z axes).',NULL,NULL,'They cannot rotate or vibrate effectively to store energy.','["physics", "kinetic-theory", "degrees-of-freedom"]','medium','Neet Buddy',4,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('2d33dcd4-2eb8-463c-9f45-e05bbee99ce4','c7c037a9-ec12-44c4-865a-c760dfe5fa0e','What are the Degrees of Freedom (f) for a rigid Diatomic gas (like O₂, N₂) at normal temperatures?','f = 5 \n(3 translational + 2 rotational degrees of freedom).',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "f = 5 (3 Translational + 2 Rotational). Rotation along its own bond axis has negligible moment of inertia."}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg viewBox=\'0 0 200 120\' width=\'100%\'><line x1=\'50\' y1=\'60\' x2=\'150\' y2=\'60\' stroke=\'black\' stroke-width=\'4\'/><circle cx=\'50\' cy=\'60\' r=\'20\' fill=\'red\'/><circle cx=\'150\' cy=\'60\' r=\'20\' fill=\'red\'/><path d=\'M 100 20 A 40 40 0 0 1 120 40\' fill=\'none\' stroke=\'green\' stroke-width=\'2\' marker-end=\'url(#arrow)\'/><text x=\'125\' y=\'30\' font-size=\'10\' fill=\'green\'>Rotation 1</text><path d=\'M 90 90 A 30 15 0 0 0 110 110\' fill=\'none\' stroke=\'blue\' stroke-width=\'2\' marker-end=\'url(#arrow)\'/><text x=\'115\' y=\'110\' font-size=\'10\' fill=\'blue\'>Rotation 2</text></svg>"}]}','At very high temperatures, non-rigid diatomic gases add 2 vibrational degrees of freedom, making f = 7.','["physics", "kinetic-theory", "degrees-of-freedom"]','medium','Neet Buddy',5,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('206fcb0e-3248-44d4-8c07-1ad18801f1e7','c7c037a9-ec12-44c4-865a-c760dfe5fa0e','Write the formula for the Total Internal Energy (U) of \'n\' moles of an ideal gas having \'f\' degrees of freedom.','U = (f/2) n R T',NULL,NULL,'Multiply the energy per mole per degree of freedom (1/2 RT) by \'f\' and \'n\'.','["physics", "kinetic-theory", "internal-energy", "formulas"]','medium','Neet Buddy',6,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('6562f6a2-e14c-4373-9ad7-fc3801084889','c7c037a9-ec12-44c4-865a-c760dfe5fa0e','What are the standard values of γ (ratio of specific heats) for Monoatomic and Diatomic gases?','Monoatomic (f=3): γ = 5/3 ≈ 1.67 \nDiatomic (f=5): γ = 7/5 = 1.4',NULL,NULL,'As atomicity increases (more degrees of freedom), the value of γ decreases towards 1.','["physics", "kinetic-theory", "specific-heat", "values"]','medium','Neet Buddy',7,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('61d7bd20-0be6-4561-9661-606043b2eb26','c7c037a9-ec12-44c4-865a-c760dfe5fa0e','How does the Mean Free Path (λ) of an ideal gas depend on its Temperature (T) and Pressure (P)?','λ ∝ T / P \n(Mean free path is directly proportional to Temperature and inversely proportional to Pressure).',NULL,NULL,'Substitute n = P / (k_B T) into the mean free path formula.','["physics", "kinetic-theory", "mean-free-path", "neet-high-yield"]','medium','Neet Buddy',8,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('3e375f33-fcd9-44f8-a010-b997b8c2f9fa','c7c037a9-ec12-44c4-865a-c760dfe5fa0e','Define Mean Free Path (λ) and provide its mathematical formula.','Mean free path is the average distance a gas molecule travels between two successive collisions. \nλ = 1 / (√2 π n d²)',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "Mean free path is the average distance a gas molecule travels between two successive collisions. \\nλ = 1 / (√2 π n d²)"}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\" xmlns:xlink=\\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\\" width=\\"1340\\" height=\\"625\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 1340 625\\">\\n  <image xlink:href=\\"data:image/png;base64,\\" x=\\"0\\" y=\\"0\\" width=\\"1340\\" height=\\"625\\"/>\\n</svg>"}]}','n = number density (molecules per unit volume), d = diameter of the molecule.','["physics", "kinetic-theory", "mean-free-path"]','medium','Neet Buddy',9,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929');
INSERT INTO `flashcards` (`id`, `deck_id`, `front`, `back`, `front_body`, `back_body`, `hint`, `tags`, `difficulty`, `source`, `position`, `created_at`) VALUES
('46871f18-b813-4c74-8804-76641b32fffa','c7c037a9-ec12-44c4-865a-c760dfe5fa0e','What is the formula for the Molar Specific Heat at Constant Volume ($C_v$) in terms of degrees of freedom (f)?','$C_v$ = (f/2) R',NULL,NULL,'Derived from the First Law: dQ = dU + dW. At constant volume, dW=0, so dQ = dU = n C_v dT.','["physics", "kinetic-theory", "specific-heat"]','medium','Neet Buddy',10,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('e3cc2eba-efef-441d-8489-6b99279a2998','c7c037a9-ec12-44c4-865a-c760dfe5fa0e','Write the formula for the Root Mean Square (RMS) speed of a gas.','vᵣₘₛ = √(3RT / M)  or  vᵣₘₛ = √(3$k_B$ T / m) \nWhere M is Molar Mass and m is the mass of a single molecule.',NULL,NULL,'RMS speed is directly proportional to √T and inversely proportional to √M.','["physics", "kinetic-theory", "speed", "formulas"]','medium','Neet Buddy',11,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('d0770e09-67b4-4a40-b503-43b9711be464','c7c037a9-ec12-44c4-865a-c760dfe5fa0e','Using Mayer\'s Relation, derive the formula for Molar Specific Heat at Constant Pressure ($C_p$) in terms of degrees of freedom (f).','Mayer\'s Relation: $C_p$ - $C_v$ = R \nSubstituting $C_v$ = (f/2)R gives: \n$C_p$ = ((f/2) + 1) R',NULL,NULL,'C_p is always greater than C_v because extra work must be done to expand the gas at constant pressure.','["physics", "kinetic-theory", "specific-heat"]','medium','Neet Buddy',12,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('2057a708-b095-452f-8bd9-47950a4f28c5','c7c037a9-ec12-44c4-865a-c760dfe5fa0e','What is the formula for the Ratio of Specific Heats (γ = $C_p$ / $C_v$) in terms of degrees of freedom (f)?','γ = 1 + (2/f)',NULL,NULL,'This ratio determines how a gas behaves during an adiabatic process (PV^γ = constant).','["physics", "kinetic-theory", "specific-heat", "neet-high-yield"]','medium','Neet Buddy',13,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('87e5e041-0878-480c-a83d-fbaa9ef3f2ed','c7c037a9-ec12-44c4-865a-c760dfe5fa0e','State the Law of Equipartition of Energy.','In thermal equilibrium, the total energy of a dynamic system is equally distributed among its various degrees of freedom. The energy associated with each degree of freedom is (1/2) $k_B$ T per molecule.',NULL,NULL,'Energy per mole per degree of freedom is (1/2)RT.','["physics", "kinetic-theory", "equipartition"]','medium','Neet Buddy',14,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('bbc42e1c-2750-411b-97d3-54c23cb5db60','c7c037a9-ec12-44c4-865a-c760dfe5fa0e','What is the formula for the Work Done (W) by a gas during expansion or compression?','W = ∫ P dV \nFor an isobaric (constant pressure) process: W = P(V₂ - V₁).',NULL,NULL,'Work done is the area under the P-V graph. Compressing a gas results in negative work done BY the gas (or positive work done ON the gas).','["physics", "kinetic-theory", "work"]','medium','Neet Buddy',15,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('cc25fcf6-af74-4bc1-aa59-e7f1ef329702','c7c037a9-ec12-44c4-865a-c760dfe5fa0e','Briefly define Boyle\'s Law and Charles\' Law.','Boyle\'s Law: P ∝ 1/V (at constant Temperature and mass).\nCharles\' Law: V ∝ T (at constant Pressure and mass).',NULL,NULL,'Gay-Lussac\'s Law: P ∝ T (at constant Volume). Combine them to get PV/T = constant.','["physics", "kinetic-theory", "gas-laws"]','medium','Neet Buddy',16,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('6e521e15-3851-4cb1-aecd-7f2d66a99866','c7c037a9-ec12-44c4-865a-c760dfe5fa0e','State Dalton\'s Law of Partial Pressures.','The total pressure exerted by a mixture of non-reacting ideal gases is equal to the sum of the partial pressures that each gas would exert if it occupied the entire volume alone. \nPₜₒₜₐₗ = P₁ + P₂ + P₃ + ...',NULL,NULL,'This is a direct consequence of the lack of intermolecular forces in ideal gases.','["physics", "kinetic-theory", "gas-laws"]','medium','Neet Buddy',17,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('41b5425f-4b98-4912-8995-ae16da0d8586','c7c037a9-ec12-44c4-865a-c760dfe5fa0e','What is the formula for the Pressure (P) exerted by an ideal gas in terms of its density (ρ) and RMS speed (vᵣₘₛ)?','P = (1/3) ρ (vᵣₘₛ)². Pressure is due to continuous elastic collisions with the walls.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "P = (1/3) ρ (vᵣₘₛ)². Pressure is due to continuous elastic collisions with the walls."}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg width=\\"800\\" height=\\"400\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 800 400\\"\\n     xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\">\\n\\n  <!-- Background -->\\n  <rect width=\\"100%\\" height=\\"100%\\" fill=\\"#f2f2f2\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Wall -->\\n  <rect x=\\"680\\" y=\\"40\\" width=\\"40\\" height=\\"300\\" fill=\\"#7a7a7a\\"/>\\n  <text x=\\"740\\" y=\\"190\\"\\n        font-family=\\"Times New Roman\\"\\n        font-size=\\"34\\"\\n        fill=\\"black\\">\\n    Wall\\n  </text>\\n\\n  <!-- Particle -->\\n  <circle cx=\\"140\\" cy=\\"190\\" r=\\"18\\" fill=\\"#0033ff\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Velocity arrow -->\\n  <line x1=\\"180\\" y1=\\"190\\" x2=\\"580\\" y2=\\"190\\"\\n        stroke=\\"#0033ff\\"\\n        stroke-width=\\"6\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Velocity label -->\\n  <text x=\\"350\\" y=\\"150\\"\\n        text-anchor=\\"middle\\"\\n        font-family=\\"Times New Roman\\"\\n        font-size=\\"38\\"\\n        fill=\\"#0033ff\\"\\n        font-style=\\"italic\\">\\n    v<tspan baseline-shift=\\"sub\\" font-size=\\"24\\">x</tspan>\\n  </text>\\n\\n  <!-- Reflected velocity (dashed) -->\\n  <line x1=\\"580\\" y1=\\"250\\" x2=\\"200\\" y2=\\"250\\"\\n        stroke=\\"#dd2222\\"\\n        stroke-width=\\"6\\"\\n        stroke-dasharray=\\"14 10\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Reflected velocity label -->\\n  <text x=\\"350\\" y=\\"310\\"\\n        text-anchor=\\"middle\\"\\n        font-family=\\"Times New Roman\\"\\n        font-size=\\"38\\"\\n        fill=\\"#dd2222\\"\\n        font-style=\\"italic\\">\\n    −v<tspan baseline-shift=\\"sub\\" font-size=\\"24\\">x</tspan>\\n  </text>\\n\\n  <!-- Momentum change equation -->\\n  <text x=\\"40\\" y=\\"370\\"\\n        font-family=\\"Times New Roman\\"\\n        font-size=\\"42\\"\\n        fill=\\"black\\"\\n        font-style=\\"italic\\">\\n    Δp = 2mv<tspan baseline-shift=\\"sub\\" font-size=\\"26\\">x</tspan>\\n  </text>\\n\\n</svg>"}]}','Density ρ = Mass / Volume. Pressure arises from the change in momentum during wall collisions.','["physics", "kinetic-theory", "pressure", "neet-high-yield"]','medium','Neet Buddy',18,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('794e8227-7d8c-4afe-a235-f0a91de1be41','c7c037a9-ec12-44c4-865a-c760dfe5fa0e','Arrange the Root Mean Square (vᵣₘₛ), Average (vₐᵥ$_g$), and Most Probable (vₘₚ) speeds of a gas in decreasing order.','vᵣₘₛ > vₐᵥ$_g$ > vₘₚ',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "vᵣₘₛ > vₐᵥ$_g$ > vₘₚ"}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg viewBox=\\"0 0 260 160\\" width=\\"100%\\" xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\">\\n  <!-- Background -->\\n  <rect width=\\"260\\" height=\\"160\\" fill=\\"white\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Axes -->\\n  <line x1=\\"25\\" y1=\\"135\\" x2=\\"240\\" y2=\\"135\\"\\n        stroke=\\"black\\" stroke-width=\\"1.5\\"/>\\n  <line x1=\\"25\\" y1=\\"135\\" x2=\\"25\\" y2=\\"15\\"\\n        stroke=\\"black\\" stroke-width=\\"1.5\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Axis Labels -->\\n  <text x=\\"205\\" y=\\"152\\"\\n        font-size=\\"11\\"\\n        font-family=\\"Arial\\">\\n    Speed (v)\\n  </text>\\n\\n  <text x=\\"6\\" y=\\"22\\"\\n        font-size=\\"11\\"\\n        font-family=\\"Arial\\">\\n    N(v)\\n  </text>\\n\\n  <!-- Maxwell Distribution Curve -->\\n  <path\\n    d=\\"M 25 135\\n       C 40 125, 52 38, 82 38\\n       C 112 38, 145 118, 235 132\\"\\n    fill=\\"none\\"\\n    stroke=\\"#0047FF\\"\\n    stroke-width=\\"2.8\\"\\n    stroke-linecap=\\"round\\"\\n  />\\n\\n  <!-- Most Probable Speed -->\\n  <line\\n    x1=\\"82\\" y1=\\"135\\"\\n    x2=\\"82\\" y2=\\"38\\"\\n    stroke=\\"#E53935\\"\\n    stroke-width=\\"1.6\\"\\n    stroke-dasharray=\\"5 4\\"\\n  />\\n\\n  <text\\n    x=\\"68\\"\\n    y=\\"150\\"\\n    fill=\\"#E53935\\"\\n    font-size=\\"11\\"\\n    font-family=\\"Arial\\">\\n    v<tspan baseline-shift=\\"sub\\" font-size=\\"8\\">mp</tspan>\\n  </text>\\n\\n  <!-- Average Speed -->\\n  <line\\n    x1=\\"105\\" y1=\\"135\\"\\n    x2=\\"105\\" y2=\\"54\\"\\n    stroke=\\"#43A047\\"\\n    stroke-width=\\"1.6\\"\\n    stroke-dasharray=\\"5 4\\"\\n  />\\n\\n  <text\\n    x=\\"92\\"\\n    y=\\"150\\"\\n    fill=\\"#43A047\\"\\n    font-size=\\"11\\"\\n    font-family=\\"Arial\\">\\n    v<tspan baseline-shift=\\"sub\\" font-size=\\"8\\">avg</tspan>\\n  </text>\\n\\n  <!-- RMS Speed -->\\n  <line\\n    x1=\\"135\\" y1=\\"135\\"\\n    x2=\\"135\\" y2=\\"78\\"\\n    stroke=\\"#8E24AA\\"\\n    stroke-width=\\"1.6\\"\\n    stroke-dasharray=\\"5 4\\"\\n  />\\n\\n  <text\\n    x=\\"122\\"\\n    y=\\"150\\"\\n    fill=\\"#8E24AA\\"\\n    font-size=\\"11\\"\\n    font-family=\\"Arial\\">\\n    v<tspan baseline-shift=\\"sub\\" font-size=\\"8\\">rms</tspan>\\n  </text>\\n</svg>"}]}','Trick: RAM (Root, Average, Most probable) reads from Highest to Lowest.','["physics", "kinetic-theory", "speed", "neet-high-yield"]','medium','Neet Buddy',19,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('5c900b11-6e05-421b-9917-526994f5d0b0','cd9b0cf4-707b-41f9-9eb4-f6fa0d814500','Write the formula for the Least Count (LC) or Vernier Constant of Vernier Calipers.','$LC = 1 \\text{ MSD} - 1 \\text{ VSD}$',NULL,NULL,'Difference between one main scale division and one vernier scale division.','["physics", "experimental-skills", "vernier-calipers"]','medium','Neet Buddy',0,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('27ca95c6-cbc9-497a-aa7c-c0bde20eaacc','cd9b0cf4-707b-41f9-9eb4-f6fa0d814500','What defines a POSITIVE zero error in Vernier Calipers?','When the jaws are closed, the zero of the Vernier scale is to the RIGHT of the zero of the Main scale.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "svg", "value": "<svg viewBox=\\"0 0 200 60\\" xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\"><line x1=\\"20\\" y1=\\"20\\" x2=\\"180\\" y2=\\"20\\" stroke=\\"black\\" stroke-width=\\"2\\"/><line x1=\\"50\\" y1=\\"10\\" x2=\\"50\\" y2=\\"20\\" stroke=\\"black\\"/><text x=\\"45\\" y=\\"8\\" font-size=\\"8\\">0 (MS)</text><line x1=\\"60\\" y1=\\"20\\" x2=\\"60\\" y2=\\"30\\" stroke=\\"red\\"/><text x=\\"55\\" y=\\"40\\" font-size=\\"8\\" fill=\\"red\\">0 (VS)</text><line x1=\\"20\\" y1=\\"30\\" x2=\\"180\\" y2=\\"30\\" stroke=\\"black\\" stroke-width=\\"1\\" stroke-dasharray=\\"2\\"/></svg>"}]}','It means the instrument reads a value greater than zero when it should be zero.','["physics", "experimental-skills", "vernier-calipers", "errors"]','medium','Neet Buddy',1,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('42aafed7-c984-4c2f-af05-7bad82a35b33','cd9b0cf4-707b-41f9-9eb4-f6fa0d814500','How do you calculate the True Reading from an Observed Reading given a zero error?','$\\text{True Reading} = \\text{Observed Reading} - (\\text{Zero Error})$',NULL,NULL,'Always SUBTRACT the zero error with its proper sign (+ or -).','["physics", "experimental-skills", "errors"]','medium','Neet Buddy',2,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('5559db71-6e05-45b3-ab12-a71a816d7e38','cd9b0cf4-707b-41f9-9eb4-f6fa0d814500','Define the \'Pitch\' of a Screw Gauge.','The linear distance moved by the screw in one complete rotation of the circular scale.',NULL,NULL,'Usually 1 mm or 0.5 mm.','["physics", "experimental-skills", "screw-gauge"]','medium','Neet Buddy',3,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('d7823d98-5b32-401b-857e-c60e71f3fa1c','cd9b0cf4-707b-41f9-9eb4-f6fa0d814500','Write the formula for the Least Count of a Screw Gauge.','$LC = \\frac{\\text{Pitch}}{\\text{Total divisions on Circular Scale}}$',NULL,NULL,'Relates linear movement to angular rotation.','["physics", "experimental-skills", "screw-gauge"]','medium','Neet Buddy',4,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('c8469e43-09ec-4945-b81d-9316d7bf0d42','cd9b0cf4-707b-41f9-9eb4-f6fa0d814500','What is \'Backlash Error\' in a Screw Gauge and how is it avoided?','Error due to wear and tear of screw threads causing a lag in linear movement upon reversing rotation. Avoided by always rotating the screw in ONE direction while measuring.',NULL,NULL,'Slippage in the threads.','["physics", "experimental-skills", "screw-gauge", "precautions"]','medium','Neet Buddy',5,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('17c36d0b-8df4-4e6f-9205-7e266c02e235','cd9b0cf4-707b-41f9-9eb4-f6fa0d814500','In the Simple Pendulum experiment, what does the slope of the $T^2$ (Y-axis) vs $L$ (X-axis) graph represent?','$\\text{Slope} = \\frac{4\\pi^2}{g}$',NULL,NULL,'Derived from $T = 2\\pi \\sqrt{L/g}$.','["physics", "experimental-skills", "simple-pendulum", "graphs"]','medium','Neet Buddy',6,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('0c8814c3-7ba8-4c77-8af4-5dbccaf8efa4','cd9b0cf4-707b-41f9-9eb4-f6fa0d814500','What is the \'Effective Length\' ($L$) of a simple pendulum?','The distance from the rigid point of suspension to the center of gravity of the spherical bob.',NULL,NULL,'$L = l + r$ (length of string + radius of bob).','["physics", "experimental-skills", "simple-pendulum"]','medium','Neet Buddy',7,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('e61acb5d-e2ea-485f-b488-df3ae303849c','cd9b0cf4-707b-41f9-9eb4-f6fa0d814500','Why is a Metre Bridge wire made of Manganin or Constantan?','Because they have a high resistivity and a very low temperature coefficient of resistance ($\\alpha \\approx 0$).',NULL,NULL,'Prevents resistance from changing due to heating during the experiment.','["physics", "experimental-skills", "metre-bridge", "materials"]','medium','Neet Buddy',8,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('02c1e114-11fa-42fd-8ded-301e3ab438dd','cd9b0cf4-707b-41f9-9eb4-f6fa0d814500','Why are thick copper strips used at the ends of a Metre Bridge?','Thick strips have negligible resistance, so they do not alter the balance condition of the Wheatstone bridge.',NULL,NULL,'$R = \\rho(L/A)$. High Area = Low Resistance.','["physics", "experimental-skills", "metre-bridge"]','medium','Neet Buddy',9,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('6e65059d-7d01-4d5b-a87f-ce0145288962','cd9b0cf4-707b-41f9-9eb4-f6fa0d814500','Why is it advised to obtain the null point near the middle (50 cm mark) of the Metre Bridge wire?','To minimize the percentage error due to end resistances and to maximize the sensitivity of the Wheatstone bridge.',NULL,NULL,'Bridge is most sensitive when all four arms have comparable resistance.','["physics", "experimental-skills", "metre-bridge", "precautions"]','medium','Neet Buddy',10,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('9ac23656-dd75-4096-9ced-9bc31d542c0d','cd9b0cf4-707b-41f9-9eb4-f6fa0d814500','In the Half-Deflection Method, write the formula for the resistance of the galvanometer ($G$).','$G = \\frac{R S}{R - S}$',NULL,NULL,'$R$ is the high resistance in series, $S$ is the shunt resistance.','["physics", "experimental-skills", "galvanometer"]','medium','Neet Buddy',11,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('2fea2f27-5211-4020-a155-d21f67d71765','cd9b0cf4-707b-41f9-9eb4-f6fa0d814500','What is the \'Figure of Merit\' ($k$) of a galvanometer?','The current required to produce a deflection of one division on the galvanometer scale. \n$k = \\frac{I}{\\theta}$',NULL,NULL,'Unit: Ampere/division.','["physics", "experimental-skills", "galvanometer"]','medium','Neet Buddy',12,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('9bd8d2fb-2a3f-4b78-aab5-e0f74938b2d0','cd9b0cf4-707b-41f9-9eb4-f6fa0d814500','In an optical bench experiment, what is \'Index Error\'?','The difference between the observed distance on the scale and the actual optical distance between the pole of the mirror/lens and the tip of the object needle.',NULL,NULL,'Requires an index correction to be added/subtracted.','["physics", "experimental-skills", "optics", "errors"]','medium','Neet Buddy',13,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('7545472a-86a5-4926-bdbd-6514f9c64ef6','cd9b0cf4-707b-41f9-9eb4-f6fa0d814500','How is \'parallax\' removed when aligning the image and object needles in optical experiments?','By adjusting the position of the needle until moving your eye left or right causes BOTH needles to move together without any relative shift.',NULL,NULL,'They must be in the exact same vertical plane.','["physics", "experimental-skills", "optics", "precautions"]','medium','Neet Buddy',14,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('15a3159c-ec71-4ab8-8863-33f2d6adbf03','cd9b0cf4-707b-41f9-9eb4-f6fa0d814500','What does the graph of Angle of Deviation ($\\delta$) vs Angle of Incidence ($i$) for a prism look like?','A U-shaped (parabolic) curve. It shows that for every deviation except the minimum, there are two angles of incidence.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "svg", "value": "<svg viewBox=\\"0 0 200 150\\" xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\"><line x1=\\"30\\" y1=\\"120\\" x2=\\"180\\" y2=\\"120\\" stroke=\\"black\\"/><line x1=\\"30\\" y1=\\"20\\" x2=\\"30\\" y2=\\"120\\" stroke=\\"black\\"/><path d=\\"M 40 40 Q 90 150 160 40\\" fill=\\"none\\" stroke=\\"blue\\" stroke-width=\\"2\\"/><line x1=\\"30\\" y1=\\"95\\" x2=\\"90\\" y2=\\"95\\" stroke=\\"red\\" stroke-dasharray=\\"4\\"/><line x1=\\"90\\" y1=\\"120\\" x2=\\"90\\" y2=\\"95\\" stroke=\\"red\\" stroke-dasharray=\\"4\\"/><text x=\\"10\\" y=\\"95\\" font-size=\\"10\\">min</text><text x=\\"80\\" y=\\"135\\" font-size=\\"10\\">i</text><text x=\\"185\\" y=\\"125\\" font-size=\\"10\\">i</text><text x=\\"10\\" y=\\"15\\" font-size=\\"10\\">Dev</text></svg>"}]}','Minimum deviation occurs at the lowest point.','["physics", "experimental-skills", "prism", "graphs"]','medium','Neet Buddy',15,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('7f5957f0-fb7d-428a-8573-4f0e102132fd','cd9b0cf4-707b-41f9-9eb4-f6fa0d814500','At the position of minimum deviation in a prism experiment, what is the relation between the angle of incidence ($i$) and angle of emergence ($e$)?','$i = e$',NULL,NULL,'The refracted ray inside the prism is parallel to its base.','["physics", "experimental-skills", "prism"]','medium','Neet Buddy',16,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('cff9a82c-08d5-4d4d-8f54-e5385914101a','cd9b0cf4-707b-41f9-9eb4-f6fa0d814500','Write the formula to determine the refractive index ($\\mu$) of a glass slab using a Travelling Microscope.','$\\mu = \\frac{\\text{Real Depth}}{\\text{Apparent Depth}} = \\frac{R_3 - R_1}{R_2 - R_1}$',NULL,NULL,'R1: mark without slab. R2: mark through slab. R3: powder on top of slab.','["physics", "experimental-skills", "optics"]','medium','Neet Buddy',17,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('e3402361-971e-4b48-ac49-10e99c53b564','cd9b0cf4-707b-41f9-9eb4-f6fa0d814500','When finding the focal length of a convex lens using the $u-v$ method, what does the $v$ vs $u$ graph look like?','A rectangular hyperbola in the second/fourth quadrant (depending on sign convention).',NULL,NULL,'As object distance decreases, image distance increases.','["physics", "experimental-skills", "optics", "graphs"]','medium','Neet Buddy',18,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('de3e59d6-e2be-47cf-8ebb-ee1efaddca30','cd9b0cf4-707b-41f9-9eb4-f6fa0d814500','In the identification of basic electronic components, how does a Multimeter (Ohmmeter) react to a healthy Capacitor?','It initially shows a low resistance (as it charges), which quickly increases to infinity (once fully charged).',NULL,NULL,'DC current cannot pass through a fully charged capacitor.','["physics", "experimental-skills", "electronics", "identification"]','medium','Neet Buddy',19,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('d5b396db-33a6-4c9f-80d1-8360161a4db9','cd9b0cf4-707b-41f9-9eb4-f6fa0d814500','In component identification, how does an Inductor behave when tested with a Multimeter?','It shows a continuous, very low, steady DC resistance.',NULL,NULL,'It is essentially just a coiled piece of wire.','["physics", "experimental-skills", "electronics", "identification"]','medium','Neet Buddy',20,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('52328c06-5bf7-421e-abfd-06610b451fa7','cd9b0cf4-707b-41f9-9eb4-f6fa0d814500','How can you experimentally distinguish an LED from a normal p-n junction diode?','When forward-biased above its threshold voltage, the LED will emit visible light, whereas a normal diode will not.',NULL,NULL,'Optoelectronic difference.','["physics", "experimental-skills", "electronics", "diodes"]','medium','Neet Buddy',21,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('215e1e2a-e9ca-47d3-920a-01ea453074b5','cd9b0cf4-707b-41f9-9eb4-f6fa0d814500','How do you identify a Zener Diode experimentally compared to a normal diode?','In reverse bias, a Zener diode will show a sharp increase in current at a specific, relatively low breakdown voltage (Zener voltage), without getting destroyed.',NULL,NULL,'Used as a voltage regulator.','["physics", "experimental-skills", "electronics", "zener"]','medium','Neet Buddy',22,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('e932e79a-249d-46a5-818f-885c3df011b6','cd9b0cf4-707b-41f9-9eb4-f6fa0d814500','What happens if a student connects the voltmeter in series instead of parallel while verifying Ohm\'s Law?','The high resistance of the voltmeter will block almost all current in the circuit. The ammeter will read near zero, and the voltmeter will read the EMF of the battery.',NULL,NULL,'Voltmeters have nearly infinite resistance.','["physics", "experimental-skills", "ohms-law", "errors"]','medium','Neet Buddy',23,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('6eee8db9-9a88-4a43-aa66-a1a44228bcee','cd9b0cf4-707b-41f9-9eb4-f6fa0d814500','What is the formula for Terminal Velocity ($v_t$) of a spherical body falling through a viscous fluid?','$v_t = \\frac{2}{9} \\frac{r^2 (\\rho - \\sigma) g}{\\eta}$',NULL,NULL,'$r$ = radius, $\\rho$ = density of body, $\\sigma$ = density of fluid, $\\eta$ = viscosity coefficient.','["physics", "experimental-skills", "viscosity"]','medium','Neet Buddy',24,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('ac3a6c78-d308-49f9-90a1-c5c05ee41e73','d1b831c3-fbb1-4a7b-95a5-1f529c314213','What is Moment of Inertia (I)?','It is the property of a body by virtue of which it resists a change in its state of rest or rotational motion. \nI = Σ mr²',NULL,NULL,'It depends on mass, the shape/size of the body, and the distribution of mass about the axis of rotation.','["physics", "rotational-motion", "moment-of-inertia"]','medium','Neet Buddy',0,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('55d7013a-7f41-4320-b33f-261be34cf6fc','d1b831c3-fbb1-4a7b-95a5-1f529c314213','State the Law of Conservation of Angular Momentum.','If the net external torque acting on a system is zero (τₑₓₜ = 0), the total angular momentum of the system remains constant (L = constant, or I₁ω₁ = I₂ω₂).',NULL,NULL,'Example: A diver tucking their body in reduces I, which increases ω to keep L constant.','["physics", "rotational-motion", "conservation-laws"]','medium','Neet Buddy',1,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('82cd2d9d-ca1b-4ef9-9536-0341ce4b79d6','d1b831c3-fbb1-4a7b-95a5-1f529c314213','Define Radius of Gyration (k).','The perpendicular distance from the axis of rotation at which the whole mass of the body could be concentrated to give the same moment of inertia. \nI = Mk² or k = √(I/M)',NULL,NULL,'It changes if you change the axis of rotation.','["physics", "rotational-motion", "radius-of-gyration"]','medium','Neet Buddy',2,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('90c445ff-c7ac-4899-88ed-c3d51dbf5ebd','d1b831c3-fbb1-4a7b-95a5-1f529c314213','How does the Center of Mass move if the net external force on the system is zero?','The Center of Mass moves with a constant velocity (acceleration of CoM is zero). Internal forces cannot change the motion of the CoM.',NULL,NULL,'Think of an exploding bomb in mid-air: the CoM of the fragments continues on the original parabolic path.','["physics", "rotational-motion", "center-of-mass"]','medium','Neet Buddy',3,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('1b7e399b-ee91-4bca-91c2-7937e4d42f8b','d1b831c3-fbb1-4a7b-95a5-1f529c314213','Define Torque (τ) and give its mathematical formula.','Torque is the rotational analogue of force (turning effect). \nτ = r × F \nMagnitude: τ = rF sin(θ)',NULL,NULL,'Unit is Newton-meter (N·m).','["physics", "rotational-motion", "torque"]','medium','Neet Buddy',4,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('f1d9e185-f868-4255-a20b-fcd08a12d720','d1b831c3-fbb1-4a7b-95a5-1f529c314213','Define Angular Momentum (L) and its relation to Linear Momentum (p).','Angular momentum is the moment of linear momentum. \nL = r × p \nMagnitude: L = rp sin(θ) = mvr sin(θ)',NULL,NULL,'For a rigid body rotating about a fixed axis, L = Iω.','["physics", "rotational-motion", "angular-momentum"]','medium','Neet Buddy',5,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('79bb3fbd-0c39-4b3f-be81-53850ad25ec1','d1b831c3-fbb1-4a7b-95a5-1f529c314213','What is the formula for the Center of Mass (CoM) of a two-particle system?','R$_c$$_m$ = (m₁r₁ + m₂r₂) / (m₁ + m₂)',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "R$_c$$_m$ = (m₁r₁ + m₂r₂) / (m₁ + m₂)"}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg viewBox=\'0 0 300 100\' width=\'100%\'><line x1=\'50\' y1=\'50\' x2=\'250\' y2=\'50\' stroke=\'gray\' stroke-width=\'3\'/><circle cx=\'50\' cy=\'50\' r=\'15\' fill=\'blue\'/><text x=\'40\' y=\'85\' font-size=\'14\'>m1 (Large)</text><circle cx=\'250\' cy=\'50\' r=\'8\' fill=\'red\'/><text x=\'230\' y=\'85\' font-size=\'14\'>m2 (Small)</text><polygon points=\'100,50 90,35 110,35\' fill=\'black\'/><text x=\'85\' y=\'25\' font-size=\'14\' font-weight=\'bold\'>CoM</text></svg>"}]}','The CoM always lies closer to the heavier mass.','["physics", "rotational-motion", "center-of-mass", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',6,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('7626bb7a-f408-4824-bc21-b00930d4a28c','d1b831c3-fbb1-4a7b-95a5-1f529c314213','What is the relationship between Torque and Angular Momentum?','The net external torque acting on a system is equal to the rate of change of its angular momentum. \nτₑₓₜ = dL/dt',NULL,NULL,'This is Newton\'s Second Law for rotational motion (analogous to F = dp/dt).','["physics", "rotational-motion", "dynamics"]','medium','Neet Buddy',7,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('c10df9a5-cfff-4a3d-ae4f-397f19a94893','d1b831c3-fbb1-4a7b-95a5-1f529c314213','What is the Moment of Inertia of a Ring and a Disc about an axis passing through the center and perpendicular to their planes?','Ring: I = MR² \nDisc: I = MR² / 2',NULL,NULL,'A disc\'s mass is distributed closer to the center than a ring\'s, hence a lower MoI.','["physics", "rotational-motion", "moment-of-inertia", "neet-high-yield"]','medium','Neet Buddy',8,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('2cde00af-0ca3-4db6-9352-c71d5ff4db59','d1b831c3-fbb1-4a7b-95a5-1f529c314213','What is the Moment of Inertia of a Solid Sphere and a Hollow Sphere (Shell) about their diameters?','Solid Sphere: I = (2/5)MR² \nHollow Sphere: I = (2/3)MR²',NULL,NULL,'(2/3) > (2/5). The hollow sphere has mass further from the axis, so higher MoI.','["physics", "rotational-motion", "moment-of-inertia", "neet-high-yield"]','medium','Neet Buddy',9,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('236f2a82-272d-41af-86f4-434756740600','d1b831c3-fbb1-4a7b-95a5-1f529c314213','State the Theorem of Perpendicular Axes.','I$_z$ = I$_x$ + I$_y$ \nThe moment of inertia of a planar body about an axis perpendicular to its plane is equal to the sum of its moments of inertia about two perpendicular axes in its plane.',NULL,NULL,'Only applicable to PLANAR (2D) bodies (like rings, discs, laminas).','["physics", "rotational-motion", "theorems"]','medium','Neet Buddy',10,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('98464d00-628c-4cc1-9ac1-484e394c4942','d1b831c3-fbb1-4a7b-95a5-1f529c314213','Write the three equations of Rotational Kinematics (for constant angular acceleration α).','1. ω = ω₀ + αt\n\n2. θ = ω₀t + (1/2)αt²\n\n3. ω² = ω₀² + 2αθ\n',NULL,NULL,'Perfectly analogous to linear kinematics: replace v with ω, a with α, and s with θ.','["physics", "rotational-motion", "kinematics"]','medium','Neet Buddy',11,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('7865b662-5452-401d-afc2-d7d4e842b30b','d1b831c3-fbb1-4a7b-95a5-1f529c314213','State the Right-Hand Thumb Rule for Vector (Cross) Product.','If you curl the fingers of your right hand from vector A towards vector B, your extended thumb points in the direction of the cross product A × B.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "If you curl the fingers of your right hand from vector A towards vector B, your extended thumb points in the direction of the cross product A × B."}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg width=\\"700\\" height=\\"450\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 700 450\\"\\n     xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\">\\n\\n  <!-- Background -->\\n  <rect width=\\"100%\\" height=\\"100%\\" fill=\\"white\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Axes -->\\n  <line x1=\\"120\\" y1=\\"360\\" x2=\\"560\\" y2=\\"360\\"\\n        stroke=\\"black\\" stroke-width=\\"6\\"/>\\n\\n  <line x1=\\"120\\" y1=\\"360\\" x2=\\"120\\" y2=\\"80\\"\\n        stroke=\\"red\\" stroke-width=\\"8\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Inclined line B -->\\n  <line x1=\\"120\\" y1=\\"360\\" x2=\\"260\\" y2=\\"90\\"\\n        stroke=\\"black\\" stroke-width=\\"6\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Curved rotation arrow -->\\n  <path d=\\"M 360 360\\n           A 170 170 0 0 0 235 205\\"\\n        fill=\\"none\\"\\n        stroke=\\"blue\\"\\n        stroke-width=\\"7\\"\\n        stroke-dasharray=\\"14 10\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Arrowhead -->\\n  <polygon points=\\"225,190 250,205 215,220\\"\\n           fill=\\"blue\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Labels -->\\n  <text x=\\"585\\" y=\\"378\\"\\n        font-family=\\"Arial\\"\\n        font-size=\\"42\\"\\n        fill=\\"black\\">\\n    A\\n  </text>\\n\\n  <text x=\\"220\\" y=\\"50\\"\\n        font-family=\\"Arial\\"\\n        font-size=\\"42\\"\\n        fill=\\"black\\">\\n    B\\n  </text>\\n\\n  <text x=\\"15\\" y=\\"130\\"\\n        font-family=\\"Arial\\"\\n        font-size=\\"42\\"\\n        fill=\\"red\\">\\n    A × B\\n  </text>\\n\\n</svg>"}]}','Used to find the direction of Torque (τ) and Angular Momentum (L).','["physics", "rotational-motion", "vectors"]','medium','Neet Buddy',12,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('54562000-a105-4094-8d0f-8bc3be6dfe67','d1b831c3-fbb1-4a7b-95a5-1f529c314213','State the Theorem of Parallel Axes.','The moment of inertia of a body about any axis is equal to its moment of inertia about a parallel axis passing through its center of mass (I$_c$ₘ) plus the product of its mass (M) and the square of the distance (d) between the axes.\nI ₌ I$_c$ₘ ₊ Md²',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "The moment of inertia of a body about any axis is equal to its moment of inertia about a parallel axis passing through its center of mass (I$_c$ₘ) plus the product of its mass (M) and the square of the distance (d) between the axes.\\nI ₌ I$_c$ₘ ₊ Md²"}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg width=\\"700\\" height=\\"450\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 700 450\\"\\n     xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\">\\n\\n  <!-- Background -->\\n  <rect width=\\"100%\\" height=\\"100%\\" fill=\\"white\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Red reference line -->\\n  <line x1=\\"120\\" y1=\\"40\\" x2=\\"120\\" y2=\\"390\\"\\n        stroke=\\"red\\" stroke-width=\\"6\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Ellipse body -->\\n  <ellipse cx=\\"380\\" cy=\\"215\\" rx=\\"220\\" ry=\\"120\\"\\n           fill=\\"none\\" stroke=\\"black\\" stroke-width=\\"4\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Blue dashed center line -->\\n  <line x1=\\"380\\" y1=\\"30\\" x2=\\"380\\" y2=\\"400\\"\\n        stroke=\\"blue\\" stroke-width=\\"5\\"\\n        stroke-dasharray=\\"14 10\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Distance line -->\\n  <line x1=\\"120\\" y1=\\"215\\" x2=\\"380\\" y2=\\"215\\"\\n        stroke=\\"black\\" stroke-width=\\"4\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Center of Mass point -->\\n  <circle cx=\\"380\\" cy=\\"215\\" r=\\"10\\" fill=\\"black\\"/>\\n\\n  <!-- Labels -->\\n  <text x=\\"390\\" y=\\"255\\"\\n        font-family=\\"Arial\\"\\n        font-size=\\"28\\"\\n        fill=\\"black\\">\\n    CoM\\n  </text>\\n\\n  <text x=\\"245\\" y=\\"195\\"\\n        font-family=\\"Times New Roman\\"\\n        font-size=\\"42\\"\\n        font-style=\\"italic\\"\\n        fill=\\"black\\">\\n    d\\n  </text>\\n\\n  <text x=\\"405\\" y=\\"55\\"\\n        font-family=\\"Times New Roman\\"\\n        font-size=\\"34\\"\\n        font-style=\\"italic\\"\\n        fill=\\"blue\\">\\n    l<tspan baseline-shift=\\"sub\\" font-size=\\"22\\">cm</tspan>\\n  </text>\\n\\n</svg>"}]}','Applicable to ALL types of bodies (1D, 2D, 3D).','["physics", "rotational-motion", "theorems", "neet-high-yield"]','medium','Neet Buddy',13,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('ddc91821-9785-40bb-8481-de59dc408a11','d1b831c3-fbb1-4a7b-95a5-1f529c314213','What is the total Kinetic Energy of a body performing pure rolling motion?','Total K.E. = Translational K.E. + Rotational K.E.\n\n',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "Total K.E. = Translational K.E. + Rotational K.E.\\n\\n"}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\" xmlns:xlink=\\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\\" width=\\"572\\" height=\\"431\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 572 431\\">\\n  <image xlink:href=\\"data:image/png;base64,\\" x=\\"0\\" y=\\"0\\" width=\\"572\\" height=\\"431\\"/>\\n</svg>"}]}','In pure rolling without slipping, v_cm = ωR.','["physics", "rotational-motion", "rolling", "neet-high-yield"]','medium','Neet Buddy',14,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('5f452b09-edae-425b-9903-84d376249f63','d3fa746e-ccfe-4a8d-b3a4-4bf2c460c285','What chemically distinguishes reducing sugars from non-reducing sugars? Give an example of a non-reducing disaccharide.','Reducing sugars contain a free aldehyde or ketone group (hemiacetal/hemiketal) and can reduce Tollens\' or Fehling\'s reagents. **Sucrose** is a non-reducing sugar because its reducing groups are tied up in the glycosidic bond.',NULL,NULL,'Table sugar does not reduce.','["chemistry", "biomolecules", "carbohydrates", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',0,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('82ee499d-3c3d-4a7a-80be-2c56d568ad00','d3fa746e-ccfe-4a8d-b3a4-4bf2c460c285','Name the constituent monosaccharide units and the specific glycosidic linkage present in Sucrose.','Constituents: **$\\alpha$-D-Glucose** and **$\\beta$-D-Fructose**.\nLinkage: Glycosidic bond between **C1 of $\\alpha$-D-glucose** and **C2 of $\\beta$-D-fructose**.',NULL,NULL,'Involves the anomeric carbons of both units.','["chemistry", "biomolecules", "carbohydrates"]','medium','Neet Buddy',1,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('6a319fb3-0a61-4596-86e3-e0cc40b3a6c9','d3fa746e-ccfe-4a8d-b3a4-4bf2c460c285','Identify the constituent monosaccharides and linkages of Maltose and Lactose.','**Maltose:** Two units of $\\alpha$-D-glucose (C1-C4 linkage).\n**Lactose:** $\\beta$-D-galactose and $\\beta$-D-glucose (C1-C4 linkage).',NULL,NULL,'Malt sugar vs Milk sugar.','["chemistry", "biomolecules", "carbohydrates"]','medium','Neet Buddy',2,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('cf66cbfd-6cc0-48a9-a02b-6a9503474de7','d3fa746e-ccfe-4a8d-b3a4-4bf2c460c285','What are anomers? Give the standard example found in carbohydrates.','Anomers are a pair of cyclic diastereomers that differ in configuration ONLY at the **anomeric carbon** (C1 for aldoses, C2 for ketoses). \nExample: **$\\alpha$-D-glucose** and **$\\beta$-D-glucose**.',NULL,NULL,'Alpha is \'down\', Beta is \'up\'.','["chemistry", "biomolecules", "carbohydrates", "isomerism"]','medium','Neet Buddy',3,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('15afc702-61c2-40e6-83f0-438645684bf6','d3fa746e-ccfe-4a8d-b3a4-4bf2c460c285','What is the main structural difference between Starch and Cellulose?','**Starch** is a branched polymer of **$\\alpha$-D-glucose** (composed of water-soluble amylose and water-insoluble amylopectin).\n**Cellulose** is a straight-chain polymer of **$\\beta$-D-glucose**.',NULL,NULL,'Humans digest alpha linkages (starch) but not beta linkages (cellulose).','["chemistry", "biomolecules", "carbohydrates", "polymers"]','medium','Neet Buddy',4,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('0b692dc2-7317-4e78-b127-3450972521af','d3fa746e-ccfe-4a8d-b3a4-4bf2c460c285','What type of chemical linkage joins individual nucleotides to form a long nucleic acid chain (polynucleotide)?','**Phosphodiester linkage**. It is formed between the **5\'-OH** group of the pentose sugar of one nucleotide and the **3\'-OH** group of the sugar of the next nucleotide.',NULL,NULL,'Connects 3\' to 5\'.','["chemistry", "biomolecules", "nucleic-acids"]','medium','Neet Buddy',5,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('6255b7b9-492c-416a-9f3f-da97006cce0e','d3fa746e-ccfe-4a8d-b3a4-4bf2c460c285','State the complementary base pairing rule in the DNA double helix and note the number of hydrogen bonds.','**Adenine** pairs with **Thymine** via **2** hydrogen bonds ($A=T$).\n**Guanine** pairs with **Cytosine** via **3** hydrogen bonds ($G\\equiv C$).',NULL,NULL,'A-T has two, G-C has three.','["chemistry", "biomolecules", "nucleic-acids", "dna"]','medium','Neet Buddy',6,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('c941e767-2fa8-4774-b776-c96337454331','d3fa746e-ccfe-4a8d-b3a4-4bf2c460c285','Differentiate between essential and non-essential amino acids.','**Essential:** Cannot be synthesized by the human body and must be obtained through diet (e.g., Valine, Leucine).\n**Non-essential:** Can be synthesized by the body (e.g., Glycine, Alanine).',NULL,NULL,'Essential means \'essential to eat\'.','["chemistry", "biomolecules", "proteins", "amino-acids"]','medium','Neet Buddy',7,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('021652f7-13bb-4af8-888a-cc0c8d931ce9','d3fa746e-ccfe-4a8d-b3a4-4bf2c460c285','What type of linkage joins amino acids to form proteins? State its chemical structure.','The **Peptide bond** (or peptide linkage). It is an **amide linkage ($-CO-NH-$)** formed by the condensation of the $-COOH$ group of one amino acid with the $-NH_2$ group of another, eliminating a water molecule.',NULL,NULL,'It\'s a condensation polymer bond.','["chemistry", "biomolecules", "proteins"]','medium','Neet Buddy',8,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('8ba9bec5-6596-45f5-82d8-db78a6bb275d','d3fa746e-ccfe-4a8d-b3a4-4bf2c460c285','What are the two most common types of secondary structures of proteins, and what main force stabilizes them?','1. **$\\alpha$-Helix**\n2. **$\\beta$-Pleated sheet**\nThey are stabilized primarily by **Hydrogen bonding** between the $-C=O$ of one amino acid residue and the $-NH-$ of another.',NULL,NULL,'Coils and folded sheets.','["chemistry", "biomolecules", "proteins", "structure"]','medium','Neet Buddy',9,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('a7084a41-145c-4abf-ae08-300619d4701e','d3fa746e-ccfe-4a8d-b3a4-4bf2c460c285','Name the four types of forces/bonds that stabilize the tertiary structure (3D folding) of proteins.','1. Hydrogen bonds\n2. Disulfide linkages ($-S-S-$)\n3. van der Waals forces\n4. Electrostatic forces of attraction (ionic bonds).',NULL,NULL,'Includes covalent and non-covalent interactions.','["chemistry", "biomolecules", "proteins", "structure"]','medium','Neet Buddy',10,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('ab570590-387c-46aa-bbdc-02f8c570c8f1','d3fa746e-ccfe-4a8d-b3a4-4bf2c460c285','What happens to a protein chemically and structurally during denaturation (e.g., boiling an egg)?','During denaturation, physical or chemical changes disrupt stabilizing bonds. The **secondary and tertiary structures unfold** and lose biological activity. Crucially, the **primary structure (amino acid sequence) remains intact**.',NULL,NULL,'Only the folding is ruined, not the chain itself.','["chemistry", "biomolecules", "proteins", "denaturation"]','medium','Neet Buddy',11,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('7e4eb136-804b-4d1f-a19d-349d1b4ce014','d3fa746e-ccfe-4a8d-b3a4-4bf2c460c285','How are vitamins broadly classified based on solubility? Provide examples.','1. **Fat-soluble:** Vitamins A, D, E, and K. (Stored in liver and adipose tissue).\n2. **Water-soluble:** B group vitamins and Vitamin C. (Excreted in urine, rarely stored).',NULL,NULL,'ADEK = Fat. B, C = Water.','["chemistry", "biomolecules", "vitamins"]','medium','Neet Buddy',12,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('48b75756-7594-4eec-9616-9913a7dfb839','d3fa746e-ccfe-4a8d-b3a4-4bf2c460c285','Which water-soluble vitamin is an exception because it can be stored in the body (specifically in the liver)?','**Vitamin B12** (Cyanocobalamin).',NULL,NULL,'The cobalt-containing vitamin.','["chemistry", "biomolecules", "vitamins", "exceptions"]','medium','Neet Buddy',13,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('9790d955-1bc4-4850-97fe-dc75cc99120e','d3fa746e-ccfe-4a8d-b3a4-4bf2c460c285','Match the following deficiency diseases to their respective Vitamins: Scurvy, Rickets, Night Blindness, Pernicious Anemia.','Scurvy $\\rightarrow$ **Vitamin C**\nRickets $\\rightarrow$ **Vitamin D**\nNight Blindness $\\rightarrow$ **Vitamin A**\nPernicious Anemia $\\rightarrow$ **Vitamin B12**',NULL,NULL,'C for citrus/scurvy, A for vision.','["chemistry", "biomolecules", "vitamins", "diseases"]','medium','Neet Buddy',14,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('a86aa87a-a3f0-4ac2-8ee2-2b9e1c154c1e','d3fa746e-ccfe-4a8d-b3a4-4bf2c460c285','Identify the specific nitrogenous bases present in DNA vs RNA.','**DNA:** Adenine (A), Guanine (G), Cytosine (C), **Thymine (T)**.\n**RNA:** Adenine (A), Guanine (G), Cytosine (C), **Uracil (U)**.',NULL,NULL,'RNA replaces Thymine with Uracil.','["chemistry", "biomolecules", "nucleic-acids"]','medium','Neet Buddy',15,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('32e6852e-2405-4526-8ad6-66dbe83475c6','d3fa746e-ccfe-4a8d-b3a4-4bf2c460c285','What is the structural difference between a Nucleoside and a Nucleotide?','**Nucleoside:** Nitrogenous Base + Pentose Sugar.\n**Nucleotide:** Nitrogenous Base + Pentose Sugar + **Phosphate Group**.','{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "Visually represent the difference between a Nucleoside and a Nucleotide."}]}','{"content": [{"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\" xmlns:xlink=\\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\\" width=\\"1514\\" height=\\"604\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 1514 604\\">\\n  <image xlink:href=\\"data:image/png;base64,\\" x=\\"0\\" y=\\"0\\" width=\\"1514\\" height=\\"604\\"/>\\n</svg>"}]}','NucleoTide has Three components.','["chemistry", "biomolecules", "nucleic-acids"]','medium','Neet Buddy',16,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('61a51558-a0df-4a2c-b1b7-c07e61484e24','d3fa746e-ccfe-4a8d-b3a4-4bf2c460c285','What are the primary biological functions of DNA and RNA?','**DNA:** Responsible for the transmission of hereditary characteristics (genetic information) and self-replication.\n**RNA:** Primarily responsible for **protein synthesis** in the cell.',NULL,NULL,'DNA holds the blueprint; RNA builds the structure.','["chemistry", "biomolecules", "nucleic-acids", "functions"]','medium','Neet Buddy',17,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('327303c5-de06-41c9-a4f5-4713c050150d','d3fa746e-ccfe-4a8d-b3a4-4bf2c460c285','What are hormones, and give an example of a steroid hormone vs a polypeptide hormone?','Hormones are intercellular messengers produced by endocrine glands and poured directly into the bloodstream.\n**Steroid hormones:** Testosterone, Estrogens, Cortisol.\n**Polypeptide hormones:** Insulin, Glucagon.',NULL,NULL,'Steroids are lipid-based; Polypeptides are protein-based.','["chemistry", "biomolecules", "hormones"]','medium','Neet Buddy',18,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('db92ccc3-98f7-4573-ac31-91eb3db0d985','d3fa746e-ccfe-4a8d-b3a4-4bf2c460c285','What are enzymes chemically, and what is their primary function in the body?','Chemically, almost all enzymes are **globular proteins**. They act as **biological catalysts** by highly specifically lowering the activation energy of biochemical reactions in the body.',NULL,NULL,'They act via a lock-and-key mechanism.','["chemistry", "biomolecules", "proteins", "enzymes"]','medium','Neet Buddy',19,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('3abf4d4a-5c53-4999-bf35-13732313b699','d3fa746e-ccfe-4a8d-b3a4-4bf2c460c285','Define a Zwitterion in the context of amino acids.','In aqueous solution, the carboxyl group ($-COOH$) loses a proton and the amino group ($-NH_2$) accepts it, forming a **dipolar, electrically neutral ion** called a zwitterion.','{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "What is a Zwitterion? Explain its formation in amino acids."}]}','{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "A neutral, dipolar ion formed by internal proton transfer."}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\" xmlns:xlink=\\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\\" width=\\"1244\\" height=\\"358\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 1244 358\\">\\n  <image xlink:href=\\"data:image/png;base64,\\" x=\\"0\\" y=\\"0\\" width=\\"1244\\" height=\\"358\\"/>\\n</svg>"}]}','Contains both positive and negative charges.','["chemistry", "biomolecules", "proteins", "amino-acids"]','medium','Neet Buddy',20,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('6e209e9e-ca85-46c8-b543-3c4b0e927255','d426ff36-ea90-4a8e-9cc2-0c28c4e0c37a','What are the 7 SI Base Quantities and their units?','Length (meter), Mass (kilogram), Time (second), Electric Current (ampere), Thermodynamic Temperature (kelvin), Amount of Substance (mole), Luminous Intensity (candela).',NULL,NULL,'Think: LMTE-TAL','["physics", "units", "neet", "class-11"]','medium','Neet Buddy',0,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('8303a7ba-d861-4630-ab7d-30f6dc0233f7','d426ff36-ea90-4a8e-9cc2-0c28c4e0c37a','Differentiate between Plane Angle and Solid Angle (Supplementary Units).','Plane Angle (θ) is 2D, measured in radians (rad). Solid Angle (Ω) is 3D, measured in steradians (sr).',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "Plane Angle: θ = ds/r (radians)\\nSolid Angle: Ω = dA/r² (steradians)"}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg viewBox=\'0 0 300 120\' width=\'100%\'><g transform=\'translate(50, 60)\'><path d=\'M 0 0 L 40 -30 A 50 50 0 0 1 40 30 Z\' fill=\'none\' stroke=\'blue\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><path d=\'M 20 -15 A 25 25 0 0 1 20 15\' fill=\'none\' stroke=\'red\'/><text x=\'25\' y=\'5\' font-size=\'12\'>θ</text><text x=\'-20\' y=\'-40\' font-size=\'12\' fill=\'blue\'>Plane Angle</text></g><g transform=\'translate(200, 60)\'><path d=\'M 0 0 L 40 -30 M 0 0 L 40 30\' stroke=\'green\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><ellipse cx=\'40\' cy=\'0\' rx=\'10\' ry=\'30\' fill=\'rgba(0,255,0,0.3)\' stroke=\'green\'/><text x=\'15\' y=\'5\' font-size=\'12\'>Ω</text><text x=\'-20\' y=\'-40\' font-size=\'12\' fill=\'green\'>Solid Angle</text></g></svg>"}]}','θ = arc/radius; Ω = Area / radius²','["physics", "units", "angles"]','medium','Neet Buddy',1,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('2ff43eb2-37ee-4182-ad28-77d68007546b','d65afa3c-7664-4e36-b666-09606bf85bef','What is the physical significance of $\\Psi$ and $\\Psi^2$ in the quantum mechanical model?','$\\Psi$ (Wave function) has no physical significance. \n$\\Psi^2$ (Probability density) represents the probability of finding an electron at a particular point in space.',NULL,NULL,'Squaring makes it real and meaningful.','["chemistry", "atomic-structure", "quantum-mechanics"]','medium','Neet Buddy',8,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('2a8ccdfd-ef3a-40d2-ba3c-cd7166dfcdca','d426ff36-ea90-4a8e-9cc2-0c28c4e0c37a','Explain the Parallax Method formula for measuring large distances.','D = b / θ. Where \'D\' is the distance to the star, \'b\' is the distance between two observation points (basis), and \'θ\' is the parallax angle in radians.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "Distance (D) = Basis (b) / Parallax Angle (θ)"}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg viewBox=\'0 0 200 120\' width=\'100%\'><circle cx=\'100\' cy=\'20\' r=\'5\' fill=\'orange\'/><text x=\'110\' y=\'25\' font-size=\'12\'>Star (S)</text><circle cx=\'50\' cy=\'100\' r=\'4\' fill=\'blue\'/><text x=\'35\' y=\'115\' font-size=\'12\'>Earth 1</text><circle cx=\'150\' cy=\'100\' r=\'4\' fill=\'blue\'/><text x=\'140\' y=\'115\' font-size=\'12\'>Earth 2</text><line x1=\'50\' y1=\'100\' x2=\'150\' y2=\'100\' stroke=\'gray\' stroke-dasharray=\'4\'/><text x=\'95\' y=\'115\' font-size=\'12\'>b</text><line x1=\'50\' y1=\'100\' x2=\'100\' y2=\'20\' stroke=\'black\'/><line x1=\'150\' y1=\'100\' x2=\'100\' y2=\'20\' stroke=\'black\'/><text x=\'65\' y=\'60\' font-size=\'12\'>D</text><path d=\'M 92 40 A 15 15 0 0 0 108 40\' fill=\'none\' stroke=\'red\'/><text x=\'96\' y=\'52\' font-size=\'10\' fill=\'red\'>θ</text></svg>"}]}','Ensure θ is strictly in radians, not degrees!','["physics", "measurements", "parallax"]','medium','Neet Buddy',2,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('b056ab70-5009-49cb-ab3e-39bdf05fba08','d426ff36-ea90-4a8e-9cc2-0c28c4e0c37a','What are the dimensional formulas for Force and Work/Energy?','Force = [M¹ L¹ T⁻²]\nWork/Energy = [M¹ L² T⁻²]',NULL,NULL,'Work = Force × Displacement. These are the two most important dimensions to memorize.','["physics", "dimensions", "neet-high-yield"]','medium','Neet Buddy',3,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('00b07429-2342-4c12-8234-19110d31a381','d426ff36-ea90-4a8e-9cc2-0c28c4e0c37a','What is the dimensional formula for Planck\'s Constant (h), and which physical quantity shares it?','Planck\'s Constant (h) = [M¹ L² T⁻¹]. \nIt has the same dimensions as Angular Momentum (L = mvr).',NULL,NULL,'E = hν (Energy = h × frequency). Frequency is [T⁻¹].','["physics", "dimensions", "neet-high-yield"]','medium','Neet Buddy',4,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('90713834-0071-4c6b-99f3-be0f5f8465a2','d426ff36-ea90-4a8e-9cc2-0c28c4e0c37a','State the Principle of Homogeneity of Dimensions.','Only physical quantities with the same dimensions can be added, subtracted, or equated.',NULL,NULL,'In equation v = u + at, the dimensions of \'v\', \'u\', and \'at\' must all be identical.','["physics", "dimensions"]','medium','Neet Buddy',5,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('5615c2de-5cc9-4f85-9738-64bac85781e4','d426ff36-ea90-4a8e-9cc2-0c28c4e0c37a','What are the rules for identifying Significant Figures regarding zeroes?','1. Trailing zeroes in a number WITHOUT a decimal are NOT significant (e.g., 1500 has 2). \n2. Trailing zeroes WITH a decimal ARE significant (e.g., 15.00 has 4).\n3. Leading zeroes are NEVER significant (e.g., 0.005 has 1).',NULL,NULL,'Zeroes between non-zero digits are always significant (e.g., 1005 has 4).','["physics", "significant-figures"]','medium','Neet Buddy',6,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('b7c4dadb-da9a-4ffc-a5d0-5d20cd9e3434','d426ff36-ea90-4a8e-9cc2-0c28c4e0c37a','Explain the rule for rounding off a number ending exactly in \'5\' (e.g., 2.75 vs 2.65).','If the preceding digit is ODD, increase it by 1. If EVEN, leave it unchanged.\n2.75 → 2.8 (7 is odd)\n2.65 → 2.6 (6 is even)',NULL,NULL,'This rule prevents statistical bias when averaging rounded numbers.','["physics", "significant-figures", "rounding"]','medium','Neet Buddy',7,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('b7346ea4-c3c0-4dc6-85f1-27adea824744','d426ff36-ea90-4a8e-9cc2-0c28c4e0c37a','How do absolute errors combine when adding or subtracting two quantities (Z = A + B or Z = A - B)?','The maximum absolute error in the result is the sum of the absolute errors of the individual quantities: ΔZ = ΔA + ΔB.',NULL,NULL,'Errors ALWAYS add up, even in subtraction! You never subtract errors.','["physics", "errors"]','medium','Neet Buddy',8,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('e13bb5b9-e4fd-4213-98ba-dfec5d8005bd','d426ff36-ea90-4a8e-9cc2-0c28c4e0c37a','Write the formula for the maximum fractional error if Z = (Aᵖ × Bᵚ) / Cʳ.','ΔZ/Z = p(ΔA/A) + q(ΔB/B) + r(ΔC/C).',NULL,NULL,'Multiply this by 100 to get the percentage error. Notice that the power \'r\' comes up as positive. Errors always maximize.','["physics", "errors", "neet-high-yield"]','medium','Neet Buddy',9,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('84c4c7f7-5bb4-4f27-830b-4941fa8b3b2b','d426ff36-ea90-4a8e-9cc2-0c28c4e0c37a','Define Least Count (LC) and give the formula for a Vernier Caliper.','The smallest value that can be measured by an instrument. \nFor Vernier Caliper: LC = 1 MSD - 1 VSD (Main Scale Division - Vernier Scale Division).',NULL,NULL,'Standard LC for a lab Vernier Caliper is usually 0.01 cm.','["physics", "instruments", "vernier"]','medium','Neet Buddy',10,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('50031d45-3882-430a-b14c-c5e16cc9c3f1','d426ff36-ea90-4a8e-9cc2-0c28c4e0c37a','Derive the dimensional formula for the Universal Gravitational Constant (G).','F = G(m₁m₂)/r², G = Fr²/(m₁m₂)\n\nDimensions: [MLT⁻²][L²]/[M²] = [M⁻¹L³T⁻²].',NULL,NULL,'A highly repeated question in NEET.','["physics", "dimensions", "neet-high-yield"]','medium','Neet Buddy',11,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('73166647-336f-4794-b705-d6de86487b81','d426ff36-ea90-4a8e-9cc2-0c28c4e0c37a','What is the formula for the Least Count of a Screw Gauge?','Least Count = Pitch / Total number of divisions on the circular scale.',NULL,NULL,'Pitch is the distance moved by the screw in one complete rotation (usually 1 mm or 0.5 mm).','["physics", "instruments", "screw-gauge"]','medium','Neet Buddy',12,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('c2afbf00-e086-48eb-81d5-d3cd8f2243ce','d426ff36-ea90-4a8e-9cc2-0c28c4e0c37a','How do you correct for Positive and Negative Zero Error in a measuring instrument?','True Reading = Measured Reading - (Zero Error with its sign).\nPositive zero error is subtracted. Negative zero error is added.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "True Reading = Measured Reading - (Zero Error with its sign).\\nPositive zero error is subtracted. Negative zero error is added."}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg width=\\"900\\" height=\\"320\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 900 320\\" xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\">\\n\\n  <style>\\n    .title {\\n      font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;\\n      font-size: 28px;\\n      font-weight: bold;\\n      fill: #111;\\n    }\\n\\n    .label {\\n      font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;\\n      font-size: 18px;\\n      fill: #222;\\n    }\\n\\n    .small {\\n      font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;\\n      font-size: 16px;\\n      fill: #222;\\n    }\\n\\n    .main-scale {\\n      fill: #d9d9d9;\\n      stroke: #999;\\n      stroke-width: 1;\\n    }\\n\\n    .vernier-scale {\\n      fill: #bfefff;\\n      stroke: #5aa9c9;\\n      stroke-width: 1;\\n    }\\n\\n    .zero-main {\\n      stroke: #000;\\n      stroke-width: 3;\\n    }\\n\\n    .zero-vernier {\\n      stroke: #d40000;\\n      stroke-width: 3;\\n    }\\n\\n    .arrow {\\n      stroke: #333;\\n      stroke-width: 1.5;\\n      fill: none;\\n      marker-end: url(#arrowhead);\\n    }\\n  </style>\\n\\n  <defs>\\n    <marker id=\\"arrowhead\\" markerWidth=\\"8\\" markerHeight=\\"8\\" refX=\\"6\\" refY=\\"3\\" orient=\\"auto\\">\\n      <polygon points=\\"0 0, 6 3, 0 6\\" fill=\\"#333\\"/>\\n    </marker>\\n  </defs>\\n\\n  <!-- LEFT: POSITIVE ERROR -->\\n  <g transform=\\"translate(40,40)\\">\\n\\n    <text x=\\"180\\" y=\\"0\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\" class=\\"title\\">\\n      Positive Zero Error\\n    </text>\\n\\n    <!-- Main Scale -->\\n    <rect x=\\"0\\" y=\\"70\\" width=\\"300\\" height=\\"28\\" class=\\"main-scale\\"/>\\n\\n    <!-- Vernier Scale -->\\n    <rect x=\\"18\\" y=\\"105\\" width=\\"300\\" height=\\"28\\" class=\\"vernier-scale\\"/>\\n\\n    <!-- Main Zero -->\\n    <line x1=\\"120\\" y1=\\"55\\" x2=\\"120\\" y2=\\"98\\" class=\\"zero-main\\"/>\\n\\n    <!-- Vernier Zero -->\\n    <line x1=\\"138\\" y1=\\"105\\" x2=\\"138\\" y2=\\"133\\" class=\\"zero-vernier\\"/>\\n\\n    <!-- Labels -->\\n    <text x=\\"120\\" y=\\"48\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\" class=\\"label\\">\\n      Main Scale Zero\\n    </text>\\n\\n    <text x=\\"138\\" y=\\"165\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\" class=\\"label\\">\\n      Vernier Zero\\n    </text>\\n\\n    <!-- Arrow -->\\n    <path d=\\"M150 185 C170 195, 185 195, 205 185\\" class=\\"arrow\\"/>\\n\\n    <text x=\\"210\\" y=\\"190\\" class=\\"small\\">\\n      Positive Error\\n    </text>\\n\\n  </g>\\n\\n  <!-- RIGHT: NEGATIVE ERROR -->\\n  <g transform=\\"translate(500,40)\\">\\n\\n    <text x=\\"180\\" y=\\"0\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\" class=\\"title\\">\\n      Negative Zero Error\\n    </text>\\n\\n    <!-- Main Scale -->\\n    <rect x=\\"0\\" y=\\"70\\" width=\\"300\\" height=\\"28\\" class=\\"main-scale\\"/>\\n\\n    <!-- Vernier Scale -->\\n    <rect x=\\"18\\" y=\\"105\\" width=\\"300\\" height=\\"28\\" class=\\"vernier-scale\\"/>\\n\\n    <!-- Vernier Zero -->\\n    <line x1=\\"92\\" y1=\\"105\\" x2=\\"92\\" y2=\\"133\\" class=\\"zero-vernier\\"/>\\n\\n    <!-- Main Zero -->\\n    <line x1=\\"120\\" y1=\\"55\\" x2=\\"120\\" y2=\\"98\\" class=\\"zero-main\\"/>\\n\\n    <!-- Labels -->\\n    <text x=\\"120\\" y=\\"48\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\" class=\\"label\\">\\n      Main Scale Zero\\n    </text>\\n\\n    <text x=\\"92\\" y=\\"165\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\" class=\\"label\\">\\n      Vernier Zero\\n    </text>\\n\\n    <!-- Arrow -->\\n    <path d=\\"M150 185 C130 195, 115 195, 95 185\\" class=\\"arrow\\"/>\\n\\n    <text x=\\"10\\" y=\\"190\\" class=\\"small\\">\\n      Negative Error\\n    </text>\\n\\n  </g>\\n\\n</svg>"}]}','Positive error means it reads too high initially. Negative means it reads too low.','["physics", "instruments", "errors"]','medium','Neet Buddy',13,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('91cba1a1-9843-455d-a228-b0e075963cb8','d426ff36-ea90-4a8e-9cc2-0c28c4e0c37a','Differentiate between Accuracy and Precision.','Accuracy: How close a measured value is to the TRUE value. \nPrecision: How close the measured values are to EACH OTHER (depends on the limit/resolution of the instrument).',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "Accuracy = Hitting the bullseye.\\nPrecision = Hitting the same spot repeatedly."}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\" xmlns:xlink=\\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\\" width=\\"542\\" height=\\"234\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 542 234\\">\\n  <image xlink:href=\\"data:image/png;base64,\\" x=\\"0\\" y=\\"0\\" width=\\"542\\" height=\\"234\\"/>\\n</svg>"}]}','You can be highly precise (consistent) but wildly inaccurate (wrong).','["physics", "measurements", "concepts"]','medium','Neet Buddy',14,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('bd366f5b-6a48-4c91-bdc0-1c013d043b1f','d65afa3c-7664-4e36-b666-09606bf85bef','Describe the graphical variation of $\\Psi^2$ vs $r$ for a 1s and a 2s orbital.','1s orbital: Probability density is maximum at the nucleus ($r=0$) and decreases exponentially. \n2s orbital: Decreases to zero (forming one radial node), then increases to a small maximum, and finally decreases.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\' viewBox=\'0 0 300 120\'><text x=\'30\' y=\'15\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'10\'>1s Orbital</text><line x1=\'20\' y1=\'10\' x2=\'20\' y2=\'100\' stroke=\'black\'/><line x1=\'20\' y1=\'100\' x2=\'120\' y2=\'100\' stroke=\'black\'/><path d=\'M 20 20 Q 40 90 110 98\' stroke=\'red\' fill=\'none\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><text x=\'160\' y=\'15\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'10\'>2s Orbital (1 Node)</text><line x1=\'150\' y1=\'10\' x2=\'150\' y2=\'100\' stroke=\'black\'/><line x1=\'150\' y1=\'100\' x2=\'280\' y2=\'100\' stroke=\'black\'/><path d=\'M 150 20 Q 170 100 190 100 T 220 90 T 270 98\' stroke=\'blue\' fill=\'none\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><circle cx=\'190\' cy=\'100\' r=\'3\' fill=\'black\'/><text x=\'180\' y=\'115\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'10\'>Node</text></svg>"}]}','2s has (2-0-1) = 1 radial node.','["chemistry", "atomic-structure", "orbitals"]','medium','Neet Buddy',11,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('a4cc8054-918f-45d1-a306-bf38ca233204','d65afa3c-7664-4e36-b666-09606bf85bef','What is the unique shape of the $d_{z^2}$ orbital compared to other d-orbitals?','Unlike the other four d-orbitals which have a double-dumbbell shape, the $d_{z^2}$ orbital has a single dumbbell shape along the z-axis with a donut-shaped (torus) electron cloud in the xy-plane.',NULL,NULL,'The \'baby pacifier\' shape.','["chemistry", "atomic-structure", "shapes"]','medium','Neet Buddy',12,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('66751cb7-ab18-4b52-a47c-5f28890b1a9f','d65afa3c-7664-4e36-b666-09606bf85bef','State the $(n+l)$ rule used in the Aufbau Principle.','Electrons fill orbitals in order of increasing $(n+l)$ value. If two orbitals have the same $(n+l)$ value, the one with the lower $n$ value is filled first.',NULL,NULL,'4s fills before 3d because (4+0=4) < (3+2=5).','["chemistry", "atomic-structure", "electronic-configuration"]','medium','Neet Buddy',13,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('40dd8c27-9b79-402f-a31f-888e4e996bb6','d65afa3c-7664-4e36-b666-09606bf85bef','State Pauli\'s Exclusion Principle.','No two electrons in an atom can have the same set of all four quantum numbers ($n, l, m_l, m_s$).',NULL,NULL,'An orbital can hold a maximum of 2 electrons, and they must have opposite spins.','["chemistry", "atomic-structure", "electronic-configuration"]','medium','Neet Buddy',14,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('7ef98e85-f97a-4eae-b3ed-bc75fc11ae87','d65afa3c-7664-4e36-b666-09606bf85bef','State Hund\'s Rule of Maximum Multiplicity.','Electron pairing in degenerate orbitals (like $p_x, p_y, p_z$) will not take place until each orbital of the given subshell is singly occupied with parallel spins.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\' viewBox=\'0 0 300 100\'><text x=\'10\' y=\'20\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'12\' font-weight=\'bold\'>Correct (Nitrogen p3):</text><rect x=\'10\' y=\'30\' width=\'30\' height=\'30\' fill=\'none\' stroke=\'black\'/><text x=\'20\' y=\'52\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'18\'>↑</text><rect x=\'40\' y=\'30\' width=\'30\' height=\'30\' fill=\'none\' stroke=\'black\'/><text x=\'50\' y=\'52\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'18\'>↑</text><rect x=\'70\' y=\'30\' width=\'30\' height=\'30\' fill=\'none\' stroke=\'black\'/><text x=\'80\' y=\'52\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'18\'>↑</text><text x=\'150\' y=\'20\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'12\' font-weight=\'bold\' fill=\'red\'>Incorrect:</text><rect x=\'150\' y=\'30\' width=\'30\' height=\'30\' fill=\'none\' stroke=\'black\'/><text x=\'160\' y=\'52\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'18\'>↑↓</text><rect x=\'180\' y=\'30\' width=\'30\' height=\'30\' fill=\'none\' stroke=\'black\'/><text x=\'190\' y=\'52\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'18\'>↑</text><rect x=\'210\' y=\'30\' width=\'30\' height=\'30\' fill=\'none\' stroke=\'black\'/></svg>"}]}','Fill singles before doubles.','["chemistry", "atomic-structure", "electronic-configuration"]','medium','Neet Buddy',15,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('c923f91f-2e09-47e6-a8a3-865db7cecf96','d65afa3c-7664-4e36-b666-09606bf85bef','What are the anomalous electronic configurations of Chromium (Cr, Z=24) and Copper (Cu, Z=29)?','Chromium: $[Ar] 4s^1 3d^5$ (not $4s^2 3d^4$) \nCopper: $[Ar] 4s^1 3d^{10}$ (not $4s^2 3d^9$)',NULL,NULL,'Half-filled and fully-filled stability.','["chemistry", "atomic-structure", "electronic-configuration", "neet-favorite"]','medium','Neet Buddy',16,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('77e5df7a-e394-458a-8a25-91aafd61d48a','d65afa3c-7664-4e36-b666-09606bf85bef','Why are half-filled and completely filled subshells exceptionally stable?','1. Symmetrical distribution of electrons minimizes electron-electron repulsion. \n2. Maximum exchange energy (electrons with parallel spins in degenerate orbitals can exchange positions, releasing energy).',NULL,NULL,'Symmetry and Exchange Energy.','["chemistry", "atomic-structure", "conceptual"]','medium','Neet Buddy',17,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('bc6729cd-4265-4f28-abf2-2bd6f844f536','d65afa3c-7664-4e36-b666-09606bf85bef','Calculate the orbital angular momentum for a 3p electron.','For a p-orbital, $l = 1$. \nOrbital angular momentum $= \\sqrt{l(l+1)} \\frac{h}{2\\pi} = \\sqrt{1(1+1)} \\frac{h}{2\\pi} = \\sqrt{2} \\frac{h}{2\\pi}$ (or $\\sqrt{2} \\hbar$).',NULL,NULL,'Formula: sqrt(l(l+1)) * (h/2pi)','["chemistry", "atomic-structure", "quantum-numbers", "numerical"]','medium','Neet Buddy',18,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('04f13081-b6af-41dd-b7e2-d331916d2dfe','d97f97c4-d3e5-43eb-80cd-7a6078fd23ce','State the Zeroth Law of Thermodynamics and the physical quantity it defines.','If two systems are each in thermal equilibrium with a third system, they are in thermal equilibrium with each other. It defines the concept of Temperature.',NULL,NULL,'Temperature is the property that determines thermal equilibrium.','["physics", "thermodynamics", "zeroth-law", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',0,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('45b36e58-7444-4ac5-bfba-dbd3c02a33e9','d97f97c4-d3e5-43eb-80cd-7a6078fd23ce','What is the Internal Energy (U) of a system? On what does it depend for an Ideal Gas?','Internal energy is the sum of macroscopic kinetic and potential energies of the molecules. \nFor an IDEAL GAS, there are no intermolecular forces (so Potential Energy = 0). Thus, U is purely kinetic and depends ONLY on Temperature.',NULL,NULL,'U is a STATE variable (depends only on the current state, not the path).','["physics", "thermodynamics", "internal-energy"]','medium','Neet Buddy',1,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('10bd34d2-317d-47d8-8ce5-6d594dac013d','d97f97c4-d3e5-43eb-80cd-7a6078fd23ce','State the First Law of Thermodynamics mathematically.','ΔQ = ΔU + ΔW \n(Heat supplied to the system = Change in internal energy + Work done BY the system).',NULL,NULL,'This is a statement of the Law of Conservation of Energy.','["physics", "thermodynamics", "first-law"]','medium','Neet Buddy',2,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('cd6c1267-124b-4e1b-85f7-4f70064b83ff','d97f97c4-d3e5-43eb-80cd-7a6078fd23ce','What is the standard Sign Convention for Heat (Q), Work (W), and Internal Energy (U)?','1. +Q: Heat supplied TO the system; -Q: Heat rejected BY the system.\n2. +W: Work done BY the system (Expansion); -W: Work done ON the system (Compression).\n3. +ΔU: Temperature increases; -ΔU: Temperature decreases.',NULL,NULL,'Expansion = Positive Work. Compression = Negative Work.','["physics", "thermodynamics", "sign-convention", "neet-high-yield"]','medium','Neet Buddy',3,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('6433fe8f-8788-4298-ac15-b95e05f9950f','d97f97c4-d3e5-43eb-80cd-7a6078fd23ce','Define an Isothermal Process and write its equation of state.','A process in which Temperature remains constant (ΔT = 0). \nEquation: PV = constant (Boyle\'s Law).',NULL,NULL,'Since ΔT = 0, the change in internal energy (ΔU) is also ZERO for an ideal gas.','["physics", "thermodynamics", "isothermal"]','medium','Neet Buddy',4,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('3918dede-f72f-4143-a48d-b47705651898','d97f97c4-d3e5-43eb-80cd-7a6078fd23ce','What does the First Law of Thermodynamics become for an Isothermal Process?','Since ΔU = 0 (because temperature is constant), the First Law (ΔQ = ΔU + ΔW) becomes: \nΔQ = ΔW',NULL,NULL,'All heat added is completely converted into work done by the gas.','["physics", "thermodynamics", "isothermal"]','medium','Neet Buddy',5,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('725d0787-75e5-4ba2-8f98-1ad22ba59efc','d97f97c4-d3e5-43eb-80cd-7a6078fd23ce','Write the formula for Work Done (W) in an Isothermal Process.','W = nRT ln(V₂ / V₁) = 2.303 nRT log₁₀(V₂ / V₁)\nOr in terms of pressure: W = nRT ln(P₁ / P₂)',NULL,NULL,'Requires a perfectly conducting cylinder and a very SLOW process.','["physics", "thermodynamics", "isothermal", "formula"]','medium','Neet Buddy',6,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('2d5de49c-0480-4ccb-a18b-768d30c41c50','d97f97c4-d3e5-43eb-80cd-7a6078fd23ce','What does the First Law of Thermodynamics become for an Adiabatic Process?','Since ΔQ = 0, the First Law (ΔQ = ΔU + ΔW) becomes: \nΔU = -ΔW \nor ΔW = -ΔU',NULL,NULL,'During adiabatic expansion (+W), the gas does work at the expense of its internal energy, so it COOLS down (-ΔU).','["physics", "thermodynamics", "adiabatic", "concepts"]','medium','Neet Buddy',7,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('e0ccf4a3-763d-4e30-b5ce-c21621a3f765','d97f97c4-d3e5-43eb-80cd-7a6078fd23ce','Write the formula for Work Done (W) in an Adiabatic Process.','W = (P₁V₁ - P₂V₂) / (γ - 1) \nor \nW = nR(T₁ - T₂) / (γ - 1)',NULL,NULL,'Requires perfectly insulating walls and a very FAST process (like a bursting tyre).','["physics", "thermodynamics", "adiabatic", "formula"]','medium','Neet Buddy',8,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('6ec0cc3e-1b59-4829-ad2c-f718aaf456f4','d97f97c4-d3e5-43eb-80cd-7a6078fd23ce','Define an Isochoric Process and state the Work Done.','A process where Volume is constant (ΔV = 0). \nWork Done: W = PΔV = 0.',NULL,NULL,'First Law becomes ΔQ = ΔU = nCvΔT. All heat added increases internal energy.','["physics", "thermodynamics", "isochoric"]','medium','Neet Buddy',9,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('3cbb3483-3e5a-4ceb-9c77-78979762f718','d97f97c4-d3e5-43eb-80cd-7a6078fd23ce','Define an Isobaric Process and state the Work Done.','A process where Pressure is constant (ΔP = 0). \nWork Done: W = P(V₂ - V₁) = nR(T₂ - T₁).',NULL,NULL,'First Law: ΔQ = ΔU + ΔW becomes nCpΔT = nCvΔT + nRΔT.','["physics", "thermodynamics", "isobaric"]','medium','Neet Buddy',10,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('10df4b2d-6a21-495a-b57e-cb249030f271','d97f97c4-d3e5-43eb-80cd-7a6078fd23ce','Compare the slopes of Isothermal and Adiabatic curves on a P-V diagram.','The Adiabatic curve is steeper than the Isothermal curve. \nSlope of Adiabatic = γ × (Slope of Isothermal).',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "Adiabatic is γ times steeper than Isothermal."}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\" xmlns:xlink=\\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\\" width=\\"1021\\" height=\\"658\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 1021 658\\">\\n  <image xlink:href=\\"data:image/png;base64,\\" x=\\"0\\" y=\\"0\\" width=\\"1021\\" height=\\"658\\"/>\\n</svg>"}]}','Since γ > 1 for all gases, the adiabatic curve always drops faster.','["physics", "thermodynamics", "graphs", "neet-high-yield"]','medium','Neet Buddy',11,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('f0e0709e-0363-46b9-b524-747907780e31','d97f97c4-d3e5-43eb-80cd-7a6078fd23ce','Define an Adiabatic Process and write its equation of state.','A process in which NO heat is exchanged with the surroundings (ΔQ = 0). \nEquation: $PV^γ$ = constant (where γ = $C_p$ / $C_v$).',NULL,NULL,'Other relations: TV^(γ-1) = constant, and (T^γ)/(P^(γ-1)) = constant.','["physics", "thermodynamics", "adiabatic"]','medium','Neet Buddy',12,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('73685090-a77e-4571-b6e2-4faf3f05c777','d97f97c4-d3e5-43eb-80cd-7a6078fd23ce','How do you evaluate Work Done and ΔU in a Cyclic Process?','In a cyclic process, initial and final states are the same, so ΔU = 0. \nTherefore, ΔQ = ΔW. \nWork done is the Area enclosed by the P-V loop.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "Clockwise = Positive Work. Anti-Clockwise = Negative Work."}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg viewBox=\'0 0 250 120\' width=\'100%\'><line x1=\'20\' y1=\'100\' x2=\'120\' y2=\'100\' stroke=\'black\'/><line x1=\'20\' y1=\'100\' x2=\'20\' y2=\'10\' stroke=\'black\'/><circle cx=\'70\' cy=\'55\' r=\'25\' fill=\'rgba(0,255,0,0.3)\' stroke=\'green\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><path d=\'M 70 30 L 75 35 L 75 25 Z\' fill=\'green\'/><text x=\'55\' y=\'60\' font-size=\'10\'>+ Work</text><line x1=\'140\' y1=\'100\' x2=\'240\' y2=\'100\' stroke=\'black\'/><line x1=\'140\' y1=\'100\' x2=\'140\' y2=\'10\' stroke=\'black\'/><circle cx=\'190\' cy=\'55\' r=\'25\' fill=\'rgba(255,0,0,0.3)\' stroke=\'red\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><path d=\'M 190 30 L 185 35 L 185 25 Z\' fill=\'red\'/><text x=\'175\' y=\'60\' font-size=\'10\'>- Work</text></svg>"}]}','Clockwise loop = Positive Work (+W). Anti-clockwise loop = Negative Work (-W).','["physics", "thermodynamics", "cyclic-process", "graphs"]','medium','Neet Buddy',13,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('5e122ed3-d61e-4a8a-a80c-771d9649336d','d97f97c4-d3e5-43eb-80cd-7a6078fd23ce','What is Free Expansion? What are the values of Q, W, and ΔU?','Free expansion is the adiabatic expansion of a gas into a vacuum. \nSince it\'s in a vacuum, W = 0. Since it\'s adiabatic, Q = 0. \nTherefore, by First Law, ΔU = 0 (Temperature remains constant).',NULL,NULL,'It is a highly irreversible process.','["physics", "thermodynamics", "free-expansion", "neet-high-yield"]','medium','Neet Buddy',14,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('7553d8da-be3d-40dd-a602-e0e557792e2d','d97f97c4-d3e5-43eb-80cd-7a6078fd23ce','State the Kelvin-Planck statement of the Second Law of Thermodynamics.','No process is possible whose SOLE result is the absorption of heat from a reservoir and the complete conversion of the heat into work.',NULL,NULL,'Simply put: No heat engine can have 100% efficiency. Some heat MUST be rejected to a cold sink.','["physics", "thermodynamics", "second-law", "heat-engine"]','medium','Neet Buddy',15,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('a0f3e566-010f-43b7-be7b-7e7ab9cd56c7','d97f97c4-d3e5-43eb-80cd-7a6078fd23ce','State the Clausius statement of the Second Law of Thermodynamics.','No process is possible whose sole result is the transfer of heat from a colder object to a hotter object.',NULL,NULL,'Simply put: Heat cannot flow from cold to hot on its own; external work (like a compressor) is required (Refrigerator Principle).','["physics", "thermodynamics", "second-law", "refrigerator"]','medium','Neet Buddy',16,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('696423fb-09d6-4145-b5f0-39babf022e51','d97f97c4-d3e5-43eb-80cd-7a6078fd23ce','What are the two essential conditions for a Reversible Process?','1. The process must be quasi-static (infinitely slow) so that thermal/mechanical equilibrium is maintained throughout.\n2. There must be NO dissipative forces (like friction, viscosity, or electrical resistance).',NULL,NULL,'All spontaneous natural processes are Irreversible.','["physics", "thermodynamics", "reversible-process"]','medium','Neet Buddy',17,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('cf401a80-7c54-40e8-8650-f9da81b14a55','d97f97c4-d3e5-43eb-80cd-7a6078fd23ce','Write the formula for the Efficiency (η) of a Carnot Engine.','η = W / Q₁ = 1 - (Q₂ / Q₁) \nFor a Carnot Engine: η = 1 - (T₂ / T₁)',NULL,NULL,'T₁ (Source) and T₂ (Sink) MUST strictly be in Kelvin (K). \nη is always < 1 (or < 100%).','["physics", "thermodynamics", "carnot", "efficiency", "neet-high-yield"]','medium','Neet Buddy',18,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('0e09ef8e-afd5-49e5-b863-c743c095bf01','d97f97c4-d3e5-43eb-80cd-7a6078fd23ce','What is the Coefficient of Performance (β or COP) of a Refrigerator?','β = Q₂ / W = Q₂ / (Q₁ - Q₂) \nFor a Carnot Refrigerator: β = T₂ / (T₁ - T₂)',NULL,NULL,'Relation between Efficiency (η) and COP (β): β = (1 - η) / η','["physics", "thermodynamics", "refrigerator", "formulas"]','medium','Neet Buddy',19,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('65891095-07ea-4460-9e05-f382bfdc2f46','d97f97c4-d3e5-43eb-80cd-7a6078fd23ce','What are the four strokes (processes) that make up the Carnot Cycle?','1. Reversible Isothermal Expansion (Heat $Q_1$ absorbed at $T_1$)\n2. Reversible Adiabatic Expansion (Cooling from $T_1$ to $T_2$)\n3. Reversible Isothermal Compression (Heat $Q_2$ rejected at $T_2$)\n4. Reversible Adiabatic Compression (Heating from $T_2$ to $T_1$)',NULL,NULL,'It consists of two isotherms and two adiabats.','["physics", "thermodynamics", "carnot"]','medium','Neet Buddy',20,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('8b5a9e5d-d3c3-4084-8dd1-a41a90865397','d97f97c4-d3e5-43eb-80cd-7a6078fd23ce','How do you calculate Work Done (W) from a Pressure-Volume (P-V) graph?','Work done is the Area under the P-V curve projected onto the Volume (V) axis. \nW = ∫ P dV',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "Work = Area under P-V curve."}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\" xmlns:xlink=\\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\\" width=\\"1166\\" height=\\"670\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 1166 670\\">\\n  <image xlink:href=\\"data:image/png;base64,\\" x=\\"0\\" y=\\"0\\" width=\\"1166\\" height=\\"670\\"/>\\n</svg>"}]}','Expansion (Volume increases) = Positive Area. Compression = Negative Area.','["physics", "thermodynamics", "graphs", "work"]','medium','Neet Buddy',21,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('66d9d2e4-ee56-45d9-9776-9d57cea92998','e012d304-ae4e-4239-a17f-f9a29f22dc63','State the Principle of Quantization of Charge.','Electric charge is always an integral multiple of the basic quantum of charge (e). \nq = ±ne, where n = 1, 2, 3... and e ≈ 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.',NULL,NULL,'You cannot have a fraction of an electron\'s charge (like 1.5e).','["physics", "electrostatics", "charge", "neet"]','medium','Neet Buddy',0,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('03a22955-765e-4769-a44a-b8b0d7859a94','e012d304-ae4e-4239-a17f-f9a29f22dc63','What are the two most important properties of Electric Field Lines?','1. They never cross (intersect) each other. \n2. They do not form closed loops (electrostatic field is conservative).',NULL,NULL,'If they intersected, there would be two different directions of the electric field at the same point, which is impossible.','["physics", "electrostatics", "electric-field"]','medium','Neet Buddy',1,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('fc097c1b-3372-4e09-8239-fffe17934781','e012d304-ae4e-4239-a17f-f9a29f22dc63','What is the direction of the Electric Dipole Moment (p) and its formula?','Direction: From the NEGATIVE charge to the POSITIVE charge. \nFormula: p = q × 2a (where 2a is the separation between charges).',NULL,NULL,'Unlike the electric field (which points + to -), the dipole moment points - to +.','["physics", "electrostatics", "dipole"]','medium','Neet Buddy',2,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('72f5aef4-2681-42ad-9b79-4c8fdfb8758f','e012d304-ae4e-4239-a17f-f9a29f22dc63','Write the formula for the Potential Energy (U) of a dipole in a uniform electric field.','U = -p · E = -pE cos(θ)',NULL,NULL,'Stable equilibrium: θ = 0° (U is minimum, -pE). Unstable equilibrium: θ = 180° (U is maximum, +pE).','["physics", "electrostatics", "dipole", "energy"]','medium','Neet Buddy',3,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('b97666bd-6f8f-4a72-a611-58a96bdca01b','e012d304-ae4e-4239-a17f-f9a29f22dc63','What is the Electric Field (E) at a distance \'r\' from an infinitely long straight uniformly charged wire?','E = λ / (2πε₀r) \nWhere λ is the linear charge density (charge per unit length).',NULL,NULL,'E is inversely proportional to r (E ∝ 1/r).','["physics", "electrostatics", "gauss-law", "applications"]','medium','Neet Buddy',4,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('2b592bbc-9952-41d8-976c-da44a31c8959','e012d304-ae4e-4239-a17f-f9a29f22dc63','What is the Electric Field (E) produced by an infinite, uniformly charged thin plane sheet?','E = σ / (2ε₀) \nWhere σ is the surface charge density.',NULL,NULL,'Notice that the electric field is UNIFORM; it does not depend on the distance \'r\' from the sheet!','["physics", "electrostatics", "gauss-law", "applications"]','medium','Neet Buddy',5,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('9a487c04-8b5d-4605-bcad-5a578c7db27d','e012d304-ae4e-4239-a17f-f9a29f22dc63','Describe the variation of Electric Field (E) inside, on, and outside a uniformly charged thin spherical shell of radius R.','Inside (r < R): E = 0 \nOn the surface (r = R): E = (1 / 4πε₀) × (q / R²) \nOutside (r > R): E = (1 / 4πε₀) × (q / r²)',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "Inside: E=0. Outside: E ∝ 1/r²."}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg viewBox=\'0 0 200 150\' width=\'100%\'><line x1=\'20\' y1=\'130\' x2=\'180\' y2=\'130\' stroke=\'black\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><line x1=\'20\' y1=\'130\' x2=\'20\' y2=\'10\' stroke=\'black\' stroke-width=\'2\'/><text x=\'185\' y=\'145\' font-size=\'12\'>r</text><text x=\'5\' y=\'20\' font-size=\'12\'>E</text><line x1=\'80\' y1=\'130\' x2=\'80\' y2=\'135\' stroke=\'black\'/><text x=\'75\' y=\'145\' font-size=\'12\'>R</text><line x1=\'20\' y1=\'130\' x2=\'80\' y2=\'130\' stroke=\'red\' stroke-width=\'3\'/><path d=\'M 80 30 Q 110 110 170 125\' fill=\'none\' stroke=\'red\' stroke-width=\'3\'/><line x1=\'80\' y1=\'130\' x2=\'80\' y2=\'30\' stroke=\'red\' stroke-dasharray=\'4\'/><text x=\'30\' y=\'120\' font-size=\'10\' fill=\'red\'>E=0</text><text x=\'110\' y=\'70\' font-size=\'10\' fill=\'red\'>E ∝ 1/r²</text></svg>"}]}','Inside a conductor or charged shell, the electric field is always zero.','["physics", "electrostatics", "gauss-law", "neet-high-yield"]','medium','Neet Buddy',6,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('7bb7f7c6-2a12-47e3-adf6-989bd1bebff3','e012d304-ae4e-4239-a17f-f9a29f22dc63','Write the relationship between Electric Field (E) and Electric Potential (V).','E = - dV / dr \n(Electric field is the negative of the potential gradient).',NULL,NULL,'The negative sign indicates that the electric field always points in the direction of DECREASING potential.','["physics", "electrostatics", "potential"]','medium','Neet Buddy',7,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('dda68df9-a139-4436-9bf3-516b9484bab4','e012d304-ae4e-4239-a17f-f9a29f22dc63','What are Equipotential Surfaces and what is the work done in moving a charge along them?','Surfaces where the electric potential (V) is the same at every point. \nWork done = q × ΔV. Since ΔV = 0 on an equipotential surface, Work Done = 0.',NULL,NULL,'Electric field lines are ALWAYS perpendicular to equipotential surfaces.','["physics", "electrostatics", "potential"]','medium','Neet Buddy',8,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('733f4461-a28b-41a5-a14b-13b5be9cf81e','e012d304-ae4e-4239-a17f-f9a29f22dc63','State Gauss\'s Law in Electrostatics.','The net electric flux (Φ) through any closed surface is equal to 1/ε₀ times the net charge enclosed by the surface. \nΦ = ∮ E · dA = qₑₙₗₒₛₑ/ ε₀',NULL,NULL,'Flux only depends on the ENCLOSED charge, not on the shape or size of the Gaussian surface.','["physics", "electrostatics", "gauss-law"]','medium','Neet Buddy',9,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('3c06b0f5-7e26-4b53-87fe-acc1a0e15aea','e012d304-ae4e-4239-a17f-f9a29f22dc63','What is the Torque (τ) experienced by an electric dipole in a uniform electric field?','τ = p × E \nMagnitude: τ = pE sin(θ), where θ is the angle between dipole moment (p) and electric field (E).',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "τ = p × E \\nMagnitude: τ = pE sin(θ), where θ is the angle between dipole moment (p) and electric field (E)."}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\" xmlns:xlink=\\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\\" width=\\"1058\\" height=\\"605\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 1058 605\\">\\n  <image xlink:href=\\"data:image/png;base64,\\" x=\\"0\\" y=\\"0\\" width=\\"1058\\" height=\\"605\\"/>\\n</svg>"}]}','Torque is maximum when θ = 90° and zero when θ = 0° or 180°.','["physics", "electrostatics", "dipole"]','medium','Neet Buddy',10,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('32c428ac-27c1-47b4-a5f3-0533ec8a36bd','e012d304-ae4e-4239-a17f-f9a29f22dc63','Write Coulomb\'s Law for the electrostatic force between two point charges and define Relative Permittivity (Dielectric Constant, K).','Answer Given Below:',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "Answer Given Below:"}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\" xmlns:xlink=\\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\\" width=\\"652\\" height=\\"125\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 652 125\\">\\n  <image xlink:href=\\"data:image/png;base64,\\" x=\\"0\\" y=\\"0\\" width=\\"652\\" height=\\"125\\"/>\\n</svg>"}]}','When placed in a medium of dielectric constant K, the force between charges decreases by a factor of K.','["physics", "electrostatics", "coulombs-law"]','medium','Neet Buddy',11,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('0fa2536e-dfe6-4803-9df0-85b156ddb3cd','e012d304-ae4e-4239-a17f-f9a29f22dc63','What happens to the Electric Field and Potential inside a charged solid conductor?','Inside a conductor: \nElectric Field (E) = 0 \nElectric Potential (V) is CONSTANT and equal to its value on the surface.',NULL,NULL,'Because E = -dV/dr, if E = 0, V must be constant. The entire volume of the conductor is an equipotential region.','["physics", "electrostatics", "conductors", "neet-high-yield"]','medium','Neet Buddy',12,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('dfc5f2c3-bdbe-4f6f-9a01-78a9ed208ba4','e012d304-ae4e-4239-a17f-f9a29f22dc63','Write the formula for the Capacitance (C) of a parallel plate capacitor in a vacuum.','C = (ε₀A) / d \nWhere A is the area of the plates and d is the distance between them.',NULL,NULL,'Capacitance depends only on the geometry (A, d) and the medium, NOT on the charge (Q) or potential (V).','["physics", "electrostatics", "capacitance"]','medium','Neet Buddy',13,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('2d7ecc8c-fcba-456d-b861-8614c5b1e441','e012d304-ae4e-4239-a17f-f9a29f22dc63','How does inserting a dielectric slab of dielectric constant \'K\' completely between the plates affect the capacitance?','The new capacitance becomes K times the original capacitance. \nC\' = K × C₀ = (Kε₀A) / d',NULL,NULL,'A dielectric polarizes and reduces the internal electric field, allowing the capacitor to store more charge at the same voltage.','["physics", "electrostatics", "capacitance", "dielectrics"]','medium','Neet Buddy',14,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('3378c43f-b41f-449e-bfa1-3973b001542a','e012d304-ae4e-4239-a17f-f9a29f22dc63','Write the three forms of the formula for Energy (U) stored in a charged capacitor.','U = (1/2)CV² \nU = (Q²) / (2C) \nU = (1/2)QV',NULL,NULL,'Use U = Q²/2C when capacitors are in series (Q is constant). Use U = 1/2 CV² when in parallel (V is constant).','["physics", "electrostatics", "capacitance", "energy"]','medium','Neet Buddy',15,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('5eabdce6-2c2b-465f-9dc7-d6eeb72120b9','e012d304-ae4e-4239-a17f-f9a29f22dc63','What is the formula for Energy Density (u) in an electric field between capacitor plates?','u = (1/2) ε₀ E² \nWhere u is the energy stored per unit volume, and E is the electric field.',NULL,NULL,'This is a fundamental property of electric fields, valid anywhere, not just inside capacitors.','["physics", "electrostatics", "capacitance", "energy"]','medium','Neet Buddy',16,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('a163fc3b-87b6-4fb8-b98e-843072492dc3','e012d304-ae4e-4239-a17f-f9a29f22dc63','When two charged capacitors (C₁, V₁ and C₂, V₂) are connected together, what is the formula for their Common Potential (V)?','V = (Total Charge) / (Total Capacitance) \nV = (C₁V₁ + C₂V₂) / (C₁ + C₂)',NULL,NULL,'During this redistribution of charge, there is always a loss of energy in the form of heat.','["physics", "electrostatics", "capacitance", "neet-high-yield"]','medium','Neet Buddy',17,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('4ff2a52c-090a-4052-b9c6-d8eecc6ca307','e012d304-ae4e-4239-a17f-f9a29f22dc63','What are the formulas for equivalent capacitance when capacitors are connected in Series and Parallel?','Series: 1/Cₑᵩ = 1/C₁ + 1/C₂ + ... \nParallel: Cₑᵩ = C₁ + C₂ + ...',NULL,NULL,'In series, Charge (Q) is constant across all. In parallel, Voltage (V) is constant across all. (Notice this is the opposite of resistors!).','["physics", "electrostatics", "capacitance"]','medium','Neet Buddy',18,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('0856abe5-1f3a-4b92-91bb-c6e1b16ca863','e012d304-ae4e-4239-a17f-f9a29f22dc63','Compare the Electric Field (E) due to a short dipole at an Axial point vs. an Equatorial point at the same distance \'r\'.','Answer Given Below:',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "Answer Given Below:"}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\" xmlns:xlink=\\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\\" width=\\"620\\" height=\\"197\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 620 197\\">\\n  <image xlink:href=\\"data:image/png;base64,\\" x=\\"0\\" y=\\"0\\" width=\\"620\\" height=\\"197\\"/>\\n</svg>"}]}','The field on the equatorial line is anti-parallel to the dipole moment vector (p).','["physics", "electrostatics", "dipole", "neet-high-yield"]','medium','Neet Buddy',19,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('4bbe1a78-274f-4690-8e9b-d41c18c04e6b','e687e6cb-2dbc-4991-ac4e-054860d979c9','Explain Hyperconjugation (No-bond resonance).','It is a permanent effect involving the delocalization of $\\sigma$ electrons of a C-H bond of an alkyl group directly attached to an atom with an unshared p-orbital (carbocation, free radical) or a $\\pi$ bond (alkene).',NULL,NULL,'More alpha-hydrogens = more hyperconjugation structures = greater stability.','["chemistry", "goc", "hyperconjugation"]','medium','Neet Buddy',0,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('c5818c93-cd6c-4c3e-98e2-1ce3a90daced','e687e6cb-2dbc-4991-ac4e-054860d979c9','What is the Electromeric Effect (E effect)?','It is a TEMPORARY effect observed in multiple bonds (C=C, C=O) only in the presence of an attacking reagent. It involves the complete transfer of a shared pair of $\\pi$-electrons to one of the atoms.',NULL,NULL,'Temporary shift of pi electrons on demand.','["chemistry", "goc", "electromeric-effect"]','medium','Neet Buddy',1,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('9104ef2a-32d8-4718-bd6e-b1b0c02b567b','e687e6cb-2dbc-4991-ac4e-054860d979c9','State Markovnikov\'s Rule for addition reactions.','When an unsymmetrical reagent (like HX) adds to an unsymmetrical alkene, the negative part of the reagent attaches to the double-bonded carbon that has FEWER hydrogen atoms.',NULL,NULL,'"The rich get richer" - Carbon with more hydrogens gets the hydrogen.','["chemistry", "organic-reactions", "addition"]','medium','Neet Buddy',2,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('0aecb92b-cab1-4a79-9b87-4183afe718d3','e687e6cb-2dbc-4991-ac4e-054860d979c9','What are the shapes and bond angles associated with $sp^3$, $sp^2$, and $sp$ hybridized carbon atoms?','$sp^3$: Tetrahedral ($109.5^\\circ$)\n$sp^2$: Trigonal planar ($120^\\circ$)\n$sp$: Linear ($180^\\circ$)',NULL,NULL,'Think of methane, ethene, and ethyne.','["chemistry", "organic-chemistry", "hybridization", "class-11"]','medium','Neet Buddy',3,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('5c88e4f6-6588-45e8-b17f-9299265f5eb6','e687e6cb-2dbc-4991-ac4e-054860d979c9','How do you calculate the number of sigma ($\\sigma$) and pi ($\\pi$) bonds in an organic molecule?','Every single bond is 1 $\\sigma$ bond. Every double bond has 1 $\\sigma$ and 1 $\\pi$ bond. Every triple bond has 1 $\\sigma$ and 2 $\\pi$ bonds.',NULL,NULL,'Multiple bonds always contain exactly one sigma bond.','["chemistry", "organic-chemistry", "bonding"]','medium','Neet Buddy',4,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('12c4ee52-6f43-41f3-8100-dc9828ac67f9','e687e6cb-2dbc-4991-ac4e-054860d979c9','What is the decreasing order of priority for principal functional groups in IUPAC nomenclature?','$-\\text{COOH} > -\\text{SO}_3\\text{H} > -\\text{COOR} > -\\text{COCl} > -\\text{CONH}_2 > -\\text{CN} > -\\text{CHO} > >\\text{C=O} > -\\text{OH} > -\\text{NH}_2 > >\\text{C=C}< > -\\text{C}\\equiv\\text{C}-$',NULL,NULL,'Acids first, then derivatives, then aldehydes/ketones, alcohols, amines.','["chemistry", "nomenclature", "class-11"]','medium','Neet Buddy',5,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('6f7ffb64-58cc-4ea7-8224-0bfe3bde3a01','e687e6cb-2dbc-4991-ac4e-054860d979c9','Explain keto-enol tautomerism. Which form is generally more stable?','It is a dynamic equilibrium between a ketone/aldehyde (keto form) and an alcohol attached to a double bond (enol form). The keto form is usually more stable due to the high bond energy of the C=O bond.',NULL,NULL,'Shifting of an alpha-hydrogen and a double bond.','["chemistry", "isomerism", "class-11"]','medium','Neet Buddy',6,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('fec66f5a-ec2b-4cf7-aefd-4a38a6871166','e687e6cb-2dbc-4991-ac4e-054860d979c9','What is the essential condition for a molecule to show geometrical (cis-trans) isomerism?','1. Restricted rotation (e.g., around a C=C double bond or in a ring).\n2. Each atom involved in the restricted rotation must be bonded to two DIFFERENT atoms or groups.',NULL,NULL,'Look for C=C where terminal carbons don\'t have identical twins.','["chemistry", "isomerism", "stereoisomerism"]','medium','Neet Buddy',7,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('381408e9-ad0d-4e6a-871f-831d7ed7e040','e687e6cb-2dbc-4991-ac4e-054860d979c9','What is a chiral center (stereocenter) in organic molecules?','An $sp^3$ hybridized carbon atom bonded to four different atoms or groups. It lacks a plane of symmetry and results in optical activity.',NULL,NULL,'4 different neighbors = chiral.','["chemistry", "isomerism", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',8,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('9de3714f-6dfe-442e-81d1-4a9ff4e64530','e687e6cb-2dbc-4991-ac4e-054860d979c9','Differentiate between Homolytic and Heterolytic bond fission.','Homolytic: Symmetrical breaking where each atom takes one electron, forming Free Radicals. Requires heat/light.\nHeterolytic: Unsymmetrical breaking where one atom takes both electrons, forming Carbocations and Carbanions. Favored by polar solvents.',NULL,NULL,'Homo = equal sharing, Hetero = unequal sharing.','["chemistry", "goc", "reaction-mechanism"]','medium','Neet Buddy',9,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('667f2a3f-e262-4712-abb7-de17101749c6','e687e6cb-2dbc-4991-ac4e-054860d979c9','What is the stability order of alkyl carbanions and why?','Order: $\\text{Methyl} > 1^\\circ > 2^\\circ > 3^\\circ$.\nReason: Alkyl groups exert a +I effect, pushing electron density onto the already negative carbon, intensifying the charge and destabilizing the ion.',NULL,NULL,'Opposite of carbocations.','["chemistry", "goc", "carbanion"]','medium','Neet Buddy',10,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('11be0bd5-6d91-4ce1-9471-af2dfd17c643','e687e6cb-2dbc-4991-ac4e-054860d979c9','What is the stability order of Carbon Free Radicals?','Order: $3^\\circ > 2^\\circ > 1^\\circ > \\text{methyl}$.\nLike carbocations, they are electron-deficient and stabilized by hyperconjugation and +I effect of alkyl groups.',NULL,NULL,'Treat them like carbocations for stability rules.','["chemistry", "goc", "free-radicals"]','medium','Neet Buddy',11,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('d8735ee3-f862-4423-a377-de60545b96c2','e687e6cb-2dbc-4991-ac4e-054860d979c9','Define Electrophiles and Nucleophiles with examples.','Electrophiles: Electron-deficient species that attack electron-rich centers. Ex: $\\text{H}^+, \\text{NO}_2^+, \\text{BF}_3$.\nNucleophiles: Electron-rich species that attack electron-deficient centers. Ex: $\\text{OH}^-, \\text{CN}^-, \\text{NH}_3, \\text{H}_2\\text{O}$.',NULL,NULL,'Electro-phile = electron lover. Nucleo-phile = nucleus (+ve) lover.','["chemistry", "goc", "reagents"]','medium','Neet Buddy',12,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('e84e06cd-f8a5-4de7-b71b-677ac13a6df1','e687e6cb-2dbc-4991-ac4e-054860d979c9','What is the Inductive effect (I-effect) and is it temporary or permanent?','It is a PERMANENT effect involving the displacement of $\\sigma$ (sigma) electrons along a saturated carbon chain whenever an electron-withdrawing (-I) or electron-donating (+I) group is present. It weakens with distance.',NULL,NULL,'Transmitted through sigma bonds only.','["chemistry", "goc", "electronic-effects"]','medium','Neet Buddy',13,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('2d9330d1-1022-43ce-902d-66599c3f01cd','e687e6cb-2dbc-4991-ac4e-054860d979c9','List common +I (electron-donating) and -I (electron-withdrawing) groups.','+I groups: Alkyl groups ($-\\text{CH}_3, -\\text{C}_2\\text{H}_5, -\\text{C}(\\text{CH}_3)_3$).\n-I groups: $-NO_2, -CN, -COOH, -F, -Cl, -Br, -I, -OH, -OCH_3$.',NULL,NULL,'+I pushes electrons away, -I pulls electrons towards itself.','["chemistry", "goc", "inductive-effect"]','medium','Neet Buddy',14,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('bea83dcb-90bb-4935-972c-7431baf24a8d','e687e6cb-2dbc-4991-ac4e-054860d979c9','What is Resonance (Mesomeric) effect? Differentiate between +R and -R groups.','It is the polarity produced by the interaction of two $\\pi$ bonds or a $\\pi$ bond and a lone pair.\n+R groups: Donate electrons to the conjugated system (e.g., $-\\text{OH}, -\\text{NH}_2, -\\text{OR}, -\\text{Cl}$).\n-R groups: Withdraw electrons from the system (e.g., $-\\text{NO}_2, -\\text{CN}, -\\text{CHO}, -\\text{COOH}$).',NULL,NULL,'Conjugation is required. Look for lone pairs (+R) or multiple bonds to heteroatoms (-R).','["chemistry", "goc", "resonance"]','medium','Neet Buddy',15,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('36db948a-7f84-4c85-8fb1-969071c0837f','e687e6cb-2dbc-4991-ac4e-054860d979c9','What is metamerism? Give an example.','A type of structural isomerism due to unequal distribution of alkyl chains on either side of a polyvalent functional group (like $-O-, -S-, -NH-$). Example: Diethyl ether and Methyl propyl ether.','{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "What is metamerism? Give an example."}]}','{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "A type of structural isomerism due to unequal distribution of alkyl chains on either side of a polyvalent functional group (like $-O-, -S-, -NH-$). Example: Diethyl ether and Methyl propyl ether."}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\" xmlns:xlink=\\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\\" width=\\"1222\\" height=\\"654\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 1222 654\\">\\n  <image xlink:href=\\"data:image/png;base64,\\" x=\\"0\\" y=\\"0\\" width=\\"1222\\" height=\\"654\\"/>\\n</svg>"}]}','Same formula, different chains around the central heteroatom.','["chemistry", "isomerism", "class-11"]','medium','Neet Buddy',16,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('6bb25c9c-bcdf-436e-b74e-86e302c5ef7c','e687e6cb-2dbc-4991-ac4e-054860d979c9','State Saytzeff\'s (Zaitsev\'s) Rule for elimination reactions.','In a dehydrohalogenation ($\\beta$-elimination) reaction, if multiple alkenes can be formed, the major product is the MOST highly substituted alkene (the one with the greater number of alkyl groups attached to the C=C double bond).',NULL,NULL,'More substituted alkene = more stable product.','["chemistry", "organic-reactions", "elimination"]','medium','Neet Buddy',17,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('36d4a89b-d44b-44f5-a117-c853b8c72a85','e687e6cb-2dbc-4991-ac4e-054860d979c9','Why do carbocations undergo rearrangement reactions (like hydride or alkyl shifts)?','Carbocations rearrange to form a more stable carbocation intermediate (e.g., moving from a $1^\\circ$ or $2^\\circ$ carbocation to a $3^\\circ$ carbocation or a resonance-stabilized benzylic/allylic carbocation) before the nucleophile attacks.',NULL,NULL,'Seeking lower energy state (stability).','["chemistry", "goc", "reaction-mechanism", "rearrangement"]','medium','Neet Buddy',18,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('513b8f85-722d-4662-ad30-6efde2477b89','e687e6cb-2dbc-4991-ac4e-054860d979c9','Differentiate between Electrophilic Substitution and Nucleophilic Substitution based on characteristic substrates.','Electrophilic Substitution ($S_E$): Characteristic of Aromatic compounds (like Benzene), where an electrophile replaces a hydrogen atom.\nNucleophilic Substitution ($S_N$): Characteristic of Alkyl halides, where a nucleophile replaces the leaving group (halide).',NULL,NULL,'Benzene loves electrophiles. Alkyl halides love nucleophiles.','["chemistry", "organic-reactions", "substitution", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',19,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('70333582-99c7-40e2-8317-90ae5090067c','e687e6cb-2dbc-4991-ac4e-054860d979c9','What is the stability order of alkyl carbocations and what effects cause it?','Order: $3^\\circ > 2^\\circ > 1^\\circ > \\text{methyl}$.\nReason: Stabilized by the +I (Inductive) effect of alkyl groups and Hyperconjugation (more $\\alpha$-hydrogens = more stable).','{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "State the stability order of carbocations and the reasons."}]}','{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "3° > 2° > 1° > methyl. Stabilized by +I effect and hyperconjugation."}, {"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\" xmlns:xlink=\\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\\" width=\\"1438\\" height=\\"472\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 1438 472\\">\\n  <image xlink:href=\\"data:image/png;base64,\\" x=\\"0\\" y=\\"0\\" width=\\"1438\\" height=\\"472\\"/>\\n</svg>"}]}','More R groups = more electron donation = more stable positive charge.','["chemistry", "goc", "carbocation"]','medium','Neet Buddy',20,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('23630b67-2fba-416c-82d0-ce10a9d08db4','e8917704-7562-446f-99be-633fa9d53ff8','Which group of drugs primarily affects the cardiovascular system, and what is its source?','Drug: Cannabinoids (marijuana, hashish, charas, ganja).\nSource: Inflorescences of the plant *Cannabis sativa*.',NULL,NULL,'Cannabis affects the heart rate.','["neet", "biology", "human-health", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',0,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('33c1c57f-a68a-481c-bf31-0ef659dbdcab','e8917704-7562-446f-99be-633fa9d53ff8','Name the three main genera of fungi responsible for Ringworm in humans.','1. *Microsporum*\n2. *Trichophyton*\n3. *Epidermophyton*',NULL,NULL,'M-T-E fungi.','["neet", "biology", "human-health", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',1,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('64c1f6ab-d9ce-44aa-a2c1-89d6d06aeb89','e8917704-7562-446f-99be-633fa9d53ff8','Which specific vector is responsible for transmitting Dengue and Chikungunya?','The female *Aedes* mosquito.',NULL,NULL,'A different vector from Malaria (Anopheles).','["neet", "biology", "human-health", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',2,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('a9a2b24c-c755-428b-b27a-48c4ca6a9996','e8917704-7562-446f-99be-633fa9d53ff8','Name the causative agent and the standard diagnostic blood test for Typhoid fever.','Causative agent: *Salmonella typhi* (bacterium).\nDiagnostic test: Widal test.',NULL,NULL,'Typhoid Mary was a classic carrier of this pathogen.','["neet", "biology", "human-health", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',3,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('bda1a070-e0ea-4fa4-81de-86fa09f8e156','e8917704-7562-446f-99be-633fa9d53ff8','What is the infective stage of *Plasmodium* for humans, and where is it stored in the mosquito?','Infective stage: Sporozoites.\nStorage: They are stored in the salivary glands of the female *Anopheles* mosquito until a bite transfers them.',NULL,NULL,'Spores injected through saliva.','["neet", "biology", "human-health", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',4,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('ea93fc14-b063-4596-bf65-d4da92a65fa1','e8917704-7562-446f-99be-633fa9d53ff8','Describe the basic structure of an antibody molecule and state its formula.','An antibody is a Y-shaped protein complex made of four polypeptide chains: two identical Light (L) chains and two identical Heavy (H) chains, linked by disulfide bonds. \nFormula: H₂L₂.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "svg", "value": "<svg width=\\"200\\" height=\\"180\\" xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\"><path d=\\"M 100 160 L 100 100 L 60 40\\" stroke=\\"#1f77b4\\" stroke-width=\\"8\\" fill=\\"none\\"/><path d=\\"M 100 160 L 100 100 L 140 40\\" stroke=\\"#1f77b4\\" stroke-width=\\"8\\" fill=\\"none\\"/><path d=\\"M 50 80 L 30 50\\" stroke=\\"#ff7f0e\\" stroke-width=\\"8\\" fill=\\"none\\"/><path d=\\"M 150 80 L 170 50\\" stroke=\\"#ff7f0e\\" stroke-width=\\"8\\" fill=\\"none\\"/><line x1=\\"96\\" y1=\\"120\\" x2=\\"104\\" y2=\\"120\\" stroke=\\"black\\" stroke-width=\\"2\\"/><line x1=\\"75\\" y1=\\"75\\" x2=\\"60\\" y2=\\"65\\" stroke=\\"black\\" stroke-width=\\"2\\"/><line x1=\\"125\\" y1=\\"75\\" x2=\\"140\\" y2=\\"65\\" stroke=\\"black\\" stroke-width=\\"2\\"/><text x=\\"110\\" y=\\"150\\" font-size=\\"12\\" font-family=\\"sans-serif\\">Heavy Chain (H)</text><text x=\\"5\\" y=\\"50\\" font-size=\\"12\\" font-family=\\"sans-serif\\">Light Chain (L)</text></svg>"}]}','Think of a \'Y\' shape with 2 heavy and 2 light arms.','["neet", "biology", "immunology", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',5,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('17512c1d-6818-4756-b3d8-bb7479807ab6','e8917704-7562-446f-99be-633fa9d53ff8','Differentiate between Humoral Immune Response and Cell-Mediated Immunity (CMI).','Humoral Immunity: Mediated by B-lymphocytes producing antibodies in blood/lymph.\nCell-Mediated Immunity: Mediated by T-lymphocytes directly attacking infected/foreign cells (responsible for graft rejection).',NULL,NULL,'Humor = fluids (blood/antibodies). Cells = T-cells.','["neet", "biology", "immunology", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',6,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('827f7ce7-e237-466a-ad21-3ce668766882','e8917704-7562-446f-99be-633fa9d53ff8','Provide one natural and one artificial example of Passive Immunity.','Natural: IgA antibodies passed via colostrum (mother\'s milk) or IgG crossing the placenta to the fetus.\nArtificial: Anti-tetanus serum (ATS) or anti-venom injections given after a bite/injury.',NULL,NULL,'Passive means readymade antibodies are directly given.','["neet", "biology", "immunology", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',7,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('8eacb6f0-8db6-4923-8ca5-651304968eb1','e8917704-7562-446f-99be-633fa9d53ff8','What are Primary Lymphoid Organs? Give two examples in humans.','Primary lymphoid organs are the sites where immature lymphocytes differentiate into antigen-sensitive lymphocytes.\nExamples: Bone marrow (for B & T cells) and Thymus (maturation of T cells).',NULL,NULL,'The \'training grounds\' for lymphocytes.','["neet", "biology", "immunology", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',8,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('92840271-6b4e-4091-afe3-2e25226a1052','e8917704-7562-446f-99be-633fa9d53ff8','Define \'Metastasis\' and identify which type of tumor exhibits this property.','Metastasis is the most feared property of cancer, where tumor cells slough off and travel via blood or lymph to distant sites, starting new tumors.\nIt is exclusively exhibited by Malignant tumors (not benign).',NULL,NULL,'Meta = beyond, stasis = staying. Moving beyond the origin.','["neet", "biology", "human-health", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',9,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('19c7a5a6-a622-4e76-9f80-da6f96441b55','e8917704-7562-446f-99be-633fa9d53ff8','What is the source plant for Opioids (e.g., heroin/smack), and where are their specific receptors located?','Source: Extracted from the latex of the poppy plant (*Papaver somniferum*).\nReceptors: Located primarily in our Central Nervous System (CNS) and Gastrointestinal (GI) tract.',NULL,NULL,'Opioids bind in the CNS and slow down body functions.','["neet", "biology", "human-health", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',10,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('5fc27fdc-80f5-4812-9777-031a44806b79','e8917704-7562-446f-99be-633fa9d53ff8','How do Lactic Acid Bacteria (LAB) improve the nutritional quality of curd?','LAB partially digest milk proteins and coagulate them to form curd. They significantly improve nutritional quality by increasing the content of Vitamin B12.',NULL,NULL,'B12 is essential for nerve health and blood formation.','["neet", "biology", "microbes", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',11,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('c46b9a15-b11f-4511-a07b-2562709dbd8a','e8917704-7562-446f-99be-633fa9d53ff8','Match the bioactive molecule to its source microbe: Statins and Cyclosporin A.','Statins: Produced by *Monascus purpureus* (Yeast). Acts as a blood-cholesterol lowering agent.\nCyclosporin A: Produced by *Trichoderma polysporum* (Fungus). Acts as an immunosuppressive agent in organ transplants.',NULL,NULL,'Statins = Monascus, Cyclo = Trichoderma.','["neet", "biology", "microbes", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',12,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('56741fc9-f3ee-4a0d-a740-dfceba3fdefb','e8917704-7562-446f-99be-633fa9d53ff8','Which microbe is genetically engineered to produce Streptokinase, and what is its clinical use?','Microbe: *Streptococcus* bacterium.\nUse: It acts as a \'clot buster\' to remove blood clots from the blood vessels of patients who have suffered myocardial infarction (heart attacks).',NULL,NULL,'Streptococcus -> Streptokinase -> Clot buster.','["neet", "biology", "microbes", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',13,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('716726a4-42c1-4af9-a9e5-97e0c993a8b8','e8917704-7562-446f-99be-633fa9d53ff8','Define Biochemical Oxygen Demand (BOD). What does a high BOD indicate?','BOD is the amount of oxygen consumed if all the organic matter in one liter of water were oxidized by bacteria.\nA high BOD indicates a high level of organic matter (sewage) and a high polluting potential.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "svg", "value": "<svg width=\\"260\\" height=\\"160\\" xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\"><rect x=\\"0\\" y=\\"0\\" width=\\"260\\" height=\\"160\\" fill=\\"#f8f9fa\\" rx=\\"5\\"/><line x1=\\"40\\" y1=\\"130\\" x2=\\"240\\" y2=\\"130\\" stroke=\\"black\\" stroke-width=\\"2\\"/><line x1=\\"40\\" y1=\\"20\\" x2=\\"40\\" y2=\\"130\\" stroke=\\"black\\" stroke-width=\\"2\\"/><path d=\\"M 40 120 Q 60 20, 120 60 T 230 120\\" stroke=\\"#d62728\\" stroke-width=\\"3\\" fill=\\"none\\"/><path d=\\"M 40 30 Q 60 130, 120 80 T 230 30\\" stroke=\\"#1f77b4\\" stroke-width=\\"3\\" fill=\\"none\\"/><text x=\\"45\\" y=\\"45\\" fill=\\"#d62728\\" font-size=\\"12\\" font-weight=\\"bold\\" font-family=\\"sans-serif\\">BOD Spike</text><text x=\\"45\\" y=\\"115\\" fill=\\"#1f77b4\\" font-size=\\"12\\" font-weight=\\"bold\\" font-family=\\"sans-serif\\">DO Drop</text><text x=\\"90\\" y=\\"150\\" font-size=\\"12\\" font-family=\\"sans-serif\\">Direction of flow (Sewage input)</text></svg>"}]}','High BOD = Low Dissolved Oxygen (DO) = Dirty water.','["neet", "biology", "microbes", "ecology", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',14,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('1c05f084-1a22-443b-bb3d-40fd9830d2e8','e8917704-7562-446f-99be-633fa9d53ff8','Name the dominant methanogen found in anaerobic sludge digesters and cattle rumen. What gases do they produce?','Microbe: *Methanobacterium* (Methanogens).\nGases: Methane (CH₄, major component), along with CO₂ and H₂.',NULL,NULL,'Biogas is predominantly Methane.','["neet", "biology", "microbes", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',15,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('baa5e087-9579-46b4-8607-03654008c1c4','e8917704-7562-446f-99be-633fa9d53ff8','What are Baculoviruses, and why are they excellent biocontrol agents?','Baculoviruses (genus *Nucleopolyhedrovirus*) are pathogens that attack specific insects and arthropods.\nThey are excellent because they are species-specific, narrow-spectrum insecticides with no negative impacts on plants, mammals, birds, or non-target insects.',NULL,NULL,'Highly specific viral bio-pesticides.','["neet", "biology", "microbes", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',16,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('f4fe5c31-e2df-4b65-b96e-6dabd22cad00','e8917704-7562-446f-99be-633fa9d53ff8','What is Mycorrhiza? Name the dominant fungal genus and state its main benefit to the plant.','Mycorrhiza is a symbiotic association between fungi and the roots of higher plants.\nGenus: *Glomus*.\nBenefit: The fungus heavily absorbs phosphorus from the soil and passes it to the plant, and also provides resistance to root-borne pathogens.',NULL,NULL,'Myco = fungus, rhiza = root.','["neet", "biology", "microbes", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',17,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('a3d5fe27-50ca-4692-aab5-bd8652f1c8c7','e8917704-7562-446f-99be-633fa9d53ff8','Name the two pathogens responsible for Filariasis (Elephantiasis) and its vector.','Pathogens: *Wuchereria bancrofti* and *Wuchereria malayi* (filarial worms).\nVector: Female *Culex* mosquito.',NULL,NULL,'Causes chronic inflammation of lymphatic vessels of lower limbs.','["neet", "biology", "human-health", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',18,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('e1d8ebe1-9931-48cd-ac61-3b88d3d88bdd','e8917704-7562-446f-99be-633fa9d53ff8','Which microbe is commonly known as \'Baker\'s yeast\' and \'Brewer\'s yeast\', and what is its scientific name?','*Saccharomyces cerevisiae*. It is used for fermenting malted cereals and fruit juices to produce ethanol, and for fermenting dough to make bread.',NULL,NULL,'The most famous industrial yeast.','["neet", "biology", "microbes", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',19,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('cbaca02e-99ec-4688-88e2-6f0a732ca90d','e8917704-7562-446f-99be-633fa9d53ff8','Explain the core mechanism by which HIV leads to immunodeficiency in an infected person.','HIV enters Macrophages (which act as a virus factory) and Helper T-lymphocytes (T$_H$ cells). It replicates and destroys the T$_H$ cells. The progressive decrease in T$_H$ cell count cripples the immune system.',NULL,NULL,'It destroys the \'helpers\' of the immune system.','["neet", "biology", "human-health", "class-12"]','medium','Neet Buddy',20,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('bdd0d4bf-dad2-4066-90db-f5cdda79f72c','f69849c1-414d-4994-a023-a27c49f264c8','What does \'Dynamic Equilibrium\' mean in a chemical reaction?','The state where the rate of the forward reaction equals the rate of the backward reaction, resulting in no net change in macroscopic properties (concentration, color, pressure).',NULL,NULL,'Reactions don\'t stop; they just balance out.','["chemistry", "equilibrium", "class-11", "fundamentals"]','medium','Neet Buddy',0,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('78b5e639-3807-41af-a46e-85c3b3aeb288','f69849c1-414d-4994-a023-a27c49f264c8','Write the mathematical relationship between $K_p$ and $K_c$.','$K_p = K_c (RT)^{\\Delta n_g}$',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "text", "value": "Where $\\\\Delta n_g = (\\\\text{moles of gaseous products}) - (\\\\text{moles of gaseous reactants})$. $R$ is the gas constant and $T$ is temperature in Kelvin."}]}','Don\'t forget that $\\Delta n_g$ is for GASES only.','["chemistry", "equilibrium", "formulas"]','medium','Neet Buddy',1,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('e36b9464-1003-409a-8788-0abf4a3fb605','f69849c1-414d-4994-a023-a27c49f264c8','How do you use the reaction quotient ($Q_c$) to predict the direction of a reaction relative to $K_c$?','If $Q_c < K_c$, reaction shifts forward (right) to reach equilibrium.\nIf $Q_c > K_c$, reaction shifts backward (left) to reach equilibrium.\nIf $Q_c = K_c$, reaction is at equilibrium.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\' viewBox=\'0 0 350 120\'><line x1=\'50\' y1=\'60\' x2=\'300\' y2=\'60\' stroke=\'black\' stroke-width=\'3\'/><circle cx=\'175\' cy=\'60\' r=\'6\' fill=\'black\'/><text x=\'170\' y=\'85\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'14\' font-weight=\'bold\'>Kc</text><circle cx=\'100\' cy=\'60\' r=\'5\' fill=\'blue\'/><text x=\'90\' y=\'45\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'12\' fill=\'blue\'>Qc &lt; Kc</text><line x1=\'110\' y1=\'60\' x2=\'155\' y2=\'60\' stroke=\'blue\' stroke-width=\'2\' marker-end=\'url(#arrowB)\'/><text x=\'115\' y=\'80\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'10\' fill=\'blue\'>Shifts Right</text><circle cx=\'250\' cy=\'60\' r=\'5\' fill=\'red\'/><text x=\'230\' y=\'45\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'12\' fill=\'red\'>Qc &gt; Kc</text><line x1=\'240\' y1=\'60\' x2=\'195\' y2=\'60\' stroke=\'red\' stroke-width=\'2\' marker-end=\'url(#arrowR)\'/><text x=\'205\' y=\'80\' font-family=\'sans-serif\' font-size=\'10\' fill=\'red\'>Shifts Left</text><defs><marker id=\'arrowB\' viewBox=\'0 0 10 10\' refX=\'5\' refY=\'5\' markerWidth=\'6\' markerHeight=\'6\' orient=\'auto-start-reverse\'><path d=\'M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\' fill=\'blue\'/></marker><marker id=\'arrowR\' viewBox=\'0 0 10 10\' refX=\'5\' refY=\'5\' markerWidth=\'6\' markerHeight=\'6\' orient=\'auto-start-reverse\'><path d=\'M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\' fill=\'red\'/></marker></defs></svg>"}]}','Think of Q trying to \'catch up\' to K.','["chemistry", "equilibrium", "predictive"]','medium','Neet Buddy',2,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('9d8b97c0-2e49-4bf2-857c-335f8900cfab','f69849c1-414d-4994-a023-a27c49f264c8','If a chemical equation is reversed, and then multiplied by a factor of 2, what happens to its new equilibrium constant ($K\'$)?','First, reversing makes it $\\frac{1}{K}$. Multiplying by 2 squares it. \nResult: $K\' = \\left(\\frac{1}{K}\\right)^2 = \\frac{1}{K^2}$.',NULL,NULL,'Multiplier = Power. Reversing = Inverse.','["chemistry", "equilibrium", "calculations", "neet-favorite"]','medium','Neet Buddy',3,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('fd4c60c1-7c1c-4b14-ab8a-1244cdd43f43','f69849c1-414d-4994-a023-a27c49f264c8','Relate Standard Gibbs Free Energy ($\\Delta G^\\circ$) with the equilibrium constant ($K$).','$\\Delta G^\\circ = -RT \\ln K$ \nOR \n$\\Delta G^\\circ = -2.303 RT \\log K$',NULL,NULL,'$\\Delta G = 0$ at equilibrium, but $\\Delta G^\\circ$ determines the value of K!','["chemistry", "equilibrium", "thermodynamics"]','medium','Neet Buddy',4,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('1734c891-ea2d-47f3-bfbd-65778470c402','f69849c1-414d-4994-a023-a27c49f264c8','According to Le Chatelier\'s Principle, how does increasing the pressure affect an equilibrium involving gases?','Increasing pressure shifts the equilibrium towards the direction with **fewer moles of gas** (to reduce the pressure).',NULL,NULL,'Higher pressure favors the side that takes up less volume.','["chemistry", "equilibrium", "le-chatelier"]','medium','Neet Buddy',5,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('716309d5-2d81-4246-8ca0-754b115e7240','f69849c1-414d-4994-a023-a27c49f264c8','How does an increase in temperature affect an exothermic reaction vs an endothermic reaction?','Exothermic ($\\Delta H < 0$): Increasing temp shifts equilibrium backward (decreases K).\nEndothermic ($\\Delta H > 0$): Increasing temp shifts equilibrium forward (increases K).',NULL,NULL,'Treat Heat as a product (Exo) or reactant (Endo).','["chemistry", "equilibrium", "le-chatelier", "temperature"]','medium','Neet Buddy',6,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('3aebb85c-f2a9-4ba5-8454-0f4a9c5390dc','f69849c1-414d-4994-a023-a27c49f264c8','What is the effect of adding an inert gas at **constant volume** vs **constant pressure** on an equilibrium system?','Constant Volume: No effect on equilibrium (partial pressures remain unchanged).\nConstant Pressure: Volume of the container increases, shifting equilibrium towards the side with **more moles of gas**.',NULL,NULL,'Constant V = no change. Constant P = needs more space = more moles.','["chemistry", "equilibrium", "le-chatelier"]','medium','Neet Buddy',7,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('975183c8-d428-4982-96a8-c1aee8664512','f69849c1-414d-4994-a023-a27c49f264c8','Define Lewis Acids and Lewis Bases. Give one example of each.','Lewis Acid: Electron pair acceptor (e.g., $BF_3$, $AlCl_3$, or transition metal cations like $Ag^+$).\nLewis Base: Electron pair donor (e.g., $NH_3$, $H_2O$, $OH^-$).',NULL,NULL,'L.A. = Acceptor. L.B. = donor (has lone pairs).','["chemistry", "ionic-equilibrium", "acids-bases"]','medium','Neet Buddy',8,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('f278bbbf-e5ab-4784-8c30-051a165e11ab','f69849c1-414d-4994-a023-a27c49f264c8','What happens to the ionic product of water ($K_w$) and the pH of pure water when temperature increases?','$K_w$ increases (because the auto-ionization of water is endothermic). \nThe pH of pure water **decreases** (becomes $< 7$), but the water remains **neutral** because $[H^+] = [OH^-]$ at all temperatures.',NULL,NULL,'At $90^\\circ\\text{C}$, the neutral pH point is roughly 6.0!','["chemistry", "ionic-equilibrium", "ph", "neet-trick"]','medium','Neet Buddy',9,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('1f5568a8-cd22-4158-a3f4-17b12869e11f','f69849c1-414d-4994-a023-a27c49f264c8','State Ostwald\'s Dilution formula for the degree of dissociation ($\\alpha$) of a weak monobasic acid (like $CH_3COOH$).','$K_a = \\frac{C\\alpha^2}{1-\\alpha}$. \nIf $\\alpha$ is very small (typically $< 0.05$ or $5\\%$), it simplifies to $\\alpha = \\sqrt{\\frac{K_a}{C}}$.',NULL,NULL,'$\\alpha$ is inversely proportional to the square root of concentration.','["chemistry", "ionic-equilibrium", "formulas"]','medium','Neet Buddy',10,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('c4643b35-629c-4e5f-85f0-0acfb1104281','f69849c1-414d-4994-a023-a27c49f264c8','Give the formula for the pH of a salt formed by a Weak Acid and a Strong Base (e.g., $CH_3COONa$).','$pH = 7 + \\frac{1}{2}(pK_a + \\log C)$',NULL,NULL,'Strong Base dominates, so pH $> 7$. The \'+\' sign helps you remember it\'s basic.','["chemistry", "ionic-equilibrium", "salt-hydrolysis"]','medium','Neet Buddy',11,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('12d63010-af06-41d6-806e-d97d15b48d53','f69849c1-414d-4994-a023-a27c49f264c8','Give the formula for the pH of a salt formed by a Strong Acid and a Weak Base (e.g., $NH_4Cl$).','$pH = 7 - \\frac{1}{2}(pK_b + \\log C)$',NULL,NULL,'Strong Acid dominates, so pH $< 7$. The \'-\' sign helps you remember it\'s acidic.','["chemistry", "ionic-equilibrium", "salt-hydrolysis"]','medium','Neet Buddy',12,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('550fbf61-397e-4805-83f1-7012a073838c','f69849c1-414d-4994-a023-a27c49f264c8','Give the formula for the pH of a salt formed by a Weak Acid and a Weak Base (e.g., $CH_3COONH_4$).','$pH = 7 + \\frac{1}{2}(pK_a - pK_b)$',NULL,NULL,'Notice that the pH of a WA-WB salt is completely independent of its concentration ($C$)!','["chemistry", "ionic-equilibrium", "salt-hydrolysis"]','medium','Neet Buddy',13,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('57b96485-ff53-4348-b53a-a1a10b766627','f69849c1-414d-4994-a023-a27c49f264c8','What constitutes an Acidic Buffer and a Basic Buffer?','Acidic Buffer: A mixture of a **Weak Acid** and its salt with a strong base (e.g., $CH_3COOH + CH_3COONa$).\nBasic Buffer: A mixture of a **Weak Base** and its salt with a strong acid (e.g., $NH_4OH + NH_4Cl$).',NULL,NULL,'Must contain a WEAK species to absorb added $OH^-$ or $H^+$.','["chemistry", "ionic-equilibrium", "buffers"]','medium','Neet Buddy',14,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('f202d3f9-e534-4a5d-9c5c-b0e388e8f8c3','f69849c1-414d-4994-a023-a27c49f264c8','Write the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation for an acidic buffer.','$pH = pK_a + \\log \\frac{[\\text{Salt}]}{[\\text{Acid}]}$ \n(or $\\log \\frac{[\\text{Conjugate Base}]}{[\\text{Acid}]}$)',NULL,NULL,'For a basic buffer, it is $pOH = pK_b + \\log \\frac{[\\text{Salt}]}{[\\text{Base}]}$.','["chemistry", "ionic-equilibrium", "buffers", "formulas"]','medium','Neet Buddy',15,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('bbb2c3ce-5d84-4c59-a160-bd0b62a74e97','f69849c1-414d-4994-a023-a27c49f264c8','What is the condition for precipitation to occur using Solubility Product ($K_{sp}$) and Ionic Product ($Q_{sp}$)?','Precipitation occurs only when $Q_{sp} > K_{sp}$.',NULL,NULL,'If Q > K, the solution is supersaturated and wants to dump solid to restore equilibrium.','["chemistry", "ionic-equilibrium", "solubility"]','medium','Neet Buddy',16,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('124fb9a5-942b-413e-83f4-f9eadac9fe81','f69849c1-414d-4994-a023-a27c49f264c8','Relate the solubility ($s$) to $K_{sp}$ for a sparingly soluble salt of type $AB_2$ (e.g., $PbCl_2$, $CaF_2$).','$K_{sp} = 4s^3$ \n(Therefore, $s = \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{K_{sp}}{4}}$)',NULL,NULL,'$AB_2 \\rightleftharpoons A^{2+} + 2B^-$. Thus, $(s)(2s)^2 = 4s^3$.','["chemistry", "ionic-equilibrium", "solubility", "neet-shortcuts"]','medium','Neet Buddy',17,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('51c6e1e2-1f9d-4b8d-a633-00eadbd360d5','f69849c1-414d-4994-a023-a27c49f264c8','What is the Common Ion Effect and how does it affect the solubility of a sparingly soluble salt?','The suppression of the dissociation of a weak electrolyte by the addition of a strong electrolyte containing a common ion. \nIt **decreases** the solubility of the sparingly soluble salt.',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\" xmlns:xlink=\\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\\" width=\\"1030\\" height=\\"416\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 1030 416\\">\\n  <image xlink:href=\\"data:image/png;base64,\\" x=\\"0\\" y=\\"0\\" width=\\"1030\\" height=\\"416\\"/>\\n</svg>"}]}','Adding product pushes the equilibrium backward towards the solid precipitate (Le Chatelier\'s principle).','["chemistry", "ionic-equilibrium", "common-ion-effect"]','medium','Neet Buddy',18,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('cfe01489-b32d-4c4d-8d2a-66a17144e5a3','f69849c1-414d-4994-a023-a27c49f264c8','What is the conjugate base of $HSO_4^-$ and the conjugate acid of $NH_3$?','Conjugate base of $HSO_4^-$ is $SO_4^{2-}$ (remove an $H^+$).\nConjugate acid of $NH_3$ is $NH_4^+$ (add an $H^+$).',NULL,'{"content": [{"type": "svg", "value": "<svg xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\" xmlns:xlink=\\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\\" width=\\"1541\\" height=\\"415\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 1541 415\\">\\n  <image xlink:href=\\"data:image/png;base64,\\" x=\\"0\\" y=\\"0\\" width=\\"1541\\" height=\\"415\\"/>\\n</svg>"}]}','Acid gives $H^+$, Base takes $H^+$.','["chemistry", "ionic-equilibrium", "conjugate-pairs"]','medium','Neet Buddy',19,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929'),
('01cd490c-e344-4476-badb-5f11fd60a2c5','fc03a186-30f9-4afa-a5ec-fc38bff49757','What is the rest mass of a photon?','Zero.',NULL,NULL,'Photons always move at the speed of light ($c$) in a vacuum.','["physics", "dual-nature", "facts"]','medium','Neet Buddy',0,'2026-08-23 08:57:59.96929');
